HT_DATA.load("detail", {"tX4H_ctggYo":{"wy":"HN commenters repeatedly point people to it as the most accessible, developer-friendly introduction to geometric algebra and its use in graphics and physics.","q":{"x":"just watch this mind blowing video","i":22213627,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22213627,"author":"crubier","month":"2020-02","story":"Let's remove quaternions from every 3D engine (2018)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Mind-blowing case for geometric algebra as the right geometry framework","quote":"just watch this mind blowing video","text":"Ok look just watch this mind blowing video: [VIDEO] After that, you can’t argue that geometric algebra is not THE right way to do geometry. Projective geometry (in any dimension) is a subset of geometric algebra. Please watch it."},{"id":31085276,"author":"orlp","month":"2022-04","story":"Exponentially Better Rotations","stance":"rec","why":"Example applications of projective geometric algebra","quote":"The talk in general has a bunch of example applications","text":"> Quite bluntly, this all sounds like an attempt to reinvent euclidean geometry following a convoluted way. I mean, what does all this buy you that applying a subset of affine transformations (scaling, translation, rotation) to an orthogonal coordinate system doesn't give you already? It is attempting to reinvent Euclidean geometry, yes. I don't think it's convoluted though. To give a prime example, take the article we're commenting on: interpolating rotations. Or more generally: interpolating transformations. Just doing this with rotations without suffering gimbal locks already brings you to quaternions. Are quaternions 'convoluted'? The fact that all objects are native to the algebra means they're composable. Take for example this slide of the formula of a 4D torus in coordinates, and in 4D PGA: [LINK] The talk in general has a bunch of example applications: [VIDEO] Questions such as \"the intersection of this line and this plane\", \"the line through two points\" \"the circle where these two spheres intersect\", \"the point at the intersection of three planes\", \"the projection of this line on this plane\" and such are trivial, native (the resulting object is part of the algebra) and exception-free in geometric algebra. E.g. two planes always intersect, it just happens that the intersection is a line at infinity if they're parallel. The exact same code used to translate and rotate a point around the origin can be used to translate and rotate a line, or a plane around the origin. Al"},{"id":29512933,"author":"adamnemecek","month":"2021-12","story":"Quaternions","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on GA at SIGGRAPH 2019 by Bivector's creator","quote":"also gave a talk on GA at SIGGRAPH 2019","text":"You should check out the bivector community dedicated to geometric algebra [LINK] . Check out a demo [LINK] Join the discord [LINK] . Enki (the guy behind bivector) also gave a talk on GA at SIGGRAPH 2019 [VIDEO]"},{"id":28051151,"author":"adamnemecek","month":"2021-08","story":"Quaternion Quantum Mechanics [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Siggraph 2019 talk by geometric algebra community founder","quote":"gave a talk at Siggraph2019","text":"Reasoning about space and time is just so much nicer in geometric algebra. There's a new cool community on geometric algebra [LINK] The founder (Steven De Keninck aka enki) gave a talk at Siggraph2019 [VIDEO] You should join the discord [LINK]"},{"id":28253499,"author":"javajosh","month":"2021-08","story":"Manim: An animation engine for explanatory math videos","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why geometric algebra beats matrices, vectors, complex numbers","quote":"He has a good video from SIGGRAPH 2019","text":"Well I've been on a real Geometric Algebra (aka Clifford Algebra) kick lately, and ran across ganja.js [1]. It's a single no deps file that is...impressive. 120k uncompressed, and with it you can construct any degree algebra (including the more esoteric hyperbolic/parabolic ones), render to canvas, svg or webgl(!). It also includes a clever little DSL parser and interpreter (it overloads the scientific notation to name basis vectors!) that lets you construct more complex things from simple things using various kinds of products. The author, Steven De Keninck, is quite impressive as well, having got his start in the demoscene some time ago. He has a good video from SIGGRAPH 2019 [2] that explains why this algebra is better than [matrices, tensors, vectors, complex numbers]. Of particular interest (to me anyway) is the 2D projective geometry. I don't want to oversell it, but ganja is fucking amazing and there is a great deal I want to do with it. For one, I'd like to recapitulate my physics degree with it. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25327212,"author":"adamnemecek","month":"2020-12","story":"Vanishing zeroes for geometric algebra in Rust","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on GA at SIGGRAPH 2019 by Bivector's creator","quote":"also gave a talk on GA at SIGGRAPH 2019","text":"You should check out the bivector community dedicated to geometric algebra [LINK] . Check out a demo [LINK] Join the discord [LINK] . Enki (the guy behind bivector) also gave a talk on GA at SIGGRAPH 2019 [VIDEO]"},{"id":25471928,"author":"adamnemecek","month":"2020-12","story":"Ganja.js: Geometric Algebra Generator for JavaScript, C++, C#, Rust, Python","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on GA at SIGGRAPH 2019 by Bivector's creator","quote":"also gave a talk on GA at SIGGRAPH 2019","text":"You should check out the bivector community dedicated to geometric algebra [LINK] . Check out a demo [LINK] Join the discord [LINK] . Enki (the guy behind bivector) also gave a talk on GA at SIGGRAPH 2019 [VIDEO]"},{"id":25477286,"author":"these_are_not_t","month":"2020-12","story":"Ganja.js: Geometric Algebra Generator for JavaScript, C++, C#, Rust, Python","stance":"rec","why":"Very interesting presentation of geometric algebra","quote":"also made a very interesting presentation of geometric algebra","text":"I think that simple should be understood here as easy to use . As a crude analogy, the mechanical principles underlying a car with an automatic shift aren't simple, but the abstraction it provides makes driving much easier to learn. A more mathematically grounded example could be the complex plane compared to the real line: when encountered for the first time, it is not simple in any way, but it offers a clean solution to many (originally real valued) problems (perhaps by the fact that it is algebraically closed?). NB: the author of this library also made a very interesting presentation of geometric algebra (using this library as vehicle) for SIGGRAPH 2019: [VIDEO] It has been posted a few times on HN before."},{"id":25143843,"author":"adamnemecek","month":"2020-11","story":"All Hail Geometric Algebra","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on GA at SIGGRAPH 2019 by Bivector's creator","quote":"also gave a talk on GA at SIGGRAPH 2019","text":"You should check out the bivector community [LINK] . Check out a demo [LINK] Join the discord [LINK] . Enki (the guy behind bivector) also gave a talk on GA at SIGGRAPH 2019 [VIDEO]"},{"id":24399014,"author":"ivan_ah","month":"2020-09","story":"Geometric Algebra for Python","stance":"rec","why":"Background on GA and applications like projections and rotations","quote":"provide some more background on GA and its applications","text":"Here is a video [VIDEO] and the associated HN thread [LINK] that provide some more background on GA and its applications. I think you're generally right that GA is a rebranding of concepts we already knew about, however representing them in a uniform manner seems like a useful thing to do. The at t=4083 in the video they show some interesting examples of projections, rotations, and other geometric operations that can be done quite simply [VIDEO]"},{"id":23702427,"author":"adamnemecek","month":"2020-07","story":"Intuitive Guide to Maxwell's Equations","stance":"rec","why":"SIGGRAPH talk on geometric algebra","quote":"Watch the SIGGRAPH talk","text":"It's one equation in geometric algebra. [LINK] Also there is a new community for geometric algbera people [LINK] . Check out the demo [LINK] Watch the SIGGRAPH talk [VIDEO] Join the discord [LINK]"},{"id":23115992,"author":"aabajian","month":"2020-05","story":"4D Toys: how objects bounce and roll in 4D – paper accepted to SIGGRAPH 2020","stance":"rec","why":"SIGGRAPH 2019 presentation on geometric algebra","quote":"checkout the presentation on Geometric Algebra from 2019 SIGGRAPH","text":"If you like this, then checkout the presentation on Geometric Algebra from 2019 SIGGRAPH: [VIDEO] You can then play around with objects in 3D, 4D, and beyond: [LINK]"},{"id":23279048,"author":"aabajian","month":"2020-05","story":"Ask HN: What startup/technology is on your 'to watch' list?","stance":"rec","why":"Unifies hard physics problems in a common framework","quote":"much more understandable","text":"Geometric algebra: [VIDEO] It makes a lot of hard physics problems (Maxwell's equations, relativity theory, quantum mechanics) much more understandable and (I'm told) unifies them in a common framework. I think it will help your average developer become comfortable with these areas of physics."},{"id":22790218,"author":"cs702","month":"2020-04","story":"Ask HN: What are you learning?","stance":"rec","why":"Developer-friendly intro with cleaner, simpler code","quote":"has a good, developer-friendly introduction to the subject","text":"The following video has a good, developer-friendly introduction to the subject that should answer your question. Take a look in particular at the code snippets that are sprinkled throughout the video and notice how much cleaner, shorter, simpler, less computationally costly, and \"universal\"/\"exception-free\" the code becomes: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24399216,"author":"greggman3","month":"2020-09","story":"Geometric Algebra for Python","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"AFAICT the proponents of GA claim it explains lots of things that don't make a whole lot of sense without it. One example is why you multiply normals by the world_inverse_tranpose. Apparently the reason is because normals are actually bivectors, not directional vectors as they are usually explained. another is that at least in code, they apparently solve lots of issues that other methods don't See: [LINK] [LINK] and: [LINK] and [VIDEO]"},{"id":24041906,"author":"guerby","month":"2020-08","story":"Who Is Alexander Grothendieck? (2008) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Geometric algebra for simplifying game engines","quote":"Geometric algebra could (will?) be used to simplify game engines","text":"Geometric algebra could (will?) be used to simplify game engines, see Siggraph2019 Geometric Algebra: [VIDEO] Web site: [LINK]"},{"id":22811703,"author":"jiggawatts","month":"2020-04","story":"Why is Maxwell's theory so hard to understand? (2007) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Maxwell's theory is hard to understand because it's based on an almost-but-not-quite-appropriate algebra. It's like stringly-typed programming. It works , but it's messy and hard to understand. Maxwell's set of coupled differential vector electromagnetic equations simplify to a hilariously short single equation in Geometric Algebra. A random Google turned up this paper comparing classic EM and the GA formulation. It's not even the simplest possible representation, because that uses natural units and a 4D GA to basically condense the entire set of EM theory into about 4 characters worth of equation that is fully relativistic for free: [LINK] It's almost a joke. To me it's reminiscent of looking at beginner programmers. You see them do crazy things like calculate a date \"next month\" by taking apart the pretty-printed date string, parsing to find the month, realising that sometimes the day part is one digit and sometimes it's two, then having to worry about m-d-y or d-m-y formats, building little tables of \"days per month\" and leap years... and so on. They can write pages and pages of error free code and it's still Wrong because the correct way is to just call \"thedatevar.AddMonth(1);\" and be done with it. PS: 3D game engine vector algebra libraries have all of this in common with the physics maths. Things like the cross product being bizarre, having to pick a basis, not being able to interpolate rotations, gymbal lock, rounding error, having different maths for 2D and 3D, a bun"}],"r":[["ymaWq5yZIYM",1],["SA8ZBJWo73E",1],["pqzcCfUglws",1],["t705r8ICkRw",1],["KUdHIatS36A",1],["nsJZ_kKjXcE",1],["1tSqSMOyNFE",1],["Y9FZ4igNxNA",1],["4moyKUHApq4",1],["PFDu9oVAE-g",1],["t4vKPhjcMZg",1],["5v1Yg6XejyE",1],["eGKFTUuJppU",1],["GL77oOnrPzY",1]]},"AvEqfg2sIH0":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a view of buildings as dynamic systems, with lessons for software architecture and long-term maintenance.","q":{"x":"It changed my view of a building from a static thing to a dynamic system, changing over time.","i":16612101,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":23761120,"author":"monkeypizza","month":"2020-07","story":"Whole Earth Software Catalog (1984)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clear amateur analysis of how the US has built over a century","quote":"It really opened my eyes to the importance of buildings.","text":"Stewart Brand has done lots of good stuff. My favorite is the 6 part BBC series on \"How Buildings Learn\". It really opened my eyes to the importance of buildings. Very relevant to the frequent discussions here about zoning, design, etc. But also just an amazing, clear, well-informed amateur analysis of how the US has been doing in this field over the last 100 years. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19604689,"author":"lqet","month":"2019-04","story":"Paint Is Colored Glue","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent BBC series on how buildings change over time","quote":"it cannot be mentioned too often. If you haven't seen it, please please do.","text":"Ah, yes, I watched this series a few years ago and just realized that it may have inspired my post above a bit. PS: I just shamelessly throw in another excellent BBC documentary series here: How Buildings Learn, by Stewart Brand [0]. I sometimes see it mentioned here on HN, but it cannot be mentioned too often. If you haven't seen it, please please do. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18087439,"author":"asplake","month":"2018-09","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best documentaries you've seen?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Documentary with takeaways for software architecture and maintainability, featuring Christopher Alexander","quote":"Definite takeaways for software architecture, and includes appearances by Christopher Alexander, a key instigator of the patterns movement.","text":"How Buildings Learn, based on (and in some ways better than) the Stewart Brand book. Definite takeaways for software architecture, and includes appearances by Christopher Alexander, a key instigator of the patterns movement. Produced by the BBC in the 90's, now on Youtube - [VIDEO] Turns out that many architects of the built environment are just as prone as those of the software kind to not caring about the usability and maintainability of their systems, let alone how how they will evolve over extended periods of time."},{"id":20784970,"author":"paloaltokid","month":"2019-08","story":"Japanese architects who treated buildings like living organisms","stance":"rec","why":"Video series version of the book, relevant to long-term software maintenance","quote":"Stewart Brand also made a video series","text":"That's a SUPER book. Everyone in software should read it. The primary expense in software is long-term maintenance, not initial construction. Stewart Brand also made a video series: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17026229,"author":"steveeq1","month":"2018-05","story":"Stewart Brand Changed the World, Twice","stance":"rec","why":"Video documentary of the book, hosted by Brand","quote":"Saw the documentary and now reading the book, it's good.","text":"video documentary of the book with Stewart Brand hosting for anyone that's interested: [VIDEO] Saw the documentary and now reading the book, it's good."},{"id":16612101,"author":"wpietri","month":"2018-03","story":"Structural engineers are unsung heroes","stance":"rec","why":"Stewart Brand's series on buildings as dynamic systems changing over time","quote":"It changed my view of a building from a static thing to a dynamic system, changing over time.","text":"Ooh, that reminds me of another excellent book on failure, Sidney Dekker's \"Field Guide to Understanding Human Error\": [LINK] It's about investigating airplane crashes, and in particular two different paradigms for understanding failure. It deeply changed how I think and talk about software bugs, and especially how I do retrospectives. I strongly recommend it. And the article made me think of Stewart Brand's \"How Buildings Learn\": [LINK] It changed my view of a building from a static thing to a dynamic system, changing over time. The BBC later turned it into a 6-part series, which I haven't seen, but which the author put up on YouTube, starting here: [VIDEO] I especially like that in the comments he writes: \"Anybody is welcome to use anything from this series in any way they like. Please don’t bug me with requests for permission. Hack away. Do credit the BBC, who put considerable time and talent into the project.\""},{"id":13669836,"author":"rwmj","month":"2017-02","story":"What makes the perfect office?","stance":"rec","why":"BBC series on how buildings evolve, covering office design","quote":"There's a really good BBC TV series about this topic","text":"There's a really good BBC TV series about this topic, which also covers the MIT Radio Lab that Tim mentions: [VIDEO] \"How Buildings Learn\" (1997)"},{"id":6329260,"author":"shavenwarthog2","month":"2013-09","story":"Glare from London 'fryscraper' blamed for melting cars","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on how buildings adapt to how people use them","quote":"A building isn't made for architects, it's for people to use","text":"Steward Brand has a documentary and book on \"How Buildings Learn\". A building isn't made for architects, it's for people to use, and people over time are going to use their building for different, often very different things. Even such trivial things like \"roof shouldn't leak\" can be handwaved around. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25229170,"author":"lqet","month":"2020-11","story":"Connections by James Burke (1978)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I fear that another BBC favorite of mine, How Buildings Learn [0], is too modern (90ies) to have been produced on film... [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":9121292,"author":"falsedan","month":"2015-02","story":"Rethinking office space","stance":"reference","why":"Episode about buildings as statements that neglect their occupants","quote":"about buildings as statements which turn out to neglect the people who occupy them","text":"Strongly reminded of How Buildings Learn's first episode[1], about buildings as statements which turn out to neglect the people who occupy them. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["jVoSQP2yUYA",1],["j-vJxez9UF8",1],["jAhiMCSvtCc",1],["TwRjO3kHxU4",1],["7jPKEyM44GU",1],["VjE0Kdfos4Y",1],["98LdFA-_zfA",1],["iGbC5j4pG9w",1],["yYAw79386WI",1],["xP5-iIeKXE8",1]]},"dCuZkaaou0Q":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it whenever someone asks how to read type-system or CS notation in papers.","q":{"x":"Excellent talk by Guy Steele explaining the history of maths/comp notations","i":31605947,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31605947,"author":"amar-laksh","month":"2022-06","story":"Ask HN: How to get better knowledge about math notations in computer science","stance":"strong_rec","why":"History of math and computer science notation, pointing to sources.","quote":"Excellent talk by Guy Steele explaining the history of maths/comp notations","text":"Excellent talk by Guy Steele explaining the history of maths/comp notations that can probably lead you to the right path through original sources: [VIDEO] Then have a look at this extremely underrated talk by Alexander Stepanov for basically falling in love with maths (he gives a long view of maths in general adorned with beautiful commentary): [LINK]"},{"id":29709430,"author":"earleybird","month":"2021-12","story":"Mathematical Notation, Past and Future (2000)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Deeper exposition on language and notation from Guy Steele.","quote":"Guy Steele[0] is the definitive authority :-)","text":"For a deeper exposition on language and notation, Guy Steele[0] is the definitive authority :-) - with some discussion here[1]. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":23264400,"author":"Jach","month":"2020-05","story":"Students are failing AP tests because the College Board can’t handle HEIC images","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great talk on computer science metanotation.","quote":"Guy Steele gave a great talk a few years ago on","text":"The context here isn't the average American, but a high school student taking an AP test involving writing math -- where scoring well counts for the same credit as having passed the equivalent entry level course(s) in college. Besides, math is already pseudo language on top of pseudo language. Learning notation is part of math, I'll concede learning to type an expression on a computer is a different notation than writing it with pen, but if you can't learn that, why learn math? Each domain does its own thing too and isn't even necessarily consistent. Guy Steele gave a great talk a few years ago on \"Computer Science Metanotation\", and that's just one area of math: [VIDEO] (Edit: And to conclude here, learning to read and write with some notation tends to be easy, it's learning what the math actually means that is the hard part.)"},{"id":37140405,"author":"dan-robertson","month":"2023-08","story":"How should I read type system notation?","stance":"rec","why":"Names and explains type-system notation like inference rule diagrams.","quote":"There was a Guy Steele talk a while ago on this topic.","text":"There was a Guy Steele talk a while ago on this topic. He also gives searchable names for some of the things (eg the ‘2d inference rule diagrams’). He calls it ‘computer science metanotation’, though I think it’s more of a PLT thing. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36784047,"author":"MathMonkeyMan","month":"2023-07","story":"Coroutines for Go","stance":"rec","why":"One of several good Guy Steele talks, on languages.","quote":"There are a few good talks that he gives","text":"Guy Steele Jr. worked on a language called \"Fortress.\" There are a few good talks that he gives: - (toy problem) [LINK] - (parallelism) [LINK] - (languages) [VIDEO]"},{"id":18348150,"author":"Jach","month":"2018-10","story":"Clojure at Netflix (2013) [slides]","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the untyped metanotation CS papers use to describe languages.","quote":"You would probably enjoy this talk by Guy Steele where he discusses the language of mathematics that computer science people use in their papers","text":"You would probably enjoy this talk by Guy Steele where he discusses the language of mathematics that computer science people use in their papers (Computer Science Metanotation) to talk about other languages. Funnily it's untyped and lacking a rigorous specification... [VIDEO]"},{"id":15633640,"author":"hyperion2010","month":"2017-11","story":"Problems of Traditional Math Notation (2004)","stance":"rec","why":"Guy Steele on computer science notation and its issues.","quote":"Guy Steel's talk at Clojure Conj [0] on computer science type notation touches on related issues and is a great watch.","text":"Not directly math but Guy Steel's talk at Clojure Conj [0] on computer science type notation touches on related issues and is a great watch. 0. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15595551,"author":"hellofunk","month":"2017-10","story":"Effective Programs: 10 Years of Clojure","stance":"rec","why":"Guy Steele talk on computer science metanotation.","quote":"Then you better stay away from another talk at the same conference, by Guy Steele [0] !","text":"> I didn't really understand his talk I think he was too abstract Then you better stay away from another talk at the same conference, by Guy Steele [0] ! [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37764940,"author":"ceving","month":"2023-10","story":"Strong typing, a hill I'm willing to die on","stance":"reference","why":"Steele notes type theorists use an untyped notation themselves.","quote":"Don't you think it is ironic that type theorists who want to talk about strongly typed languages talk to themselves with an untyped language","text":"Guy L. Steele: \"Don't you think it is ironic that type theorists who want to talk about strongly typed languages talk to themselves with an untyped language\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":37141279,"author":"carapace","month":"2023-08","story":"How should I read type system notation?","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on the ambiguous, unspecified notation used across computer science.","quote":"","text":"You beat me to it. > The most popular programming language in computer science has no compiler, interpreter, or complete specification. It is remarkably concise. It grew over decades; today, dozens of variations are in use. Its complexity has reached the point where it needs to be re-explained every time it is used—but too often it is not; instead, each variant is more or less taken for granted. This has led to ambiguities and inconsistencies. Much effort has been spent in hand-translating, in both directions, between this language and other languages that do have compilers, primarily for the purpose of implementing yet other languages. => [LINK] Guy Steele => [LINK] \"A Cobbler's Child\" talk at Code Mesh 2017 => [LINK] \"A Cobbler's Child\" (video on youtube) => [VIDEO] \"It's Time for a New Old Language\" (video on youtube) => [LINK] Discussion on HN => [LINK] Slides To me this seems like a pretty sorry state of affairs. The folks trying the hardest to speak precisely about the most precise human art use ambiguous and inconsistent notation. wat?"},{"id":24655920,"author":"bpyne","month":"2020-10","story":"Programming language notation is a barrier to entry","stance":"reference","why":"Development of the computer science metalanguage.","quote":"He presents the development of the Computer Science Metalanguage.","text":"Ironically, I ran into this talk by Guy Steele today. He presents the development of the Computer Science Metalanguage. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23142187,"author":"carapace","month":"2020-05","story":"In Further Praise of Dependent Types","stance":"reference","why":"Guy Steele names and explains computer science metanotation.","quote":"arcane knowledge that is seldom reified","text":"It's part of an arcane knowledge that is seldom reified. \"It's Time for a New Old Language\" – Guy Steele [video] (youtube.com) [VIDEO] [LINK] He calls it \"Computer Science Metanotation\"."}],"r":[["P76Vbsk_3J0",1],["stxVBJem3Rs",1],["FjHJ7FmV0M4",1],["495nCzxM9PI",1],["KZokQov_aH0",1],["5TFDG-y-EHs",1],["ftcIcn8AmSY",1],["dPK6t7echuA",1],["0wtvQZijPzg",1],["vyenmLqJQjs",1]]},"QG0Apol-oj0":{"wy":"HN engineers cite Hartley as the intuitive, field-based alternative to cargo-cult PCB advice, especially on differential pair routing, reference planes and EMI.","q":{"x":"this is absolutely worth your time if you design PCBs.","i":35639927,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":47933799,"author":"dgroshev","month":"2026-04","story":"How I leared what a decoupling capacitor is for, the hard way","stance":"strong_rec","why":"A lecture the commenter counts among favourites ever","quote":"which is still one of my all time favourite lectures ever.","text":"I found Phil's lab content [1] [2] indispensable for just this. Phil is a great communicator and gives in-depth explanations, so I didn't just watch most of his youtube, but also bought his mixed signals course and was very happy with it. Phil also recommends this lecture in one of his videos [3], which is still one of my all time favourite lectures ever. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":44549063,"author":"aaronmdjones","month":"2025-07","story":"Vibe-Coding a PCB – surprisingly good","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Part of a recommended Hartley video sequence","quote":"they are all worth watching in this order.","text":"I have some further video recommendations: [LINK] (\"How to achieve proper grounding\") [LINK] (\"Keys to control noise, interference, and EMI in PCBs\") [VIDEO] (\"What your differential pairs wish you knew\") [LINK] (\"Secrets of PCB optimization\") These are all by Rick Hartley (from GP's comment) and they are all worth watching in this order."},{"id":36107667,"author":"mordae","month":"2023-05","story":"The Mysterious 50 Ohm Impedance: Where It Came from and Why We Use It","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains signals traveling on high-speed boards","quote":"go watch Rick Hartley.","text":"The article is not that good, but whatever. I am new to electronics myself and quite fascinated how has the art progressed in the past hundred or so years. There are people remembering \"we were doing it wrong\". Incredible. Anyway, if you are interested in signals traveling inside those cute green multi-GHz boards you make your living off, go watch Rick Hartley. [LINK] (How to Achieve Proper Grounding) [VIDEO] (What your Differential Pairs Wish You Knew)"},{"id":35639927,"author":"thrtythreeforty","month":"2023-04","story":"High Speed Printed Circuit Board (PCB) Design Guidelines","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Intuitive explanation instead of cargo cult PCB advice","quote":"this is absolutely worth your time if you design PCBs.","text":"Basically, go watch every Rick Hartley video on YouTube. He is awesome at explaining. It will become intuitive instead of being a bunch of cargo cult advice. (Normally I dislike long videos but this is absolutely worth your time if you design PCBs.) [VIDEO]"},{"id":41511199,"author":"aaronmdjones","month":"2024-09","story":"The magic of DC-DC voltage conversion (2023)","stance":"rec","why":"Material on plane splits and reference planes","quote":"Some interesting material on the subject","text":"> so wouldn't it be better to put power/ground on the outer layers and keep signals in the middle? Signals must never cross a break or split in the plane they're referencing (usually 0V or \"\"ground\"\"). This creates huge EMI problems. Your proposal would have signals on layer 2 crossing a split in the ground plane on layer 1 (that split caused by power traces). Some interesting material on the subject: [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":35184802,"author":"datenwolf","month":"2023-03","story":"PCIe for Hackers: The Diffpair Prelude","stance":"rec","why":"Longer talk on differential pair routing myths","quote":"If you want a longer talk on the topic","text":"> First off, you want to keep both of the pair’s signals close to each other throughout their length. The closer the two signals are, the better external interference cancellation works, and the less noise they radiate (…) Wrong! This is a myth that's stubbornly difficult to weed out. The coupling between the wires of a pair is only ~12% the amount of coupling to the reference plane. On a PCB it's best to treat each signal on its own and route it as a single ended, coplanar waveguide surrounded by reference planes. This allows to use far thicker widths, which reduces inductance, which also reduces the coupling to radiating EM fields. If you want a longer talk on the topic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38813621,"author":"MrBuddyCasino","month":"2023-12","story":"The Art and Science of PCB Design","stance":"reference","why":"Hartley's opinion that most application notes are incorrect","quote":"I avoid application notes as if they were the plague, because frankly, more than 95% of them are incorrect.","text":"> But focus on the two most important words in EE: REFERENCE DESIGNS. „I avoid application notes as if they were the plague, because frankly, more than 95% of them are incorrect.“ [VIDEO]"},{"id":29600434,"author":"foxfluff","month":"2021-12","story":"I bought 1000 meters of wire to settle a physics debate [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Hartley's claim that he never length-matches differential pairs","quote":"I have never in my life length matched the two lines of a differential pair, including at 10 GHz. Never.\" - Rick Hartley","text":"\"I have never in my life length matched the two lines of a differential pair, including at 10 GHz. Never.\" - Rick Hartley [VIDEO]"},{"id":29601273,"author":"dkbrk","month":"2021-12","story":"I bought 1000 meters of wire to settle a physics debate [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Referenced earlier as source for fields-not-wires view of signals","quote":"","text":"> He claims that the way EE is taught is a \"lie\" The thing is: this is sort of true. Now, all electrical engineers are very familiar with transmission line theory, that's pretty much their bread and butter. And all EEs know that if you're not working with well-defined transmission lines (like coaxial cables), you need to use a field solver. 2D field solvers are often sufficient, but if not 3D field solvers can and will be used. And then most of those same EEs, despite having just used a field solver which clearly shows that all the power is in the fields, which are in the dielectric space between the conductors, persist in using the mental model that electrical power moves in wires. This isn't just a pedantic quibble. There are real, practical effects. If you're designing a PCB and you have two signal lines with overlapping fields, those signals are going to couple, which will create common mode current, which will cause an EMI problem. You can stop those signals coupling by making them reference different ground planes, which makes the fields no longer overlap. If you route a signal line from one side of a ground plane to the other, you have to provide a path for the fields to get to the other side of the ground plane (i.e. \"route\" the dielectric, generally with a ground via), because if you don't, they will find their own path anyway and you won't like the results. If you persist in thinking that electrical power flows through wires, these sorts of effects are mysterious an"},{"id":29601977,"author":"dkbrk","month":"2021-12","story":"I bought 1000 meters of wire to settle a physics debate [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Hartley says most engineers don't understand EMI fundamentals","quote":"at 1:00:23 in that same video I linked before ( ), Rick Hartley says this","text":"> Is there really an epidemic of EE's who know how to use a field solver, but don't know to consider coupling between signal lines? Now, I'm not an EE myself, just someone who took undergraduate electrodynamics, decided to read up the subject, and found some truly excellent videos on youtube. But, at 1:00:23 in that same video I linked before ( [VIDEO] ), Rick Hartley says this: > I spend most of my consulting time solving EMI problems because most of the engineers I meet have no clue about any of this. My job is so easy and I make such a ridiculous amount of money doing it. It's just unbelievable; I solve most EMI problems by simply adding returned vias to boards or changing the positions of decoupling caps, I mean the things are so simple and ridiculous it's amazing and if these guys would educate themselves they wouldn't need to hire me. So I gather that there is a problem. It's not that EEs don't know to consider coupling between signal lines, but because most of them persist in thinking that signals travel in wires, rather than in the fields, they don't understand when coupling will occur and when it won't. Sure, they can look at the result of the field solver and realize they have a problem, but without thinking in terms of fields they don't know how to solve it. So, they fix it either with trial-and-error until the field solver is happy, or by following design patterns that are passed down as an oral tradition, but without actually understanding why it works or what th"}],"r":[["ySuUZEjARPY",2]]},"7ttG90raCNo":{"wy":"Strongly recommended whenever HN discusses CGI, practical effects, or trusting behind-the-scenes footage.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend this 5-part essay series","i":46238167,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":49014778,"author":"Fr0styMatt88","month":"2026-07","story":"'The Odyssey' Proves It: Audiences Want Movies Made the Hard Way","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Series debunking no-CGI claims and defending VFX artists.","quote":"this series is HIGHLY worth a watch","text":"On the specific no/low-CGI bent — I find the obsession with using this (mostly false) claim so utterly disrespectful to the people in VFX that have poured thousands upon thousands of hours of their lives into the craft, only to be actively derided (or outright denied) by producers hoping to capitalise on whatever this weird obsession is. For anyone that hasn’t seen it but is interested in the topic, this series is HIGHLY worth a watch: “NO CGI” is really just INVISIBLE CGI - 1 [VIDEO]"},{"id":46238167,"author":"troupo","month":"2025-12","story":"French supermarket's Christmas advert is worldwide hit (without AI) [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Five-part essay on hidden CGI in modern films.","quote":"I highly recommend this 5-part essay series","text":"I highly recommend this 5-part essay series \"NO CGI\" is really just INVISIBLE CGI\" [VIDEO] (it starts with \"Top Gun: Maverick\"). Current-era CGI is insanely good . The problem is that it's used and abused everywhere, often with very little consideration for whether it's needed, or if there's time to do all the VFX shots etc."},{"id":43605926,"author":"jsheard","month":"2025-04","story":"'Minecraft Movie' Leaks Online: Full Unfinished Version Shared","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Exposes doctored behind-the-scenes footage hiding green screen and CGI.","quote":"This series is a great watch on the subject","text":"Typically the way it goes is that the studio underplays how much CGI they used during the marketing campaign, while the VFX vendors are forced to bite their tongues, and then 6 months after the movie debuts their NDA expires and they finally get to show how much CGI there really was. This series is a great watch on the subject: [VIDEO] In the later parts there's some really egregious examples where promotional behind-the-scenes footage was actually doctored to key out the green screens or present composited shots as if they were done in-camera."},{"id":47716305,"author":"rcxdude","month":"2026-04","story":"Creating the Futurescape for the Fifth Element [2019]","stance":"rec","why":"Breakdown of no-CGI marketing including doctored behind-the-scenes footage.","quote":"this video series has a good breakdown on it","text":"This has tended to be significantly overblown recently with a huge amount of 'no CGI' advertising coming from studios, which often verges into utter BS. There's an incredible amount of CG at every level of modern productions, regardless of how much stuntwork and practical effects were done as well. (this video series has a good breakdown on it, which has included studios releasing doctored 'behind the scenes' footage! [VIDEO] ). That's not to say that doing these things is pointless or unimpressive, but it's often used to denigrate and minimize the work of a lot of already quite underappreciated artists."},{"id":47747349,"author":"CyberDildonics","month":"2026-04","story":"Creating the Futurescape for the Fifth Element [2019]","stance":"rec","why":"Five-part series on how little is truly practical.","quote":"This is 5 part series.","text":"It's not true either way. Very little is actually practical, it's just that when something looks good people think it is practical because they want to believe that. Marketing feeds into this and tells people movies were done all practical or made \"heavy use of practical effects\" and it's just lies. This is 5 part series. [VIDEO] Even before this people were saying stuff like mad max was done \"almost all practical\" because they saw behind the scenes stuff of flipping a few cars even though the movie is wall to wall digital effects. Sometimes the elaborate \"practical effects\" don't even move right and are used for reference and completely replaced."},{"id":45675608,"author":"jsheard","month":"2025-10","story":"Ovi: Twin backbone cross-modal fusion for audio-video generation","stance":"rec","why":"Good CGI goes unnoticed.","quote":"Obligatory Jonas Ussing plug","text":"Yeah that's the thing, with good execution of a plausible effect you don't even realize you're looking at CGI. It's the toupée fallacy. Obligatory Jonas Ussing plug: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41244381,"author":"4ggr0","month":"2024-08","story":"Captain Disillusion debunks David Beckham beach kicks [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Series exploring misleading no-CGI claims in movies.","quote":"i really enjoyed the 4 videos made by 'The Movie Rabbit Hole'","text":"i really enjoyed the 4 videos made by 'The Movie Rabbit Hole' which explores the \"No CGI was used in this movie\"-lie/exaggeration/misdirection. [VIDEO]"},{"id":46704852,"author":"sagacity","month":"2026-01","story":"Director Gore Verbinski: Unreal Engine is the greatest slip backwards for movie","stance":"reference","why":"Behind-the-scenes featurettes may be untrustworthy.","quote":"I'm not sure you can trust behind the scenes featurettes like this anymore.","text":"I'm not sure you can trust behind the scenes featurettes like this anymore. See [VIDEO] for more details."},{"id":41514211,"author":"CyberDildonics","month":"2024-09","story":"Max Headroom and the World of Pseudo-CGI (2013)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Most of the effects fly by and you have no idea.","text":"CG still looks more often than not, too clean (even if trying to look dirty), too polished, too uniform, no character, and just feels like CGI for CGIs sake. The truth is that this is what you notice. Most of the effects fly by and you have no idea. When you see slivers that don't work as well you think of that as 'all cg' and the 'cg look'. \"NO CGI\" is really just INVISIBLE CGI [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"}],"r":[["5eo8nz_niiM",1],["gWVmPtr9O0g",1],["V2P8mjWRqpk",1],["jB0vBmiTr6o",1],["AbgsfeGvg3E",1],["GNO_CYUjMK8",1],["Ox5P7QyL774",1],["RKT-sKtR970",1],["W3aieHjyNvw",1],["-iCIZQX9i1A",1]]},"F9cO3-MLHOM":{"wy":"HN commenters repeatedly recommend it as the clearest explanation of regulatory capture, and cite it in threads on AI regulation, GDPR and telecom.","q":{"x":"I strongly encourage watching Bill Gurley's talk from the All-In Summit","i":37793116,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37793116,"author":"filereaper","month":"2023-10","story":"Congress Should Regulate Artificial Intelligence","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Gurley on what happens when Congress regulates technology.","quote":"I strongly encourage watching Bill Gurley's talk from the All-In Summit","text":"I strongly encourage watching Bill Gurley's talk from the All-In Summit about whenever Congress gets involved with regulations and technology. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37828230,"author":"filereaper","month":"2023-10","story":"Canada Plans to Regulate Search and Social Media Use of AI","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Gurley on government involvement in technology regulation.","quote":"I strongly encourage watching Bill Gurley's talk from the All-In Summit","text":"I strongly encourage watching Bill Gurley's talk from the All-In Summit about whenever Government gets involved with regulations and technology. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37646652,"author":"nycdatasci","month":"2023-09","story":"Saint Helena Island Communications","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Bill Gurley's talk on regulation and incumbents.","quote":"it's worth watching the entire Bill Gurley talk at the recent All-In Summit","text":"Relatedly, it's worth watching the entire Bill Gurley talk at the recent All-In Summit. Edit: Updating link to jump to the relevant section of the talk. [VIDEO]"},{"id":44720788,"author":"shivasaxena","month":"2025-07","story":"EU Commission finds Temu in breach of online platform rules","stance":"rec","why":"Gurley's talk on regulatory capture.","quote":"Would recommend Bill Gurly's talk about regulatory capture if anyone is interested","text":"I work for a Swiss POS firm. We buy all our peripherals like tablet stands etc, and merchandising from Temu since it's 5-10x time cheaper and is same or better quality. We are competing with well funded companies like lighspeed/toast so it's been great help for us. Funny incident our CEO just mentioned to me yesterday: She ordered stickers from a local shop without asking the price, and on delivery had to pay 200 CHF for 20 stickers so 10 CHF/sticker. Next time she ordered from temu and got them at 20 CHF for 200 stickers so 0.1 CHF/sticker. At least to me it's clear this is plain and simple regulatory capture by inefficient european retailers. In supermarkets I have to pay 12 EUR for a bottle opener that I can get for 2 EUR on temu. Amazon.de for example launched free delivery to estonia and other small european countries only recently post rise of temu. These actions by EU commision are vehemently anti-consumer and anti-business under the transparent guise of being pro-consumer and pro-business. Would recommend Bill Gurly's talk about regulatory capture if anyone is interested [VIDEO]"},{"id":40111204,"author":"nycdatasci","month":"2024-04","story":"Anti-municipal broadband budget amendment gets nixed in New York","stance":"rec","why":"Perspective on regulatory capture shaping legislation.","quote":"provides interesting perspective on the type of regulatory capture that leads to bill modifications like this","text":"Bill Gurley’s talk during the 2023 All-In Summit provides interesting perspective on the type of regulatory capture that leads to bill modifications like this. It’s worth listening to if you have an idle 30 mins. [VIDEO] Glad to see the clause was nixed!"},{"id":39189943,"author":"miketery","month":"2024-01","story":"Everyone hates the electronic medical record","stance":"rec","why":"Covers why fixing regulated industries is so difficult.","quote":"I recommend watching Bill Gurley's talk [1] on regulatory capture from 4 months ago.","text":"Related, I recommend watching Bill Gurley's talk [1] on regulatory capture from 4 months ago. It basically covers why the path to solving this is a difficult if not impossible one. 1 - [VIDEO]"},{"id":38103828,"author":"WestCoastJustin","month":"2023-11","story":"Establishment of the U.S. Artificial Intelligence Safety Institute","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how incumbents use regulation against competitors.","quote":"Great way to understand how all this works is watching All-In Summit: Bill Gurley presents 2,851 Miles","text":"Great way to understand how all this works is watching All-In Summit: Bill Gurley presents 2,851 Miles[1]. Basically, regulate your competition into the ground. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":49022216,"author":"positron26","month":"2026-07","story":"OpenAI and Anthropic unite against open-weight AI risks to their bottom line","stance":"reference","why":"Gurley's talk on regulation and incumbents.","quote":"Bill Gureley's talk on regulation comes to mind","text":"Bill Gureley's talk on regulation comes to mind: [VIDEO] The reason barking at open source will only get the dog thrown into the river is because there is a whole lot more cumulative power distributed among companies and consumers who don't want two incumbents to dictate which kinds of alternatives can exist. Note that those rushing to the \"all corpos evil\" talking points will turn around and advocate delegating these decisions to \"the government.\" This is the same government that they also claim is bought by the corporations. It's also the same government that will enforce the regulatory capture on behalf of the yucky corporation against the broad, diverse interests. Thinking big vs small while embracing the biggest of the big is some kind of inconsistency that people need to deal with by looking in the mirror and finding some awareness. Nuance. If your problem model is wrong, so is every solution you propose except if your wrong model may get lucky from time to time. Not every broken model is a broken clock. Some broken models cannot admit the right answer ever. The real dynamic on display here is broad, correlated mass of overlapping interests, big and small. The big pile of people and entities that don't want to be governed by OpenAI and Anthropic can push harder and win the tug of war. You don't have to like your temporary allies all the time, but at least know who they are and when you are aligned."},{"id":42142009,"author":"Seanambers","month":"2024-11","story":"Analysis of economic and productivity losses caused by cookie banners in Europe","stance":"reference","why":"Gurley's regulatory capture argument applied to GDPR.","quote":"GDPR is basically exactly what Bill Gurley talks about here","text":"This is the EU in a nutshell. You also have quite a few people defending this. GDPR is basically exactly what Bill Gurley talks about here ; [VIDEO] Regulatory capture."}],"r":[["9XaYdCdwiWU",1],["G4hL5Om4IJ4",1],["j-vJxez9UF8",1],["coGpmA4saEk",1],["0qKp-0h9P18",1],["Nb2tebYAaOA",1],["2qv3fcXW1mg",1],["PDylgzybWAw",1],["QNznD9hMEh0",1],["P-2P3MSZrBM",1],["WRdJCFEqFTU",1],["2y5Pv4yN0b0",1],["Hqw57GJSrac",1],["b5P3QDm61go",1],["BzB5xtGGsTc",1],["HSxSgbNQi-g",1]]},"8S0FDjFBj8o":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a funny deconstruction of TED talk style, and as a favorite example of the appearance of meaning without content.","q":{"x":"spent 6 minutes talking about nothing","i":30257910,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":41763949,"author":"friendzis","month":"2024-10","story":"Google’s AI thinks I left a Gatorade bottle on the moon","stance":"strong_rec","why":"","quote":"I absolutely love this TED talk","text":"Pseudo-intellectual consumerism, as I like to call it. How many people do you know who turn on the news on TV, sit in front and take notes? Who put on some music, relax in the sweet spot and immerse themselves? Who read a book, stop and ponder, continue? Versus people who turn on the TV while cooking, who put on headphones with music while working out for background noise, who put on sped up version of audiobook while driving to tick off another checkmark? It's form over substance all over the place. I absolutely love this TED talk: [VIDEO] The more pseudo-intellectual consumerism infiltrates our collective psyche, the more the substance becomes irrelevant. Nuance requires thinking. \"For every complex problem there is an answer that is clear, simple, and wrong\" -- HL Mencken. But knowing this answer makes you feel smart."},{"id":30243149,"author":"light_cone","month":"2022-02","story":"What Was the Ted Talk?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows appearance of meaning is not actual meaning","quote":"Personally the TED talk I liked the most.","text":"Obligatory meta TED talk: [VIDEO] Personally the TED talk I liked the most. It's funny but I actually find it interesting, showing that appearance of meaning is not actual meaning."},{"id":11142915,"author":"randycupertino","month":"2016-02","story":"We Are Now at Peak TED","stance":"strong_rec","why":"TED talk about nothing","quote":"is one of my favorites","text":"That's why the \"Ted Talk about Nothing\" is one of my favorites: [VIDEO] Their manner of speaking reminds me of American Dad making fun of Ira Glass. Why is pausing??! Doesn't he know what he's going to say next??? AAAAHH. [LINK]"},{"id":30257910,"author":"johncessna","month":"2022-02","story":"What Was the Ted Talk?","stance":"rec","why":"Six minutes talking about nothing","quote":"spent 6 minutes talking about nothing","text":"I used to binge watch Ted Talks and then ran across How to sound Smart in your Ted Talk[1] Ted Talk where William Stephen spent 6 minutes talking about nothing. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27009529,"author":"Stratoscope","month":"2021-05","story":"GitHub repository for Sedgewick's Algorithms is taken down","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"There are some great TED[x] talks","text":"There are some great TED[x] talks, like \"Ducks Go Quack, Chickens Say Cluck\": [LINK] And \"How to sound smart in your TEDx Talk\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":9574301,"author":"JohnTHaller","month":"2015-05","story":"The Sound of TED: A Case for Distaste","stance":"rec","why":"Fun deconstruction of the pacing of TED talks","quote":"is a fun deconstruction of the pacing and process of most TED talks","text":"Will Stephen's How to sound smart in your TEDx Talk is a fun deconstruction of the pacing and process of most TED talks: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["TLm7Q92xMjQ",1],["ywWBy6J5gz8",1],["nYg6jzotiAc",1],["YNTARSM-Fjc",1],["kAG39jKi0lI",1],["rRi8LptvFZY",1],["GJVSDjRXVoo",1],["7TqqdOcX4dc",1],["ZLWMQLMiTPk",1],["snAhsXyO3Ck",1],["FITJMJjASUs",1],["bpQFCcSI0pU",1]]},"iFTWM7HV2UI":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it whenever engagement-optimizing feeds, micro-targeting or ad infrastructure come up, as an accessible warning about algorithmic amplification.","q":{"x":"This TED talk[0] on persuasion architectures brings up some interesting moral arguments against this idea.","i":23318123,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":49282869,"author":"kaizenb","month":"2026-08","story":"uBlock Origin Is Giving Up the Fight to Keep Ads Off Facebook","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Eye-opening talk on ad algorithms controlling access to information.","quote":"Recommend watching","text":"uBlock team is the modern times heroes fighting the good fight for us. Thanks to them I've been navigating the web with peace of mind for a long time. Sometimes I find myself thinking how our lives would be if couple of guys didn't build shitty tech startups. We've gone from creative advertising to these manipulative online marketing scams, that attack our brains, especially targeting young generation, which effects how our societies, behaviours, art and culture develop. Recommend watching: \"We're building an artificial intelligence-powered dystopia, one click at a time, says technosociologist Zeynep Tufecki. In an eye-opening talk, she details how the same algorithms companies like Facebook, Google and Amazon use to get you to click on ads are also used to organize your access to political and social information. And the machines aren't even the real threat. What we need to understand is how the powerful might use AI to control us -- and what we can do in response.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":47009176,"author":"DarkUranium","month":"2026-02","story":"The EU moves to kill infinite scrolling","stance":"rec","why":"Very good talk on algorithmic amplification of outrage.","quote":"There's also a very good TED talk on this topic from 8 years ago","text":"There's literally a name for using this on purpose: stochastic terrorism. There's also a very good TED talk on this topic from 8 years ago : [VIDEO]"},{"id":25746808,"author":"inglor_cz","month":"2021-01","story":"Facebook to staff: Avoid company-branded clothing for own safety","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting talk about platforms pushing recommended content.","quote":"There is an interesting speech by Zeynap Tufekci on this topic","text":"That is not the case of YouTube, though. YT pushes recommended videos on you. There is an interesting speech by Zeynap Tufekci on this topic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23318123,"author":"save_ferris","month":"2020-05","story":"Facebook executives shut down efforts to make the site less divisive","stance":"rec","why":"Moral arguments against data-driven ads exploiting addictive behaviors.","quote":"This TED talk[0] on persuasion architectures brings up some interesting moral arguments against this idea.","text":"> Anecdotally of course, as one of those tech-folks who's got nothing against good and relevant ads. Be careful what you wish for. This TED talk[0] on persuasion architectures brings up some interesting moral arguments against this idea. If we generally accept that ad conversion is a metric of an ad's success, how do we handle the situation where people that are susceptible to addictive behaviors like gambling and compulsive shopping are exposed to ads that exploit their condition? Data-driven advertising systems are built particularly well to find and exploit these kinds of people. We already see this a lot in the scam call/email world. Once you fall for one, the amount of inbound traffic you receive from scammers increases dramatically. 0: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16380357,"author":"redux13","month":"2018-02","story":"The FBI, CIA and NSA say American citizens shouldn't use Huawei phones","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how effective social media micro-targeting campaigns can be.","quote":"check out the Ted talk by Zeynep Tufekci","text":"This comment is highly understating the power of social media micro-targeting by dubbing it as buying a few ads on FB and running twitterbots. I would request you to check out the Ted talk by Zeynep Tufekci ( [VIDEO] ) to get a sense of how effective social media campaigns can be."},{"id":15957248,"author":"greggman","month":"2017-12","story":"A friend of mine died and I didn't know because of algorithms","stance":"rec","why":"Scary talk on engagement-optimizing algorithms nobody understands.","quote":"there is a really scary Ted talk suggesting that it's possible Facebook is using deep learning techniques to optimize for engagement","text":"you may have seen it but there is a really scary Ted talk suggesting that it's possible Facebook is using deep learning techniques to optimize for engagement and they have no idea why it picks what it picks but it's possible making people choose sides and argue happens to be the best way to meet its goals [VIDEO]"},{"id":18036597,"author":"zawerf","month":"2018-09","story":"Google workers discussed tweaking search after travel ban","stance":"reference","why":"Ad infrastructure that segments buyers can also segment voters.","quote":"the same ad serving infrastructure that can segment populations into whether they are likely to buy X, can also segment people by political affiliation","text":"> Now, is it worth discussing the enormous power Google has over people's perceptions as a result of their market dominance? Absolutely. There was a TED talk covering this titled \"We're building a dystopia just to make people click on ads\"[1]. It talks about how the same ad serving infrastructure that can segment populations into whether they are likely to buy X, can also segment people by political affiliation. Then you can simply target them and remind them to vote on election day (and it actually works and statistically affects voter turnout/results). It is kind of crazy to think that the next election might be won by whoever can make a deal with Google/Facebook to target the precise swing voters they need. Facebook/google might even do the kingmaking themselves if they can figure out how to do it without backlash/leaks. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18097014,"author":"tobr","month":"2018-09","story":"Facebook Network Breach Impacts Up to 50M Users","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"You’re repeating what I wrote - Big tech is likely to produce new tech, but new tech comes from other places too. This discussion is mostly irrelevant to the fact that this particular company is completely reckless and unethical. The technology they accidentally produce while building a dystopia to make people click on ads[1] does not justify anything. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16686782,"author":"HenryBemis","month":"2018-03","story":"We're building a dystopia just to make people click on ads (2017) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"15m 34s, Zeynep says: \"as a public and as citizens\" [1] And HERE is the problem. Facebook sees us ONLY as Product (our data) that can manipulate/buy/sell - and treat us accordingly. Advertisers see us ONLY as Consumers they can sell to - and treat us accordingly. Politicians see us ONLY as Votes (not voteRs) they can snatch - and treat us accordingly. And we let them. [1]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["TH9VCN6UkyQ",1],["Q2aEzeMDHMA",1],["mpbeOCKZFfQ",1]]},"iwcYp-XT7UI":{"wy":"HN recommends it for Hotz's reasoning on cameras versus lidar and for an opinionated, original thinker's overview of comma and openpilot.","q":{"x":"I strongly recommend Lex Firdman’s recent interview with George Hotz","i":20715214,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":46740659,"author":"nomel","month":"2026-01","story":"Open-source self-driving for 325 car models from 27 brands","stance":"strong_rec","why":"One of several Lex Fridman podcasts with comma's founder.","quote":"Highly recommend them: #31","text":"There's a few Lex Fridman podcasts with George Hotz, the founder. Highly recommend them: #31 [VIDEO] #132 [LINK] #387 [LINK]"},{"id":25554140,"author":"bko","month":"2020-12","story":"Ask HN: Favorite Podcast Episode of 2020?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Interview with an opinionated original thinker who runs comma.ai.","quote":"I loved this interview Lex Fridman had with George Hotz.","text":"I loved this interview Lex Fridman had with George Hotz. He's very opinionated original thinker with the credentials to back it up. He runs comma.ai, sellers of a device that can plug into many cars and drive for you. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20715214,"author":"mark_l_watson","month":"2019-08","story":"Camera vs. Lidar","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hotz and Fridman discuss camera versus lidar in detail.","quote":"I strongly recommend Lex Firdman’s recent interview with George Hotz","text":"I strongly recommend Lex Firdman’s recent interview with George Hotz where they covered this topic in some detail [VIDEO]"},{"id":21987585,"author":"kick","month":"2020-01","story":"Comma Two Devkit","stance":"rec","why":"Interview explains Comma's responsibility compared with Tesla's driver monitoring.","quote":"This Lex Friedman interview is pretty explanatory on the topic of responsibility.","text":"[VIDEO] This Lex Friedman interview is pretty explanatory on the topic of responsibility. A couple of highlights, which point to Comma being at minimum more responsible than Tesla: Comma doesn't advertise itself as anything beyond L2, unlike Tesla; Comma doesn't fuck up eye-detection, it refuses to cooperate with a driver who has their eyes off the road."},{"id":21564521,"author":"czr","month":"2019-11","story":"Show HN: Autopilot written in Keras for Self Driving Cars","stance":"rec","why":"High-level overview of comma and openpilot from geohot.","quote":"geohot has done interviews ([0], [1]) and that give overview of comma/openpilot at high level.","text":"for specifics, would recommend to simply read the code. for overall context, geohot has done interviews ([0], [1]) and that give overview of comma/openpilot at high level. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20711698,"author":"rmason","month":"2019-08","story":"Camera vs. Lidar","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent interview on why Hotz thinks cameras beat lidar.","quote":"Just saw an excellent two hour interview by Lex Fridman of George Hotz","text":"I suspect this is yet another story sponsored by the Tesla shorts. Just saw an excellent two hour interview by Lex Fridman of George Hotz and he goes into details why he thinks camera will win over lidar. But he also admits that presently Google is ahead of everyone in the race for level 5, but raises the question of whether they can ever do it economically enough to make money on it? [VIDEO] 2 hours! Money quote is when Lex tells him, \"\"Some non-zero part of your brain has a madman in it\" I'd argue that is true of many of the greatest inventors of our time."},{"id":20785652,"author":"dumbfoundded","month":"2019-08","story":"How Uber Got Lost","stance":"rec","why":"Good talk about the future of self-driving cars.","quote":"There's a pretty good talk about the future self-driving cars here","text":"I agree. Self-driving cars will take a really long time to emerge. The progress will be gradual. According to George Holtz of Comma ai, great lane-keeping is the first major milestone. The problem with your logic is the connection to Uber. Uber doesn't control the cars their drivers use. Sure they can set restrictions but they never collect actual data that is useful for self-driving. The only data Uber collects is when and where people want to travel. Google & Apple both have this dataset (probably superior) through their map apps. The real data gathering will come in the way Tesla does. Gather data from real-time drivers by putting the necessary sensors in cars you sell. Maybe Uber could force their drivers to install kits but I doubt it. Self-driving cars will emerge over time as new features in cars you buy. This is already happening (ie brake assist). You'll then just get updates to your car when new features become available. There's a pretty good talk about the future self-driving cars here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32715412,"author":"merryje","month":"2022-09","story":"On Device Learning","stance":"reference","why":"First of two Lex Fridman podcasts with Hotz, August 2019.","quote":"He was on the Lex Fridman podcast twice.","text":"He was on the Lex Fridman podcast twice. He's definitely an interesting guy. I've been following comma.ai for some time now it's really cool seeing how much they've accomplished. August 2019 - [VIDEO] October 2020 - [LINK]"}],"r":[["ZecQ64l-gKM",1],["_L3gNaAVjQ4",1],["lkIicfzPBys",1],["MRpEV2tmYz4",1],["x7qPAY9JqE4",1],["_2NI6t2r_Hs",1],["aMcJ1Jvtef0",1],["EY6q5dv_B-o",1],["QPKKQnijnsM",1]]},"oKg1hTOQXoY":{"wy":"Treated as a foundational talk, cited constantly for Kay's views on object-oriented programming and for rethinking software practice.","q":{"x":"I encourage all developers to watch that talk and do some thinking about the current state of our practice","i":7071827,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":19587194,"author":"gambler","month":"2019-04","story":"Correctness – A paradigm for sustainable software development","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Famous Kay talk full of great ideas about reasoning about systems","quote":"Most Alan Kay's presentations I've seen were worth watching. Tons of great ideas in each.","text":"Most Alan Kay's presentations I've seen were worth watching. Tons of great ideas in each. They really improved how I reason about systems. There is this famous talk from OOPSLA 1997: [VIDEO] If you want to see what \"practical\" OOP looked like in the 80s, this is a good video: [LINK]"},{"id":7071827,"author":"jgon","month":"2014-01","story":"WebKit.js: Pure JavaScript Port of WebKit","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Kay's keynote on what a browser should be and the state of practice","quote":"I encourage all developers to watch that talk and do some thinking about the current state of our practice","text":"You should check out Alan Kay's OOPSLA keynote \"The Compute Revolution Hasn't Happened Yet\" at [VIDEO] specifically about 20 mintues in where he says that a browser should be exactly what you say here. On the one hand, he was talking about this almost 20 years ago, on the other hand you're in good company because he's a pretty smart guy! I encourage all developers to watch that talk and do some thinking about the current state of our practice and where we want to go from here."},{"id":39616119,"author":"mpweiher","month":"2024-03","story":"Alan Kay's talk at UCLA – Feb 2024 [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Kay's keynote on messaging and system design, listed among key readings","quote":"The Computer Revolution hasn't happened yet , OOPSLA 1997 keynote","text":"The Computer Revolution hasn't happened yet , OOPSLA 1997 keynote [VIDEO] Others have already mentioned The Early History of Smalltalk , highly recommended. You'll probably want to read it a couple of times, revisit from time to time. The big idea is messaging, or rather \"ma\" [LINK] \"The key in making great and growable systems is much more to design how its modules communicate rather than what their internal properties and behaviors should be.\" \"I think I recall also pointing out that it is vitally important not just to have a complete metasystem, but to have fences that help guard the crossing of metaboundaries.\" \" I would say that a system that allowed other metathings to be done in the ordinary course of programming (like changing what inheritance means, or what is an instance) is a bad design. (I believe that systems should allow these things, but the design should be such that there are clear fences that have to be crossed when serious extensions are made.)\" \"I would suggest that more progress could be made if the smart and talented Squeak list would think more about what the next step in metaprogramming should be -- how can we get great power, parsimony, AND security of meaning?\""},{"id":31439374,"author":"chongli","month":"2022-05","story":"460M Indian Internet Users Are Now on IPv6","stance":"rec","why":"Kay's vision of small, understandable systems a single person can grasp","quote":"Alan Kay has put a ton of thought into this issue [1].","text":"operating systems which are totally transparent and easily understood by their users I sort of glossed over this part so now I have a chance to elaborate. Alan Kay has put a ton of thought into this issue [1]. He firmly believes that we can build an operating system and application software with an extremely small footprint (LOC's) so that a single person can understand the whole thing. Since he gave that talk, we've moved further and further away from Kay's vision. We've made things more and more complex, opaque, centralized, and difficult to change. We've given away our future to big tech companies. Heck, we've even given away the past. We've lost much of the freedom we had back in the 90's, let alone the 70's and 80's when Kay did so much of his work. We're going to have to work incredibly hard just to regain what we've lost. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12199519,"author":"e12e","month":"2016-08","story":"Restoring Y Combinator's Xerox Alto, day 4: What's running on the system","stance":"rec","why":"Kay's talk to programmers on Alto, Dynabook and children using the tech","quote":"I recently (re)discovered Alan Kay's 1997 OOPSLA talk","text":"I recently (re)discovered Alan Kay's 1997 OOPSLA talk: [VIDEO] For others that have been following his work (and talks), it doesn't really contain much new stuff, but the context of a talk to an audience of programmers brings out some different contexts. He touches on the the fact that they didn't \"just\" spend extra money to prototype the dynabook in the form of the Alto - but they built 2000 machines - and part of that is of course so they could invite (among others) whole classes of school children to come and play with the tech."},{"id":12219828,"author":"infinite8s","month":"2016-08","story":"Alan Kay on AI, Apple and Future","stance":"rec","why":"Kay on computers as metatools augmenting cognition, unlike today's devices","quote":"See his OOPSLA talk \"The computer revolution hasn't happened yet","text":"No, compared to the early vision (1950s/60s) of computers as metatools that humans can use to augment their cognition. The current crop of mobile devices (and even most desktop devices) are nowhere nearer to that glorious goal. See his OOPSLA talk \"The computer revolution hasn't happened yet\" - [VIDEO]"},{"id":44075308,"author":"fidotron","month":"2025-05","story":"MCP is the coming of Web 2.0 2.0","stance":"reference","why":"Kay's idea that every object should have an IP","quote":"Alan Kay's legendary OOPSLA talk in 1997","text":"I would agree with that if there were no distinction between clients and servers. i.e. agents and LLMs are resources that should be discovered and exploited in the same exact way as anything else, and switchable in the same ways. The whole thing reminds me of stuff like Alan Kay's legendary OOPSLA talk in 1997 ( [VIDEO] ) \"Every object should have an IP\" (Also \"Arrogance in computer science is measured in nano Dijkstras\")."},{"id":41647986,"author":"randito","month":"2024-09","story":"Giving T cells extra batteries supercharges them against cancer","stance":"reference","why":"Led the commenter to biochemistry books","quote":"I found my way there after an Alan Kay video","text":"For people who want to learn biochemsistry and subsequently be in awe and the complexity and mechanisms, there's a great beginner book, The Machinery of Life. [LINK] I found my way there after an Alan Kay video -- OPSLA 1997 - The computer revolution hasnt happened yet: [VIDEO] On the subject of awe, here's another from Kurszsegat - The Most Complex Language in the World: [LINK]"},{"id":40380506,"author":"igouy","month":"2024-05","story":"Learning from the Object Oriented Mania","stance":"reference","why":"Kay praises The Art of the Metaobject Protocol around minute 54","quote":"Here's Alan Kay lavishing praise minute 54","text":"Here's Alan Kay lavishing praise minute 54 [VIDEO] on \"The Art of the Metaobject Protocol\" [LINK]"},{"id":34890326,"author":"surprisetalk","month":"2023-02","story":"Ask HN: How to protect blog from AI search?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"[11] Alan Kay","text":"This is an extremely difficult question that I've been grappling with myself. My first strategy is to create writings that can't be summarized. I try to make essays that are dripping with idiosyncrasy. I can't win in an information war, but I can stay competitive for ~10 years in a creative clash: [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK] I myself draw lots of my inspiration from my \"sages of style\", in the same way that AIs will try to replicate my voice. None of these great thinkers can be summarized or replicated in any meaningful way: [6] David Foster Wallace: [LINK] [7] George Carlin: [LINK] [8] Derek Sivers: [LINK] [9] John Green: [LINK] [10] Roman Mars: [LINK] [11] Alan Kay: [VIDEO] [12] Alan Watts: [LINK] [13] Richard Feynman: [LINK] [14] Raymond Smullyan: [LINK] But style only takes you so far. My second strategy is to socialize IRL. This year, I'm starting a tech conference called \"Outland\" to foster community in an age where community might be all that matters. [15] [LINK]"},{"id":34035019,"author":"gjvc","month":"2022-12","story":"Show HN: A self-updating list of the most current useragents","stance":"reference","why":"Kay's criticism of HTML presupposing a browser that understands formats","quote":"HTML on the Internet has gone back to the dark ages because it presupposes that there should be a browser that should understand its formats.","text":"\"I really would like you to contrast that with what you have to do with HTML on the Internet. Think about it. HTML on the Internet has gone back to the dark ages because it presupposes that there should be a browser that should understand its formats. This has to be one of the worst ideas since MS-DOS.\" -- [VIDEO]"},{"id":31178500,"author":"oumua_don17","month":"2022-04","story":"Ask HN: Top inspiring books on crucial CS ideas?","stance":"reference","why":"Kay praises the Art of the Metaobject Protocol as profound and practical","quote":"Alan Kay called it \"the best book anybody's written in ten years","text":"The Art of metaobject protocol In his 1997 talk at OOPSLA, Alan Kay called it \"the best book anybody's written in ten years\", and contended that it contained \"some of the most profound insights, and the most practical insights about OOP\", but was dismayed that it was written in a highly Lisp-centric and CLOS-specific fashion, calling it \"a hard book for most people to read; if you don't know the Lisp culture, it's very hard to read\" [2] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30658730,"author":"danuker","month":"2022-03","story":".NET Myths Dispelled","stance":"reference","why":"Identifies the keynote where Kay says he didn't have C++ in mind","quote":"I suspect it is this one, at 10:33 Alan says","text":"> Alan Kay’s 97 OOPSLA keynote Is it this one? [VIDEO] Edit: I suspect it is this one, at 10:33 Alan says \"Actually I made up the term <<object-oriented>>, and I can tell you I did not have C++ in mind.\""},{"id":27412705,"author":"Rochus","month":"2021-06","story":"The Repeated Deaths of OOP (2015)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for Kay's full statement on Smalltalk","quote":"Kay in the sentence next to the one quoted in the article said \" the important thing here is that I have many of the same feelings about Smalltalk","text":"Kay is said to have coined the term \"object-oriented\", but his conception of object orientation differs considerably from that of Dahl and Nygaard as well as from all current OO languages. Dahl and Nygaard invented the first object-oriented programming language (Simula 67) but didn't use the term. And yes, active objects reacting to messages (events) passed to them were already part of Simula I (1962). The referenced article - like many others - moreover quotes Kay incompletely; Kay in the sentence next to the one quoted in the article said \" the important thing here is that I have many of the same feelings about Smalltalk \"; see [VIDEO] . Smalltalk-76 and 80 (in contrast to Smalltalk-72) implement inheritance and virtual methods as it was done in Simula 67, just like C++ and Java do."},{"id":19417898,"author":"4lch3m1st","month":"2019-03","story":"Alan Kay on the Meaning of “Object-Oriented Programming” (2003)","stance":"reference","why":"Kay on which languages realize his OO vision","quote":"From his talk on OOPSLA[1], I'd say that only Smalltalk and Common Lisp (through CLOS) were able to perform OO as Kay envisioned.","text":"From his talk on OOPSLA[1], I'd say that only Smalltalk and Common Lisp (through CLOS) were able to perform OO as Kay envisioned. Erlang is another story: it would probably be better to call it just actor model. But again, Kay himself says[2] that there is not a lot of difference between actor model and his OOP. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":18527249,"author":"timbit42","month":"2018-11","story":"The faster you unlearn OOP, the better for you and your software","stance":"reference","why":"Kay says C++ wasn't what he had in mind by object-oriented","quote":"I invented the term object-oriented, and I can tell you that C++ wasn't what I had in mind.\" - Alan Kay","text":"The best arguments against OOP, as it is done in most languages today, are made by Alan Kay, the creator of Smalltalk and the term \"object-oriented\". Alan should have used the term \"message passing\" so people wouldn't miss this component of Smalltalk that made it so great. \"I invented the term object-oriented, and I can tell you that C++ wasn't what I had in mind.\" - Alan Kay, Source: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17350286,"author":"leoc","month":"2018-06","story":"Qt for WebAssembly","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Isn't progress wonderful? 2010: [LINK] Speaking of which, the linked page is no longer up, so here's a Wayback Machine impression: [LINK] ... and ofc the Google Video link in my comment [LINK] > 1 point by leoc on Jan 16, 2010 | parent | favorite | on: QT on Google Native Client > 1997^H^H^H^H1961 is calling; someone just picked up the rotary phone. [LINK] . is now broken too, so here's a link into a YouTube upload: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13089231,"author":"gnipgnip","month":"2016-12","story":"Let’s Stop Bashing C","stance":"reference","why":"Kay on architecture driving language choices and hardware speed versus power gains","quote":"Alan Kay in his famous OOPSLA talk mentions how the architecture itself leads to these language choices.","text":"Alan Kay in his famous OOPSLA talk mentions how the architecture itself leads to these language choices. [VIDEO] He mentions how Xerox PARC machines with tagged architectures from the 80s were only 1/50 the speed of the x86 systems in '97. This, in contrast to the 50000x increase in computing power over the same time frame."},{"id":6738114,"author":"aufreak3","month":"2013-11","story":"What's wrong with Object-Oriented Programming and Functional Programming","stance":"reference","why":"Kay apologizes for the term object oriented and points to Ma","quote":"he says that he's \"apologized profusely over the last 20 years for introducing the term 'object oriented'","text":"The Alan Kay reference is from his OOPSLA'97 talk [2], where he says that he's \"apologized profusely over the last 20 years for introducing the term 'object oriented'\" and suggests that the Japanese notion of \"Ma\" or \"the unseen stuff that goes between objects\" as what is important. (edit) [3] is a quote from a communication with Kay in 2003 - \"OOP to me means only messaging, local retention and protection and hiding of state-process, and extreme late-binding of all things. It can be done in Smalltalk and in LISP. There are possibly other systems in which this is possible, but I'm not aware of them.\" [2] [VIDEO] (around 38minutes) [3] [LINK]"},{"id":6209959,"author":"PuercoPop","month":"2013-08","story":"The Future of Programming in Node.js","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"We don't know how to design systems so let's not turn it into a religion yet.","text":"I should just probably say, clearly you haven't seen Common Lisp's defpackage, modules are actually first class objects there and are completely decoupled from the file system. But most importantly as Barbara Liskov mentions in this video[1] we don't know what is a module exactly or how to use them yet. Which is an specific statement aligned with Alan Kay's famous \"We don't know how to design systems so let's not turn it into a religion yet.\"[2] tl;dr; 1) Innovation is good. 2) Javascript's module is a half assed implementation of Common Lisp's defpackage. (Don't get me wrong is still way better than Python's abhorrent ninja goto: import) [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["YR5WdGrpoug",1],["YyIQKBzIuBY",1],["QjJaFG63Hlo",1],["gTAghAJcO1o",1],["sWgDk-o-6ZE",1],["p6K4iIMlouI",1],["eX9m3g5J-XA",1],["LiWlvBro9eI",1],["YnWhqhNdYyk",1],["EKWGGDXe5MA",1],["_ahvzDzKdB0",1],["dK3zRXhrFZY",1],["Av0PQDVTP4A",1],["6wZmYMWKLkY",1],["hEx5DNLWGgA",1],["4bM3Gut1hIk",1],["Eg_ToU7m1MI",1],["hOE6nVVr14c",1],["p2LZLYcu_JY",1],["30jNsCVLpAE",1]]},"wJWksPWDKOc":{"wy":"HN recommends it, often emotionally, as an auditory depiction of memory loss, in threads about dementia and aging.","q":{"x":"It's haunting and beautiful and quite memorable.","i":41064902,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39672485,"author":"fallinditch","month":"2024-03","story":"Flowers for Algernon (1965) [pdf]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Six albums depicting gradual degradation of dementia","quote":"A brilliantly imaginative work.","text":"Check out the 6 album cycle that depicts the gradual degradation of dementia: Everywhere at the end of time by The Caretaker. A brilliantly imaginative work. [VIDEO] Chatgpt: This ambitious project was released in stages (or \"stages\") from 2016 to 2019, across six albums, each representing a different phase of dementia. Through his distinctive use of manipulated pre-World War II ballroom music, The Caretaker, aka Leyland Kirby, explores the progression of the disease, from subtle early signs to the total disintegration of memory and understanding. The work is both an artistic exploration and an empathetic representation of the condition's impact on the human mind and memory."},{"id":25478988,"author":"Red_Tarsius","month":"2020-12","story":"'I am mine': Alzheimer's at 41 (2018)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Emotionally overwhelming six-album depiction of memory loss","quote":"I wish I had the writing skills necessary to convey the emotional turmoil I went through","text":"I've recently discovered Everywhere at the End of Time by the Caretaker (James Kirby). It's a series of six music albums exploring and depicting memory loss, Alzheimer's disease and dementia. I wish I had the writing skills necessary to convey the emotional turmoil I went through by listening to the six stages in one sleepless night. [VIDEO] I also appreciated the attention to the details from the track titles to the choice of album covers. The last image – a broken canvas seen from behind – is especially haunting. It's the kind of experience that makes you re-evaluate many life choices."},{"id":43020226,"author":"rglover","month":"2025-02","story":"The hallucinatory thoughts of the dying mind","stance":"rec","why":"Albums tracking progression of dementia","quote":"The music is written to track the timeline of dementia progressing","text":"Semi-related: not delirium, but if you haven't come across it, check out the \"Everywhere at the end of time\" series of albums. The music is written to track the timeline of dementia progressing [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":41064902,"author":"saulpw","month":"2024-07","story":"Memory for music doesn't diminish with age","stance":"rec","why":"Haunting, beautiful music about aging; later albums hard listening","quote":"It's haunting and beautiful and quite memorable.","text":"Thematically related is the music of The Caretaker. It's haunting and beautiful and quite memorable. Check out An Empty Bliss Beyond This World[0], which is kind of a nice album for some melancholy reflection on age and the experience of dementia. His final 6-album sequence, Everywhere At The End Of Time[1], is less accessible, particularly the final 3 albums which are mostly noise (the whole 6-hour work was a terrifying listening challenge on TikTok a few years ago). [0] [LINK] [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29096119,"author":"datameta","month":"2021-11","story":"Organ transplant patients may not get dementia","stance":"rec","why":"Album as auditory approximation of dementia","quote":"This [0] is an album that is an auditory approximation of it.","text":"The statistical prevalence of dementia is absolutely shocking to me... It is bar none one of the worst experiences one can go through. This [0] is an album that is an auditory approximation of it. I sincerely hope for more discourse and research dedicated to understanding, preventing, and treating it. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25191943,"author":"pantelisk","month":"2020-11","story":"I created an alternative to the YouTube algorithm to stop me wasting time","stance":"reference","why":"Example of YouTube recommending unexpected music loved later","quote":"youtube manages to recommend to me things that aren't in my alley, but I somehow end up loving","text":"(Please see the reply I left to Krasnol). So, if you just gave me a search-bar and have me play music, it would be post-rock or metal. And what I get on spotify is a bunch of post rock and black/death metal. Which is fine, but predictable and kinda boring. But youtube manages to recommend to me things that aren't in my alley, but I somehow end up loving. (just a random sample from music) \"Heilung - Lifa\" - [LINK] \"The caretaker at the end of time\" - [VIDEO] \"Babe Rainbow - Secret Enchanted Broccoli Forest\" - [LINK] A whole new genre called DungeonSynth - [LINK] And one called Witchhouse - [LINK] And Sovietwave goth - [LINK] Uhh, drum n bass n sax?? - [LINK] And the latest weird recommendation a San Jose band called Sunami. Oh And This! - [LINK] ---- I wouldn't be searching for any of that on my own. But I love all of it (ended buying merch from quite a few \"youtube recommended\" artists). To me that is as successful a recommendation system as it can be. It shows me things I didn't even know I 'd love! From Jazz, to Funk, to Ambient, to SovietWave goth, to psychedelic rock, the list is always expanding.... I was never that open minded, but now I am. Maybe this is more on me and how my personality evolved through the years than on youtube's recommendations... --- But you can see that many people had the same happen to them by how popular comments like this are -> \"You didnt find this video. It found you.\" Or \"I would love to know what I did to make this come up in my suggested v"}],"r":[["RNFesa01llk",1],["7OpM_zKGE4o",1],["86gyW0vUmVs",1],["PHe0bXAIuk0",1],["9vz06QO3UkQ",1]]},"NVl9_6J1G60":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as a first-hand overview of Heap's Postgres/Citus architecture and its distinctive indexing approach.","q":{"x":"Dan from Heap Analytics has a great talk about how they use Postgres and Citus.","i":12396191,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":11763683,"author":"Judson","month":"2016-05","story":"TrailDB – An Efficient Library for Storing and Processing Event Data","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Heap's use of Citus and Postgres for event processing","quote":"excellent talk from one of the Heap Analytics engineers","text":"I was just watching this[0] excellent talk from one of the Heap Analytics engineers on their use of CitusDB + Postgres to do event processing. What use-case would TrailDB be the obvious, hands-down way to go (vs maybe Postgres + Citus)? [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14543483,"author":"macobo","month":"2017-06","story":"Running 10M PostgreSQL Indexes in Production","stance":"rec","why":"High-level details of how and why Heap uses Postgres","quote":"Dan gave a pretty good talk about the high-level details of how/why postgres","text":"You need a queue in front of your database irregardless of write latency. Otherwise you tie your availability to database availability as downtime (even for upgrades) is often unavoidable and network problems are common. Dan gave a pretty good talk about the high-level details of how/why postgres a couple of years back: [VIDEO] . One reason of why pg is that SQL is really powerful for building complex queries."},{"id":13443175,"author":"macobo","month":"2017-01","story":"How Discord Stores Billions of Messages Using Cassandra","stance":"rec","why":"Relevant to sharded Postgres workflows","quote":"You might find interesting.","text":"Regarding a), a sharded postgresql (i.e. with citus data) can easily accomodate that workflow with just a tiny bit of extra overhead. Re c) this strongly depends on the usecase, I've seen companies use a) to avoid split-brain problems and having to manage two data pipelines to great success at similar scales. You might find [VIDEO] interesting."},{"id":12409289,"author":"simiano","month":"2016-09","story":"When to Avoid JSONB in a PostgreSQL Schema","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of Heap's architecture","quote":"I like this[1] talk, it gives you an overview of their architecture.","text":"Keep in mind that Heap collects a huge amount of data and has a huge dataset. I like this[1] talk, it gives you an overview of their architecture. Great post though, thank you for sharing. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12396191,"author":"devbug","month":"2016-08","story":"Building a Scalable Postgres Metrics Backend Using the Citus Extension","stance":"rec","why":"How Heap uses Postgres and Citus","quote":"Dan from Heap Analytics has a great talk about how they use Postgres and Citus.","text":"If you haven't already seen it, Dan from Heap Analytics has a great talk about how they use Postgres and Citus. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22379373,"author":"malisper","month":"2020-02","story":"Launch HN: PostHog (YC W20) – open-source product analytics","stance":"reference","why":"Heap CTO talks about Heap's infrastructure","quote":"Heap's CTO, Dan Robinson, has given a number of talks on how it works[0][1]","text":"I'm curious as to how you plan to scale PostHog to larger users. As the person who scaled Heap, here is my honest opinion of this. I think there is going to be a huge challenge ahead in scaling query performance. This was perpetually a challenge at Heap and was for a long time the main limitation on Heap's growth. The challenge was tough enough for Heap and PostHog is going to be at a huge disadvantage due to the lack of multi-tenancy. When you use Heap, your data is stored across Heap's entire cluster of machines. When you run a query, that query is ran simultaneously against every single machine in Heap's cluster. Even though your data may be taking up something like .1% of the total disk space, when you run a query, 100% of the disk throughput of Heap's cluster will go to processing your query. It's not an overstatement to say this alone results in a >50x improvement to query performance. I honestly think Heap wouldn't be possible without multi-tenancy. It's hard enough as is to get queries that process multiple terabytes of data to return in seconds when you have a fleet of dozens of i3s available. I'm not sure how you would do that with a fleet a tiny fraction of that size. If you're curious about Heap's infrastructure, Heap's CTO, Dan Robinson, has given a number of talks on how it works[0][1]. That's not to say that PostHog won't work for anyone. I previously tried (and failed) to start a company based on optimizing people's Postgres instances. One of the big takeaways"},{"id":12409489,"author":"malisper","month":"2016-09","story":"When to Avoid JSONB in a PostgreSQL Schema","stance":"reference","why":"Heap's specific indexing strategy","quote":"a very specific indexing strategy (discussed about in a talk Dan gave[0])","text":"As of right now, our schema is literally: user_id | event_id | time | data where data is a JSONB blob that contains every other piece of information about an event. Currently, we get a row estimate of one for pretty much every query. We've been able to work around the lack of statistics by using a very specific indexing strategy (discussed about in a talk Dan gave[0]) that gives the planner very few options in terms of planning and additionally by turning off nested loop joins. We are planning on pulling out the most common properties that we store in the data column, which will give us proper statistics on all of those fields. I am currently experimenting with what new indexing strategies we will be able to use thanks to better statistics. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12416552,"author":"malisper","month":"2016-09","story":"When to Avoid JSONB in a PostgreSQL Schema","stance":"reference","why":"Dan explains Heap's indexing strategy","quote":"our indexing strategy which Dan talks about in this talk[0]","text":"That get's into our indexing strategy which Dan talks about in this talk[0]. Currently our tables aren't completely insert only, so a BRIN index wouldn't work for us, as one row in the wrong place can cause a huge amount of extra reads. [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"VUhlNx_-wYk":{"wy":"Strongly recommended in lambda calculus and Y-combinator threads as a mind-expanding half hour that clarifies the ideas.","q":{"x":"An awesome video which changed my perspective towards lambda calculus and programming in general","i":9301755,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":9301755,"author":"tacticiankerala","month":"2015-04","story":"A Short and Painless Introduction to the Lambda Calculus [pdf]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Changed commenter's perspective on lambda calculus and programming","quote":"An awesome video which changed my perspective towards lambda calculus and programming in general","text":"An awesome video which changed my perspective towards lambda calculus and programming in general: programming with nothing: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8292324,"author":"gamegoblin","month":"2014-09","story":"Church Numerals","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Implements FizzBuzz using only lambda calculus and Church encoding","quote":"I cannot recommend enough investing a half hour in watching the","text":"For anyone who finds this interesting, I cannot recommend enough investing a half hour in watching the \"Programming With Nothing\" talk: [VIDEO] In it, the speaker implements fizzbuzz using only lambda calculus (making significant use of church encoding)."},{"id":6710858,"author":"mekishizufu","month":"2013-11","story":"What is a y-combinator? (2008)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Builds FizzBuzz from pure lambdas in Ruby","quote":"I strongly recommend watching these videos","text":"If you are curious about the topic, I strongly recommend watching these videos: Y Not- Adventures in Functional Programming by Jim Weirich [LINK] Programming with Nothing by Tom Stuart [VIDEO]"},{"id":18799134,"author":"stan_rogers","month":"2019-01","story":"FizzBuzz in ten languages","stance":"rec","why":"Good explanation of FizzBuzz in lambda calculus using restricted Ruby","quote":"he does a lambda calculus implementation of FizzBuzz in what's left of Ruby","text":"There's a good elaboration of it in Tom Stuart's \"Programming With Nothing\" talk[0], in which he does a lambda calculus implementation of FizzBuzz in what's left of Ruby if you leave everything out except the things strictly necessary to do lambda calculus - with the exception of what you could call C-style macros (basically text search-and-replace, so you can call a section of code FOO and just type FOO everywhere you would have typed that section of code). There's also the little matter of output - you need to translate the resulting Church numerals into readable form for those who can't see that you've obviously produced correct FizzBuzz. There's a good enough explanation that what needs to be sort of hand-waved for brevity (things that are shown but not really explained in detail) aren't entirely opaque - you will be able to figure it out. Keep in mind, too, that the lambda calculus is a lot like machine language in one respect - the same little bit of \"code\" can have more than one meaning to the programmer even if the \"machine\" is doing exactly the same thing. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13915601,"author":"stan_rogers","month":"2017-03","story":"Whiteboard problems in pure Lambda Calculus","stance":"reference","why":"Same ideas in Ruby stabby-proc notation","quote":"The same thing, but in Ruby stabby-proc notation (from 2012) by Tom Stuart","text":"The same thing, but in Ruby stabby-proc notation (from 2012) by Tom Stuart: [VIDEO] Well, perhaps not exactly the same thing, but only because Ruby requires the Z-combinator due to applicative order."}],"r":[["jcKRGpMiVTw",1],["YuIIjLr6vUA",1],["-EVqrDlAqYo",1],["FITJMJjASUs",1]]},"vYA0f6R5KAI":{"wy":"HN points to it as evidence of SpaceX's serious simulation capability and as a showcase of GPU compute for CFD.","q":{"x":"This talk was given at NVIDIA GTC 2015 and inspired me to go into manufacturing","i":41906726,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41906726,"author":"paulsutter","month":"2024-10","story":"Can SpaceX land a rocket with 1/2 cm accuracy?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk on full-scale combustion simulation for Raptor; inspired the commenter's career","quote":"This talk was given at NVIDIA GTC 2015 and inspired me to go into manufacturing","text":"The key was the full scale combustion simulator software that SpaceX developed to design the Raptor. This talk was given at NVIDIA GTC 2015 and inspired me to go into manufacturing [VIDEO] title: \"GPUs To Mars: Full Scale Simulation of SpaceX's Mars Rocket Engine\""},{"id":32782414,"author":"danielvf","month":"2022-09","story":"Some things I realized about AI while contemplating slide rule prices on eBay","stance":"rec","why":"Amazing SpaceX simulation video","quote":"Here's one of several amazing videos from SpaceX simulations.","text":"Or maybe it is that SpaceX is doing simulations on a level ahead of what everyone else does. Here's one of several amazing videos from SpaceX simulations. (There are more, but I don't have the link to the others) [VIDEO]"},{"id":19887895,"author":"swimfar","month":"2019-05","story":"Trump Administration Might “Re-Examine” Climate Modeling","stance":"rec","why":"Presentation on tricky fluid dynamics modeling for rocket engines","quote":"There's a good video presentation on it here","text":"I remember that. Fluid dynamics is a very tricky thing to model and the work they did was really cool. There's a good video presentation on it here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10715529,"author":"cma","month":"2015-12","story":"The first plasma: the Wendelstein 7-X fusion device is now in operation","stance":"rec","why":"Adaptive grid and wavelet compression in rocket engine simulation","quote":"I think these are still 2D, but pretty amazing","text":"There are bigger optimizations than 10X with some of the adaptive grid / wavelet compression stuff. I think these are still 2D, but pretty amazing: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10715530,"author":"cma","month":"2015-12","story":"The first plasma: the Wendelstein 7-X fusion device is now in operation","stance":"rec","why":"Adaptive grid and wavelet compression in rocket engine simulation","quote":"I think these are still 2D, but pretty amazing","text":"There are bigger optimizations than 10X with some of the adaptive grid / wavelet compression stuff. I think these are still 2D, but pretty amazing: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9872121,"author":"T-A","month":"2015-07","story":"Ask HN: What companies are doing work related to scientific computing?","stance":"rec","why":"SpaceX rocket engine GPU simulation talk with further links","quote":"Here's a fun recent link","text":"Here's a fun recent link: [VIDEO] Plenty more options to explore in the links under the video, starting from [LINK] Then there is NVIDIA's CUDA Zone with lists like [LINK]"},{"id":40932435,"author":"timschmidt","month":"2024-07","story":"Johns Hopkins medical school will be free for most after $1B donation","stance":"reference","why":"SpaceX simulation software as evidence of capability","quote":"And SpaceX has the best simulation software in the business","text":"> I'm dead inside. No kidding? > Also watch some Thunderf00t Somehow I knew that tool was your \"source\". He's never done anything but complain on the internet, often parroting outright falsehoods. Watch his videos from a few years back and compare his bullshit with what actually happened. Huffing that stuff will kill your brain cells. Scott works in the engineering professions. > I'd trust Bezos over Musk any day. I'd welcome another vendor of reusable rockets. Really love Rocketlab and Stoke who are doing same. Still waiting on Bezos though. I hope he succeeds. Meanwhile, SpaceX keeps launching and breaking records. > It is a key thing if you never re-lit your engines in vacuum and zero-g. Like they did for the landing burn? Yeah, they checked that one off the list already. Firing ullage thrusters before lighting engines is technically rocket science, but not the hard part. > I can guess that there was some money up front This isn't pictionary. Guesses don't count. > Ability for modelling and simulations and to test things properly And SpaceX has the best simulation software in the business: [VIDEO] It is not sufficient to molecularly model a system as large and complex as Starship, nor as fast as building and flying a rocket."},{"id":40264386,"author":"paulsutter","month":"2024-05","story":"Computers Reduce Efficiency: Case Studies of the Solow Paradox (2023)","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Raptor designed with a combustion simulator","quote":"SpaceX Raptor engine was designed using a full engine combustion simulator, without which the engine would have been impossible","text":"SpaceX Raptor engine was designed using a full engine combustion simulator, without which the engine would have been impossible [1]. Not to mention the rapid evolution from Raptor 1 to 3 [2] Jet aircraft are 70% more efficient since 1967, largely from simulation [3], similar in automotive Unclear how the NVIDIA H100 would have been designed by hand-drawing 80 billion transistors Net-net: Computers necessary, but we need much better UIs and systems. Maybe AI will help us improve this [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] > Jet airliners became 70% more fuel efficient between 1967 and 2007, 40% due to improvements in engine efficiency and 30% from airframes."},{"id":35527038,"author":"mlindner","month":"2023-04","story":"SpaceX closing in on first Starship Super Heavy launch","stance":"reference","why":"SpaceX talk showing supercomputer aerodynamics is long established","quote":"SpaceX has even done talks that can be found on youtube on it themselves.","text":"Except nothing in SLS was strictly new. The engines are literally re-used, the solid rocket boosters are not new and are 5 segment versions of the boosters used on Shuttle, the tank design is a load-bearing version of the Shuttle. It's all 70s/80s tech at best. There's nothing new in using supercomputers to compute aerodynamics. It's been done for decades with ever further precision. SpaceX has even done talks that can be found on youtube on it themselves. [VIDEO] The heat protection technology is literally the exact same material as used on Apollo, except re-formed into tiles, something SpaceX has been doing with their own heat shielding technology for decades. The landing/pacifying procedures are variants of Shuttle procedures as the same propellants are used (which are also very old). They're even the same engines and fuel used on Apollo and the Shuttle. (Also, I do not describe \"happened to die during period of employment\" as people \"giving their lives for the program\". That's just extreme propaganda.) So what experience was gained that was not in the form of \"re-learning things that were forgotten\"?"},{"id":29405884,"author":"Const-me","month":"2021-12","story":"WebGPU computations performance in comparison to WebGL","stance":"reference","why":"Example of compute-bound GPU workload from SpaceX","quote":"SpaceX presentation about rocket engines","text":"> are there other good applications the GPU pipeline tends to be useful for? Pretty much everything compute-bound. SpaceX presentation about rocket engines: [VIDEO] Unreal Engine 5 presentation about rendering many millions of small triangles using compute shaders: [LINK] > Are there guidelines on how complex a program you can run on GPU cores and still reap benefit? Arbitrarily complex. Many real-world GPGPU programs are split over multiple compute shaders / kernels. When implemented properly, they even run in parallel. This is critically important on Windows with DirectCompute, because 2 seconds TDR timeout. When a single compute shader takes more than 2 seconds, the OS concludes the GPU’s hang, resets the hardware and reloads the driver. > What are (vague, order of magnitude) limits on program size and size of the different memories you have access to? Technically unlimited because you can stream data from system memory, disk, or even internets. Practically, most modern GPUs come with 6-12 GB VRAM, for optimal performance you’d want your data to fit there. When doing micro-optimizations, another important number is amount of on-chip memory, the order of magnitude is 64kb per core. > Can you do monte carlo simulations, decision trees etc? Monte Carlo is a great fit for GPUs, just don’t forget to seed your RNG with SV_DispatchThreadID or an equivalent. Trees are tricky. GPUs share instruction decoder and instruction pointer over 32-64 hardware threads. A straightforward impl"},{"id":27144324,"author":"hwillis","month":"2021-05","story":"SpaceX launches Starlink sats in record 10th liftoff, landing of reused rocket","stance":"reference","why":"Talk by SpaceX engineers on GPU solvers for engine simulation","quote":"Here's a talk by the guys","text":"To put those phrases in context, I think they would have required more effort than the Apollo project. > Obviously, although I have doubts that the computations being performed by these \"GPU solvers\" really require a modern custom-built GPU and couldn't have been done on FPGAs or arrays of microcontrollers from the 80s. Here's a talk by the guys: [VIDEO] The Cray 2 could do <2 gigaflops. So a single simulation with 10,000 points per side at 1 microsecond timesteps for .1 seconds with ~50 flops per cell-step would have taken 79 years to run, assuming the Cray had 50 cycle fetch/store times. Engineers need to run lots of simulations to come up with a working design. And that's just simulating something around the scale of a single nozzle- you'll need to be doing sims for a 40 meter tall stage as it flips around. Also, the cray had a 256 Mword memory. So you could only store a single timestep of a grid of 400x400x400 cells anyway. The supercomputers of the 80s were really just not very useful for things like this."},{"id":26009775,"author":"NortySpock","month":"2021-02","story":"SpaceX SN9 Explodes on Landing","stance":"reference","why":"SpaceX ran high-fidelity CFD simulations by 2015","quote":"they were doing high fidelity high performance computational fluid dynamics simulations and aerodynamic simulations by 2015.","text":"[VIDEO] Yeah, they were doing high fidelity high performance computational fluid dynamics simulations and aerodynamic simulations by 2015. So I think they've done the sims, and now they're doing it in real life."},{"id":24062172,"author":"paulsutter","month":"2020-08","story":"SpaceX test flight of Starship SN-5 [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Simulator behind SpaceX's Raptor methane engine design","quote":"SpaceX developed an incredibly advanced simulator to make it happen","text":"In addition to being full-flow, Raptor runs on methane (CH4, the simplest hydrocarbon), which already exists many places in the solar system (including Mars), and is easier to manufacture than kerosene or liquid hydrogen. SpaceX developed an incredibly advanced simulator to make it happen: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16569761,"author":"Const-me","month":"2018-03","story":"Tesla GPU Accelerator Bang for the Buck, Kepler to Volta","stance":"reference","why":"SpaceX presentation on GPU rocket simulations using adaptive grids","quote":"Here’s an old SpaceX presentation about their GPU simulations of rockets","text":"> that would be complex and severely impact performance on GPU architectures Agree about the complexity. However, adaptive grids can be a huge win in terms of performance. The GPU doesn’t need to simulate the whole 8G grid nodes, it only simulates much fewer nodes, only in the areas of large gradients / higher frequencies of the simulated fields. Here’s an old SpaceX presentation about their GPU simulations of rockets: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15458771,"author":"DiThi","month":"2017-10","story":"Hyperloop One Becomes ‘Virgin Hyperloop One’","stance":"reference","why":"Shows SpaceX engineers use advanced simulation tools","quote":"Rocket engineers with advanced simulation tools[0] have been working on this for 5 months before releasing the paper.","text":"How about you also copy the sentence that lies just between those two? \"As land slowly settles to a new position over time, the damper neutral position can be adjusted accordingly.\" And if that doesn't work, the tube can have some kind of rails or just to roll over wheels on top of the pylons. (my own thoughts as non expert) Rocket engineers with advanced simulation tools[0] have been working on this for 5 months before releasing the paper. Do you really think they somehow missed this obvious issue? [0] [VIDEO] Edit: Further in the paper it says: \"The tube will be supported by pillars which constrain the tube in the vertical direction but allow longitudinal slip for thermal expansion as well as dampened lateral slip to reduce the risk posed by earthquakes.\" So pretty much what I've said. The other quoted sentence refers to placement of pylons themselves."}],"r":[["XSoOrlh5i1k",1],["63vqob-MljQ",1],["xn76r0JxqNM",1],["EfGD2qveGdQ",1],["rRbY3TMUcgQ",1],["vq2nnJ4g6N0",1],["k2h2lvhzMDc",1]]},"ymaWq5yZIYM":{"wy":"Quoted heavily in moderation and free speech debates, particularly the line about freedom of speech and reach.","q":{"x":"This is a thought-provoking speech. Do yourself a favor and watch it in its entirety","i":21608168,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21608168,"author":"cs702","month":"2019-11","story":"Sacha Baron Cohen Uses ADL Speech to Tear Apart Mark Zuckerberg and Facebook","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Thought-provoking speech on social media responsibility","quote":"This is a thought-provoking speech. Do yourself a favor and watch it in its entirety","text":"This is a thought-provoking speech. Do yourself a favor and watch it in its entirety -- before commenting: [VIDEO] Among the many thorny issues and questions raised by Cohen: * The business model of social media companies is powered by engagement , which is greatest for content that arouses the basest instincts and feelings of human beings, including fear and hatred. Social media companies earn more with the basest content. * Social media companies are ideal propaganda machines, enabling anyone willing to appeal to the worst in human nature to reach billions of people with a click. * Do social media companies bear responsibility for the negative impact their products have on society, in the same way that, say, car companies bear responsibility for faulty engines or airplane manufacturers bear responsibility for faulty plane designs? * Are social media companies publishers , like broadcast TV networks, magazines, and newspapers? Should social media companies be held to decency standards, like all publishers? I'm barely scratching the surface. Do yourself a favor and watch the whole thing!"},{"id":21608174,"author":"cs702","month":"2019-11","story":"Sacha Baron Cohen: Facebook would have let Hitler buy ads for 'final solution'","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Thought-provoking speech on social media responsibility","quote":"This is a thought-provoking speech. Do yourself a favor and watch it in its entirety","text":"This is a thought-provoking speech. Do yourself a favor and watch it in its entirety -- before commenting: [VIDEO] Among the many thorny issues and questions raised by Cohen: * The business model of social media companies is powered by engagement , which is greatest for content that arouses the basest instincts and feelings of human beings, including fear and hatred. Social media companies earn more with the basest content. * Social media companies are ideal propaganda machines, enabling anyone willing to appeal to the worst in human nature to reach billions of people with a click. * Do social media companies bear responsibility for the negative impact their products have on society, in the same way that, say, car companies bear responsibility for faulty engines or airplane manufacturers bear responsibility for faulty plane designs? * Are social media companies publishers , like broadcast TV networks, magazines, and newspapers? Should social media companies be held to decency standards, like all publishers? I'm barely scratching the surface. Do yourself a favor and watch the whole thing!"},{"id":45354323,"author":"breadwinner","month":"2025-09","story":"YouTube says it'll bring back creators banned for Covid and election content","stance":"rec","why":"Excerpts on social media amplifying absurdities and bigotry","quote":"Freedom of speech is not freedom of reach.","text":"It is social media that is the root problem. On the internet everything can appear equally legitimate. Breitbart looks as legit as the BBC. Sacha Baron Cohen [VIDEO] Excerpts: Voltaire was right when he said \"Those who can make you believe absurdities can make you commit atrocities.\" And social media lets authoritarians push absurdities to millions of people. Freedom of speech is not freedom of reach. Sadly There will always be racists, misogynists, anti-Semites, and child abusers. We should not be giving bigots and pedophiles a free platform to amplify their views and target their victims. Zuckerberg says people should decide what's credible, not tech companies. When 2/3rds of millennials have not heard of Auschwitz how are they supposed to know what's true? There is such a thing as objective truth. Facts do exist."},{"id":32028529,"author":"petilon","month":"2022-07","story":"Notice of termination of Twitter merger agreement","stance":"rec","why":"Answers free speech question about social media amplification","quote":"Freedom of speech is not freedom of reach.","text":"I am going to let Sacha Baron Cohen, of all people, answer your question [1]: Voltaire was right when he said \"Those who can make you believe absurdities can make you commit atrocities.\" And social media lets authoritarians push absurdities to millions of people. President Trump using Twitter has spread conspiracy theories more than 1700 times to his 67 million followers. Freedom of speech is not freedom of reach. Sadly There will always be racists, misogynists, anti-Semites, and child abusers. We should not be giving bigots and pedophiles a free platform to amplify their views and target their victims. Zuckerberg says people should decide what's credible, not tech companies. When 2/3rds of millennials have not heard of Auschwitz how are they supposed to know what's true? There is such a thing as objective truth. Facts do exist. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32028579,"author":"petilon","month":"2022-07","story":"Notice of termination of Twitter merger agreement","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why unrestricted free speech is not beneficial","quote":"Sacha Baron Cohen explains it best","text":"Disagree, there is no absolute freedom of speech, for example you can't yell \"fire\" in a crowded theater. Also disagree that such unrestricted free speech is constructive or beneficial. Sacha Baron Cohen explains it best: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25731884,"author":"flowerlad","month":"2021-01","story":"Facebook Admits It Was Used to Incite Violence in Myanmar (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"Excerpts on social media amplifying absurdities and bigotry","quote":"Freedom of speech is not freedom of reach.","text":"On the internet everything can appear equally legitimate. Breitbart looks as legit as the BBC. Sacha Baron Cohen [VIDEO] Excerpts: Voltaire was right when he said \"Those who can make you believe absurdities can make you commit atrocities.\" And social media lets authoritarians push absurdities to millions of people. President Trump using Twitter has spread conspiracy theories more than 1700 times to his 67 million followers. Freedom of speech is not freedom of reach. Sadly There will always be racists, misogynists, anti-Semites, and child abusers. We should not be giving bigots and pedophiles a free platform to amplify their views and target their victims. Zuckerberg says people should decide what's credible, not tech companies. When 2/3rds of millennials have not heard of Auschwitz how are they supposed to know what's true? There is such a thing as objective truth. Facts do exist."},{"id":25753341,"author":"flowerlad","month":"2021-01","story":"How Silicon Valley destroyed Parler","stance":"rec","why":"Excerpts on social media amplifying absurdities and bigotry","quote":"Freedom of speech is not freedom of reach.","text":"> do we really want a handful of unelected billionaires deciding what is acceptable speech Historically newspapers, television and radio have decided what is acceptable to be published through their media. There is no reason modern internet-based media should be any different. On the internet everything can appear equally legitimate. Breitbart looks as legit as the BBC. Sacha Baron Cohen [VIDEO] Excerpts: Voltaire was right when he said \"Those who can make you believe absurdities can make you commit atrocities.\" And social media lets authoritarians push absurdities to millions of people. President Trump using Twitter has spread conspiracy theories more than 1700 times to his 67 million followers. Freedom of speech is not freedom of reach. Sadly There will always be racists, misogynists, anti-Semites, and child abusers. We should not be giving bigots and pedophiles a free platform to amplify their views and target their victims. Zuckerberg says people should decide what's credible, not tech companies. When 2/3rds of millennials have not heard of Auschwitz how are they supposed to know what's true? There is such a thing as objective truth. Facts do exist."},{"id":25778320,"author":"flowerlad","month":"2021-01","story":"Facebook Incubated the Insurrection","stance":"rec","why":"Social media as greatest propaganda machine in history","quote":"All this hate and violence is being facilitated by a handful of internet companies that amount to the greatest propaganda machine in history.","text":"Conspiracy theories once confined to the fringe are going mainstream. It is as if the age of reason -- the era of evidential argument -- is ending. Knowledge is de-legitimized and scientific consensus is dismissed. Democracy which depends on shared truths is in retreat, and autocracy, which depends on shared lies, is on the march. All this hate and violence is being facilitated by a handful of internet companies that amount to the greatest propaganda machine in history. The above is by Sacha Baron Cohen, see: [VIDEO] More excerpts: Voltaire was right when he said \"Those who can make you believe absurdities can make you commit atrocities.\" And social media lets authoritarians push absurdities to millions of people. President Trump using Twitter has spread conspiracy theories more than 1700 times to his 67 million followers. Freedom of speech is not freedom of reach. Sadly There will always be racists, misogynists, anti-Semites, and child abusers. We should not be giving bigots and pedophiles a free platform to amplify their views and target their victims. Zuckerberg says people should decide what's credible, not tech companies. When 2/3rds of millennials have not heard of Auschwitz how are they supposed to know what's true? There is such a thing as objective truth. Facts do exist."},{"id":25363387,"author":"flowerlad","month":"2020-12","story":"YouTube to remove content that alleges widespread election fraud","stance":"rec","why":"Excerpts on social media amplifying absurdities and bigotry","quote":"Freedom of speech is not freedom of reach.","text":"On the internet everything can appear equally legitimate. Breitbart looks as legit as the BBC. Sacha Baron Cohen [VIDEO] Excerpts: Voltaire was right when he said \"Those who can make you believe absurdities can make you commit atrocities.\" And social media lets authoritarians push absurdities to millions of people. President Trump using Twitter has spread conspiracy theories more than 1700 times to his 67 million followers. Freedom of speech is not freedom of reach. Sadly There will always be racists, misogynists, anti-Semites, and child abusers. We should not be giving bigots and pedophiles a free platform to amplify their views and target their victims. Zuckerberg says people should decide what's credible, not tech companies. When 2/3rds of millennials have not heard of Auschwitz how are they supposed to know what's true? There is such a thing as objective truth. Facts do exist."},{"id":21653494,"author":"dfsegoat","month":"2019-11","story":"Twitter CEO Endorses DuckDuckGo","stance":"rec","why":"Well put together award speech on manipulation at scale","quote":"I found it to be pretty well put together","text":"He gets into the relevant details in his speech at below timestamp [1]: > \"Just imagine what Goebbels [2] could have done with Facebook\" I think the bottom line is he is good at social engineering, and he recognizes the signs of manipulation at scale. Beyond that, he is smart enough to consume the literature. Note: Video is his award speech from ADL. But has sensitive wording and is NSFW. I found it to be pretty well put together - maybe reaching a bit in parts. [1] - [VIDEO] [2] - [LINK]"},{"id":21657291,"author":"mattigames","month":"2019-11","story":"Facebook's only Dutch factchecker quits over political ad exemption","stance":"rec","why":"Explains harm of social media propaganda better","quote":"This guy can explain it better than I do","text":"I can convince you in the same degree you are willing to understand any evidence I give, a similar challenge arises with anti-vaxxers, a group that few years ago was just a laughing stock but now thanks to the online fear propaganda through social media now has created some focus of kids without vaccines creating disease risks believed to be long gone, such risks of social network are measureable, but there are things that are practically harder to measure like if the \"pedophile hillary pizza place\" changed what people think about that political candidate; in general the way people are influenciated by propaganda is not immediate but take years to slowly aggregate with other falsehoods or uncorrelated facts in order to disrupt society. Of course I see the problem of trying to separate truth/false stuff and you can get all philosophical about it but at the end of the day just trying to remove the blatant lies like \"Hillary is an alien\" and \"Vaccines cause autism\" would give a better outcome than doing nothing at all that is what Facebook does right now about it. This guy can explain it better than I do: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31747410,"author":"minsc_and_boo","month":"2022-06","story":"Japan passes revised penal code with harsher punishment for cyberbullying","stance":"reference","why":"Sums up the case against unrestricted speech","quote":"Sacha Baron Cohen also sums it up nicely in his speech","text":"It sounds like you have an opinion but would rather sea lion. In the event that you are being genuine, there have been multitudes of instances. - Post jan 6 insurrection has left Republicans trying to justify their seditious invasion of the U.S. capital as protests protected by speech: [LINK] - The \"Cancel culture\" pejorative has created a double standard where a certain political party cries foul for being censored on private platforms (for violating TOS) and push for government regulation of said platforms to protect their political opinions. They are even trying to redefine private websites as the public square to do it. - Sacha Baron Cohen also sums it up nicely in his speech: [VIDEO] If you really are genuine with your questions and asking for citations, you can google more topics about any of the above."},{"id":25364223,"author":"flowerlad","month":"2020-12","story":"YouTube to remove content that alleges widespread election fraud","stance":"reference","why":"More on source legitimacy online","quote":"More on that by Sacha Baron Cohen here","text":"We won't have that problem here. There will still be New York Times, Wall Street Journal, etc. More weight should be given to these legit sources than to social media. On the internet everything can appear equally legitimate. Breitbart looks as legit as the BBC. That's a problem. More on that by Sacha Baron Cohen here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22468277,"author":"runarberg","month":"2020-03","story":"Cambridge Analytica were the tip of the iceberg","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as origin of the discussion","quote":"I think this came up after Sacha Baron Cohen gave his famous speech at the ADL","text":"I think this came up after Sacha Baron Cohen gave his famous speech at the ADL[1]. What makes social media political campaign so much more dangerous then traditional campaigns is that the investigative journalists don’t have the same access to the campaign claims as the targets, and hence cannot scrutinize, contextualize and compare them. You are not alone in not seeing any evidence. Professional journalists with years of experience have the same problem. But until we either learn how to overcome this limitation with new investigative techniques, or—better yet—regulate political campaigns with national laws, I suggest we remember the fact that: “Absence of evidence is not evidence of absence.”[2] [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":22007536,"author":"gonzo41","month":"2020-01","story":"Things that I think will be important in the next decade","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Mark is lazy and value signalling. --There are a number of areas where I believe governments establishing clearer rules would be helpful, including around elections, harmful content, privacy, and data portability. Mark could do a lot on his own without waiting for government. Mark could: Ban harmful content, he is a US citizen and could start with keeping a tighter lid on content that doesn't conform with non extremist western values. And then integrate other value systems shortly over time. Mark could: get out of all election advertising and push Facebook to be a place where everyone doesn't talk about that stuff and ban targeted political adds and just do other stuff. Mark could: Easily respect peoples privacy. I hardened my facebook profile a while back to remove most public content and the level of granularity to the privacy controls seemed like it was intentionally made difficult to do what I was doing. Dark patterns. Mark could show leadership. he really could, he doesn't. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21620087,"author":"hackersean","month":"2019-11","story":"TikTok: Cheerfulness and censorship","stance":"reference","why":"Speech highlights a real societal problem","quote":"Having just watched this it seems like TikTok is working to solve what is a real problem for our society.","text":"Having just watched this it seems like TikTok is working to solve what is a real problem for our society. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21653389,"author":"pwython","month":"2019-11","story":"Twitter CEO Endorses DuckDuckGo","stance":"reference","why":"ADL description of the award","quote":"","text":"From the Anti-Defamation League: > Sacha Baron Cohen is the well-deserved recipient of ADL’s International Leadership Award, which goes to exceptional individuals who combine professional success with a profound personal commitment to community involvement and to crossing borders and barriers with a message of diversity and equal opportunity. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21605621,"author":"aww_dang","month":"2019-11","story":"Sacha Baron Cohen Uses ADL Speech to Tear Apart Mark Zuckerberg and Facebook","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Six unelected individuals in silicon valley imposing their vision on the rest of the world","text":"From the video: [VIDEO] \"This is ideological imperialism. Six unelected individuals in silicon valley imposing their vision on the rest of the world, unaccountable to any government...\" \"Now here's an idea, instead of letting the silicon six decide the fate of the world, let our elected representatives voted for by the people of every democracy of the world have at least some say...\" Democracy is a political system, not a system of rational inquiry. It can not establish facts, outside of voting statistics. Dressing up censorship with the pretenses of democracy is nothing more than appealing to the tyranny of the masses. Democracy as we know it requires a free and open forum for discussion. The trend towards 'fact checking' disappoints me. Individuals must be capable of processing and consuming information without authoritarian hand-holding. To pessimistically dismiss this possibility is to refute the premises of democracy. It baffles me how proponents of censorship feel that they can have it both ways here. Furthermore, the underlying philosophy of fact-checking dismisses the fallible nature of man. Information is constantly emerging. The horizon is contentious by definition. What would fact checkers have said to Copernicus? That a scientific consensus has already been formed? The popular view that we have somehow escaped these human limitations is nothing more than special pleading: \"This time is different. This is the age of reason and science. Dogma is dead.\" This time is "}],"r":[["SA8ZBJWo73E",1],["tX4H_ctggYo",1],["pqzcCfUglws",1],["t705r8ICkRw",1],["yx7-yvXf6f8",1],["gDdq2rIqAlM",1]]},"IroPQ150F6c":{"wy":"HN cites it as one of the best talks on data-oriented design, applying struct-of-arrays and compact data layouts to a real compiler.","q":{"x":"A good introduction to SoA (for anyone curious) are the two most famous Data-Oriented Design talks","i":49012929,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":49012929,"author":"Rendello","month":"2026-07","story":"Everyone Should Know SIMD","stance":"rec","why":"One of the two most famous data-oriented design talks, good intro to SoA.","quote":"A good introduction to SoA (for anyone curious) are the two most famous Data-Oriented Design talks","text":"A good introduction to SoA (for anyone curious) are the two most famous Data-Oriented Design talks by Mike Acton (game engine dev) [1] and Andrew Kelley (Zig lead dev) [2] respectively. I read a book book about DoD [3] really which confused me at first with all its talk about database table design (in a book about a high-performance C++ game engine?), but when it finally clicked it was amazing. The point is that you want to think hard about your access patterns and what could constitute good \"primary keys\", then model it accordingly. SoA ends up being useful a lot of the time, because having your data in homogeneous arrays/vectors is great for cache locality and branch elimination. Even without SIMD you can get huge speedups from that, but that's also where your compiler (or you as a programmer) can get incredible SIMD gains. SoA is not a silver bullet as it may not align well with your access patterns, but it can great to add to your toolkit. --- Mike Acton: Data-Oriented Design and C++ : [LINK] Andrew Kelley: A Practical Guide to Applying Data Oriented Design : [VIDEO] Richard Fabian: Data-Oriented Design : [LINK]"},{"id":46666189,"author":"gethly","month":"2026-01","story":"Why Object of Arrays beat interleaved arrays: a JavaScript performance issue","stance":"rec","why":"Video of interest on struct-of-arrays layouts.","quote":"here's few videos of interest","text":"SoA is nothing new. Not sure why it needs to be even discussed... Anyway, here's few videos of interest: [LINK] [VIDEO] Odin also supports SoA natively [LINK]"},{"id":45610403,"author":"Rendello","month":"2025-10","story":"Your data model is your destiny","stance":"rec","why":"Data-oriented design applied in the Zig compiler.","quote":"DoD in the Zig compiler","text":"I recently spent a week or so creating a library for my project. There's not a lot of code, but it was hard to reason about the data model, what I wanted the API to look like, and what I wanted actually rendered on the other side. I was proud after getting it working, but when I had to run dozens of files through it, it was horribly slow. I don't tend to write a lot of hot code, so I was excited by the fact I had to profile it and make it efficient. I decided that I should rewrite the system, as my mental model had improved and the code was doing a lot more than it should be doing for no reason. I've spent the last few days redesigning it according to Data-Oriented Design. I'm trying to get the wall-clock time down by more than an order of magnitude. I'm not sure how it's going to turn out, wish me luck :) Since I mentioned DoD, these three links will probably come up in conversation: Mike Acton's famous performance talk: [LINK] DoD in the Zig compiler: [VIDEO] The DoD book: [LINK]"},{"id":44740628,"author":"iamcreasy","month":"2025-07","story":"Writing memory efficient C structs","stance":"rec","why":"Applies data-oriented design ideas to the Zig toolchain.","quote":"there is a related talk by Andrew Kelley implementing these ideas on Zig toolchain","text":"This reminds of an amazing talk by Mike Acton on Data oriented design: [LINK] And there is a related talk by Andrew Kelley implementing these ideas on Zig toolchain: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42084310,"author":"emmanueloga_","month":"2024-11","story":"Rust for tokenising and parsing","stance":"rec","why":"Strategies to reduce memory footprint and make programs cache friendly.","quote":"Another great talk about making efficient lexers and parsers is Andrew Kelley's \"Practical Data Oriented Design","text":"That talk is great, but I remember some discussion later about Go actually NOT using this technique because of goroutine scheduling overhead and/or inefficient memory allocation patterns? The best discussion I could find is [1]. Another great talk about making efficient lexers and parsers is Andrew Kelley's \"Practical Data Oriented Design\" [2]. Summary: \"it explains various strategies one can use to reduce memory footprint of programs while also making the program cache friendly which increase throughput\". -- 1: [LINK] 2: [VIDEO]"},{"id":49062106,"author":"lioeters","month":"2026-07","story":"Introduction to Data-Oriented Design [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Entry in a reading and watching list on data-oriented design.","quote":"","text":"- Data-Oriented Design Revisited: Type Safety in the Zig Compiler - Matthew Lugg - [LINK] - Andrew Kelley Practical Data Oriented Design (DoD) - [VIDEO] - CppCon 2014: Mike Acton \"Data-Oriented Design and C++\" - [LINK] - Why You Shouldn’t Forget to Optimize the Data Layout - [LINK] - Handles are the better pointers - [LINK] - Enum of Arrays - [LINK] - Data oriented design book - [LINK] - Data-oriented design in practice - Stoyan Nikolov - [LINK] - Programming without Pointers - Andrew Kelley - [LINK] - More Speed & Simplicity: Practical Data-Oriented Design in C++ - Vittorio Romeo - CppCon 2025 - [LINK] - Rust Handle - [LINK]"},{"id":48399032,"author":"Rendello","month":"2026-06","story":"Learn SQL Once, Use It for 30 Years","stance":"reference","why":"Explains Zig compiler speedups using data-oriented design principles.","quote":"Andrew Kelley's talk [3] where he explains speedups in the Zig compiler using DoD principles","text":"I think I had a pretty good understanding of set theory through programming, and although I tried to get into the more mathematical side of it, I found most things to be 1. trivial (because I was used to thinking in lists, sets, hashmaps, etc.), or 2. irrelevant to me. The latter was a shame, because I've always liked the abstraction of math, but I couldn't help but feel I wasn't learning anything actionable when learning more about sets (though maybe I'm wrong). What had a big impact on me was the relational model, specifically after reading Richard Fabian's Data-oriented Design book [1]. I had watched Mike Acton's famous Data-oriented Design talk [2], then Andrew Kelley's talk [3] where he explains speedups in the Zig compiler using DoD principles (largely using methods from Acton's talk), but Fabian's book tied these concepts to database normalization and the relational model. Most DoD advice is very \"exercise left to the reader\", because it's about matching a specific problem, but using the relational model and considering your data's primary and foreign key relations can be really powerful. I just wish more of that power was exposed through regular programming language interfaces, rather than having to pull in and marshal data through a DB. I might have to try C# and Linq. 1. [LINK] 2. [LINK] 3. [VIDEO]"},{"id":46154026,"author":"publicdebates","month":"2025-12","story":"Thoughts on Go vs. Rust vs. Zig","stance":"reference","why":"Kelley's data-oriented design talk as context for Zig's design.","quote":"","text":"Good write up, I like where you're going with this. Your article reads like a recent graduate who's full of excitement and passion for the wonderful world of programming, and just coming into the real world for the first time. For Go, I wouldn't say that the choice to avoid generics was either intentional or minimalist by nature. From what I recall, they were just struggling for a long time with a difficult decision, which trade-offs to make. And I think they were just hoping that, given enough time, the community could perhaps come up with a new, innovative solution that resolves them gracefully. And I think after a decade they just kind of settled on a solution, as the clock was ticking. I could be wrong. For Rust, I would strongly disagree on two points. First, lifetimes are in fact what tripped me up the most, and many others, famously including Brian Kernighan, who literally wrote the book on C. Second, Rust isn't novel in combining many other ideas into the language. Lots of languages do that, like C#. But I do recall thinking that Rust had some odd name choices for some features it adopted. And, not being a C++ person myself, it has solutions to many problems I never wrestled with, known by name to C++ devs but foreign to me. For Zig's manual memory management, you say: > this is a design choice very much related to the choice to exclude OOP features. Maybe, but I think it's more based on Andrew's need for Data-Oriented Design when designing high performance applicatio"},{"id":45647918,"author":"Rendello","month":"2025-10","story":"Forth: The programming language that writes itself","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on data-oriented design in Zig, part of the commenter's research.","quote":"","text":"TL;DR: I'm trying to Forth a Lisp. This is long winded, but maybe you have some thoughts here. I've been building a DOM-builder API recently in Rust. The existing options (there are many) tend to use textual templating, which can't reason well about anything, or basic macros, which never support indentation. I wanted something that was just code, where I'd be in full control on the indentation (or lack thereof) programmatically. The closest equivalent is Python's Dominate [1], though its indentation mechanisms are buggy. So I built a system using the traditional tree where Nodes own other Nodes at random addresses, and I built a renderer that renders those nodes and concatenates their strings recursively. It ended up working but it was hacky and very slow for the large inputs. In release mode, it was taking almost a minute to render 70 files, and I want about two orders of magnitude lower. I ran it through profilers and optimized it a bit, but wanted to see if I could simplify the architecture and reduce the amount of work the computer could do. I read about flattening ASTs [2] and how through optimizing that format, you can end up with a sort of bytecode [3]. I also looked into Data-Oriented Design, watching Mike Acton's famous talk [4], Andrew Kelley's talk about DoD in Zig [5], and reading through the DoD book by Richard Fabian [6]. I ended up with something that works quite well for traversing and rendering, which is a stack that can be traversed and rendered in O(n), but"},{"id":41019295,"author":"brabel","month":"2024-07","story":"Aro – Zig's new C compiler","stance":"reference","why":"Talk showing compiler metrics improving by 30% or more.","quote":"","text":"The talk I am referring to is from 2024 and shows some metrics improving by 30% or more. And Andrew said that's not yet shipped: [VIDEO] So, are you sure about that?? EDIT: ok, the talk was uploaded just last week, but someone in the comments clarified that: > The original talk is from 2021.. Thank you for reuploading. I feel stupid now :/"}],"r":[["UCgoxQCf5Jg",1],["RSTNhvDGbYI",1],["wlvKAT7SZIQ",1],["vHWiDx_l4V0",1],["fhOHn9TClXY",1],["ARKbfWk4Xyw",1],["DpXy041BIlA",1],["wo84LFzx5nI",1],["VVUeqxXwCA0",1],["rQhzEnWCgHA",1],["KSWqx8goqSY",1],["tt2AYafET68",1],["Uhx-26GfCBU",1],["rX0ItVEVjHc",1],["whPWKecazgM",1]]},"_3loq22TxSc":{"wy":"Repeatedly recommended in best-talks threads as a fun, mind-blowing demonstration of unexpected computation.","q":{"x":"Strong recommend!","i":25541357,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":38615716,"author":"gnatman","month":"2023-12","story":"Ask HN: Best tech talks you have heard in 2023?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fun talk on programming in PowerPoint.","quote":"Really fun, kinda blew my mind.","text":"\"Great Impractical Ideas in Computer Science: PowerPoint Programming\" Tom Wildenham (2019) [VIDEO] Really fun, kinda blew my mind. Guy got a job at Microsoft shortly after giving this talk."},{"id":25541357,"author":"gregsadetsky","month":"2020-12","story":"Ask HN: Best Talks of 2020?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hour-long talk on PowerPoint Turing-completeness with mind-melting fractal ending.","quote":"Strong recommend!","text":"I would also very highly recommend you watch this talk about PowerPoint’s Turing-completeness — [VIDEO] It’s from the same person (Tom Wildenhain) as your video, but this is an hour long, there’s an actual audience (that’s supportive and spends most of the talk in disbelief), and the end with recursive slides / fractals is an absolute mind melt. Strong recommend! :) Thanks for the suggestions/reminder!"},{"id":41030425,"author":"jjmarr","month":"2024-07","story":"Ask HN: What's the best YouTube lecture you've seen recently?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows PowerPoint transitions and slides are Turing complete without macros.","quote":"He does not use VBA macros, just shows that PowerPoint transitions and slides are Turing Complete.","text":"[VIDEO] PowerPoint programming by Tom Wildenhain. He does not use VBA macros, just shows that PowerPoint transitions and slides are Turing Complete."},{"id":22040853,"author":"K0SM0S","month":"2020-01","story":"The “No Code” Delusion","stance":"rec","why":"Hour-long guest lecture on PowerPoint programming.","quote":"Recording of a guest lecture he gave for the Esoteric Programming Languages course at CMU.","text":"I think this drives your point with incredible wit: “On The Turing Completeness of PowerPoint (SIGBOVIK)” by Tom Wildenhain[1]. (5 minutes video) [1]: [LINK] (Nerding out should always be hilarious like that!) [2]: Recording of a guest lecture he gave for the Esoteric Programming Languages course at CMU. (~1h) [VIDEO]"},{"id":48050558,"author":"ndiddy","month":"2026-05","story":"Photoshop's challenges with focus, pt. 2","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how PowerPoint's morph feature interacts with existing features.","quote":"demonstrates how this feature interacts with the enormous existing PowerPoint feature set in interesting ways.","text":"Microsoft actually does a fairly good job with this. Here's a part of a talk that goes over a single feature in PowerPoint (a slide animation that morphs the contents of one slide into a different slide) and demonstrates how this feature interacts with the enormous existing PowerPoint feature set in interesting ways. [VIDEO] It's obviously a stupid gimmicky feature but whatever team Microsoft put on it were clearly overachievers."}],"r":[["8--5LwHRhjk",1],["C7JQ7Rpwn2k",1],["RzWB5jL5RX0",1],["MQufJBVvAtY",1],["uNjxe8ShM-8",1],["LMbI6sk-62E",1],["mVVNJKv9esE",1],["izxXGuVL21o",1]]},"wAIP6fI0NAI":{"wy":"HN commenters bring it up in threads about online mobs and cancellation as a relevant talk by a journalist who interviewed shamed people.","q":{"x":"I'm reminded of this TED talk in these scenarios, by a journalist and author who interviewed many people who have been publicly shamed","i":44637365,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44637365,"author":"dkdcio","month":"2025-07","story":"The sadist assault on the 'Coldplay couple'","stance":"rec","why":"Talk by journalist who interviewed publicly shamed people","quote":"I'm reminded of this TED talk in these scenarios, by a journalist and author who interviewed many people who have been publicly shamed","text":"I'm reminded of this TED talk in these scenarios, by a journalist and author who interviewed many people who have been publicly shamed: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34854531,"author":"josephg","month":"2023-02","story":"Hundreds of changes made to latest editions of Roald Dahl's books","stance":"rec","why":"TED talk on how tweets ruin lives","quote":"the TED talk \"How one tweet can ruin your life\" from 2015","text":"Yep; to say nothing of the psychological effects of being told that the whole world hates you. That sounds really traumatic. I'm not generally a contrapoints fan, but I really enjoyed the video she made about her experience with this on twitter: [LINK] Or the TED talk \"How one tweet can ruin your life\" from 2015: [VIDEO] We studied To Kill a Mockingbird and The Crucible when I was in highschool. I remember thinking how barbaric and despicable \"mob justice\" was. I didn't understand it, and I assumed I never would - I thought it was something we reference from history. But twitter really has brought the mob justice style witch hunts back. I don't understand how anyone can claim its not a real phenomenon. Being cancelled is obviously quite a real experience for the people it happens to."},{"id":27295840,"author":"Zealotux","month":"2021-05","story":"Life after an internet mob attack","stance":"rec","why":"Relevant talk on online shaming","quote":"A relevant TED Talk I always think of when reading about these stories","text":"A relevant TED Talk I always think of when reading about these stories: [VIDEO] Which makes me think: who are these people with enough time of their hands to be morality warriors on internet? Also who are these _friends_ who jumps on conclusion without allowing for a defense? I shouldn't be difficult, in this digitalized world, to provide solid proof of wrongdoing. I may not be the most aware of social cues, or maybe it's a cultural thing, but some of these people seems like absolute sociopaths."},{"id":24652917,"author":"sebmellen","month":"2020-10","story":"My friend starts her job today, after learning to program in prison","stance":"rec","why":"Ronson on how a tweet can ruin lives","quote":"Jon Ronson has a great TED talk about this","text":"I would hope that all agree a violent crime is worse than a racist tweet. However, there is a sentiment of sympathy afforded to murderers (that they're somehow deserving of rehabilitation) which is not always extended to those who have tweeted racist things. Perhaps this comes because a murder can be seen as a sad result of circumstances. Perhaps the criminal only had to murder because they had a bad upbringing and needed the cash and it was a horrible mistake. On the other hand, people perceived as racist are permanently marked as being ideologically dangerous. Mainly, I think this comes because racist tweeters can be seen as more directly responsible for their actions than a murderer. Of course, this line of reasoning is nonsense, but it appears to be quite common. Jon Ronson has a great TED talk about this, titled \"How one tweet can ruin your life\": [VIDEO] . I would recommend that the grandparent post watch this."},{"id":17075368,"author":"stcredzero","month":"2018-05","story":"How Social Media Exploits Our Moral Emotions","stance":"rec","why":"Great TED talk on the original Twitter kerfuffle","quote":"Great TED talk on the original twitter kerfuffle referenced in the article","text":"Great TED talk on the original twitter kerfuffle referenced in the article: [VIDEO] CGP Grey's take on how outrage is used to exploit us through social media: (Yes, I link this often, because the message is valuable.) [LINK]"},{"id":26619248,"author":"aboringusername","month":"2021-03","story":"John Sullivan resign as executive director of the Free Software Foundation","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as example of multinational storm over a joke","quote":"","text":"> I don't want to live in a world without American freedom of speech - where we can't be blunt and speak our minds. I take issue with this. I'm not hugely adept at history but from my understanding there has never been a time where you can just blurt out a communication and have it be \"accepted\" by society - this seems to me very American and appears very detached from reality and real world experience. Yes, you are accountable for the communications you make, whether via keybord, orally, or any other way. Yes, you can be killed for the communications you make - this is not new, neither shocking and has been a staple of humanity from the beginning. Yes, you will be cancelled if your joke is taken out of context, or if some journalist finds a tweet and somebody is on an airplane and at the center of a multi-national storm [0]. Textareas like the one you typed in to make your comment give you a lot of freedom, but also a lot of power, and humans are learning with great power wields responsibility. People will take offense at your crass jokes and delete you for it, perhaps it's best to step away and not click \"reply\" afterall. Your conversation with a friend may be recorded or overheard, perhaps somebody is stalking you and waiting for a golden opportunity to \"get\" you, perhaps it's possible to form a narrative the media can use that's pieced together just to convince a group then let it spread like wildfire. None of these things are new to humanity, and sometimes there's just n"},{"id":19498131,"author":"stcredzero","month":"2019-03","story":"Appeal accepted: Google removed my ads-free app for “deceptive ads”","stance":"reference","why":"Supplementary link on shaming culture","quote":"","text":"Also sadly, some of those same companies seem to be enabling this shaming culture. (Twitter banning random users for just using the LearnToCode hashtag, while not banning journalists who called for the doxxing and harassment of a minor. Also: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":19504201,"author":"stcredzero","month":"2019-03","story":"Facebook to ban white nationalist content","stance":"reference","why":"Linked as context for social media era","quote":"","text":"Company towns were a bizarre (and, arguably, pathological) special case in American history. Social media in the late 20-teens were a bizarre (and, arguably, pathological) special case in world history. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19100985,"author":"stcredzero","month":"2019-02","story":"Roger McNamee says the 'like' button was 'beginning of the end'","stance":"reference","why":"Supports point about Twitter exploitation","quote":"","text":"The thing about mechanics like upvotes/downvotes, is that they're ripe for exploitation by evil/sociopathic people. (For virality. Often for outrage-driven virality.) That's okay on a place like reddit, because things are largely pseudonymous, and if things go south, you can go to a different place or just take a break or even get a new username. Your real life network of friends and family isn't so easily abandoned or replaced. Not so sure what the difference is with Twitter, but maybe it's one of granularity. There are no boundaries there, so people have to police things themselves with block lists, or appeal to Twitter itself to get people banned. It's also clearly ripe for exploitation by sociopaths. [LINK] [VIDEO] (What of HN? HN is moderated.)"},{"id":19213405,"author":"stcredzero","month":"2019-02","story":"Dystopia Is What Results from the Attempt to Create Utopia","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as example of dehumanization","quote":"","text":"From the article: Two key identifying marks of dystopia are social regimentation and an inevitable totalitarianism. \"Conform or be cast out.\" --Rush, Subdivisions, Signals, 1982 \"Freedom of speech doesn't mean freedom from consequences.\" From the article: Next, dystopia is marked by dehumanization How one tweet can ruin your life | Jon Ronson -- [VIDEO] From the article: The abuse of technology is another characteristic of dystopia. Google. Facebook. From the article: Next, dystopia empowers a new class of rulers and uses propaganda and state terror to maintain the power of the regime...The ultimate result of dystopia can be seen in the tragedy of an individual. Instead of only relying on state terror, there is a new brand of current day authoritarianism which is trying to use non-state power through media, social media, and non-governmental civic groups. The new twist is to erode freedom of speech, but not have it be technically illegal, because the government isn't doing it. The new twist is to erode due process and presumption of innocence, but it's not technically illegal, because trial by public opinion and media isn't a judicial process. The new twist is to disenfranchise people, but not in person, only online and in social media, because it's not \"technically illegal.\" However, all of the above violates various principles of individual rights in spirit. To me, they are all dishonest means of having coercive, authoritarian effects on people at large, without being \"tech"},{"id":19218647,"author":"stcredzero","month":"2019-02","story":"Dystopia Is What Results from the Attempt to Create Utopia","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for lives ruined by Twitter storms","quote":"Tell that to people whose lives have been ruined.","text":"Historically a strongman is summoned as an excuse to end mob rule (real or imagined) resulting in an authoritarian government. Napoleon, Franco Mussolini are some examples. One also sees people jumping up with a banner in the front of the mob. Lenin and Kim-il Sung, for example. Of course the issues are real and need to be discussed and criticized. But fortunately stories like Evergreen State College are rare But the incidences of tenured professors who are no longer free to say what they actually think anymore are legion. And Twitter storms are not oppression, they are just people being wrong on the internet. Tell that to people whose lives have been ruined. [VIDEO] So while events like Evergreen certainly need to be criticized, the more serious threat is that this is being systematically used by cynical parties in a dangerous way to portray a nation plagued by oppressive mobs who need to be put in line. Downtown Portland was tied up by such mobs as well. There have been hundreds of incidences of violence (a number closer to 1000 than zero) including assaults with serious injury. Why did tens of thousands of people surround a bunch of mostly normal, ordinary people, including a number of brown people, claiming they were \"white supremacists\" in Boston? The Left in general is plagued by the zeitgeist of an authoritarian, angry mob, and wordplay/splitting hairs about what exactly should be classified as \"authoritarian\" is simply silly when you've been actually mobbed. Thugs goi"}],"r":[["n691pLhQBkw",1],["PsPX0nElJ0k",1],["rE3j_RHkqJc",1],["1AcEt073Uds",1],["RNFesa01llk",1],["Eg_ToU7m1MI",1],["IrbJYsep45E",1],["msVuCEs8Ydo",1],["aMcjxSThD54",1],["cVaTc15plVs",1],["2wZ1pCpJUIM",1],["JcJSW7Rprio",1],["bNfAAQUQ_54",1],["-6BsiVyC1kM",1],["Y65FRxE7uMc",1],["v3XcQtoja_Y",1],["XZ3w_jec1v8",1],["OjMPJVmXxV8",1],["vTMF6xEiAaY",1],["jVkLVRt6c1U",1]]},"-I_jE0l7sYQ":{"wy":"HN people recommend it as a reading list of historical CS ideas worth knowing, and as a favorite among Armstrong's talks, especially after his death.","q":{"x":"Ted has a knack for reframing things in ways that make the arbitrariness of certain historical decisions clear","i":16857613,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":33432262,"author":"jeffreygoesto","month":"2022-11","story":"Why I program in Erlang (2012)","stance":"rec","why":"Gem among Joe Armstrong's talks, humble and concise explanations.","quote":"one other gem being \"The forgotten ideas in computer science","text":"Definitely! I also like \"How we program multicores\" [0], it made \"iff you want the same guarantees Erlang gives you, you'll be in the same performance ballpark in any language, yes, C++ also\" click for me. Joe could explain the basic ideas and where they came from so concise and humble [1], you can probably just binge watch this whole list, one other gem being \"The forgotten ideas in computer science\" [2]... [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19707221,"author":"namelosw","month":"2019-04","story":"Joe Armstrong has died","stance":"rec","why":"Great talk by Joe Armstrong, remembered after his death.","quote":"I just watched his great talk last year not long ago","text":"This is so sad. RIP Joe. I'll miss him so much. Erlang is one of the most Perlis languages for me. I'm always a little bit cynical and think Erlang deserve more attention. Several months ago Joe post this tweet about a game company refused to tell other people they are using Erlang. [LINK] I just watched his great talk last year not long ago - The Forgotten Ideas in Computer Science: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18743000,"author":"smilesnd","month":"2018-12","story":"Ask HN: Best talks of 2018?","stance":"rec","why":"Keynote by Joe Armstrong.","quote":"I love any talk with Joe Armstrong or Alan Kay in it.","text":"I love any talk with Joe Armstrong or Alan Kay in it. Joe Armstrong - Keynote: The Forgotten Ideas in Computer Science - Code BEAM SF 2018 [VIDEO]"},{"id":16857613,"author":"enkiv2","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: What is your favourite tech talk?","stance":"rec","why":"Laundry list of historical CS ideas worth knowing before your next project.","quote":"it's a laundry list of things you should look up and be aware of before you start your next project","text":"Ted Nelson's Computers for Cynics probably doesn't contain technical information that's new to any of you, but Ted has a knack for reframing things in ways that make the arbitrariness of certain historical decisions clear: [LINK] On the subject of hypertext, The Web That Wasn't gives a nice history of the idea (for anybody who thinks it starts with TBL -- surprisingly many people!): [LINK] Another reframing-oriented talk is Clay Shirky's \"It's not information overload, it's filter failure\", which ultimately leads to Shirky suggesting the kinds of user-oriented filtering features that Mastodon has implemented: [LINK] At the intersection of neurology and information science, Peter Watts always has something interesting to say, and as a former marine biologist focusing on the nervous system of starfish, this is absolutely in his wheelhouse: [LINK] This one (\"moving away from defensive programming\") justified strong typing in a pretty clear way: [LINK] Dan Dennett is just as relevant as Doug Hofstadter when it comes to metacognition: [LINK] Forgotten Ideas in Computer Science starts slow, but if you don't have much of a historical background (like, if you're only vaguely aware of what happened in CS in the 70s), it's a laundry list of things you should look up and be aware of before you start your next project: [VIDEO] Everybody should understand procedural generation: [LINK] Likewise, since AI is hyped up right now, we should all remind ourselves that IA is a thing too: [LINK] M"},{"id":22523514,"author":"cryo","month":"2020-03","story":"The Call of the Ocean: Hypertext, Universal and Open (1988)","stance":"reference","why":"Armstrong urges reading everything from Ted Nelson.","quote":"[Read] everything from Ted Nelson","text":"\"[Read] everything from Ted Nelson\" – Joe Armstrong Keynote: The Forgotten Ideas in Computer Science - Code BEAM SF 2018: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19707936,"author":"ignoramous","month":"2019-04","story":"Joe Armstrong has died","stance":"reference","why":"Linked as source for his list of forgotten ideas.","quote":"","text":"Devastating. I'd like to point out a recent tweet @joeerl shared [0][1] that contained things he felt were good wisdom but lost over time: -- Ideas that we forgot: 1. Flow based programming. 2. Pipes. 3. Linda Tuple Spaces. 4. Hypertext (=/= HTML). Computer Science 101: 1. Observational equivalence. 2. Isolation. 3. Composition. 4. Causality. 5. Physics. Two papers to read: 1. The Emperor's Old Clothes - ACM. 2. A Plea for Lean Software - Nikalus Wirth. Two videos to watch: 1. The Computer Revolution Hasn't Happened Yet - Alan Kay. 2. Computers for Cynics - Ted Nelson. Four old tools to learn: Emacs, Bash, Make, Shell. Three books to read: 1. Algorithms + Data Structure = Programs. 2. The Mythical Man Month. 3. How to Win Friends and Influence People. Correct a typo: ?? Learn git -> locate program that creates page -> locate typo -> correct -> send push [sic] request. !! Select text -> type in correction -> people see change. Two projects: 1. Link to content hash not a name (request content by sha256, immune to pepole in the middle). 2. Elastic Links (links should not break if you move an endpoint). -- Easily one of the best thinkers of his generation [2]. RIP. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":16933370,"author":"alexashka","month":"2018-04","story":"Programming Bottom-Up (1993)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source of a quote about prolific programmers.","quote":"","text":"It seems like what Paul is describing, is the niche filled by libraries/frameworks. When you have a website to build, you reach for an existing web framework, because it has already done the bottom-up steps for you. The same could be said of choosing a language, OS, hardware, etc. Existing technologies allow for increased productivity, at the cost of it becoming increasingly difficult to understand the bigger picture. The 'bottom-up' part at this point, is choosing which Ruby/Java/Python/etc libraries to use. The productivity bar has been set high, leaving little time for bottom-up decision making or education. Hence MongoDB and the never-ending tales of mediocre solutions gaining adoption due to marketing hype. Curiously enough - academia isn't able to solve this problem well, because they're encouraged to produce 'novelty', rather than sit down and digest the mess we're in, to help clarify matters a little. They're in the same rat-race, of getting things done on a 6-12 month schedule. There's a quote that I really like - \"Prolific programmers contribute to certain disaster.\" [0] Ah well :) [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["LabqeJEOQyI",1],["KdnGPQaICjk",1],["SxdOUGdseq4",1],["NH1Tta7purM",1],["mVVNJKv9esE",1],["48sCx-wBs34",1],["Mlrrc_vy79E",1]]},"PQccNdwm8Tw":{"wy":"HN points to it as a sharper explanation of why outsourcing and specialization erode deep expertise, in threads on Intel, Boeing and software decline.","q":{"x":"this excellent video talks about the problems with outsourcing everything","i":40816191,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40816191,"author":"textlapse","month":"2024-06","story":"US sanctions Boeing for sharing information about 737 MAX 9 investigation","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows the dangers of outsourcing core competency, leaving a weak systems integrator.","quote":"this excellent video talks about the problems with outsourcing everything","text":"Probably a repost, but this excellent video talks about the problems with outsourcing everything (i.e. the L J Hart-Smith report) [VIDEO] - the report itself is fairly accessible and an eye-opener on what happens when you outsource all of your core competency. The only remaining thing is a weak 'systems integrator'. And the best part is the suppliers have more leverage and less compliance/liability than you do! Because nobody even knows their name."},{"id":32503800,"author":"scrollaway","month":"2022-08","story":"Jonathan Blow: Software Is in Decline","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains why specialization and offshoring kill innovation and expertise.","quote":"a MUCH BETTER version of a similar point was outlined by Bert Hubert","text":"I quite like Jonathan Blow. I think this talk is kind of poor though, I remember watching it a couple of years ago in its full context (linked in the youtube description: [LINK] ). IIRC it had a bit of a \"kids these days\" / \"they don't make 'em like they used to\" feel to it. It's not untrue , but it doesn't really explain anything. IMO, a MUCH BETTER version of a similar point was outlined by Bert Hubert in this article: [LINK] - How Tech Loses Out over at Companies, Countries and Continents (Video version: [VIDEO] ) In short, this goes beyond software; it's in general about why the incentives around specialization and outsourcing/offshoring cause innovation and expertise to die out. Bert Hubert takes the example of toasters, but it's of course very generalizable: > The problem is that this is not just a toaster problem. This is a continental problem. All over Europe, this is happening simultaneously, where we’re saying, look, we’re not that much into actually building things anymore. > So we’re just getting everyone else to build stuff for us. [...] We’re just thinking about things and then telling some other people how to do their stuff. > In the end, you cannot survive if all you create is intellectual property."},{"id":32614778,"author":"scrollaway","month":"2022-08","story":"Jonathan Blow on Societal Collapse (2019)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains why specialization and offshoring kill innovation and expertise.","quote":"a MUCH BETTER version of a similar point was outlined by Bert Hubert","text":"I quite like Jonathan Blow. I think the talk mentioned in this post is kind of poor though, I remember watching it a couple of years ago in its full context ( [LINK] ). IIRC it had a bit of a \"kids these days\" / \"they don't make 'em like they used to\" feel to it. It's not untrue, but it doesn't really explain anything. IMO, a MUCH BETTER version of a similar point was outlined by Bert Hubert in this article: [LINK] - How Tech Loses Out over at Companies, Countries and Continents (Video version: [VIDEO] ) In short, this goes beyond software; it's in general about why the incentives around specialization and outsourcing/offshoring cause innovation and expertise to die out. Bert Hubert takes the example of toasters, but it's of course very generalizable: > The problem is that this is not just a toaster problem. This is a continental problem. All over Europe, this is happening simultaneously, where we’re saying, look, we’re not that much into actually building things anymore. > So we’re just getting everyone else to build stuff for us. [...] We’re just thinking about things and then telling some other people how to do their stuff. > In the end, you cannot survive if all you create is intellectual property."},{"id":32219908,"author":"scrollaway","month":"2022-07","story":"Ask HN: Is the world suffering from shallow knowledge?","stance":"rec","why":"On how companies lose deep knowledge and stop innovating.","quote":"you will likely be interested in this talk by Bert Hubert","text":"OP, you will likely be interested in this talk by Bert Hubert: How Tech Loses Out over at Companies, Countries and Continents: On how companies lose deep knowledge and stop innovating [LINK] As a video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30718948,"author":"pabs3","month":"2022-03","story":"Intel financialized and lost leadership in semiconductor fabrication (2021)","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on how companies and regions lose technical leadership.","quote":"There is an article and talk about this general process of losing technical leadership here","text":"There is an article and talk about this general process of losing technical leadership here: [LINK] [VIDEO] and here is one about the EU & 5G outsourcing: [LINK]"}],"r":[["9OfBpQgLXUc",1],["ZZ3F3zWiEmc",1],["wOlcB-JxkFw",1],["ytpRnRke6I0",1],["i70wkxmumAw",1],["9zinZmE3Ogk",1],["7J52mDjZzto",1],["Pae2AMnmUVA",1],["rwhUpu9MfZ0",1],["7NfX0vlCa4k",1],["kYHTzqHIngk",1],["90NsjKvz9Ns",1],["NVGuFdX5guE",1],["nSKp2StlS6s",1],["7Pq-S557XQU",1],["FjA0EQPeUGM",1]]},"l6T9xIeZTds":{"wy":"Treated as the essential primary source for Tesla's battery and cost-reduction plans, and a feast for engineering nerds.","q":{"x":"for anyone even remotely interested in this space and this topic, I think it is an essential watch.","i":27856735,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27856735,"author":"clomond","month":"2021-07","story":"Tesla’s new patent involves using table salt to mine lithium","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Key battery day announcements on manufacturing and lithium","quote":"for anyone even remotely interested in this space and this topic, I think it is an essential watch.","text":"This is directly related to their set of battery day announcements [1]. Some of the key points from their battery day are: - focus on simplifying process from ore to EV in order to maintain cost declines per unit energy while enabling scale up - key to that is simplifying the lithium extraction process (noted patent for this thread), they spoke about the core of this patent before - getting enough factories constructed and dealing with expected limitations in raw material - reducing costs by simplifying manufacturing process / reducing parts - reduce parts by having bigger batteries (also more energy dense) and single casted sections of the vehicles - also interesting work on incorporating Si (which increases energy density) while solving the issue of cycle life when increasing Si ratio. Overall, for anyone even remotely interested in this space and this topic, I think it is an essential watch. [1] keynote starts at 33 mins [VIDEO]"},{"id":24564027,"author":"dayaz36","month":"2020-09","story":"Tesla says its battery innovations will deliver its goal of a $25,000 car","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Groundbreaking battery announcements: cost, range, manufacturing","quote":"Along with many other things announced in a 3+ hour technical video","text":"Copy pasting previous comment since the number of people misrepresenting the ground breaking tech that was announced is to high to spend energy on: 1)56% reduction in cost (which blows way past the $100/kwh holy grail for EV to sub $40). This by itself is insane and most people in the industry thought it wont happen for at least another decade. This puts the last nail in the coffin for the future of ICE cars. 2) 54% increase in range. The new Model S will have minimum 520mi of range and much more actually when they start production. Range anxiety gone out the window. 3) New manufacturing tech along with vertical integration will allow Tesla to scale 55x their current rate of production and become the world's largest auto manufacturer by many multiples. Tesla will not only produce their own battery cells but they're even getting into mining!! Along with many other things announced in a 3+ hour technical video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27694016,"author":"maximus-decimus","month":"2021-06","story":"Tesla just announced a brand new car - a $25K hatchback","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed presentation on new cell design","quote":"Seriously, they made a 2 hours presentation about it. Total nerd porn","text":"The five times more energy dense and 6 times the power refers to the design explained on Tesla's Battery day. Seriously, they made a 2 hours presentation about it. Total nerd porn : [VIDEO] This really applies to the cells, not the battery itself, I think they just misunderstood. This is in comparison to the current design. They did that mostly by... making the cells bigger! I think the battery itself is 35% smaller because this removes the need for using tabs and the cell volume/surface area is greater. Also note that \"six times the power\" doesn't mean six times the range. Power is energy per unit of time so it really only tells you how fast you can use the energy in your battery (and I'm assuming how fast you can charge.)"},{"id":27633514,"author":"beambot","month":"2021-06","story":"Panasonic sells its $3.6B Tesla stake","stance":"reference","why":"Tesla's vertical integration plans","quote":"At \"Battery Day\", Tesla alluded to their plans to make their organization more vertically integrated","text":"At \"Battery Day\", Tesla alluded to their plans to make their organization more vertically integrated: Lithium mining, battery production, and new vehicle designs that incorporate their own cells directly into the vehicle structure [1]. It has been reported in the press that Tesla will (likely) move away from Panasonic as a vendor [2]. Panasonic probably sees this as a good reason to divest and focus on future customers. From [2]: > Tesla will continue buying batteries from longtime Japanese supplier Panasonic until at least 2022 despite the U.S. electric vehicle maker's plans to produce its own cheaper alternative. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":25488646,"author":"cronix","month":"2020-12","story":"Why Tesla stock is vastly overpriced","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"What are your thoughts on the upcoming battery tech from Tesla they announced during their \"battery day\" in September? Your comments don't seem to reflect what they've been working on and what's coming. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25196610,"author":"rrmoelker","month":"2020-11","story":"First Responders Unprepared for Electric Vehicle Fires: Report","stance":"reference","why":"Tesla mention of iron batteries for cheaper vehicles","quote":"Tesla mentioned using iron in the lower end electric vehicles","text":"Tesla mentioned using iron in the lower end electric vehicles [1]. Though I don't know if that is the safe composition you are mentioning. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["wh4aWZRtTwU",1],["ghDvyItIHTY",1]]},"qTBAW-Eh0tM":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a typical example of Tom7's technically astounding and playful videos.","q":{"x":"Definitely give it a watch.","i":35826042,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35826042,"author":"_jholland","month":"2023-05","story":"I found the best anagram in English (2017)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Breaks letters into atoms for freer anagrams, with whimsical style.","quote":"Definitely give it a watch.","text":"This is great! It reminds me of a Tom7 video of a similar ilk: Anagrams, but where you can break apart letters: \"Anagraphs\" - [VIDEO] . Similar to this, he produces a standard form for each word, but breaks each letter into letter pieces or 'atoms' which gives much more freedom for moving between words. Definitely give it a watch. If you are not familiar with Tom7's videos, he has a hilarious whimsical style while also bringing to life completely out there ideas with some brilliant technical skill."},{"id":24647353,"author":"ryanfox","month":"2020-10","story":"When Fonts Fall","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Splits letters into pieces to build anagrams; Tom7 makes technically astounding videos.","quote":"All of his videos are technically astounding","text":"> I want fonts to be made out of just a handful (at a minimum) of primitives -- e.g. here's a vertical stroke, a horizontal stroke, diagonal strokes, what various serif endings look like, here are what the bowls of letters look like, etc. Tom7, who makes excellent videos about computers, has one about splitting letters up to create anagrams in this way.[0] All of his videos are technically astounding, like the one where he frankensteined a raspberry pi into a game cartridge to play SNES games on a NES, or the one where he designed and built a computer using IEEE 754 NaN and infinity instead of 0 and 1. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26668159,"author":"xmprt","month":"2021-04","story":"Uppestcase and Lowestcase Letters","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting video on anagraphs, best paired with the kerning follow-up.","quote":"His video on anagraphs is interesting","text":"His video on anagraphs is interesting ( [VIDEO] ) especially if watched alongside his followup video proving that generalized kerning is an undecidable problem ( [LINK] )"}],"r":[["L-x8DYTOv7w",1],["4hZxo96x48A",1],["1qSTcxt2t74",1],["lvTqbM5Dq4Q",1],["ett_8wLJ87U",1],["dKx1wnXClcI",1],["xx7Lfh5SKUQ",1]]},"4s9IjkMAmns":{"wy":"HN points to it whenever someone underestimates how hard email parsing, validation or building a mail client really is.","q":{"x":"If you want to read about the insanity behind some of the protocols watch that talk.","i":32578923,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31097079,"author":"lkbm","month":"2022-04","story":"Stop Validating Email Addresses with Regex (2012)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Beloved talk on email's quirks.","quote":"am sad it's not available in higher quality","text":"I've always loved the \"Email Hates The Living\" talk[0] and am sad it's not available in higher quality. This is a great alternative. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32578923,"author":"calvinmorrison","month":"2022-08","story":"Email doesn't suck – it's email clients that need improving","stance":"rec","why":"Tour of the insane quirks of email protocols.","quote":"If you want to read about the insanity behind some of the protocols watch that talk.","text":"Here's a talk from one of the heads of Fastmail: \"Email Hates the Living\" [VIDEO] Email itself is a very weird quirk filled system. If you want to read about the insanity behind some of the protocols watch that talk. That's why things like JMAP are being developed."},{"id":10742191,"author":"clscott","month":"2015-12","story":"Email made me miserable, so I decided to build my own email app from scratch","stance":"rec","why":"Talk showing why building email is harder than it looks.","quote":"I was reminded of this presentation by Rik Signes at YAPC::NA (2008)","text":"This is an interesting read, when I saw a few of the comments here ask \"how hard can it be?\" I was reminded of this presentation by Rik Signes at YAPC::NA (2008) Email Hates the Living [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["2KZb2_vcNTg",1],["an5abNFba4Q",1],["e08kOj2kISU",1]]},"bJ5ppf0po3k":{"wy":"HN shares it as a funny, technical story about phonetic profanity filtering, relevant to Soundex and content moderation threads.","q":{"x":"terrific little video about the battle between him and his viewers","i":17339000,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17339000,"author":"hbosch","month":"2018-06","story":"How Peppa Pig became a video nightmare for children","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Funny, technical tale of viewers abusing a talking banana bot","quote":"terrific little video about the battle between him and his viewers","text":"You might enjoy this video. It’s part humor and part technical discussion on how his talking “Banana” bot was brigades by users on Twitch in an effort to make him say racist words and phrases. After he got a strike on Twitch, he patched the software and then it got circumvented... terrific little video about the battle between him and his viewers and ultimately ends with his approach to sterilizing input so that the banana couldn’t speak anything harmful. I thought it was great. * [VIDEO]"},{"id":28785766,"author":"tonfreed","month":"2021-10","story":"The naughty username checking system used by Twitch","stance":"rec","why":"Builds a pronunciation-based filter for incoming messages","quote":"He made a filter that did analysis of the pronunciation of the messages that were being sent.","text":"You say that there's no \"non-hacky\" way, but... [VIDEO] He made a filter that did analysis of the pronunciation of the messages that were being sent. His breakdown of it starts at around 13:30"},{"id":17160410,"author":"rasz","month":"2018-05","story":"Soundex","stance":"rec","why":"Talking banana Twitch story involving a phonetic profanity filter","quote":"you will enjoy Talking Banana Twitch ban story by Useless Duck Company","text":"If you are interested in phonetic algorithms, sorting, recognizing and filtering, you will enjoy Talking Banana Twitch ban story by Useless Duck Company : [VIDEO]"},{"id":36329071,"author":"nomel","month":"2023-06","story":"NPM won't publish packages containing the word keygen","stance":"reference","why":"Clever attempt to detect bad words so a talking banana can stream.","quote":"a pretty impressive attempt to detect bad words","text":"Related, here's a pretty impressive attempt to detect bad words, to allow a talking banana on a Twitch live stream, without being banned: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28781274,"author":"sorenjan","month":"2021-10","story":"The naughty username checking system used by Twitch","stance":"reference","why":"Phonetic filter beat enumerating character permutations","quote":"That apparently worked much better than trying to think of every permutation of characters that sounds like bad words.","text":"Reminds me of the guy that streamed a talking banana on Twitch, where viewers could make it say things. People submitted variations of the n-word and got him banned, and after trying to filter out all character combinations he could think of he wrote a phonetic filter. That apparently worked much better than trying to think of every permutation of characters that sounds like bad words. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["tUAX0gnZ3Nw",1],["8b7erU_DOfE",1],["3MghYhf-GhU",1],["xn76r0JxqNM",1]]},"gRRvjZ_XNog":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a thoughtful critique of the great-leader-as-jerk myth in threads on difficult bosses.","q":{"x":"here's the best video I've found that breaks apart the idea of an \"asshole\"","i":18119225,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18119225,"author":"whitepoplar","month":"2018-10","story":"What I Learned from Having Steve Jobs Swear at Me","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best breakdown of the asshole idea via givers and takers","quote":"here's the best video I've found that breaks apart the idea of an \"asshole\"","text":"For anyone who's curious, here's the best video I've found that breaks apart the idea of an \"asshole\": [VIDEO] Essentially, people can be dicks and non-dicks, while simultaneously being givers or takers. Nobody likes a taker, even if they're nice, because that person doesn't help one grow. On the other hand, someone can be a real dick, but in a \"giving\" way that helps achieve a better outcome for both individuals. In the case of Jobs, one could argue that his personality was justified not only because great work came out of it, but also because the people doing that work became the best version of themselves in the process. If someone is capable of great work and they produce good work, there's a certain kindness to telling them that's not acceptable. But saying the exact same thing to someone who's less capable can be mean."},{"id":23234463,"author":"ak39","month":"2020-05","story":"The Great Asshole Fallacy","stance":"rec","why":"Video essay on the great-leader-as-jerk meme","quote":"Here's a good video essay on the same subject by a filmmaker, Max Joseph","text":"I think the writer is correct: there is a meme out there that in order to be great, a leader, a trailblazer etc you have to become inevitably the arsehole. So much so, that arseholism is not just tolerated but celebrated. I wonder why the author did not feel persuaded to mention our current poster child of arseholism, the quintessential kind: Donald Trump. Here's a good video essay on the same subject by a filmmaker, Max Joseph: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19793073,"author":"azhenley","month":"2019-04","story":"Career advice I wish I’d been given when I was young","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on whether great leaders must be jerks","quote":"This 30 minute Youtube documentary on the topic is very interesting","text":"Being pleasant is one of my personal rules/goals for life. But it is interesting to analyze great leaders who have been jerks. This 30 minute Youtube documentary on the topic is very interesting: \"DICKS: Do you need to be one to be a successful leader?\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":23822336,"author":"softfalcon","month":"2020-07","story":"Be More Unlikeable","stance":"reference","why":"Strong leaders make tough choices; people want a leader with vision","quote":"You don't have to be a dick, but being a strong and passionate leader will result in making tough choices","text":"This reminds me of a short documentary called \"DICKS: Do you need to be one to be a successful leader?\" [VIDEO] tldr; You don't have to be a dick, but being a strong and passionate leader will result in making tough choices that will inevitably not make everyone happy. tldr 2; People don't want a boss who is a friend, they want a leader with a vision."}],"r":[["PJy8vTu66tE",1],["A4UMyTnlaMY",1],["u5um8QWWRvo",1],["lWZ2Bz0tS-s",1]]},"lcjdwSY2AzM":{"wy":"Recommended as high-quality physics communication and as an introduction to Noether and energy non-conservation in cosmology.","q":{"x":"thought wow this is good. Much better than the physics lectures I had at Cambridge Uni.","i":44996270,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44996270,"author":"tim333","month":"2025-08","story":"Why the Internet Is Turning to Shit","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Example of high-quality physics content online.","quote":"thought wow this is good. Much better than the physics lectures I had at Cambridge Uni.","text":"Rather than turning to shit, I think the internet may be getting more diverse stuff in general. More good stuff and more shit. As an example of anti-shit I watched Veritasium \"The Biggest Misconception in Physics\" [VIDEO] yesterday and thought wow this is good. Much better than the physics lectures I had at Cambridge Uni."},{"id":46142286,"author":"qnleigh","month":"2025-12","story":"Lie groups are crucial to some of the most fundamental theories in physics","stance":"rec","why":"Energy non-conservation in an expanding universe.","quote":"See this excellent Veritasium video [0].","text":"The typical example people use to illustrate that energy isn't conserved is that photons get red-shifted and lose energy in an expanding universe. See this excellent Veritasium video [0]. But there's a much more striking example that highlights just how badly energy conservation can be violated. It's called cosmic inflation. General relativity predicts that if empty space in a 'false vacuum' state will expand exponentially. A false vacuum occurs if empty space has excess energy, which can happen in quantum field theory. But if empty space has excess energy, and more space is being created by expansion, then new energy is being created out of nothing at an exponential rate! Inflation is currently the best model for what happened before the Big Bang. Space expanded until the false vacuum state decayed, releasing all this free energy to create the big bang. Alan Guth's book, The Inflationary Universe, is a great book on the topic that is very readable. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":43788757,"author":"cultofmetatron","month":"2025-04","story":"You Can Be a Great Designer and Be Completely Unknown","stance":"rec","why":"Introduces Noether's work and why she deserves fame.","quote":"this video was the first time I had even heard of her.","text":"reminds me of this video I found the other day [VIDEO] if I'm understanding correctly the implications of Emily Noether's work, its an absolute travesty that she isn't famous in the same breath as Einstein and Feynman. Yet this video was the first time I had even heard of her."},{"id":43729516,"author":"ccorcos","month":"2025-04","story":"A Math Lesson From Hitler’s Germany (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Covers Emmy Noether's contributions.","quote":"The most recent Veritasium video touched on this","text":"The most recent Veritasium video touched on this — Emmy Noether worked under Hilbert and made significant contributions to general relativity, worked at University of Göttingen (Jewish, and first woman professor) until 1933. [VIDEO]"},{"id":44567563,"author":"dataflow","month":"2025-07","story":"LIGO detects most massive black hole merger to date","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Veritasium recently claimed otherwise","text":"> Energy and momentum are always conserved in EVERY physical process. Veritasium recently claimed otherwise [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["IkdziSLYzHw",1],["7k1ehaE0bdU",1],["YMqgiXLjvRs",1],["jbkSRLYSojo",1],["OxhTQdcieQE",1],["ob_GX50Za6c",1],["baFaEvBywGc",1],["obCjODeoLVw",1],["iSVHp6CAyQ8",1],["J-M98KLgaUU",1],["9NOncx2jU0Q",1],["Ak4on5uTaTg",1],["d8RRE2rDw4k",1],["IgF3OX8nT0w",1],["GVsUOuSjvcg",1],["XRr1kaXKBsU",1],["T71ibcZAX3I",1]]},"2AnbYNudAyM":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as an impressive showpiece of the demoscene and a nostalgic career retrospective of a legendary group.","q":{"x":"This Razor1911 career retrospective demo from Revision was pretty impressive","i":48506793,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":49099212,"author":"MatMercer","month":"2026-07","story":"User Interfaces of the Demo Scene","stance":"rec","why":"Demo that the article helps explain.","quote":"What a great thing to add to this","text":"What a great thing to add to this: [VIDEO] I'm not from this era and this article explains a lot of things from the video!"},{"id":48506793,"author":"RIMR","month":"2026-06","story":"Keygen.music","stance":"rec","why":"Impressive Razor1911 career retrospective demo.","quote":"This Razor1911 career retrospective demo from Revision was pretty impressive","text":"It's so cool to see that some of these groups are still making incredible pieces of digital art as recently as this year! This Razor1911 career retrospective demo from Revision was pretty impressive: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47687965,"author":"uzyn","month":"2026-04","story":"Razor1911. A demo 40 years in the making","stance":"rec","why":"Full-length version of the Razor1911 demo including credits.","quote":"This is so sick!","text":"This is so sick! Revision faded out the credits part, which is still really cool on its own. The full version (10m 16s) can be viewed at [VIDEO]"}]},"AIcfMMVcYZg":{"wy":"Cited in tuition-cost threads for the argument that easy government-backed loans enable high prices.","q":{"x":"Here's a solid video on the topic where Peter Schiff explains why college tuition is so high","i":1906639,"y":"2010"},"m":[{"id":17964090,"author":"mixmastamyk","month":"2018-09","story":"Why Is College in America So Expensive?","stance":"rec","why":"Schiff on how subsidies raise costs","quote":"There's an interesting video by Peter Schiff on the subject","text":"When you subsidize industries they no longer attempt to contain costs. Unintuitively, instead of getting cheaper they get more expensive. There's an interesting video by Peter Schiff on the subject: [VIDEO]"},{"id":2239189,"author":"anonymoushn","month":"2011-02","story":"Why Does College Cost So Much?","stance":"rec","why":"Hypothesis that government-backed loans enable high tuition","quote":"I suspect that Peter Schiff is closer to the mark.","text":"I suspect that Peter Schiff is closer to the mark. [VIDEO] His hypothesis is that increasing the capacity of students to pay (to almost arbitrarily high levels) by providing government guarantees of student loans allows allows schools to charge ridiculously high tuition. To some extent, this is correct, and there is no reason for the people involved not to want to increase tuitions. For the school, higher tuition will mean the school can improve itself and become more attractive to students. For the lenders, higher tuition means more loans returning 5% or more for which the government assumes all the risk. It isn't entirely accurate to say \"the government assumes all the risk\" either. Student debts are the most toxic debts around, and cannot be thrown off in bankruptcy. If you refuse to pay, the government is authorized to garnish your wages or raid your savings. This isn't to say that Schiff's hypothesis explains the entirety of the increase in the cost of tuition, but I think that it plays a much larger role than what is discussed in the article."},{"id":1906639,"author":"josh33","month":"2010-11","story":"'Malemployment' on the Rise","stance":"rec","why":"Schiff explains why college tuition is so high","quote":"Here's a solid video on the topic where Peter Schiff explains why college tuition is so high","text":"Agreed. Here's a solid video on the topic where Peter Schiff explains why college tuition is so high: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["wqn3gR1WTcA",1]]},"VVp8UGjECt4":{"wy":"HN points to it as a short, funny and genuinely informative take on economic principles such as trade and opportunity cost.","q":{"x":"a great 5min video","i":37994372,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37994372,"author":"Qwertious","month":"2023-10","story":"Two Kinds of Techno-Optimism","stance":"rec","why":"Great five-minute translation of economic principles, e.g. trade benefits everyone.","quote":"a great 5min video","text":"\"AI can improve society\" reminds me of \"Principles of Economics, Translated\" (a great 5min video, [VIDEO] ) - \"\\#5: Trade can make everyone better off.\" The same translation can be applied, methinks."},{"id":3217535,"author":"scott_s","month":"2011-11","story":"Open letter to the Mac/iOS dev community regarding conduct","stance":"rec","why":"Example of topical, appropriate, funny humor in professional settings.","quote":"This is what topical, appropriate and actually funny humor in a professional setting looks like","text":"I actually don't think they're funny. That's not code for \"I think they are inappropriate\" (which at a developer's conference, I think they are inappropriate), but rather I don't find them humorous. It's the kind of easy humor people try for when they're not actually funny. I find listening to this sort of thing painful because I feel sorry for the speaker - trying to be funny, failing, and having to deal with it. With that said, even if the speaker did as you said and was completely deadpan, I would still think it's inappropriate. It's just not the appropriate place to even attempt to make those references, no matter how you do it. Among friends, I make and laugh at raunchy jokes. But professional environments should be more inclusive than that. This is what topical, appropriate and actually funny humor in a professional setting looks like: [VIDEO]"},{"id":1851726,"author":"jkent","month":"2010-10","story":"Things Economists Agree On","stance":"rec","why":"Light-hearted economics comedy for interested people.","quote":"economists and interested people might be interested in the light-hearted","text":"Whilst it doesn't really support or refute any points, economists and interested people might be interested in the light-hearted [VIDEO]"},{"id":13385762,"author":"qwrusz","month":"2017-01","story":"The Curious Case of Missing Global Productivity Growth","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Right. In terms current thinking I can't speak for an entire field, and I should say from what I have seen economics maybe still follows the lead of hard sciences where coming up with a theoretical model and a beautiful equation on paper which is then strongly supported by empirical research is still sort of a holy grail. But today, the availability of empirical real world data or a way to go get it if not yet collected is night and day different compared to 60 years ago when many of the old economic theories were being thought up and became popular. Go read the old papers there is no data just theory. Today, economic theories and formulas can often very quickly be tested with empirical research and data. And in my opinion current economics has turned toward empirical work having weight over theoretical work How do you disaggregate? You do the best you can. And yes we do try to look at each part individually (not in a vacuum though). Because it turns out aggregating is worse. When you look at TFP the way you describe, you are not just aggregating technologies you are aggregating everything...aggregating all technologies and also whatever else is happening that K and L didn't capture. Who needs that, it doesn't tell you anything practical but that \"other stuff is happening\". Technology might turn out to be only <5% of TFP. There's enough evidence that TFP has too many \"factors\" in it to be lumped as just technology. Physicists didn't give up when they realized 95% of the unive"},{"id":10237299,"author":"asgard1024","month":"2015-09","story":"Efficiency up, turnover down: Sweden experiments with six-hour working day","stance":"reference","why":"Shows problems with opportunity cost depending on how free you are.","quote":"one of my favorite videos","text":"I am not sure I entirely accept the concept of \"opportunity cost\". It certainly has problems, it depends on how free you really are, as has been nicely shown in one of my favorite videos: [VIDEO] In any case, I think this was mostly resolved in modern society, with concept of overtime pay. (I assume, when talking about 6-hour work day, we are implicitly talking about employment.) No one is prevented working more, given that someone is willing to pay more (to the same person). And reason for this arrangement is precisely to give people the choice to cash on the free time or the extra value."},{"id":418807,"author":"jerf","month":"2009-01","story":"The question is wrong","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"nobody says \"I have a girl. I have another girl. I have another girl.","text":"Really, a (not the , but a ) problem is that almost everybody is right. We are certainly fooled by probability. But also, if someone volunteers that one out of their two children is a girl, then it's effectively-100% that the other is a boy, in real life . Slightly amending a quote in a similar context from \"Principles of Economics, Explained\" ( [VIDEO] ), nobody says \"I have a girl. I have another girl. I have another girl.\" One way of interpreting this perennial debate is as proof of how just how fuzzy English can be. People aren't just arguing about the answer, they argue about the question too."}],"r":[["r9rGX91rq5I",1],["Mc_4Z1oiXhY",1],["Ik1AK56FtVc",1],["uTRUQ-RKfUs",1],["UWEjxkkB8Xs",1]]},"_x0eyNkNpL0":{"wy":"Recommended as a relatively clear introduction to Zizek's thinking, especially his account of ideology and unknown knowns.","q":{"x":"when it comes to discussing ideology, he's doing the best outreach bar none.","i":10533885,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":30364989,"author":"ksarw","month":"2022-02","story":"Love is biological bribery?","stance":"rec","why":"Relatively clear overview of Zizek's thoughts across many stances.","quote":"it is relatively clear and covers a large number of stances","text":"I used to watch some of Zizek's videos, though I hadn't seen the one that was linked until now. I'm no student of philosophy though, just a casual viewer so I don't think I can help much in answering your questions. I will say that he is definitely using uncommon definitions of the words \"love\" and \"evil\" as far as I can tell. As to who is this guy, I personally would recommend this video if you are trying to get a better sense of his thoughts as it is relatively clear and covers a large number of stances (the analogy at 7:15 is quite amusing): [VIDEO] On a side note, I am pleased though that you find him entertaining to watch regardless. I find it disappointing when people take speakers like Zizek too seriously; I think even if 80% of what is said is not insightful/intelligible, the occasional grain of truth can be very much worth it."},{"id":10533885,"author":"RodericDay","month":"2015-11","story":"We can't see our culture very well, because we see with it","stance":"rec","why":"Best outreach on discussing ideology.","quote":"when it comes to discussing ideology, he's doing the best outreach bar none.","text":"This concept has been developed very well, imo, by people like Zizek. I honestly don't care much for his political follies or his real academic research- when it comes to discussing ideology, he's doing the best outreach bar none. Here's him giving a talk at Google: [VIDEO] A friend of mine also parsed it well like this: > \"I think there is something to be said to the effect that laziness is actually where we can find a lot of insight - as it's when we're lazy, when we cannot be bothered, or at least where we think it is not essential to focus our attention for the sake of the work at least, that we tend to defer to the implicit shared attitudes or beliefs about the world to do the lifting for us.\""},{"id":4072424,"author":"taliesinb","month":"2012-06","story":"Gmail security warnings for suspected state-sponsored attacks","stance":"rec","why":"Zizek on unknown knowns and unperceived Western ideology.","quote":"Here is exactly when he starts talking about it in his Authors@Google talk","text":"This. The philosopher Zizek has a great bit about the \"unknown knowns\" (the quadrant Rumsfeld wasn't smart enough to articulate). Meaning, of course, the unperceived fabric of Western ideology -- that works far better than explicit propaganda ever could. Here is exactly when he starts talking about it in his Authors@Google talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4087268,"author":"sneak","month":"2012-06","story":"High school teacher tells graduating students: you’re not special ","stance":"rec","why":"Explains unknown knowns of ideology.","quote":"Watch this video of Žižek talking about the \"unknown knowns\" of ideology","text":"Watch this video of Žižek talking about the \"unknown knowns\" of ideology to understand why this is such a big deal: [VIDEO]"},{"id":1049006,"author":"borism","month":"2010-01","story":"A new approach to China","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"in order to say 'don't be evil' you must already dwell in that space of evil","text":"\"in order to say 'don't be evil' you must already dwell in that space of evil\" -Slavoj Zizek at Google: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["2vuMzGhc1cg",1],["DC8tO16xdrY",1],["DleXA5ADG78",1],["MEUWxNifJJ8",1],["PIPjmmmh_os",1],["m05abdGSOxY",1]]},"lr4MmmWQtZM":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as an accurate account of everyday Japanese eating and of why the US food environment is so much worse.","q":{"x":"This youtube video goes into more detail about the US problem compared to japan","i":23503416,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25818306,"author":"presentation","month":"2021-01","story":"Japanese people may have gained longevity by balancing their diets","stance":"rec","why":"Accurate account of Japanese eating habits.","quote":"I'd say it's pretty accurate [2]","text":"Re diet: putting aside that alcohol-per-capita consumption here in Japan is less than the OECD average [1], and also how the article is actually about how the introduction of meat into the Japanese diet has caused a decrease in strokes (i.e. this is probably off-topic); it is true that if you are eating out cheaply, you'll likely find lots of rice and meat stuff like gyuudon (beef on rice), katsudon (breaded meat on rice) and what not. But a normal Japanese meal generally has a pretty good mix of stuff - some fermented side dishes (tsukemono), some veggies, miso soup, some rice or noodles, some fish, maybe some meat. Perhaps my wife is an outlier, but every meal she's cooked for our household has had at least 3 dishes of different categories as a part of it, and my impression from seeing other households and just looking at a typical bento box, is that that's a pretty normal expectation for any healthy meal. Someone else pointed me to this Youtube video on the topic on a different thread, and I'd say it's pretty accurate [2]. When I go to drinking bars, izakayas, with Japanese people they usually tend to do the same and order a variety of small dishes too - it's not common to just get fried chicken (karaage), but rather people almost always also throw in salty drinking foods (otsumami), which usually include seafood, pickles and vegetables (shiokara, takowasa, various tsukemono and so on). Even then, if someone orders sweets or something, they tend to be a lot less unhealthy "},{"id":23503416,"author":"novok","month":"2020-06","story":"Biohacking Lite","stance":"rec","why":"Details the US food environment problem versus Japan.","quote":"This youtube video goes into more detail about the US problem compared to japan","text":"Yup I agree. I call it the food gravity problem of america. Trying to eat healthy here is like trying to beat gravity: This youtube video goes into more detail about the US problem compared to japan: \"Why is it so Easy to be Thin in Japan?\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":16226313,"author":"mtgx","month":"2018-01","story":"Curry spice turmeric boosts memory by nearly 30%, eases depression, study finds","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why Japanese people are thin compared to the US.","quote":"I recently saw an interesting video about why Japanese people are so thin, compared to say the US","text":"Speaking of Japan, I recently saw an interesting video about why Japanese people are so thin, compared to say the US: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38147912,"author":"Mawr","month":"2023-11","story":"U.S. Sugar consumption trend from 1970-2021","stance":"reference","why":"Author describes the average person's food experience in Japan.","quote":"its author talks about the experience of the average person","text":"I'd be surprised if the ratios matched up exactly. That'd mean the only factor dictating the level of obesity is food culture. Not likely. I'm sure Japan has a variety of unhealthy foods, I don't see how that matters. That unhealthy food exists in Japan doesn't tell us anything about how much of it people actually eat. That's why I linked the video, its author talks about the experience of the average person, here's an exact link: [VIDEO] . I cannot stress this enough, the only thing that matters is what is readily available to the average person. That's the determining factor of most people's diets, not what's theoretically possible to purchase."},{"id":34182653,"author":"Mawr","month":"2022-12","story":"A day in the life of almost every vending machine","stance":"reference","why":"Shows healthy drink availability in Japan versus west.","quote":"The healthy/unhealtly food availability ratio in the western world is like 10%/90% whereas in Japan it seems closer to 70%/30%.","text":"> Are you saying these drinks machines only dispense water? Kind of? [VIDEO] The healthy/unhealtly food availability ratio in the western world is like 10%/90% whereas in Japan it seems closer to 70%/30%. Personally, I have never seen a vending machine in the western world that had a healthy drink in it that wasn't plain water."}],"r":[["rStL7niR7gs",2],["daS5RHVAl2U",1],["MEqxaH47DTs",1],["WTYet-qf1jo",1],["tk862BbjWx4",1],["LMbI6sk-62E",1],["qp0HIF3SfI4",1],["nJPERZDfyWc",1],["mw2z9lV3W1g",1],["v3pK0dccQ38",1],["dBnniua6-oM",1],["1gEz__sMVaY",1],["amJ-ev8Ial8",1]]},"oSmqbnhHp1c":{"wy":"HN cites it whenever Lisp in games, Naughty Dog or Racket in production comes up, as the main first-hand account.","q":{"x":"There's a talk by one of the engine devs at RacketCon from a few years ago on youtube about how they use these DSLs","i":18904632,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":29988565,"author":"neilv","month":"2022-01","story":"OpenGOAL: Port of Jak and Daxter, written in GOAL, a custom Lisp by Naughty Dog","stance":"rec","why":"Liebgold explains the Racket-based DC language for The Last of Us","quote":"Related talk by Dan Liebgold from Naughty Dog, at RacketCon 2013","text":"Related talk by Dan Liebgold from Naughty Dog, at RacketCon 2013: [VIDEO] Starting at around 10m into the talk, Liebgold says their DC language for TLOU (to get what they most needed from a DSL even if using C++ instead of GOAL) was implemented in Racket. For anyone not familiar with TLOU, it was a critically-acclaimed game, and my personal all-time favorite game for storytelling that somehow grabbed me. (I dislike all zombie franchises, but at a key point towards the end of the nonlinear narrative and playing in the game, I was surprised how much I really wanted to protect the kid. Which I'll partly attribute to a Lispy DSL that helped them execute immersive storytelling in a zombie stealth shooter.) [LINK] This is one of the Racket success stories. They seem to happen because someone already knows they want a Lisp, they end up picking Racket without needing anyone else's permission, and the people using it are capable of designing and building whatever missing pieces they need."},{"id":18904632,"author":"steinuil","month":"2019-01","story":"Halo was written with a Lisp dialect called blamscript (2014)","stance":"rec","why":"How Naughty Dog's Racket DSLs work and originated","quote":"There's a talk by one of the engine devs at RacketCon from a few years ago on youtube about how they use these DSLs","text":"Other than the games they wrote entirely in GOAL, all of their PS3+ games use a series of lispy DSLs implemented in Racket that get compiled to C and C++ for defining data to be loaded in the game and dependencies between these data, light scripting, dialogues, stuff like that. There's a talk by one of the engine devs at RacketCon from a few years ago on youtube about how they use these DSLs and how they came to be: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7654144,"author":"kevingadd","month":"2014-04","story":"Racket on the Playstation 3? It's Not What you Think [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Naughty Dog replacing GOAL with Racket in their toolchain","quote":"Interesting to hear how they replaced GOAL in their toolchain.","text":"The speaker actually begins talking about racket and how they use it at about 6 minutes into the video. Here's a link: [VIDEO] Interesting to hear how they replaced GOAL in their toolchain. The people I've met who used GOAL in the past talked at length about how much they miss it."},{"id":25508990,"author":"franknine","month":"2020-12","story":"GameLisp: Scripting language for Rust game development","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"It seems Naughty Dog later decided not to maintain their own Lisp and moved to Racket","text":"Found an old postmortem on Jak and Daxter from 2002 talking about pros and cons of using GOAL: [LINK] And Andy Gavin talked briefly about GOOL on Ars Technica Crash Bandicoot interview: [LINK] It seems Naughty Dog later decided not to maintain their own Lisp and moved to Racket: [VIDEO] I am really curious about how they use Lisp nowadays."},{"id":20945388,"author":"neilv","month":"2019-09","story":"Technical Breakdown of a new NES game written in Lisp","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Neat, I didn't know Naughty Dog also used Allegro CL, in addition to Racket: * \"RacketCon 2013: Dan Liebgold - Racket on the Playstation 3? It's Not What you Think!\" [VIDEO] * \"Functional mzScheme DSLs in Game Development\" (MzScheme was the name of the main PLT Scheme interpreter that was renamed Racket) [LINK]"},{"id":17141879,"author":"lunchladydoris","month":"2018-05","story":"Lisp in Jak and Daxter","stance":"reference","why":"Using Racket in PS3 development","quote":"Dan Liebgold from Naughty Dog did a talk on using Racket during development for the PlayStation 3","text":"Dan Liebgold from Naughty Dog did a talk on using Racket during development for the PlayStation 3 at Racketcon a few years ago [0]. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16618781,"author":"tominated","month":"2018-03","story":"Luxe: free, cross platform, open source, rapid development game engine","stance":"reference","why":"Naughty Dog's use of Racket in games","quote":"Racket is definitely used by Naughty Dog in their games.","text":"A little off topic, but Racket is definitely used by Naughty Dog in their games. Here's a talk they did a while back [VIDEO]"},{"id":11180082,"author":"samth","month":"2016-02","story":"The Lisp Curse (2011)","stance":"reference","why":"Naughty Dog's Racket-based DC DSL for game data","quote":"Dan Leibgold gave a talk about their system at RacketCon a few years ago","text":"That's not quite right. These days Naughty Dog uses a DSL called DC built in Racket to write all the \"data\" in the game (everything from cut scenes to character attributes). Running DC produces data files shipped on the DVD of the game, and used by the big C++ engine that's running on the PlayStation. Dan Leibgold gave a talk about their system at RacketCon a few years ago: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9813280,"author":"stchang","month":"2015-07","story":"John Carmack working on Scheme as a VR scripting language","stance":"reference","why":"Use of Racket in developing The Last of Us","quote":"Here's a talk discussing the use of Racket in developing The Last of Us","text":"They switched to Racket when moving to the PS3. Here's a talk discussing the use of Racket in developing The Last of Us: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9224324,"author":"59nadir","month":"2015-03","story":"John Carmack: “I just dumped the C++ server I wrote for a new one in Racket”","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"[0] - Naughty Dog's scripting language","text":"While I understand your frustration with 'too many languages', Racket is older than quite a few (started in '95). I also think the danger of having this attitude is that if we all thought like this nothing would become better. If no one wanted to get out of C++ we wouldn't have had many languages. The premise behind Racket, in broad strokes, is to provide a platform and programming language for making new programming languages. The most important identifier/keyword in Racket is '#lang' (specified at the top of a file to tell Racket which language the file is in). It's a whole ecosystem in which a multitude of languages exist and the only thing they really need to have in common is that on some level they speak Racket. Personally, I think the above paragraph explains how exciting Racket as a language and platform is, but it doesn't sound that interesting on the surface. It does allow people to create things like this[0] and this[1], though, which displays real practical use; shaping the way you solve the problem to fit the actual problem. [0] [VIDEO] - Naughty Dog's scripting language [1] [LINK] - A web book publishing language"},{"id":9269925,"author":"fa","month":"2015-03","story":"Why Racket? Why Lisp? (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'm working through the admittedly beautiful and very thorough documentation [1]. It's a very simple idea, really: you write your document and sprinkle racket s-expressions wherever you want. You can put your definitions at the top of your document, or in another file. You describe your templates in Racket also, because like Butterick says in his RacketCon video [2], \"S-expressions and XML are the same thing\". You can use tags that you haven't defined, they just get placed in the resulting HTML. You still have to roll your own CSS and any JavaScript. As far as I can tell it doesn't help you out with things like keeping track of footnotes numbering or citations and such: you roll your own for things like this, Pollen is no LaTeX. This is partly why I say it's a really simple system. It makes a certain set of web authoring things simple, but it doesn't try to be a one-stop shop, which is excellent because this makes it a supremely flexible tool. It also has some backend fancy sauce where you can save your file and refresh the page [3]. Also I admit I'm not really a big fan of DrRacket: I've just been using it for the tutorials because I have no idea what to expect from Racket, but I'm slowly moving to Emacs, and Racket seems to work fine there. Definitely worth checking out Pollen if only to get a light introduction to the thoroughness of Racket documentation (they definitely do things differently in Racket-land!), but stay for the tools you need to roll your own ultimate stati"},{"id":9052341,"author":"soegaard","month":"2015-02","story":"How to Program Racket","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Dan Liebgold (Naughty Dog) at RacketCon 2013.","text":"Dan Liebgold (Naughty Dog) at RacketCon 2013. \"Racket on the Playstation 3? It's Not What you Think!\" [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["vGAqYNFQdZ4",1]]},"vWkgEddb4-A":{"wy":"HN commenters point newcomers to it as a solid starting point for getting to know Julia.","q":{"x":"I've found these talks useful as a starting point","i":10569645,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":10569645,"author":"jarmitage","month":"2015-11","story":"Julia Computing Granted $600k by Moore Foundation","stance":"rec","why":"Starting point for getting to know Julia.","quote":"I've found these talks useful as a starting point","text":"For anyone interested in trying to get to know Julia (like me), I've found these talks useful as a starting point: Introduction to Julia - Part 1 | SciPy 2014 | David Sanders - [VIDEO] Get To Know JuliaLang - Guest Talk by Co-Inventor Viral Shah - [LINK] If anyone can recommend other resources that would be appreciated."},{"id":8643239,"author":"srparish","month":"2014-11","story":"Julia Package Ecosystem Pulse","stance":"rec","why":"Two-part introduction to Julia.","quote":"scipy had a two-part introduction to Julia talk that's worth watching","text":"Not a book, but scipy had a two-part introduction to Julia talk that's worth watching: part 1: [VIDEO] part 2: [LINK]"},{"id":8073778,"author":"ViralBShah","month":"2014-07","story":"The Julia Express [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Video introduction to Julia from SciPy 2014.","quote":"this one by David Sanders at SciPy 2014","text":"For those looking for a video tutorial, this one by David Sanders at SciPy 2014 [VIDEO] is a great start. More stuff at [LINK]"}],"r":[["mSgXWpvQEHE",1],["19zm1Fn0S9M",1],["jEbRVNxL44c",1],["Wpv-Qb-dB6g",1]]},"03ccxwNssmo":{"wy":"HN commenters bring it up in book-scanning threads as an impressive non-destructive method that is not commercially available.","q":{"x":"very very impressive but to my knowledge not commercially available","i":38908603,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":38908603,"author":"grumpyfox96","month":"2024-01","story":"DIY Book Scanner","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Astonishing 250 pages/min book scanner.","quote":"very very impressive but to my knowledge not commercially available","text":"There is this classic – very very impressive but to my knowledge not commercially available and probably never commercialized. 250 pages/min is astounding. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18073825,"author":"ebcode","month":"2018-09","story":"Simple Thought Experiment Shows Why We Need Quantum Gravity","stance":"rec","why":"High-speed automated book digitizing from University of Tokyo.","quote":"The University of Tokyo seems to have a pretty good one","text":"Do you happen to know if any of this mysterious researcher's papers might be on the arxiv? And have you looked into automated digitizing machines for your collection? The University of Tokyo seems to have a pretty good one: [VIDEO] :)"},{"id":38871354,"author":"ortusdux","month":"2024-01","story":"There's Waldo is a robot that finds Waldo (2018) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I do enjoy the various methods of automatic page turning used by book scanning machines. None of them are simpler than a toy robot arm and a cheap USB webcam! - Flipping - [VIDEO] - Arm with suction - [LINK] - Linear vacuum (google) - [LINK] - Vacuum prism scanner (40+ ppm!) - [LINK]"},{"id":29971215,"author":"tablespoon","month":"2022-01","story":"€2.6M spent for a book at auction, believed they would own the IP","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the concept of scanning bound books non-destructively.","quote":"it shows the concept","text":"> Oddly enough - the process in a library for digitising a copy of a book can be fairly destructive, as far as I understood from those doing it (at CUDL). The spine is chopped off, to make it easier to put it into a machine for scanning all the pages. I don't think it necessarily has to be that way. I recall seeing a photo or video of a book-scanner that would project laser grid-lines onto the book, to non-destructively correct for the deformations caused by the binding in software. Edit: not sure if this is the one, but it shows the concept: [VIDEO]"}]},"Z2o_Sp2-aBo":{"wy":"HN people recommend it as a fantastic maker project among Apollo anniversary builds.","q":{"x":"This video of building a custom Apollo electroluminescent glass panel DSKY display is also fantastic!","i":36655732,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36655732,"author":"MochaDen","month":"2023-07","story":"VCF Southwest – Restoring the Apollo Guidance Computer with CuriousMarc [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Building a custom Apollo DSKY electroluminescent display.","quote":"This video of building a custom Apollo electroluminescent glass panel DSKY display is also fantastic!","text":"So cool I'm going to have to go play with the PDP-11/15 in my basement to get my old hardware jones on. This video of building a custom Apollo electroluminescent glass panel DSKY display is also fantastic! [VIDEO]"},{"id":20449514,"author":"LeonM","month":"2019-07","story":"Apollo 11 50th Anniversary","stance":"rec","why":"Replica DSKY electroluminescent display build.","quote":"there were some pretty cool projects going on on Youtube in preparation for the 50th anniversary","text":"Not so much an article about the 50th anniversary, but just the main article about Apollo 11. If you are into space stuff, and haven't seen it yet, there were some pretty cool projects going on on Youtube in preparation for the 50th anniversary: - CuriousMarc's restoration series of an actual ACG [0] - Applied Science made a replica DSKY electroluminescent glass panel display [1] - Project Egress: A maker collaboration on building a replica of the Apollo 11 door (Adam Savage, Jimmy Diresta, This old tony, Blondihacks, etc) [2] [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":26374071,"author":"tal8d","month":"2021-03","story":"Don’t use 7-segment displays (2011) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It has been done a few times, but I doubt there would be any kind of market for it. [LINK] Electroluminescent glass panels on the other hand... [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["2KSahAoOLdU",1]]},"_zfN9wnPvU0":{"wy":"HN uses it as a reference for AI slop, LLMs citing LLM output, and the growing difficulty of fact checking.","q":{"x":"Kurzgesagt made a great video on how this stuff gets generated","i":48931051,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":48931051,"author":"ColdStream","month":"2026-07","story":"How AI Slop Is Killing World War II History (YouTube Video)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains the feedback loop of AI-generated citations","quote":"Kurzgesagt made a great video on how this stuff gets generated","text":"I'm glad they go in deep on the data feed back on this. Kurzgesagt made a great video on how this stuff gets generated, an article uses this, data sets get trained on this, and then future outputs will use the article as citation. [VIDEO] This is the paradox to be addressed. In trying to make LLM's more accurate, we try to pin it to citations, but then that means having to trust that the citations are accurate. But having the citations generated by an LLM means it basically becomes impossible to confirm the accuracy. As it has been said anything after 2022 cannot be reliably trusted. You can do you due diligence but that is a lot of work if you can do it. Really highlights the idea that a lie will travel around the world before the truth puts its shoes on."},{"id":45514384,"author":"zwnow","month":"2025-10","story":"Vibe engineering","stance":"rec","why":"AI makes verifying knowledge harder","quote":"Kurzgesagt recently uploaded a video about this, its a lot harder to verify statements due to AI.","text":"Absolutely. If the knowledge is verifiable. Kurzgesagt recently uploaded a video about this, its a lot harder to verify statements due to AI. Not easier. Here is the video in case you are interested: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45617823,"author":"ikiris","month":"2025-10","story":"You did no fact checking, and I must scream","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how hard fact checking is becoming","quote":"I suggest watching the video kurzgesagt just did on the topic.","text":"If you think this is trivial, I suggest watching the video kurzgesagt just did on the topic. It’s much harder than you appreciate and getting massively worse as the days go by due to ai garbage. [VIDEO]"},{"id":46963496,"author":"Joel_Mckay","month":"2026-02","story":"I started programming when I was 7. I'm 50 now and the thing I loved has changed","stance":"reference","why":"Linked among skeptical references on LLMs","quote":"","text":"You seem very passionate about your opinions, but are you happy? The fact remains LLM can't reach comparable human error rates without consuming 75% of the energy output of our entire local galaxy. While I find true Neuromorphic computing topics more interesting, the emergence of the LLM \"AI\" true believer is deeply concerning to those that understand how they are actually built. =3 [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":46223284,"author":"Joel_Mckay","month":"2025-12","story":"Is it a bubble?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as example of clickbait LLM slop","quote":"LLM slop doesn't have aspirations at all, its just click bait nonsense.","text":"LLM slop doesn't have aspirations at all, its just click bait nonsense. [VIDEO] Drives people insane: [LINK] And LLM are economically and technologically unsustainable: [LINK] These have already proven it will be unconstrained if AGI ever emerges. [LINK] The LLM bubble will pass, as it is already losing money with every new user. =3"},{"id":45808963,"author":"Joel_Mckay","month":"2025-11","story":"Lessons from interviews on deploying AI Agents in production","stance":"reference","why":"Cited loosely in a comment on AI hype","quote":"","text":"The people outside the business of selling hype will not be keen on paying to break their business with popular liabilities. =3 [VIDEO]"},{"id":45943624,"author":"Joel_Mckay","month":"2025-11","story":"Blocking LLM crawlers without JavaScript","stance":"reference","why":"AI training on its own slop","quote":"AI\" is eating its own slop, and that is a problem","text":"You are still not evil enough to know this... be thankful =3 \"AI\" is eating its own slop, and that is a problem: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45652516,"author":"likium","month":"2025-10","story":"Wikipedia says traffic is falling due to AI search summaries and social video","stance":"reference","why":"Example of LLMs citing LLM-generated content","quote":"has had issues with LLMs citing LLM generated content","text":"Citing is not referencing links, it's finding primary sources and fact-checking. Kurzgesagt[1], an informational YouTube channel, has had issues with LLMs citing LLM generated content. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45671331,"author":"CaptainOfCoit","month":"2025-10","story":"AI assistants misrepresent news content 45% of the time","stance":"reference","why":"Concrete example of AI sources citing AI hallucinations","quote":"A recent Kurzgesagt goes into the dangers of this","text":"> Actually there was a Wikipedia article of this name, but it was deleted in June -- because it was AI generated. Unfortunately AI falls for this much like humans do. A recent Kurzgesagt goes into the dangers of this, and they found the same thing happening with a concrete example: They were researching a topic, tried using LLMs, found they weren't accurate enough and hallucinated, so they continued doing things the manual way. Then some weeks/months later, they noticed a bunch of YouTube videos that had the very hallucinations they were avoiding, and now their own AI assistants started to use those as sources. Paraphrased/remembered by me, could have some inconsistencies/hallucinations. [VIDEO]"},{"id":45748759,"author":"Joel_Mckay","month":"2025-10","story":"The end of the rip-off economy: consumers use LLMs against information asymmetry","stance":"reference","why":"Cited to show LLM-derived cons increase","quote":"","text":"Except LLM derived cons also increase. [VIDEO] Technology changes, but on average human-beings do not. =3"},{"id":45773477,"author":"Joel_Mckay","month":"2025-10","story":"How OpenAI uses complex and circular deals to fuel its multibillion-dollar rise","stance":"reference","why":"Cited loosely alongside skepticism of AI hype","quote":"","text":"Silicon valley capital investment firms have always exploited regulatory capture to \"compete\". The public simply has a ridiculously short memory of the losers pushed out of the market during the loss-leader to exploit transition phase. Currently, the trend is not whether one technology will outpace the other in the \"AI\" hype-cycle ( [LINK] ), but it does create perceived asymmetry with skilled-labor pools. That alone is valuable leverage to a corporation, and people are getting fired or ripped off anticipating the rise of real \"AI\". [VIDEO] One day real \"AI\" may exist, but a LLM or current reasoning model is unlikely going to make that happen. It is absolutely hilarious there is a cult-like devotion to the AstroTurf marketing. The question is never whether this is right or wrong... but simply how one may personally capture revenue before the Trough of disillusionment. =3"}]},"agw-wlHGi0E":{"wy":"HN users mine it for design wisdom and history from luminaries, and often quote Guy Steele's remarks.","q":{"x":"I'd also highly recommend that anyone interested in this kind of thing listen to all three of the Dynamic Languages Wizards Series panels","i":45937838,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45937838,"author":"EdwardCoffin","month":"2025-11","story":"Designing a Language (2017)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Panel worth listening to; audio suffices as visuals add little","quote":"I'd also highly recommend that anyone interested in this kind of thing listen to all three of the Dynamic Languages Wizards Series panels","text":"In a similar vein is this 2003 post in an MIT discussion forum by Scott McKay [1]. I'd also highly recommend that anyone interested in this kind of thing listen to all three of the Dynamic Languages Wizards Series panels from 2001: runtime [2], language design [3], and compilation [4] Note that though these are videos, there isn't that much compelling in the visual portion, you could easily rip them to audio files and lose little. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":9949329,"author":"EdwardCoffin","month":"2015-07","story":"Ask HN: What are some good resources on the history of programming languages?","stance":"rec","why":"Panel of luminaries discussing language design history","quote":"A lot of history was discussed in their 5+ hours.","text":"In 2001 MIT had the Dynamic Languages Wizards Series [1], which consisted of three panels of luminaries in the field (videos and participants names on the linked page). A lot of history was discussed in their 5+ hours. They are also on YouTube: Panel on Runtime: Richard Kelsey, David Moon, Tucker Withington, Kim Barrett, Scott McKay [2] Panel on Compilation: David Detlefs, Will Clinger, Martin Rinard, and Mat Hostetter [3] Panel on Language Design: Paul Graham, John Maeda, Jonathan Rees, Guy Steele [4] --- [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [VIDEO]"},{"id":40112262,"author":"nomilk","month":"2024-04","story":"Ruby vs. Python comes down to the for loop (2021)","stance":"reference","why":"Guy Steele on doing familiar things exactly in language design","quote":"","text":"First impressions apply to languages too. A lot of mathematicians went into AI but didn't start out with any programming experience. They dabbled in some languages and gravitated toward the one(s) they found most useful. Paraphrasing Guy Steele [1]: > It's important that when you design a language that does a familiar thing, that you do the familiar thing exactly... it's criminal that you can write 1/2 and get values nowhere near one-half. Python >>> 1/2 0.5 Ruby irb(main):001> 1/2 0 To a novice programmer, ruby's result makes no sense, and introduces an incredible degree of doubt into the mind of the user. Sure, they could explore why it gives that result, but if that's not helping them get toward solving the problem they're using the programming language for in the first place, then it could seem like an indulgent tangent. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37306941,"author":"EdwardCoffin","month":"2023-08","story":"Practical Parsing with Flex and Bison","stance":"reference","why":"Guy Steele on using Yacc as a grammar check","quote":"Be sure that your language will parse.","text":"I used Lex and Yacc (as the article mentions, the direct predecessors of the Flex and Bison it talks about) a bit a long time ago, but more recently when I've written parsers I've just used recursive descent - but I was parsing a well-defined grammar. It did leave me with the knowledge of how to describe grammars in Yacc though, which I have found useful. Guy Steele said something about a use of Yacc that stuck with me : Be sure that your language will parse. It seems stupid to sit down and start designing constructs and not worry how they will fit together. You can get a language that's difficult if not impossible to parse, not only for a computer, but for a person. I use YACC constantly as a check of all my language designs, but I very seldom use YACC in the implementation. I use it as a tester, to be sure that it's LR(1) ... because if a language is LR(1) it's more likely that a person can deal with it. From the Dynamic Languages Wizards series, in the panel on language design (1:09:05) [1] I've not yet employed Yacc in this fashion, but it did give me a tool for thinking about object models. A while ago when I was puzzling over how some classes in an entity relationship diagram should be related, and I considered it from the point of view of how would I design a grammar for serializing an instance of the model into text. This essentially made my decision for me in a principled way, though I never reached the point of writing up a grammar for the whole model, just consider"},{"id":22497157,"author":"EdwardCoffin","month":"2020-03","story":"Why not to use (f)lex, yacc or bison","stance":"reference","why":"Guy Steele on using YACC to check a language parses","quote":"Be sure that your language will parse.","text":"Guy Steele said this in the Dynamic Languages Wizards series, in the panel on language design [1]: Be sure that your language will parse. It seems stupid to sit down and start designing constructs and not worry how they will fit together. You can get a language that's difficult if not impossible to parse, not only for a computer, but for a person. I use YACC constantly as a check of all my language designs, but I very seldom use YACC in the implementation. I use it as a tester, to be sure that it's LR(1) ... because if a language is LR(1) it's more likely that a person can deal with it. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22736971,"author":"EdwardCoffin","month":"2020-03","story":"Open access to ACM Digital Library during coronavirus pandemic","stance":"reference","why":"Guy Steele on higher-order functionals and thinking in APL","quote":"thinking in APL should improve your Lisp code","text":"> I'm biased because this one is effectively \"What if we make an APL Lisp?\" You might like this excerpt from the MIT Dynamic Languages Wizard's panel, where Guy Steele said [1]: > Higher order functionals, I think, are underrated, and can be very good. As the years pass, the more I use Common Lisp, the more I find myself using the sequence functions, including a lot of mapcars. I had to do a matrix tensor product routine about two weeks ago, and I was puzzling over how to do the nested do loops and so forth, and finally I realized it was just mapcar of mapcar of apply of append to mapcar and mapcar, done. No big deal. In other words, thinking in APL should improve your Lisp code. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14618313,"author":"lukego","month":"2017-06","story":"What Challenges and Trade-Offs Do Optimising Compilers Face?","stance":"reference","why":"Paul Graham on designing languages for different audiences","quote":"","text":"Paul Graham once made a point about designing languages for yourself, or for people who are not as smart as you are, or for people who are smarter than you are. [VIDEO] Somehow this blog and the debate with djb reminds me of this point. What kind of person is are the compiler optimizations intended to benefit?"},{"id":9780960,"author":"platz","month":"2015-06","story":"What to know before debating type systems (2010)","stance":"reference","why":"Guy Steele defines a dynamic language","quote":"A dynamic language is one that defers as many decisions as possible until runtime..","text":"Guy Steele: \"A dynamic language is one that defers as many decisions as possible until runtime..\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":7642966,"author":"EdwardCoffin","month":"2014-04","story":"Great Programmers (2004)","stance":"reference","why":"Guy Steele on establishing design principles early","quote":"It's important to establish design principles early on, that is in effect to do meta-design","text":"This reminds me of a tip Guy Steele gave in his talk on the Language Design panel of the Dynamic Languages Wizards Series in 2001 [1]. He said: It's important to establish design principles early on, that is in effect to do meta-design, because as you evolve the design, or as the language grows later, you want to remain true to an initial vision. This has to do with keeping the description small, and if you can resort to a meta-principle to help you resolve a particular design decision, it keeps the language more consistent. (17:09-17:29) [2] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["hV41QEKiMlM",1],["D9H9qTdserM",1],["-NL76uQOpI0",1],["bmSAYlu0NcY",1],["pXOK-ZVJMaU",1]]},"bnoUBfLxZz0":{"wy":"Offered as a clear, well-made look at a real, working drone-delivery system, often in answer to skepticism about delivery drones.","q":{"x":"Wendover Productions did a great video detailing this a few years back.","i":31150439,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31150439,"author":"Slow_Hand","month":"2022-04","story":"Drones have transformed blood delivery in Rwanda","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Detailed look at blood drone delivery; channel on global logistics.","quote":"Wendover Productions did a great video detailing this a few years back.","text":"Wendover Productions did a great video detailing this a few years back. It’s so cool what they’ve done. [VIDEO] Also, Wendover have a fantastic Youtube channel that’s focused on global logistics. It’s endlessly fascinating and extremely well executed. Strongly recommended."},{"id":21020998,"author":"Fabricio20","month":"2019-09","story":"Google Wing Launching US Drone Deliveries with FedEx, Walgreens","stance":"rec","why":"Zipline's catapult and parachute drone delivery in Rwanda.","quote":"have really nice videos about their system currently deployed in Rwanda.","text":"The catapult launches the drones into the air, which then parachute the package to the location. Real Engineering [1] and Wendover Productions [2] have really nice videos about their system currently deployed in Rwanda. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36765865,"author":"jeffalyanak","month":"2023-07","story":"Ask HN: What ever happened to the “coming wave” of delivery drones?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"doing slingshot launches.","text":"Ironically one of the areas it _is_ being used successfully is in delivery of blood to some areas in Rwanda, but they're using fixed-wing drones and are even doing slingshot launches. [1] The economics are totally different, though. The \"product\" is very high value and has a short shelf life, so being able to deliver it quickly is a big benefit. On top of that, using a fixed-wing delivery vehicle and launching it with a human-powered slingshot makes it actually reasonably energy-efficient. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19633810,"author":"michaelt","month":"2019-04","story":"Google Wing will deliver to about 100 homes in Canberra","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"There's a better video of the drones here: [LINK] Google's delivery drones combine a multirotor design with fixed-wings. It seems like an eminently sensible design to me; fixed-wing operation will be quieter and more power-efficient, allowing longer travel distances (unlike the early Amazon Prime Air videos [1]), while hover capability avoids the need to parachute the load (unlike the current Zipline drones [2]) and provides a hover-and-land-almost-anywhere capability I imagine is useful. Presumably the winch-down-the-load design reduces the risk of a user putting their hands into the blades - not to mention reducing the risk from customers choosing an inappropriate landing location; I imagine the drone can also jettison the winch cord if it gets caught on anything. It precludes delivering directly to apartment windows - but AFAIK no-one else is trying that either. It's a pity they haven't released clear info on these drones' range, round-trip time and weight carrying capacity. Which are of course critical to working out the profitability of a citywide coffee delivery service. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["jEbRVNxL44c",1],["XGo4PJd1lng",1],["Eq3bUFgEcb4",1]]},"hyYCn_kAngI":{"wy":"Commenters recommend the whole talk as practical, tactical advice for early customer acquisition and outbound sales.","q":{"x":"The entire video is really good actually I recommend watching the entire thing.","i":36691784,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36691784,"author":"PUSH_AX","month":"2023-07","story":"Ask HN: What is your base template for first time communication with a prospect?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Whole video worthwhile for getting first customers.","quote":"The entire video is really good actually I recommend watching the entire thing.","text":"I watched this just yesterday [VIDEO] The entire video is really good actually I recommend watching the entire thing."},{"id":46407398,"author":"jppope","month":"2025-12","story":"Ask HN: Resources to get better at outbound sales?","stance":"rec","why":"Included among YC videos in an outbound sales reading list.","quote":"YC videos (e.g. , , , etc)","text":"I spent the first chunk of my career doing sales (2nd half spent in software engineering). Theres a lot of good books out there but you're going to find a lot of the direct advice to be non-applicable (as I'm sure you can pick up from the comments). Most of the Literature out there is directed at professional sales people, who for the most part are non-technical, have the backing of a marketing org, and are also different than a founder. Anyway, heres a reading list. Most of the trainings I've been a part of were custom built to the org or market, and frankly I learned more from Rules of the Game by Neil Strauss. I did enjoy \"the Wedge\" training by Randy Schwantz... that one you should do the video not the book. It also sounds like you could use some marketing stuff so I'll throw some of that in there too. In no particular order, and please keep in mind this is off the top of my head: * Influence (the classic) * YC videos (e.g. [LINK] , [LINK] , [VIDEO] , etc) * Thinking Fast and Slow * Purple Cow * Clayton Christensen stuff * Spin Selling * Challenger Sale * Guerrilla marketing (for the mental muscle) * Jeffrey Gitomer (basic but useful) * Lean Startup (for positioning) * Charisma Myth * Minimalist Entrepreneur (bits and pieces) * The presentation secrets of steve jobs (just a good book on presentations, framed around Steve Jobs to sell more) For what its worth this question comes up fairly often. It seems like technical people would like a \"technical people\" guide on how to"},{"id":34217740,"author":"O__________O","month":"2023-01","story":"AI Generated Section Names and Links for Existing YouTube Videos","stance":"reference","why":"Example of YC using AI-generated sections in video descriptions.","quote":"for example, look at the description of this video","text":"Not sure if this is YC company, but YC has been using this recently on their YouTube Startup School videos; for example, look at the description of this video: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["csyL9EC0S0c",1],["a-BOSpxYJ9M",1],["kb-m2fasdDY",1]]},"ip641WmY4pA":{"wy":"HN people hold it up as a polished solo hardware project and the go-to example of haptic knobs, including in discussions of physical car controls.","q":{"x":"I made a rotary input device that provides software-defined \"virtual\" detents and end-stops, implemented using a BLDC gimbal motor.","i":37044192,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":41083968,"author":"laurencei","month":"2024-07","story":"Reverse-engineering my speakers' API to get reasonable volume control","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Demo of an open-source buildable haptic smart knob.","quote":"its amazing","text":"Hey OP - if your thinking about a smart knob with haptic feedback etc - check this open source project out that you can build yourself - its amazing: [LINK] Video demo here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44425964,"author":"rzzzt","month":"2025-06","story":"Ask HN: What Are You Working On? (June 2025)","stance":"rec","why":"Related encoder-knob project that might interest the reader.","quote":"two encoder wheel projects on YouTube that might interest you","text":"Vaguely related - two encoder wheel projects on YouTube that might interest you: - \"Wireless High Resolution Scrolling is Amazing\": [LINK] - \"DIY haptic input knob: BLDC motor + round LCD\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":48002988,"author":"crote","month":"2026-05","story":"Mercedes-Benz commits to bringing back physical buttons","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a haptic dial for car controls.","quote":"a big fat dial with haptic feedback[2]","text":"I'm not convinced it is that easy. Cars traditionally have very generic button clusters, like [0]. It is even very common to have dummy buttons in there. Combine that with today's cars where those buttons are hooked up to some MCU to send a CAN message instead of being hardwired to a function-specific cable in a giant loom, and it is suddenly quite easy to change button functionality quite late in the design process for basically zero cost: you just need a slightly different label print and a small firmware patch! Or, if you want to be 100% flexible, go with the ATM approach where physical buttons are placed next to an icon shown on a screen[1]. All of the flexibility and all of the tactile feedback! You can even go for a multi-level layout, with a top row of mode selection buttons, a bottom row of mode-specific function buttons, and perhaps even a big fat dial with haptic feedback[2]. Or even go all-out Elgato Stream Deck[3]. And sure, the fact that slapping in a giant touchscreen lets them decouple UX design from physical controls is going to play a big role. But it is by far the laziest and least user-friendly way of doing so. If that's the best you can come up with, you probably shouldn't be doing UX design at all. [0]: [LINK] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO] [3]: [LINK]"},{"id":38423452,"author":"yonatan8070","month":"2023-11","story":"Tesla Normal Operating Sounds","stance":"reference","why":"Brushless-motor knob giving software-defined tactile clicks and end stops.","quote":"a knob built on a burshless motor that's controlled to provide the user with software defined feedback like tactile clicks and end stops","text":"This reminded me of the SmartKnob View I saw on YT a while back, it's a knob built on a burshless motor that's controlled to provide the user with software defined feedback like tactile clicks and end stops [VIDEO]"},{"id":37044192,"author":"scottbez1","month":"2023-08","story":"Ask HN: What's the coolest physical thing you've made?","stance":"reference","why":"Creator's own demo of a BLDC haptic knob with round LCD.","quote":"I made a rotary input device that provides software-defined \"virtual\" detents and end-stops, implemented using a BLDC gimbal motor.","text":"I made a rotary input device that provides software-defined \"virtual\" detents and end-stops, implemented using a BLDC gimbal motor. It can dynamically switch from completely smooth unbounded rotation, to having detents with configurable spacing and strength and \"end-stops\" that spring back if you try to rotate past them. [VIDEO] It's got a round LCD on the front of the knob (wired and supported via the hollow shaft of the motor) and uses the flex of the PCB and strain gauge sensors (in the latest revision, simply SMD resistors whose resistance changes when stretched) to detect when the knob is pressed down. It's open source hardware and software - [LINK] HN folks might appreciate that it communicates with host software on the computer via protobuf-encoded USB serial messages -- nanopb is awesome for embedded C protobuf support, and having the defined schema, autogenerated serialization code, and compile-time type safety is so much nicer than ArduinoJson or hand-written binary protocols! I'd love to get it hooked up to some real software eventually (video editors or home-assistant control are my 2 main ideas), but it's really just been a fun project to tinker with and try out some new ideas and parts I've never used before."},{"id":35988691,"author":"ly3xqhl8g9","month":"2023-05","story":"Where have all the hackers gone?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a polished individual hardware project.","quote":"examples would be something in the tier of Bartosz Ciechanowski [3], scottbez1 [4]","text":"Two answers: (i) late stage capitalism: who cares about going through a 400 pages spec sheet to try and reverse engineer the ludicrous Chinglish [1] in which all non-mainstream manuals are written today (wanted to declare war on China by myself when parsing through a Xinje PLC manual recently) when you could be fired at any time, a health emergency can bankrupt you, you generally feel more and more powerless, from your daily life to the once-in-a-lifetime-but-happening-every-week events such as economic crises, pandemics, wars, automated labor, x-flation, climate change, and so on and so forth. (ii) product refinement: we no longer are interested in a few lines of assembly which barely show two lines and a dot moving on the screen [2], we want that feeling, silky smooth user experience which some companies spend billions to provide, but very few individuals can offer by themselves, examples would be something in the tier of Bartosz Ciechanowski [3], scottbez1 [4], or Chris from Clickspring [5], for a more hardware bent. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [VIDEO] [5] [LINK]"}],"r":[["GtQdIYUtAHg",1],["4Q7FTjhvZ7Y",1],["0yKf8TrLUOw",1],["ouvi-fwrfIY",1]]},"plcc6E-E1uU":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a good, non-sensational presentation of Loeb's actual argument, usually linked in Oumuamua threads.","q":{"x":"There's a good interview with Avi Loeb on the Lex Fridman podcast about this very topic.","i":27675126,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":25847878,"author":"cronix","month":"2021-01","story":"Have we already been visited by aliens?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Good interview with Avi Loeb about Oumuamua","quote":"Lex Fridman had a really good interview with Avi Loeb about Oumuamua, among other things, about a week ago. Highly recommend!","text":"Lex Fridman had a really good interview with Avi Loeb about Oumuamua, among other things, about a week ago. Highly recommend! [VIDEO]"},{"id":27675126,"author":"erellsworth","month":"2021-06","story":"Oumuamua, alien artefact or interstellar debris?","stance":"rec","why":"Loeb's actual argument is compelling and not sensational","quote":"There's a good interview with Avi Loeb on the Lex Fridman podcast about this very topic.","text":"There's a good interview with Avi Loeb on the Lex Fridman podcast about this very topic. His actual argument is fairly compelling and doesn't really sound sensational at all, IMO. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37317079,"author":"echelon","month":"2023-08","story":"Discovery of Spherules of Likely Extrasolar Composition in the Pacific Ocean [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Background on researcher Avi Loeb","quote":"","text":"For context, this is a paper by Avi Loeb [1,2], the \"Oumuamua is an interstellar spaceship\" researcher. In this paper, Avi explores a possible interstellar origin meteorite. He used data from US air defence senors that had tracked a highly unusual atmospheric entry nearly a decade ago. This object was theorized as a possible extrasolar trajectory meteorite when it had entered the earth's atmosphere. [3,4] Avi recently chartered a research expedition in an attempt to dredge up samples of this meteorite from the sea floor. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":32444245,"author":"MichaelCollins","month":"2022-08","story":"A meteor or alien tech: Harvard professor plans UFO expedition","stance":"reference","why":"Loeb discussing looking for alien visitors","quote":"He has an interview with Lex Fridman here","text":"He has an interview with Lex Fridman here: [VIDEO] I haven't listened to the whole thing, but he seems to think that aliens visiting our solar system is likely enough that it makes sense to look for. He falls short of the confident certainty I usually expect from cranks though, he's talking about investigating possibilities. I think he's eccentric, but I wouldn't call him a crank."},{"id":27297407,"author":"segfault720","month":"2021-05","story":"When scientific orthodoxy resembles religious dogma","stance":"reference","why":"Author discussing these issues further on a podcast","quote":"has been on several podcasts including Lex Fridman's, where he has discussed these issues further","text":"The author of this article has been on several podcasts including Lex Fridman's, where he has discussed these issues further: [VIDEO] ."}],"r":[["2wq9x2QcZN0",1],["-q7ZVXOU3kM",1],["aB8zcAttP1E",1],["eJ3RzGoQC4s",1]]},"uTdyb-RWNKo":{"wy":"HN recommends the documentary itself as a story of an epic early failure in mobile computing, and the trailer is the linked entry point.","q":{"x":"Highly recommend the documentary on General Magic.","i":42365502,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":42365502,"author":"brunorsini","month":"2024-12","story":"He Built Apple's 1980s iPad Concept [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Documentary on General Magic's early attempt at building the iPhone.","quote":"Highly recommend the documentary on General Magic.","text":"Highly recommend the documentary on General Magic. They were actually trying to build the iPhone as early as 1989 (and into the 90s). My former boss Meghan Smith was part of that team, she loved it — and so many Silicon Valley companies came out of that project. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25915160,"author":"the_af","month":"2021-01","story":"Halt and Catch Fire Syllabus","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary about General Magic and the people behind early mobile computing.","quote":"I found it interesting.","text":"Have you watched the (pretty good) documentary \"General Magic\"? If features a lot of the same people than folklore.org, to the point one could consider it an Apple \"side story\", though there are also future Googlers and people who built Android. Here's the trailer: [VIDEO] I found it interesting. There is a bit of hagiography going on, though (and note the trailer seems way more antagonistic towards Sculley than the documentary actually is.) One thing I found striking is that none of these people seem to need or care about money. They say so explicitly, \"we didn't care about the money\", and that it was all about the vision and passion. Yet somehow they managed to remain clothed and well fed. If that's not the definition of privilege, I don't know what is!"},{"id":42680230,"author":"psc","month":"2025-01","story":"Microsoft Bob: Microsoft's biggest flop of the 1990s","stance":"reference","why":"Documentary on General Magic and the Magic Link device.","quote":"There's a documentary too","text":"I recently read the story of the Magic Link: [LINK] > When General Magic finally shipped in 1994 — under the threat of Apple’s Newton — they hadn’t made the Pocket Crystal that Porat first dreamed of in 1989. Instead, they released something they called the Sony Magic Link. It weighed 1.5 lbs (0.68 kg) and was priced at US$800 (US$1560 in 2022 dollars). It offered futuristic features like a touchscreen, downloadable apps and animated emojis — the first of its kind. Fadell thought it would be revolutionary — people could now carry a personal computer with them wherever they went. But nobody bought it. In the end only three to four thousand Magic Link devices were sold, and mostly to family and friends. There's a documentary too: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["-FENubmZGj8",1]]},"-gPuH1yeZ08":{"wy":"Cited in threads about taste and terrible music as a famously engineered 'worst song', mostly for its curiosity value.","q":{"x":"They surveyed people to find the things they liked least in music and put it all together.","i":42141817,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":42141817,"author":"endgame","month":"2024-11","story":"An oral history of \"We Built This City,\" the worst song of all time (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"A creatively awful song assembled from least-liked musical elements.","quote":"They surveyed people to find the things they liked least in music and put it all together.","text":"I know your description includes \"still get regular radio play\", but one of my favourite pieces of troll music is \"The Most Unwanted Song\". They surveyed people to find the things they liked least in music and put it all together. I don't want to spoil just how creatively awful it is or what's in there, so I'll just drop the link and go. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35358013,"author":"jonnycat","month":"2023-03","story":"","stance":"rec","why":"Komar and Melamid's song of everything people dislike in music.","quote":"the results are amazing/hilarious","text":"A tangent from the point of the post, but Komar & Melamid did a similar \"experiment\" with music to produce both the most wanted and most unwanted songs, and the results are amazing/hilarious. The most unwanted song, for example, features all the thing people dislike in music (according to their surveys): opera singers rapping about cowboys, bagpipes, uncomfortably slow tempos, and choirs of children singing ad jingles among other things. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29231059,"author":"Sohcahtoa82","month":"2021-11","story":"Is there such a thing as good taste?","stance":"reference","why":"A deliberately unlikeable song engineered from survey data.","quote":"A few years ago, a group engineered the most un likeable song ever","text":"> written by a team constructed by a label to create the most generically likeable music possible A few years ago, a group engineered the most un likeable song ever: [VIDEO] Featuring bagpipes, a rapping opera singer, country music, and a children's choir singing holiday music that ends with telling you to do your shopping at Wal-mart."},{"id":15670319,"author":"tinus_hn","month":"2017-11","story":"Electric Sheep on Ubuntu Linux 17.10","stance":"reference","why":"An odd mishmash song built from what people dislike.","quote":"the most unwanted song is a bizarre mishmash that is kind of interesting to listen to.","text":"Reminds me of the process used to build the most wanted and unwanted song. The most wanted song is a terribly cheesy pop song, the most unwanted song is a bizarre mishmash that is kind of interesting to listen to. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12407813,"author":"cpeterso","month":"2016-09","story":"An experiment in crowd-sourced songwriting","stance":"reference","why":"A novelty song built from survey results on disliked music.","quote":"designed to incorporate lyrical and musical elements that were annoying to most people.","text":"Somewhat related: \"The Most Unwanted Song\" is a novelty song created by artists Komar and Melamid and composer Dave Soldier in 1997. The song was designed to incorporate lyrical and musical elements that were annoying to most people. These elements included bagpipes, cowboy music, an opera singer rapping, and a children's choir that urged listeners to go shopping at Walmart. [LINK] Most Unwanted Song: [VIDEO] Most Wanted Song: [LINK]"},{"id":15594131,"author":"jpfed","month":"2017-10","story":"What Are the Most Disliked Programming Languages?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"so bad it's good","text":"The next step is obviously to take the most-disliked technologies and combine them into something so bad it's good, like \"the most unwanted music\" ( [VIDEO] )."},{"id":10263909,"author":"InclinedPlane","month":"2015-09","story":"List of music considered the worst","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"made a song featuring those, calling it The Most Unwanted Song","text":"If you want to listen to The Shaggs: [LINK] Also, someone figured out all of the least generally desirable aspects of songs and made a song featuring those, calling it The Most Unwanted Song: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["prXuyMCgbTc",1],["JWD1Fpdd4Pc",1],["W3aieHjyNvw",1]]},"5pidokakU4I":{"wy":"The go-to reference in threads on chord progressions, pop music similarity and music copyright.","q":{"x":"well worth watching","i":30795188,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":35870737,"author":"BrandoElFollito","month":"2023-05","story":"Ed Sheeran Demonstrates How Copyright Is Destroying Musicians","stance":"rec","why":"Good example of chords versus songs.","quote":"A good example of chords vs. songs","text":"I think nobody linked to Axis of Awsome's 4 Chords Song yet. A good example of chords vs. songs: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30795188,"author":"cycomanic","month":"2022-03","story":"I became the world's most prolific DJ, using code","stance":"rec","why":"Comedy song showing pop songs share the same chords.","quote":"well worth watching","text":"I suspect the \"appealing\" melodies amongst this are likely a small subset of the the total set. The whole story reminded me of this axis of awesome (OZ comedy group) song (well worth watching): [VIDEO]"},{"id":43557028,"author":"tsumnia","month":"2025-04","story":"Shared DNA in Music","stance":"reference","why":"Chord progression song poking fun at pop similarity and inspiration.","quote":"Axis of Awesome also has a \"chord progression song\" [2] that makes fun of how similar many pop songs are.","text":"There's a great video essay series known as \"Everything is a Remix\" by Kirby Ferguson [1] if you have an hour to spare. Ferguson uses the trends of music sampling as a base for how artists draw inspiration from others. The controversy of sampling has been used across genres, include around minute 9 Ferguson points out that Led Zepplin were considered ripoffs for their appropriation of Jake Holmes. Axis of Awesome also has a \"chord progression song\" [2] that makes fun of how similar many pop songs are. The video draws a nice parallel to how so much in our world serves as \"inspiration\" for our own works, but acknowledges the controversy around using a single source as \"too much inspiration\". Especially as we move into whatever copyright arguments are getting made toward AI right now, I think it serves as a nice outline on why this is such a complicated matter. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":43727708,"author":"svag","month":"2025-04","story":"I analyzed chord progressions in 680k songs","stance":"reference","why":"Comedy sketch showing most pop songs use four chords.","quote":"There is a nice standup sketch from an Australian comedy group","text":"There is a nice standup sketch from an Australian comedy group, the Axis of Evil, that most of the pop songs are using only four chords, [VIDEO]"},{"id":42249799,"author":"ben_w","month":"2024-11","story":"The AI reporter that took my old job just got fired","stance":"reference","why":"Example of pop music sounding the same.","quote":"which is why all modern popular music sounds so similar that this video got made 14 years ago","text":"Novelty itself is easy, the hard part is the kind of novelty that is familiar enough to be engaging while also unusual enough to attract all the people bored by the mainstream. Worse, as people attempt to automate novelty, they will be (and have been) repeatedly thwarted by the fact that the implicit patterns of the automation system themselves become patterns to be learned and recognised… which is why all modern popular music sounds so similar that this video got made 14 years ago: [VIDEO] (This is already a thing with GenAI images made by people who just prompt-and-go, though artists using it as a tool can easily do much better). But go too soon, be too novel, and you're in something like uncanny valley: When Saint-Saëns' Danse macabre was first performed, it was poorly received by violating then-current expectations, now it's considered a masterpiece."},{"id":38265887,"author":"aidenn0","month":"2023-11","story":"Google Sues Men Who Weaponized DMCA Notices to Crush Competition","stance":"reference","why":"Live version of the four-chord medley idea.","quote":"I like the live version of that better[1]","text":"I like the live version of that better[1], and while the Axis of Awesome wins in the shear number of songs, I like Rob P's presentation[2] of the same idea more. 1: [VIDEO] 2: [LINK]"},{"id":35824771,"author":"shagie","month":"2023-05","story":"Ed Sheeran Wins Copyright Case over Marvin Gaye’s ‘Let’s Get It On’","stance":"reference","why":"Video played by Sheeran's defense showing songs sharing four chords.","quote":"his defense played a video of Australian comedy band Axis of Awesome performing a medley of dozens of songs with the same four chords","text":"Gift link: [LINK] My favorite part from it: > “We have spent the last eight years talking about two songs with dramatically different lyrics, melodies and four chords which are also different and used by songwriters every day, all over the world,” Mr. Sheeran continued. “These chords are common building blocks which were used to create music long before ‘Let’s Get It On’ was written and will be used to make music long after we are all gone.” From a different article about the trial: [LINK] > Before Sheeran’s Thursday testimony, his defense played a video of Australian comedy band Axis of Awesome performing a medley of dozens of songs with the same four chords. The video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35869414,"author":"jemmyw","month":"2023-05","story":"Ed Sheeran Demonstrates How Copyright Is Destroying Musicians","stance":"reference","why":"Supports point that common diatonic progressions are shared across songs.","quote":"","text":"They have a similar (but not actually the same) chord progression. They're in a different key and the melody is different. If you could copyright similar chord progressions then that would be the end of new pop music. > similar enough that even Sheeran played them as a live medley That doesn't really mean much, any good sounding diatonic (chords all from the same key) song is going to share the progression with lots of other songs [VIDEO]"},{"id":32561027,"author":"dcow","month":"2022-08","story":"An odd discovery on Spotify","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the progression appears frequently in pop music.","quote":"appears very frequently in pop music","text":"The songs in the playlist are the first 4 chords of [LINK] essentially looped for 47 seconds. I V vi iii appears very frequently in pop music [VIDEO] . If one were trying to exploit the algorithm..."},{"id":31110207,"author":"bambax","month":"2022-04","story":"What chords do you need?","stance":"reference","why":"The canonical four-chords comedy medley.","quote":"The obligatory video is of course","text":"The obligatory video is of course \"Four Chords\" by Axis of Awesome: [VIDEO] Rick Beato has kind of the opposite video about \"the most complex song ever\": [LINK] The song in question is Never Gonna Let You Go by Sergio Mendes; plenty of videos exist of it; here's one live: [LINK]"},{"id":28786161,"author":"lathiat","month":"2021-10","story":"Canyon.mid","stance":"reference","why":"Medley of pop songs sharing the same four chords.","quote":"Medley of a bunch of pop songs on the premise they all use the same 4 chords.","text":"When I think four chord song I think of Axis Of Awesome :) Medley of a bunch of pop songs on the premise they all use the same 4 chords. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25582713,"author":"merolish","month":"2020-12","story":"A Generative Grammar for Jazz Chord Sequences (1984)","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates how simple most pop music chord structures are.","quote":"Most pop music is even simpler, as illustrated by Axis of Awesome.","text":"Jazz guitarist here, and yes. One of the nice things about the instrument is that a relatively small number of forms for chords and scales are transposable up and down the fretboard, so once you burn the usual patterns into your head (iim7-V7-IM7, etc.) reading a new sheet usually doesn't take too much work. Most pop music is even simpler, as illustrated by Axis of Awesome. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17050091,"author":"Double_Cast","month":"2018-05","story":"Conversations with a six-year-old on functional programming","stance":"reference","why":"Covers the ubiquitous I V vi IV four-chord pop progression.","quote":"This video is about the infamous","text":"I didn't learn music through Youtube. So unfortunately, I'm not familiar with any videos that I would consider comprehensive or canonical. At best, I can share videos that highlight a particular pattern, or share examples of popular songs that I recognize as having a commonality. Which might give you a better feel for what music theory is. Another thing to keep in mind is that musical patterns accrete into genres. And there's a wide variety of genres. So it's difficult for any single video to cover everything in detail. I think it's more common for videos to cover the patterns of a particular genre. E.g. if you're interested in Blues, consider searching for videos about Blues. ----- [LINK] I remember the above Vox video highlights a unique chord which sounds \"melty\" and is often used in holiday music. The chord is known as a \"minor_7th, diminished_5th\" aka \"m7d5\". [VIDEO] This video is about the infamous \"4-chord pop progression\". It's ubiquitous on the radio. Elitists and connoisseurs tend to look down on it for appealing to casuals. The progression is \"I V vi IV\" (capital denotes major chords, lowercase denotes minor chords). [LINK] When I think of the \"12-Bar Blues (Chord Progression)\", I think of Stevie Ray Vaughn's \"Pride and Joy\". But there's tons of educational videos on this. It's very popular in not only Blues, but Rock & Roll. It's \"I I I I; IV IV I I; V IV I I\". But things get interesting here because musicians often substitue \"jazzy\" chords like Dominant_7th's (ak"},{"id":16669463,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2018-03","story":"Appeals Court Says It's Okay to Copyright an Entire Style of Music","stance":"reference","why":"Pop reuses the four-chord progression","quote":"the infamous \"4 chords\"[1]","text":"> a lot of pop is made by knowingly and deliberately copying elements from various source songs One of the defining characteristics of \"pop\" music is the reuse of major structural elements like the primary harmonic progression (e.g. the infamous \"4 chords\"[1]). Listeners that do not have a lot of music theory knowledge and experience can still follow along with the \"plot\" of the song because it reuses simple structures they already know. Appreciation of more complex harmonic structure and other unique features requires a lot more experience with complex types of music theory. As with all art/writing, knowing your intended audience is important. If the audience includes people without music experience/training, limiting the \"vocabulary\" top what they know is absolutely necessary. For a very good explanation of this that includes examples, see this[2] video essay. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":10572721,"author":"skwosh","month":"2015-11","story":"The Musicians Behind One of the Most Sampled Songs in History Finally Got Paid","stance":"reference","why":"Music as reuse and recycling","quote":"Music is intrinsically about reuse, recycling, and reinvention [1].","text":"It is kind of absurd. Music is intrinsically about reuse, recycling, and reinvention [1]. Sampling is really just a continuation of that. [1] Axis of Awesome, The Four Chord Song, [VIDEO]"},{"id":10030549,"author":"TheOtherHobbes","month":"2015-08","story":"Classical music generation with recurrent neural networks","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Pop has actually ossified over the last ten years or so. Dubstep is more than fifteen years old now, and the Paul Van Dyk album I've just listened to sounds a lot like the last Paul Van Dyk album a few years ago. Then you get this: [LINK] and this: [LINK] and the now famous this: [VIDEO] and you start to wonder how much entropy there is to analyse. Modern classical has gone the way of modern art. It's now basically a marketing exercise. The musical experience is secondary. But then I expect creative AIs to be better at marketing too..."},{"id":7127687,"author":"joelrunyon","month":"2014-01","story":"One Man Has Written Virtually Every Major Pop Song Of The Last 20 Years.","stance":"reference","why":"Viral song showing similarity of hits using four chords","quote":"it demonstrated the similarities between major popular songs spanning multiple decades through the use of just four chords..","text":"Interesting to see similarities in musical preferences & identifying common threads that lead to popularity. This video / song [1] was viral a few years ago because it demonstrated the similarities between major popular songs spanning multiple decades through the use of just four chords.. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":4103248,"author":"fferen","month":"2012-06","story":"I analyzed the chords to 1300 popular songs for patterns. This is what I found.","stance":"reference","why":"Four chord progression across many pop songs","quote":"the \"Four Chord Song\" by Axis of Awesome","text":"When I was young and learning music theory in piano lessons, one day I realized that literally half the songs on the radio used the chord progression \"I V vi IV\". This was a huge revelation to me! To my dismay, I couldn't find any evidence on the internet that anyone else had noticed this, until very recently I saw the \"Four Chord Song\" by Axis of Awesome ( [VIDEO] ). I was hoping to see some mention of that in this article, but was disappointed."},{"id":15224127,"author":"peterburkimsher","month":"2017-09","story":"Deep Learning Techniques for Music Generation – A Survey","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Four chords are enough to make me happy.","text":"I'm a simple man. Four chords are enough to make me happy. [VIDEO] You can experiment with different progressions at this CodePen: [LINK] Maybe it's not Beethoven or Chopin, but if it's good enough to compare with modern pop songs, then there should be a market for it."},{"id":10321652,"author":"timrichard","month":"2015-10","story":"A melody written by a crowd","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Axis of Awesome put it better","text":"> This melody will eventually become a song with a chord structure of C, G, Am, F <sarcasm> Thanking my lucky stars that someone has finally thought of this </sarcasm> Axis of Awesome put it better : [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["If61baWF4GE",1],["17oCQakzIl8",1],["5OtKEetGy2Y",1],["5hVmeOCJjOU",1],["I3l4XLZ59iw",1]]},"7N41TEcjcvM":{"wy":"HN points to it as the clearest short demo of iMUSE, considered a high point in video game music.","q":{"x":"considered a high point in video game music. 1. A video demo","i":43970017,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43970017,"author":"sirtaj","month":"2025-05","story":"Understanding LucasArts' iMUSE System","stance":"rec","why":"Video demo of seamless music transitions in Monkey Island 2.","quote":"considered a high point in video game music. 1. A video demo","text":"Also done very well in Monkey Island 2 with iMuse as well, in which a lot of care was taken in transitioning music with custom bridges. It was quite subtle and lovely, and is considered a high point in video game music. 1. A video demo: [VIDEO] 2. Some details: [LINK]"},{"id":27075244,"author":"silvester23","month":"2021-05","story":"Happy Hacking: Music Implementation in NieR:Automata (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstrates iMUSE adaptive music in action.","quote":"iMUSE [0] by the fantastic Michael Land needs to be mentioned here. Here it is in action","text":"I feel like iMUSE [0] by the fantastic Michael Land needs to be mentioned here. Here it is in action: [1] [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36694448,"author":"ant6n","month":"2023-07","story":"How the design of Disney parks affects our perspective","stance":"reference","why":"Shows layered adaptive music in Monkey Island 2.","quote":"different music layers get added and removed as you move aeound","text":"How do they do it, like the imuse system of in mokey island 2, where different music layers get added and removed as you move aeound? (See [VIDEO] )"}]},"AHeZHyOnsm4":{"wy":"It is the primary source for the original Starlink concept, so commenters cite it for Musk's stated goals on latency, hops, pricing and timelines.","q":{"x":"Here is a video where elon explains the system very well in 2015.","i":12983805,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12983805,"author":"andrewtbham","month":"2016-11","story":"SpaceX plans worldwide satellite Internet with low latency, gigabit speed","stance":"rec","why":"Musk explains the satellite internet system in 2015","quote":"Here is a video where elon explains the system very well in 2015.","text":"no one saw it coming? they anounced this in 2015. Here is a video where elon explains the system very well in 2015. [VIDEO]"},{"id":9328881,"author":"matznerd","month":"2015-04","story":"Elon Musk wants to bring the Internet in space","stance":"rec","why":"Musk goes over the entire satellite internet game plan","quote":"He pretty much goes over the entire game plan in this video","text":"Elon covered all of this and more during the launch of the SpaceX Seattle offices. He pretty much goes over the entire game plan in this video -> [VIDEO]"},{"id":42230957,"author":"schiffern","month":"2024-11","story":"Starlink Direct to Cell","stance":"reference","why":"Musk noted dense city limits in the first announcement","quote":"Musk mentions this in the very first announcement of Starlink.","text":"Not really feasible. The only reason Starlink DTC works is because it's not trying to supply coverage inside dense cities. Musk mentions this in the very first announcement of Starlink. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37629830,"author":"mlindner","month":"2023-09","story":"SpaceX’s Starlink surpasses 2M active customers","stance":"reference","why":"2015 presentation with Elon's prototype timeline","quote":"he mentioned that they plan to launch prototypes in \"3 months\" (standard Elon time)","text":"They did not \"expect over 20M by now\". That article is some bad reporting. It pulls from a private forecast given very early on to investors in 2015 that depended on a lot of things going different than they actually did. For example, the pandemic not happening, and Starship actively flying. 2015 is when they first opened the early development facility in Redmond. No hardware, not even prototype hardware, no government applications filed. Didn't even have an office building. The entire design of the system went through multiple massive revisions before it even launched the first test satellites, as well as a firing of most of the entire executive staff (who went to work for Amazon, which still has yet to launch any of their satellites). In a presentation[1] in 2015 to a group of people (early employees?) he mentioned that they plan to launch prototypes in \"3 months\" (standard Elon time), they didn't launch any prototypes until 2018. That'll kind of throw off your plans to getting to 20 million. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12974786,"author":"schiffern","month":"2016-11","story":"SpaceX FCC Application for over 4000 Internet Satellites","stance":"reference","why":"Primary source for the constellation plan","quote":"Most of what we know about the plan comes from this video.","text":"Completely agree on WISPs. I think SpaceX will be pursuing mesh networking too. Could an OTA update (and repointing 90°) turn a satellite terminal into an auto/calibrating point-to-point MIMO link? >global data infrastructure is made up of things like the Hibernia Atlantic cable Surprisingly the stated primary goal of the SpaceX constellation is actually to compete with long-distance fiber backhauls. Their sell for satellite backhaul is that it's lower latency (no need to avoid continents, 50% faster speed of light, fewer hops) and works everywhere. Giving global gigabit internet to rural areas and ~10% of urban customers (with the rest on fiber) is only a bonus. :) Most of what we know about the plan comes from this video. Timestamp is to the start of the juicy bits. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12984991,"author":"schiffern","month":"2016-11","story":"SpaceX plans worldwide satellite Internet with low latency, gigabit speed","stance":"reference","why":"Musk's stated goals for the constellation from January 2015","quote":"This matches with Musk's stated goals for the project from January 2015","text":">An astounding O(54% of global internet traffic) [2]! Thanks for working this out. This matches with Musk's stated goals for the project from January 2015 (\"the majority\" of internet traffic): [VIDEO] >The focus is going to be on creating a global communications system. This is quite an ambitious effort. We're really talking about something which is... in the long term it will be like rebuilding the internet in space. The goal will be to have the majority of long distance internet traffic go over this network , and about 10% of local and business traffic. Probably 90% of people's local access will still come from fiber, but we'll do about 10% of business and consumer direct, and more than half I think of the long distance traffic. >As you guys may know, the speed of light in a vacuum is somewhere between 40-50% faster than in fiber. So you can actually do long distance communication faster if you route it through vacuum than you can if you route it through fiber. >And you can also go through far fewer hops. Let's say if you want to communicate from Seattle to South Africa. If you look at the actual path that it takes it's extremely convoluted[1]. It will follow the outline of the continents. It will go through 200 routers and repeaters, and the latency is extremely bad. Whereas if you did it with a satellite network you could actually do it in two or three hops. Well maybe four hops; it depends on what the altitude of the satellites are, and what the cross-links are. >Let's s"},{"id":12985065,"author":"schiffern","month":"2016-11","story":"SpaceX plans worldwide satellite Internet with low latency, gigabit speed","stance":"reference","why":"Musk on latency, hops and why satellites beat fiber","quote":"In the linked video Musk says 4 hops to go from Seattle to South Africa","text":"> That means routers and intelligence which means uplink, interlink, downlink hops in both directions. 600 ms here we come again. The story from Musk is 20-30 ms latency anywhere on Earth. [VIDEO] Fiber has an order of magnitude greater number of hops/routers/repeaters, moves 50% slower, and has to travel more distance to route around continents. See [LINK] In the linked video Musk says 4 hops to go from Seattle to South Africa, so they're skipping satellites. It works out that at 1100 km altitude they have vacuum line-of-sight for any satellite within 6000 km ground track in any direction. >I don't know how they're going to ... >Managing that ... hurts my head. It is indeed a Hard Problem. [VIDEO] > So it's something that would both provide optionality for people living in advanced economies, as well as people in poor countries that don't even have electricity or fiber or anything like that. So it's a real enabler for people in poor regions of the world and it gives optionality for people in wealthier countries. > So I think it's something that definitely needs to be done. And it's a really difficult technical problem to solve. So that's why we need the smartest engineering talent in the world to solve the problem. > And, you know, at the same time to make sure that we don't create Skynet. So... [laugh] Ironically, the server room at Spacex (jokingly!) was called Skynet. [laugh] Though fate has a great sense of irony, so we really need to make sure that doesn't come true..."},{"id":12985164,"author":"schiffern","month":"2016-11","story":"SpaceX plans worldwide satellite Internet with low latency, gigabit speed","stance":"reference","why":"Musk discusses Teledesic's mistakes at the announcement","quote":"Musk mentioned Teledesic the very first day it was announced (back in January 2015).","text":">it's curious noone mentions how this have been attempted and failed Musk mentioned Teledesic the very first day it was announced (back in January 2015). [VIDEO] >Things to consider about success? Well, I think it's important to assume that terrestrial networks will get much better over time. >You know, one of the mistakes that, say, Teledesic made was not assuming that terrestrial networks would get much better over time. So we need to make sure that the system we design is good even taking into account significant improvements in the terrestrial systems. >But I do there there are some important differences between what we're doing and, let's say, Teledesic. In the case of Teledesic they were trying to talk to phones, and that gets back to that problem of a roof penetrating situation. And particularly when stuff is coming from space, like if you're in a skyscraper and it's got to go through 27 floors to reach you, that's not going to happen. There's nothing that will... you know, short of like a neutrino [laughter] ...a neutrino phone [laughter]. >In the case of Teledesic there were some fundamental issues there."},{"id":12985223,"author":"schiffern","month":"2016-11","story":"SpaceX plans worldwide satellite Internet with low latency, gigabit speed","stance":"reference","why":"Musk's initial announcement on long-distance versus local traffic","quote":"Musk said this during the initial announcement","text":">Is this going to scale? ...not that much bandwidth for any given densely populated area. We're going to keep having our local ISP in cities (they're giving between 0.1 and 1 Gb/s on fiber right now) Except the goal isn't to replace fiber in densely populated areas. Musk said this during the initial announcement: [VIDEO] > The goal will be to have the majority of long distance internet traffic go over this network, and about 10% of local and business traffic. Probably 90% of people's local access will still come from fiber, but we'll do about 10% of business and consumer direct, and more than half I think of the long distance traffic. [VIDEO] > And then space is also really good for sparse connectivity. So if you've got large swaths of land where there's relatively low density of users, space is actually ideal for that. > It would also be able to service, like I say, probably 10% of people in relatively dense urban and suburban environments. And so in cases where people have, you know, are sort of stuck with Time Warner or Comcast or something, this would provide an opportunity to... [unintelligible due to cheering] >I wonder if there are chances that those antennas can be made so small to fit into a phone and tablet. As revealed in the above video, it's a rooftop antenna about the size of a pizza box. No wall/roof penetrating spectrum. Of course it automatically aims itself, so as a mobile AP it should be \"plug and play\" unlike current GEO internet user terminals which need "},{"id":12985412,"author":"schiffern","month":"2016-11","story":"SpaceX plans worldwide satellite Internet with low latency, gigabit speed","stance":"reference","why":"Musk says the service will not be free","quote":"Not free according to Musk.","text":">Free, Not free according to Musk. [VIDEO] > Well it can't be free. Because then we'll, like, we'll go out of business. [laugh] > No, no, it can't be free to the user. I don't think so. > This would be... this would cost a lot to build. Ultimately over time to build the full version of the system, we're talking about something that would be 10 or 15 billion dollars to create. Maybe more. And the user terminals would be at least 100, to maybe 300 dollars, depending on which type of terminal. > And this is intended to generate a significant amount of revenue, and help fund a city on Mars. So looking into the long term, and saying, \"what's needed to create a city on Mars?\" Well one thing's for sure... a lot of money. [laugh] So we need things that will generate a lot of money."},{"id":9328869,"author":"matznerd","month":"2015-04","story":"Elon Musk wants to bring the Internet in space","stance":"reference","why":"Musk explains vacuum light speed advantage for long-distance communication","quote":"The speed of light in vacuum is 40-50% faster than in fiber.","text":"It's faster and more direct to go earth->satellite->earth for certain routes/types of data, than traversing through various routers and undersea cables. Roughly paraphrased quote from Elon Musk SpaceX Seattle Office Opening: \"The speed of light in vacuum is 40-50% faster than in fiber. So you can do long distance communication through space(vacuum), a lot better than if you route it through fiber and use far fewer hops. Lets say you want to communicate from Seattle to South Africa, the path is extremely convoluted, follows outline of continent, goes through 200 routers and repeaters, if done with satellite network, it can be done in 4 hops\" Video link to time of quote -> [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["O_azyt1JhI0",1],["QNznD9hMEh0",1],["1Evwgu369Jw",1]]},"AnZ0uTOerUI":{"wy":"HN recommends it for the idea of giving data instead of asking for it, and ties it to zero values and exception-free design.","q":{"x":"giving data instead of asking for data avoids error conditions","i":22227745,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22227745,"author":"hinkley","month":"2020-02","story":"You’re better off using Exceptions","stance":"rec","why":"Explains avoiding error conditions by giving data instead of asking for it.","quote":"giving data instead of asking for data avoids error conditions","text":"I was watching this the other day: [VIDEO] Unconditional Code by Michael Feathers One solution to this problem is discussed toward the middle: giving data instead of asking for data avoids error conditions. Doing so pushes use of the data farther down the call tree and pulls acquisition up near the top, where we are closer to the user and thus able to more clearly decide what if anything to do about these corner cases."},{"id":18745508,"author":"raghava","month":"2018-12","story":"Ask HN: Best talks of 2018?","stance":"rec","why":"One of several insightful GOTO 2018 talks recommended.","quote":"I find almost all from GOTO 2018 very insightful.","text":"Blockchains Are a Bad Idea (James Mickens) [LINK] I find almost all from GOTO 2018 very insightful. [LINK] esp ones listed below. GOTO 2018 • The Do's and Don'ts of Error Handling • Joe Armstrong [LINK] GOTO 2018 • Pragmatic Event-Driven Microservices • Allard Buijze [LINK] GOTO 2018 • Why Business Cases are Toxic • Chris Matts [LINK] GOTO 2018 • Unconditional Code • Michael Feathers [VIDEO]"},{"id":28749979,"author":"cratermoon","month":"2021-10","story":"Things Go needs more than generics","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on unconditional programming enabled by meaningful zero values.","quote":"Having a zero value enables, for example, Unconditional Programming","text":"Please no on the Universal nil. Meaningful zero values for types are essential. It may be confusing to some that nil is a valid zero value for some types and not others, in which case that needs to be refined, but having a type that can either be a value, a zero value, or a nil introduces complexity where there should be simplicity. Having a zero value enables, for example, Unconditional Programming: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["TTM_b7EJg5E",1],["15RTC22Z2xI",1],["SxdOUGdseq4",1],["aokNwKx7gM8",1],["gNRnrn5DE58",1],["EK32jo7i5LQ",1]]},"EkpQAGQiv4Q":{"wy":"HN commenters use it as a well-researched account of what the Wrights actually solved, in threads on airplane physics and invention credit.","q":{"x":"This video has a nice delineation of the collection of breakthroughs needed","i":40841842,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":43463320,"author":"dj_gitmo","month":"2025-03","story":"The Wright brothers invented the airplane, right? Not if you're in Brazil","stance":"rec","why":"Well researched documentary on the Wrights","quote":"Check out Gregs Airplanes and Automobiles for really well researched aeronautic docs.","text":"Check out Gregs Airplanes and Automobiles for really well researched aeronautic docs. He made one specifically about the Wrights [VIDEO]"},{"id":40841842,"author":"mcarmichael","month":"2024-07","story":"The physics of airplane flight","stance":"rec","why":"Lays out the breakthroughs the Wrights needed","quote":"This video has a nice delineation of the collection of breakthroughs needed","text":"Control was an almost completely unaddressed issue before the Wrights took it up, even though it is also crucial to useful glider development. This video has a nice delineation of the collection of breakthroughs needed: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40842615,"author":"ahazred8ta","month":"2024-07","story":"The physics of airplane flight","stance":"reference","why":"Backs up point about Wrights' wind tunnel understanding","quote":"","text":"Basically, understanding airfoils. Back in the 1840s they put miniature steam engines in model planes and got them to fly. There were powered ground-effect planes in the 1800s that had more powerful engines than the Wright Flyer, but the Wrights were the first people to invent the wind tunnel, so they had a better understanding of lift-to-drag and control surface issues. They adapted the flow-pattern analysis tanks used by ship designers. As mcarmichael said [VIDEO]"},{"id":37157387,"author":"somat","month":"2023-08","story":"How Thomas Edison Tricked the Press into Believing He’d Invented the Light Bulb","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim Wrights did the real engineering","quote":"","text":"Not as narrow as many want to believe. The Wright brothers were the only ones doing actual engineering on the subject. They were 5 years ahead of everybody else and remember that being second is much much easier than being first, for one somebody else is the one making all the mistakes. [VIDEO]"}]},"FyKRubB5N60":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a detailed, accurate explanation of why Betamax lost, and cites it in debates over whether VHS was actually the worse format.","q":{"x":"Here's a very good video going into details","i":30279179,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":34909064,"author":"voxadam","month":"2023-02","story":"Toshiba HD-DVD firmware archive","stance":"rec","why":"Good videos on how and why Betamax failed","quote":"has a few good videos talking about how and why Betamax failed.","text":"The YouTube channel Technology Connections has a few good videos talking about how and why Betamax failed. Why Sony's Beta Videotape System Failed--and failed hard (Part 1) - [VIDEO] Why Sony's Beta Videotape System Failed--Part 2 - [LINK] The VHS cassette was more clever than Beta - [LINK]"},{"id":30279179,"author":"anyfoo","month":"2022-02","story":"Netflix will falter","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed explanation of why VHS beat Beta","quote":"Here's a very good video going into details","text":"From the article: \"I’m not a binge watcher. I’ve never been much of a TV viewer. I didn’t even own a television until my mid-30s. [...] but I get restless if I spend too much time staring into that flickering screen. My wife Tara has a similar attitude.\" This honestly makes me wonder \"ok, why should I listen to you, then?\" It doesn't help that they then go ahead and give an, at best, oversimplified explanation of why VHS won against Beta.[1] Not a single mention of one of VHS's killer features versus Beta: Tape length. (And calling Windows , of all OSes, an \"open operating system\"... oh dear, I get the point you are trying to make, but \"open\" and 90's anti-trust Microsoft in the same sentence is pretty bold.) Maybe the conclusions are still valid, I can't tell. I remember that what made Netflix so immensely attractive to me, a \"binge watcher\" of many many TV shows and with many friends who were at least to some extent binge watchers, was that Netflix was the platform that had all the movies and especially TV shows that I wanted to binge watch. [1] Here's a very good video going into details: [VIDEO]"},{"id":49142428,"author":"leoc","month":"2026-08","story":"Atom is better than RSS, in ways that matter","stance":"reference","why":"Argues VHS was not worse overall than Beta","quote":"it doesn't seem that VHS was in fact the worse format overall","text":"While a worse format surely could have won something like the video wars, it doesn't seem that VHS was in fact the worse format overall: [VIDEO] (Part 2: [LINK] . Quality comparison: [LINK] ) Beta was basically optimised for one thing, reasonable cassette size, and the tradeoffs turned out probably not to be worth it."},{"id":45170892,"author":"tracker1","month":"2025-09","story":"How RSS beat Microsoft","stance":"reference","why":"Cited on tape length and quality tradeoffs between Beta and VHS","quote":"BetaMax tapes were recorded in an extended play format, while most VHS tapes were in Standard.","text":"Betamax's \"Standard\" playback was better than VHS's \"standard\" playback... the issue was VHS \"standard\" could get something like 2 hours to a typical tape and BetaMax was like half an hour. For actual content, BetaMax tapes were recorded in an extended play format, while most VHS tapes were in Standard. This dramatically reduced BetaMax quality to be comparable or worse. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ZsRRCnque2E",1],["HyzD8pNlpwI",1],["5TFDG-y-EHs",1],["ar9WRwCiSr0",1],["yHXx3orN35Y",1],["bRCNenhcvpw",1],["kZA9Hnp3aV4",1]]},"Nbv9L-WIu0s":{"wy":"Used in software bloat debates as a measured look at why binaries got larger.","q":{"x":"Well worth a viewing.","i":9825958,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":9825958,"author":"vezzy-fnord","month":"2015-07","story":"Software bloat makes me sad","stance":"rec","why":"Compares sizes of Unix utilities over decades","quote":"Well worth a viewing.","text":"A good article a while back looked at common unix utilities, comparing the size of commands like cp from the 1980's to the present. Most of the bloat had to do with features that almost nobody ever uses. That was actually a talk called \"Bloat: How and Why UNIX Grew Up (and Out)\": [VIDEO] Well worth a viewing."},{"id":7770646,"author":"pdw","month":"2014-05","story":"Pokémon Crystal disassembled source code","stance":"rec","why":"Investigates why modern binaries like ls grew so large","quote":"There was an interesting talk on this at Linux.conf.au a couple of years ago, trying to figure out why modern binaries are so large.","text":"There was an interesting talk on this at Linux.conf.au a couple of years ago, trying to figure out why modern binaries are so large. [VIDEO] > Bloat: How and Why UNIX Grew Up (and Out) - Rusty Russell, Matt Evans > The 'ls' binary on the original release of Unix (version 6) was 4920 bytes long. Thirty six years later, 'ls' on Ubuntu is 105776 bytes. Is this the laziness of modern coders? Increasing features? Does 'cat' really now do 313 times more stuff, or is there something else going on?"},{"id":7613649,"author":"monochr","month":"2014-04","story":"All software sucks","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as showing replacement tools use similar resources","quote":"This was brought nowhere better to the front than in this video","text":"Now you just need to design a balanced ternary computer which uses lambda calculus instead of Boolean logic and you will be a quarter of the way to getting rid of the gunk left over from the birth of computers when people were arguing if relays or vacuum tubes were the way of the future. The one thing I have noticed all the people with \"philosophical\" objections to bloat have in common is that they are largely ignorant of the real hardware and software they are coding on. This was brought nowhere better to the front than in this video [VIDEO] where they showed that the tools written because gnu tools were too big when measured in a running computer take up just as many resources."},{"id":7614121,"author":"copergi","month":"2014-04","story":"All software sucks","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"it doesn't appear that what you are suggesting was even brought up much less demonstrated?","text":">This was brought nowhere better to the front than in this video [VIDEO] where they showed that the tools written because gnu tools were too big when measured in a running computer take up just as many resources. At what time in the video does that happen? I didn't have time to watch the whole thing, but it doesn't appear that what you are suggesting was even brought up much less demonstrated?"}]},"OgRFOjVzvm0":{"wy":"HN uses it as the canonical explanation from Sussman himself of why SICP ended at MIT.","q":{"x":"we can't mention SICP without referencing this (relatively recent) video","i":43683335,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43683335,"author":"OhMeadhbh","month":"2025-04","story":"Why Fennel?","stance":"rec","why":"Video on why MIT moved intro classes from Scheme to Python.","quote":"we can't mention SICP without referencing this (relatively recent) video","text":"Also... HtDP (How to Design Programs) is a good follow-on to SICP. Oh... and I think we can't mention SICP without referencing this (relatively recent) video about why MIT moved from Scheme to Python for intro classes: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34658419,"author":"yazzku","month":"2023-02","story":"Sussman Explains the End of SICP","stance":"rec","why":"Sussman talk on modern computing's complexity and why SICP ended.","quote":"Don't miss Sussman's talk linked from the post","text":"Don't miss Sussman's talk linked from the post: [VIDEO] This stuff resonates with me deeply. The reality of the modern computing world is a clusterfuck that no single person understands, especially because, like Sussman said, parts are entirely secret/proprietary anyway. Therefore I think an engineer should be skilled in a) removing the unnecessary bits of that clusterfuck that are not necessary to their problem and b) getting stuff done without having a complete understanding of the whole system. (b) sounds scary as fuck, but I think that's the (sad?) reality we live in in the software and hardware world. Embrace the cluster; embrace the fuck."},{"id":43501964,"author":"MathMonkeyMan","month":"2025-03","story":"Tracing the thoughts of a large language model","stance":"reference","why":"Sussman on why MIT moved from Scheme to Python.","quote":"Gerald Sussman discusses the idea when talking about why MIT switched their introductory programming course from Scheme to Python","text":"In a sense this has been true of conventional programs for a while now. Gerald Sussman discusses the idea when talking about why MIT switched their introductory programming course from Scheme to Python: < [VIDEO] >."},{"id":36804360,"author":"jstx1","month":"2023-07","story":"Ask HN: Is SICP/HtDP still worth reading in 2023? Any alternatives?","stance":"reference","why":"Sussman says programming changed since SICP.","quote":"Sussman says so himself","text":"Not necessarily as relevant, programming has changed a lot since SICP came out. Sussman says so himself - [VIDEO]"},{"id":35397008,"author":"efortis","month":"2023-04","story":"Programming Isn’t Coding","stance":"reference","why":"Sussman explains why MIT dropped 6.001.","quote":"Gerald Jay Sussman explains why they pulled the plug on MIT 6001 (SICP) here","text":"Related, Gerald Jay Sussman explains why they pulled the plug on MIT 6001 (SICP) here: [VIDEO] To me, part of the problem is that devs continuously change tech stacks without poking at the layers underneath. So only a few become proficient enough to focus on the abstractions. Similarly, now many designers are not more than technicians of graphic design tools."},{"id":34335081,"author":"kagevf","month":"2023-01","story":"Learn Lisp the Hard Way","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why SICP's authors wanted to update the curriculum.","quote":"the authors of SICP themselves wanted to update the curriculum","text":"My understanding is that the authors of SICP themselves wanted to update the curriculum - not just a language change, but to cover something at a higher level of abstraction. See: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["wo84LFzx5nI",1]]},"TgFdics3uKo":{"wy":"Pointed to in threads on training absolute pitch in children as a practitioner's account.","q":{"x":"Rick Beato has some videos about how he trained his kids","i":21834569,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":43907808,"author":"lylejantzi3rd","month":"2025-05","story":"Infants and Babies can become pitch-perfect with early exposure to certain music","stance":"rec","why":"Beato explains how children develop perfect pitch","quote":"Rick Beato has done a lot of videos on perfect pitch.","text":"Rick Beato has done a lot of videos on perfect pitch. His son has perfect pitch, but he does not. How Children Develop Perfect Pitch - [VIDEO] Perfect Pitch vs Relative Pitch: Which Is More Important? - [LINK] Why Adults Can't Develop Perfect Pitch - [LINK]"},{"id":21834569,"author":"mantesso","month":"2019-12","story":"Ask HN: Playlists for teaching perfect pitch to toddlers?","stance":"rec","why":"Rick Beato on how children develop perfect pitch","quote":"Rick Beato has some videos about how he trained his kids","text":"Rick Beato has some videos about how he trained his kids: [VIDEO] I never tried anything similar nor have a music background but found it fascniting to watch it"},{"id":35757503,"author":"avodonosov","month":"2023-04","story":"Children aged 2-6 successfully trained to acquire absolute pitch","stance":"reference","why":"Beato on early exposure to complex music building perfect pitch","quote":"In another video Rick Beato said he facilitated development of his son's perfect pitch by exposing him to a lot of complex music in early age.","text":"In another video Rick Beato said he facilitated development of his son's perfect pitch by exposing him to a lot of complex music in early age. [LINK] His hypothesis, based on some studies, is that children in ealy age have ability for perfect pitch but later loose it, if it's not utilized. The same way as young children are able to percieve any phoneme of any language, but later loose this ability and only recognise phonemes of the language spoken in the family (that's a known scientific fact). [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Bea4FS-zDzc",1],["amJ-ev8Ial8",1]]},"-dk3NOEgX7o":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an eye-opener on how recycling was promoted by the plastics industry despite not working.","q":{"x":"If you want to have your eyes opened about just how little of the material you probably think you are recycling actually gets recycled","i":24441196,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24441196,"author":"kbos87","month":"2020-09","story":"Pringles tube tries to wake from 'recycling nightmare'","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows how little plastic is actually recycled.","quote":"If you want to have your eyes opened about just how little of the material you probably think you are recycling actually gets recycled","text":"If you want to have your eyes opened about just how little of the material you probably think you are recycling actually gets recycled, watch the recent Frontline episode called “Plastic Wars”. The sad truth is that while recycling is worthwhile, it’s also a woefully insufficient solution that has been held up by the petroleum industry as a cure all to prevent a backlash against plastic. Even products that are labeled recyclable often aren’t once they are processed. One thing that sticks with me (and this is just one representative example) is that almost none of those plastic salad containers that have overtaken supermarkets are ever recycled. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36887885,"author":"gghffguhvc","month":"2023-07","story":"Scientists Say Recycling Has Backfired Spectacularly","stance":"reference","why":"Source for claim recycling served plastic producers.","quote":"Recycling turned out just the way the plastic producers wanted it.","text":"Recycling turned out just the way the plastic producers wanted it. Source - Frontline Documentary “Plastic Wars”. Full documentary at [VIDEO] ."},{"id":28469426,"author":"echopurity","month":"2021-09","story":"California aims to ban recycling symbols on things that aren’t recyclable","stance":"reference","why":"Documentary on recycling as industry scam.","quote":"The whole movement to recycle plastic was an industry scam to knowingly profit from destroying the planet.","text":"The whole movement to recycle plastic was an industry scam to knowingly profit from destroying the planet. [VIDEO]"}]},"IQPsKvG6WMI":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it in political and disinformation threads as a source on Soviet psychological operations, a contested claim.","m":[{"id":30999076,"author":"zackees","month":"2022-04","story":"“Decolonising Math”: Durham U Asks Professors to Consider Race of Mathematicians","stance":"strong_rec","why":"KGB defector lecture explaining ideological subversion of society","quote":"Here's a one hour lecture from a KGB defector who's explains what's going on","text":"> What’s the point, to achieve paralysis? Yes. Here's a one hour lecture from a KGB defector who's explains what's going on: 15 min lecture: [VIDEO] 1 hour lecture: [LINK] TL;DR: It's intentional, it's going to get way worse, and if you get too loud in opposition you will find yourself ostracized and possibly without a job."},{"id":30630951,"author":"Lio","month":"2022-03","story":"DuckDuckGo “down-rank sites associated with Russian disinformation”","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source that KGB policy was to mess with truth","quote":"Given that it was a KGB policy to mess with the truth[1]","text":"I’m OK with this. Given that it was a KGB policy to mess with the truth[1] I think it’s fine to try to inoculate yourself from it. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26663206,"author":"mmmeff","month":"2021-04","story":"French left tears itself apart over ‘non-white’ meetings","stance":"reference","why":"KGB defector on Soviet psy-ops subverting the US","quote":"This goes back to Soviet cold-war-era psy-ops.","text":"This goes back to Soviet cold-war-era psy-ops. They planted the seeds in the US and now the US is exporting it to the rest of the world. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24404773,"author":"29athrowaway","month":"2020-09","story":"Is Spotify Killing the Top 40?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Many conspiracies can be just explained through emergent behavior. For example, there could be demand for that music, and the labels are just creating content to respond to that demand... without any ethical considerations about what they're promoting, that is. But to be honest, a conspiracy of some kind could be an explanation, who knows. For some people, there are substantial tangible, material benefits to dumbing down society. For example, the \"gangster rapper\" stereotype is pushed aggresively by the music industry. The popularity of \"gangster rap\" results in \"gangster\" aesthetics becoming fashionable, even among law abiding people who are not gangsters. However, when people see a person that resembles the \"gangster\" stereotype (purely based on their attire) pushed by the entertainment industry, they will be more likely to call 911, eventually resulting in an innocent person being killed for no valid reason. Is this a racist conspiracy? who knows. Likewise, oversexualization results in people having unplanned kids, causing them to ultimately become poor, and more susceptible to predatory loans, low wages, political manipulation and all the machinery that exists only to abuse the poor. Similar to anti-intellectualism. And then, glorification of violence, competition, non-compliance, etc... this just makes people very divisive, and in order to dominate a large population you want them to be as divided as possible, and constantly distracted with immaterial disputes while you "},{"id":24614386,"author":"dfischer","month":"2020-09","story":"Judge temporarily blocks U.S. ban on TikTok downloads from U.S. app stores","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Most definitely cia has a history with manipulating information. But at the point of information, how do you know what to even trust at any level? Evidence can be provided for anything. Information and disinformation all at the same time. This is only increasing - how will anyone be able to sort themselves out? I’m not so sure it’s possible. [VIDEO] Also 60 seconds of video is a ton of information. And it’s not just one video at a time. The total session aggregated per user per active user is way higher than that. These are behaviors where users use the app all day. Their entire worldview is based on the content within the social network experience."},{"id":24242128,"author":"m0zg","month":"2020-08","story":"TikTok to challenge U.S. order banning transactions with the video app","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> sponsored Why the past tense? Seems to me like someone is still \"sponsoring\" racial tensions in the US. This has been in the works for quite some time, and it's paying dividends now: [VIDEO] . The entirety of the US media apparatus, as well as the entirety of the political establishment is happy to play along."}]},"dDsWTyLEgbk":{"wy":"HN recommends it because hearing pioneers explain early computing in their own words conveys the context and excitement of the era.","q":{"x":"Hearing concepts explained by those who pioneered them really brings them home","i":20426955,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20426955,"author":"charlysl","month":"2019-07","story":"Fernando Corbató, a Father of Your Computer (and Your Password), Has Died","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Pioneer explains the context and excitement of early computing and Fortran.","quote":"Hearing concepts explained by those who pioneered them really brings them home","text":"Exactly, this is why I shared this video. Hearing concepts explained by those who pioneered them really brings them home, because it highlights the context in which they were created, with a description of what was the trouble with computing back then and how it was solved, and the excitement of the achievement. For instance, he situates his breaktrought in a timeline where the main milestones are, first, getting computers to work at all, and, then, high level languages, to make them much easier to program (Fortran, [VIDEO] ). Then, time sharing made them much easier to use. The birth of the terminal, no less, which is still around and which we all take for granted (I know it's deeper than this, multiprocessing, but this is the most visible manifestation) ... Judging from this video, it also seems that he was a very good communicator."},{"id":49324312,"author":"metazip","month":"2026-08","story":"History of John Backus's functional programming project [draft]","stance":"reference","why":"Lists related Backus videos on FL and FP with the original oral history.","quote":"Videos to FL and FP","text":"Videos to FL and FP: John Backus: Function Level Programming and the FL Language 1987 ( [LINK] ), John Backus Group Meeting - IBM Research - 5 July 1989 ( [LINK] ), Backus on functional programming ( [LINK] ) from Original: Oral History of John Backus ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":29673562,"author":"mpweiher","month":"2021-12","story":"Why Architecture Oriented Programming Matters (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> If \"constraining architecture\" means any separation of concerns or interface. It doesn't. It means you work with constraints, for example dataflow constraints. For example: [LINK] > I also thought the implementation was a little muddled Yes! That was the point of that section of the paper, that doing the straightforward thing leads to a muddle: \"At this point, it becomes clear that our original strategy of updating the UI from the individual setter methods is probably not tenable in the long run.\" A little later: \"As we have seen, even a conceptually very simple application such as a temperature converter quickly attracts significant complexity with non-obvious trade-offs once the requirements of an interactive version of that application are taken into account.\" The fact that you need to create these centralised updater methods is non-obvious, and leads to a bunch of related non-local changes. Also a muddle. \"This complexity is not the result of a complicated domain model, but rather of the architectural embellishments required to move data from location to location in order to keep the different parts of the application (model, user interface, persistence) synchronized. In the next section, we will look at a mechanism for simplifying this kind of overhead.\" > ReactJS (uses HOM like Objective-Smalltalk) ReactJS uses Higher Order Messaging? Where? > Chatty: \"We highlight a strong coupling between languages and architecture,...\" Which was my point, yes, thank you! We are lim"}],"r":[["cKPlPJyQrt4",1],["AD4b-52jtos",1]]},"pmfHHLfbjNQ":{"wy":"It is the canonical example HN brings up in threads about music hacks on floppy drives, printers and other junk hardware.","q":{"x":"this is probably the best example of a hack like this that I've seen.","i":2231626,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":2231626,"author":"torme","month":"2011-02","story":"Star wars theme played by a floppy disk hack.","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best example of a hardware music hack, Radiohead contest entry.","quote":"this is probably the best example of a hack like this that I've seen.","text":"If you haven't seen this yet, this is probably the best example of a hack like this that I've seen. It was submitted for a contest that radiohead held, and I believe was the winner (it takes about a minute to get rolling): [VIDEO] The sound of the scanner is pretty awesome. And the original by radiohead in case you need some context. [LINK]"},{"id":31733311,"author":"LegitShady","month":"2022-06","story":"The Floppotron 3.0","stance":"reference","why":"Radiohead cover on hard drives, printer, scanner and ZX Spectrum.","quote":"James Houston's cover of Radiohead's Nude on hard drives, a dot matrix printer, a scanner, and a sinclair ZX Spectrum.","text":"Not exactly the same thing but it reminds me of James Houston's cover of Radiohead's Nude on hard drives, a dot matrix printer, a scanner, and a sinclair ZX Spectrum. [VIDEO] This says 14 years ago."},{"id":22912783,"author":"ciabattabread","month":"2020-04","story":"Academics steal data from air-gapped systems using PC fan vibrations","stance":"reference","why":"Radiohead cover using old computer equipment and hard drives as speakers.","quote":"the Radiohead cover that used old computer equipment, including hard drives as speakers.","text":"For a brief second I thought your link was the Radiohead cover that used old computer equipment, including hard drives as speakers. Here’s that link: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11180205,"author":"esaym","month":"2016-02","story":"Hacked Together 118 GB Floppy Disk","stance":"reference","why":"Example of beautiful music from hobbyist hardware.","quote":"Sorta like the radiohead hardware hobbyist video","text":"Ah, disappointing. I was expecting some giant array of them all running in unison and making beautiful \"music\". Sorta like the radiohead hardware hobbyist video [VIDEO]"},{"id":9288489,"author":"sspiff","month":"2015-03","story":"Dot matrix printer playing “Eye of the Tiger” [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Hard drive speaker rendition of Radiohead song.","quote":"You can see some of them in action in a rendition of Radiohead's \"Big Ideas: don't get any","text":"This is brilliant. It reminds me of the old HP ScanJets, which came with SCSI commands to play musical notes[0] (someone at HP must have had too much free time on his hands ;)). Some videos of the scanners are at [1] and [2]. Another interesting hardware hack to produce music is the \"hard drive speaker\". You can see some of them in action in a rendition of Radiohead's \"Big Ideas: don't get any\" [3], as well as various printers and other devices used for their auditive side effects. [0]: [LINK] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [VIDEO]"}]},"xZePwin92cI":{"wy":"HN uses it as a favorite old explainer for mechanical telephone switching.","q":{"x":"My favorite old explainer video, telephone switching (1951)","i":36900860,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36900860,"author":"jetrink","month":"2023-07","story":"Hydraulic Steering – Principles of Operation (1956) [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Favorite old explainer on Strowger telephone switching.","quote":"My favorite old explainer video, telephone switching (1951)","text":"My favorite old explainer video, telephone switching (1951): [VIDEO] About the Strowager Switch, which was invented by an undertaker who was fed up with the local telephone operators whom he suspected of diverting phone calls to his competitor."},{"id":32483076,"author":"kkielhofner","month":"2022-08","story":"DigitalOcean Region Information","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for mechanical telecom switching in old colocation buildings.","quote":"","text":"In terms of the cloud providers other than the big three/four (AWS, GCP, Azure, Oracle) most of your Digital Oceans, Linodes (perhaps pre-Akamai?), etc of the world are almost all certainly colocated. As others have noted the \"big ones\" typically operate with a blend - green field physical construction for the big and dense facilities combined with floors/cages/etc in colocation facilities for areas around the world with lower hardware requirements. Colocation facilities are interesting (I've been in a lot of them) and they generally (physically) fall in two buckets: 1) Repurposing of old telecom (and even telegraph!) buildings. CoreSite (a colocation provider) has a few floors (last I looked) in The Telegraph Building in Chicago[0]. If you look at the pictures of the exterior of the building it still says Western Union on the side! There are some interesting aspects of these - most of them are located in downtown areas. If you start looking around in major cities you will start to notice gigantic nondescript buildings with minimal signage, almost no foot traffic, and almost no (if any) windows[1]. If they have any signage at all it will typically be \"Something Bell\", Western Union, AT&T, etc. These facilities are interesting for a variety of reasons. Because they are repurposed telegraph and telecom buildings going back to the days of mechanical telecom switching[2] and DC battery banks they have floor load ratings that are just incredible. Something like 20x or whatever of "},{"id":32093594,"author":"kristopolous","month":"2022-07","story":"The History of User Interfaces","stance":"reference","why":"Strowger switches as part of the history of interfaces.","quote":"there's things like Strowger switches ( ) which I think count","text":"I was honestly expecting it to start with things like arithmometers ( [LINK] ), Cooke and Wheatstone 5-needle telegraphs ( [LINK] ), or perhaps a dictaphone ( [LINK] ) and what about the rich history of fax machines such as the Pantelegraph ( [LINK] ). Here's a newspaper being printed by radiofax in the 1930s for instance ( [LINK] ) Speaking of communications, there's things like Strowger switches ( [VIDEO] ) which I think count because that's what people were interfacing whilst using the phone (this became crossbars and relays in the 1910s and 20s respectively) Relays and telephone tech btw is how Electro the smoking robot from the 1939 world's fair worked ( [LINK] ) Even if we want to limit to computers, we have batch versus time-sharing flow, teletype versus graphical terminals and along those lines you have the work of groups like Evans & Sutherland in the 1970s (such as the line drawing system 1 [LINK] ) or Scientific Data Systems which was later acquired by Xerox. You also have the world of analog computers, the most famous of which is the slide rule. Even then you can go back to the 19th century Jacquard Loom, which even has a solution to \"Hello World\" on Rosetta Code ( [LINK] ) Then you have specialty machines such as the Fairlight CMI synthesizer with the lightpen interface ( [LINK] ) which really laid the groundwork of most DAWs to this day. For music there's lots of things. The theremin which you all know about but also things like Nikolai Voinov's \"Paper Sound\" [L"},{"id":23343449,"author":"skrtskrt","month":"2020-05","story":"The Vintage Beauty of Soviet Control Rooms (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Shows step-by-step switches still in service at AT&T.","quote":"we were told AT&T still has a handful of these in service in the US.","text":"Worked at a telecom API provider and we were told AT&T still has a handful of these in service in the US. [VIDEO]"}]},"yFVXsjVdvmY":{"wy":"Mentioned whenever the optimal stopping or secretary problem comes up in relation to choosing a partner.","q":{"x":"Her ted talk about the math of love is so great.","i":49048559,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":49048559,"author":"balls187","month":"2026-07","story":"Hannah Fry Wins the Leelavati Prize in 2026 for Mathematics Outreach","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hannah Fry's math of love talk","quote":"Her ted talk about the math of love is so great.","text":"Her ted talk about the math of love is so great. [VIDEO] And when she straight laughs in Michael Steven's face about the price of a pencil. [LINK]"},{"id":29505487,"author":"nighthawk454","month":"2021-12","story":"Optimizing Your Wife","stance":"reference","why":"Hannah Fry covers the same stopping problem","quote":"Hannah Fry has a TED Talk covers this topic as well","text":"Hannah Fry has a TED Talk covers this topic as well: [VIDEO] In this framing, it's based on time/age instead of number of candidates, so you don't need to know N."},{"id":28200651,"author":"devoutsalsa","month":"2021-08","story":"A Programmer’s Introduction to Mathematics","stance":"reference","why":"Hannah Fry on picking a partner mathematically","quote":"This reminds me of Hannah Fry","text":"This reminds me of Hannah Fry’s TED talk on the mathematics of love, specifically point #2 on how to pick the perfect partner. [1] It was basically about not committing to the very first person you meet, but also not searching for the perfect person in perpetuity. To paraphrase, you should pick reject the first two people that come along, and then commit to the first person that is better than everyone you previously dated. It’s not a perfect similarity, but I think the point of making a good faith effort a couple times and then really going for it once you understand a little bit makes sense. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["u6XAPnuFjJc",1]]},"z9quxZsLcfo":{"wy":"Recommended in TDD and unit-testing threads as a balanced debate among three prominent developers that avoids dogma.","q":{"x":"I can't recommend the discussion between Kent Beck, David Hansson and Martin Fowler enough.","i":9085876,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":9085876,"author":"ollysb","month":"2015-02","story":"Ask HN: How do I start with test driven development?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Beck, Hansson and Fowler discuss where TDD fits and how to mock.","quote":"I can't recommend the discussion between Kent Beck, David Hansson and Martin Fowler enough.","text":"I can't recommend the discussion between Kent Beck, David Hansson and Martin Fowler enough. They look at how and where it should be used and in particular discuss their approach to mocking which I feel is an often misunderstood tool. Part 1 - [VIDEO] Part 2 - [LINK] Part 3 - [LINK] Part 4/5 - [LINK]"},{"id":31776360,"author":"MarquesMa","month":"2022-06","story":"Ask HN: In these uncertain times, how do you handle anxiousness","stance":"reference","why":"Debate on TDD cited for the idea that unit tests give confidence.","quote":"Unit tests make you confident. You write it, you forget it. It makes you sleep at night.","text":"It says that \"Nintendo\" or \"任天堂\" the brand name refers to an old Chinese saying \"謀事在人，成事在天\" which means \"Man proposes, God disposes\", or \"do your best and leave it to the fate\". The idea is not far from the stoic ones - try your best and stop worrying. It's like an idea from this classic debate on TDD [0]: Unit tests make you confident. You write it, you forget it. It makes you sleep at night. In my first few years of my career, I was constantly and unconsciously thinking about the code, the business requirement, the team, even outside the working hours. That stressed and drained me a lot. In the end I realized I shouldn't have done that. If I am to worry about those things, I just book myself a calendar and go over those things within the time-box. If I need more time, I book another time block for the next day - as simple as that. Now write that \"unit test\", forget it, and sleep well at night. If you really need to worry about them, you should have a \"quarterly/monthly worrying day\" to reflect about your life, your career, strategies and all that. Outside that day, you just live your happy life. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30942593,"author":"dhzhzjsbevs","month":"2022-04","story":"Unit Testing is Overrated (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Hot take. Unit testing is not overrated, if you feel this way you likely just suck at optimising your suite for high RoI tests or chase some stupid metric like code coverage getting mad when you find out you wasted a bunch of time writing worthless tests. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18671284,"author":"ailideex","month":"2018-12","story":"Ask HN: What prominent and high profile companies use TDD?","stance":"reference","why":"Fowler admits TDD does not suit much of his current programming.","quote":"there are problems where TDD doesn't work terribly well","text":"I'm not sure I buy this. Quote from Martin Fowler: [VIDEO] > I share with with David and Kent, there are problems where TDD doesn't work terribly well, and sadly most of the programming I do these days is not conducive to TDD and I miss it because actually I really like the TDD flow that really works for me just as it works the Kent."},{"id":7739796,"author":"ChrisGaudreau","month":"2014-05","story":"The DHH Problem","stance":"reference","why":"DHH, Fowler and Beck debate where TDD helps or hurts design.","quote":"If you watch the conversations between DHH, Martin Fowler, and Kent Beck, it seems to me that they all agree","text":"> Because DHH is just one person. And he only has DHH's experiences. Yep. Uncle Bob is one person, too. tl;dr Think for yourself and don't listen to somebody just because they hurt your feelings. > To pick a topical example: Is TDD hard in Rails apps because TDD is dead or because Rails makes TDD hard? This is a misrepresentation of what DHH has said [1], which is that strict adherence to TDD can make your designs worse: > Over the years, the test-first rhetoric got louder and angrier, though. More mean-spirited. And at times I got sucked into that fundamentalist vortex, feeling bad about not following the true gospel. Then I'd try test-first for a few weeks, only to drop it again when it started hurting my designs. > Used as a hammer to beat down the nonbelievers, declare them unprofessional and unfit for writing software. A litmus test. If you watch the conversations between DHH, Martin Fowler, and Kent Beck, it seems to me that they all agree: testing is good, test-driven design can be bad, and mocking everything is bad [2][3]. I took some time last week to re-read Uncle Bob's book, Agile Software Development, Principles, Patterns, and Practices [4], and Kent Beck's book on TDD [5]. There was a huge difference: Uncle Bob focuses on test-driven design , while Kent Beck focuses on test-driven development . Uncle Bob claims that test-driven development (especially through mocks) universally improves your design, because it reduces coupling and enables refactoring. Kent Beck t"}]},"1ZTJ9omXOQ0":{"wy":"HN cites it as the authoritative statement of Carbon's memory safety plans, including its borrow-checker-like direction.","q":{"x":"Talks like better reflect more recent plans around this topic.","i":42238380,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":42238380,"author":"josh11b","month":"2024-11","story":"The two factions of C++","stance":"rec","why":"Reflects recent Carbon plans around memory safety, per a Carbon developer.","quote":"Talks like better reflect more recent plans around this topic.","text":"(Carbon lang dev here.) Carbon is intended to be memory safe! (Not sure whether you intended to write a double negative there.) There are a few reasons that might not be clear: * Carbon has relatively few people working on it. We currently are prioritizing work on the compiler at the moment, and don't yet have the bandwidth to also work on the safety design. * As part of our migration-from-C++ story, where we expect code to transition C++ -> unsafe Carbon -> safe Carbon, we plan on supporting unsafe Carbon code with reasonable ergonomics. * Carbon's original focus was on evolvability, and didn't focus on safety specifically. Since then it has become clear that memory safety is a requirement for Carbon's success, and will be our first test of those evolvability goals. Talks like [VIDEO] better reflect more recent plans around this topic."},{"id":40555356,"author":"Vt71fcAqt7","month":"2024-06","story":"Circle C++ with memory safety","stance":"reference","why":"Recent talk by lead developer on Carbon's planned borrow checker.","quote":"From a recent talk[0][1] by one of the lead developers","text":">Carbon wants to ship a finished working Carbon language, then bolt on safety (somehow) but, only for some things, not data races for example, so, you still don't actually have Rust's Memory Safety I'm not sure this is correct. As I understand it, Carbon's plan is to add a borrow checker like Rust's. From a recent talk[0][1] by one of the lead developers: >Best candidate for C++ is likely similar to Rust’s borrow checker [0] slides: [LINK] [1] relevant timestamps: [VIDEO] [VIDEO]"},{"id":40555721,"author":"Vt71fcAqt7","month":"2024-06","story":"Circle C++ with memory safety","stance":"reference","why":"Slide in the talk confirming the earlier point.","quote":"","text":"You're right. In fact it was in the previous slide[0] from that same talk. Thanks for pointing that out. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39644374,"author":"Vt71fcAqt7","month":"2024-03","story":"Secure by Design: Google's Perspective on Memory Safety","stance":"reference","why":"Talk states Carbon's best safety candidate resembles Rust's borrow checker.","quote":"It is stated clearly in the talk that this is true for Carbon as well","text":"Unless something has chnaged since this talk[0][1], I don't think that is accurate. >Best candidate for C++ is likely similar to Rust’s borrow checker It is stated clearly in the talk that this is true for Carbon as well [0] [LINK] [1] relevant timestamps: [VIDEO] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38141371,"author":"synergy20","month":"2023-11","story":"Toward a TypeScript for C++\"","stance":"reference","why":"Carbon's plan for memory safety and C++ interop.","quote":"Now it also wants to be a memory safe","text":"After the talk, the activity on it is nearly zero since then, in a world where ideas are cheap, show me the code, this does not seem promising to me. On the other hand, carbon has been very actively developed since its announcement, which also claims to be a typescript-for-c++. Now it also wants to be a memory safe: [VIDEO] I'm forced to learn rust but I hope carbon will take off ASAP so I can be more productive and my (carbon) code can interface with c/c++ code easier than rust."},{"id":38448988,"author":"raphlinus","month":"2023-11","story":"C++ Memory Model: Migrating from x86 to ARM","stance":"reference","why":"Carbon roadmap targeting single-threaded memory safety.","quote":"","text":"I haven't carefully read the relevant part of the spec, but I imagine that pointers have to be modeled as not subject to tearing, otherwise safety guarantees would completely fall apart. The flip side of this is that adding \"fat pointers\" safely to Java would be very difficult. Fat pointers are widely used in languages such as Go, Rust, and Swift to represent both slices (pointer + length) and various forms of dynamic dispatch (pointer + vtable). In Rust, this is safe because data races are forbidden, enforced by the type system. I consider Go and Rust to be \"safe-ish\" in that data races can happen, and can violate safety. Apparently this hasn't led to huge problems in Go. Thus, the roadmap for Carbon[1] targets a comparable level of safety, not trying to prevent data races, but aiming for memory safety in single threaded contexts. [1]: [VIDEO]"}]},"3AtBE9BOvvk":{"wy":"Mentioned as a delightful stunt whenever emoji input or keyboards come up.","q":{"x":"emoji-ized keyboards like Tom Scott did (as a stunt)","i":20860315,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":23160681,"author":"jedimastert","month":"2020-05","story":"In Praise of AutoHotKey","stance":"rec","why":"Every emoji on 14 keyboards with hand-placed stickers","quote":"it's beautiful.","text":"Fun AutoHotKey abuse: Tom Scott's emoji keyboard[0]. It's every single emoji (at the time of recording) on 14 full keyboards with 1,000s of hand-placed bespoke stickers. He goes into more detail here[1], but it's beautiful. [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK]"},{"id":41329317,"author":"Bjartr","month":"2024-08","story":"A symbol for the Fediverse","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"There's no* emoji keyboards, but people use emoji all the time.","text":"Or if editors swap ***, :fediverse:, etc. for it automatically. There's no* emoji keyboards, but people use emoji all the time. * Well, there is, but there isn't a widely available consumer product [VIDEO]"},{"id":20860315,"author":"chiph","month":"2019-09","story":"Why the Emoji Was Inevitable (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Tom Scott's stunt emoji keyboard built from many keyboards","quote":"emoji-ized keyboards like Tom Scott did (as a stunt)","text":"So you just litter your desk with about a dozen emoji-ized keyboards like Tom Scott did (as a stunt) [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["8PmM6SUn7Es",1],["Oo0jm1PPRuo",1],["GsyhGHUEt-k",1],["XGo4PJd1lng",1],["lIFE7h3m40U",1],["Fy0aCDmgnxg",1],["i70wkxmumAw",1],["LnzuMJLZRdU",1],["WZskjLq040I",1],["aVwxzDHniEw",1],["jGWFg7EDnyY",1],["goePYJ74Ydg",1],["OHPkdMGI6D4",1],["zwAD6dRSVyI",1]]},"CAOnSbDSaOw":{"wy":"HN cites it as a real academic source to back up or correct claims about emotion, anger and anxiety in the brain.","q":{"x":"Good presentations.","i":19251444,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19251444,"author":"mattdeboard","month":"2019-02","story":"Practical Facts about the Human Brain","stance":"rec","why":"Academic lecture clarifying brain claims.","quote":"Also an actual academic lecture on the Limbic System which contextualizes & clarifies some of the other assertions","text":"The \"active amygdala = no empathy\" point does not jibe with my lay understanding of the physical phenomenon of empathy. That's the only one that I don't believe models our physiology in a useful way. The rest match up with some of the stuff I've read/watched. Watch some videos by Robert Sapolsky on YouTube. He's a neurostuff prof at Stanford. Good presentations. There's \"Why Zebras Don't Get Ulcers\" [LINK] Also an actual academic lecture on the Limbic System which contextualizes & clarifies some of the other assertions in the OP blog post. [VIDEO] There are more but hopefully YouTube's algorithm helps you out there. The Stanford lecture series is very interesting though Sapolsky doesn't deliver all the lectures. edit: The only plausible, good faith explanation of the amygdala-empathy claim I can think of is that there are facial recognition studies designed to activate the amygdala [LINK] that showed some correlation between anxiety levels and ACC-amygdala connectivity. The ACC, or anterior cingulate cortex, is held to be responsible for empathy. So it may be an interpretation of that knowledge the author is talking about. I know that facial recognition tests are used for evaluation of empathy, sexual attraction and so on. This is probably the kernel of accuracy in the claim. Or there may be other science on the topic that reaches a conclusion closer to what the author said. The linked study is extremely interesting read."},{"id":26774959,"author":"h0l0cube","month":"2021-04","story":"Anxiety is in your body, not your mind","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Valium relaxing muscles.","quote":"Source here, happy to hear from another domain expert refuting this","text":"The argument makes more sense when you realise that Valium/Diazepam is basically a muscle relaxant. Edit: Source here, happy to hear from another domain expert refuting this [VIDEO]"},{"id":26775397,"author":"h0l0cube","month":"2021-04","story":"Anxiety is in your body, not your mind","stance":"reference","why":"Sapolsky on Valium and muscle tone in anxiety.","quote":"got this from Robert Sapolsky (Stanford neurobiology professor)","text":"Maybe it's not strictly true, but got this from Robert Sapolsky (Stanford neurobiology professor): [VIDEO] His opinion, or the best understandings on this, might have changed though. From the YT transcript: > Here's somebody taking Valium for anxiety, and here's somebody taking Valium to relax their muscles. What's up with that? It's the exact same thing. And it is a very James-Lange moment. What's part of anxiety about? It's monitoring the level of tension in your body. It's getting feedback from muscle tone. He seems to be suggesting that the mode of action for Valium to reduce anxiety is that of reducing muscle tone. Not an expert, so I can't argue about it."},{"id":19404255,"author":"maire","month":"2019-03","story":"How Inuit parents teach kids to control their anger","stance":"reference","why":"Sapolsky on anger being chemical.","quote":"See Sapolsky's discussion of the Limbic system","text":"Anger is chemical and not psychological and will pass. See Sapolsky's discussion of the Limbic system: [VIDEO] When I am angry I am self aware enough to wait until the chemicals are gone in order to discuss things. I don't think this is unhealthy in any way. In fact, I usually don't like the feeling of anger and look forward with happiness to the calm that will follow. It is interesting that the original source of anger becomes resolved once the chemicals are gone. I found it interesting that the Inuits do the same thing."}],"r":[["D9H9qTdserM",1]]},"EtX7WnFhxtQ":{"wy":"HN points to it as a detailed production example of mesh shaders and GPU-driven rendering, though some find it daunting.","q":{"x":"A related talk of using Mesh shaders for Alan Wake 2","i":41839516,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41839516,"author":"dagmx","month":"2024-10","story":"Mesh Shaders – The Future of Rendering [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Related talk on mesh shaders in Alan Wake 2","quote":"A related talk of using Mesh shaders for Alan Wake 2","text":"A related talk of using Mesh shaders for Alan Wake 2 [VIDEO] And I find Apple’s intro to mesh shading to be a fairly good primer for someone unfamiliar with Mesh shading [LINK]"},{"id":41848842,"author":"neomantra","month":"2024-10","story":"Drawing Graphics on Apple Vision with the Metal Rendering API","stance":"rec","why":"Good explanation and demo of mesh shading","quote":"","text":"This is a great read, thank you for sharing! I learned a lot from reading it and I'm pretty experienced in this space! I enjoyed checking out your other work too. OPs article heavily covers Vertex Amplification. I didn't realize VisionOS used Vertex Amplification for this, but it makes sense since the hardware supports it. For those interested in Mesh Shading (a few good videos released this week [1][2][3]), Vertex Amplification is a key tech for Mesh Shading, where one writes Object/Mesh functions (names in Metal API, called Task/Mesh/Amplification shaders in other APIs). Introduced by Nvidia in 2018 and only really available en masse for the last couple years, Vertex Amplification was the first time a GPU could create vertices (or \"destroy\" them by not emitting anything), versus the fixed mesh inputs. It's so cool and powerful and a different way of thinking about the pipeline. This article shows the same concept, but in Vertex Shaders for multiple render targets. While you might not make a Vision app, it could be worthwhile read to further understand this architecture. I've spent a few months in Metal Mesh Shading and hadn't realized this application of it at all. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO] (good explanation and demo)"},{"id":44244367,"author":"flashblaze","month":"2025-06","story":"Ask HN: What cool skill or project interests you, but feels out of reach?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of advanced talk that feels out of reach","quote":"Watching these kinds of talks I realize, I'm not built for this","text":"Definitely reverse engineering and shaders. Watching these kinds of talks I realize, I'm not built for this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42366675,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2024-12","story":"Pat Gelsinger was wrong for Intel","stance":"reference","why":"Example of GPU-driven rendering with mesh shaders","quote":"","text":"For example OTOY uses CUDA renderering. [LINK] If you prefer actual code, [LINK] and \"Mesh Shaders - The Future of Rendering\" [LINK] \"GPU driven Rendering with Mesh Shaders in Alan Wake 2\" [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Kr3quGh7pJA",1]]},"FlApbxLz6pA":{"wy":"HN uses it as the standard reference for how the Netherlands solves cyclist and driver conflicts at intersections.","q":{"x":"here is this dutch video showing that exact design in use - today -","i":8299114,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":10078307,"author":"IkmoIkmo","month":"2015-08","story":"Münster’s iconic ‘waste of space’ photo keeps on giving","stance":"rec","why":"Short intro to Dutch junction design","quote":"There's quite a few interesting videos on traffic design in the Netherlands, junction design is a big topic, as well as separating lanes.","text":"Welcome :) There's quite a few interesting videos on traffic design in the Netherlands, junction design is a big topic, as well as separating lanes. Videos like these might be fun to watch: [VIDEO] If it piques your interest, there's more comprehensive videos too like this one which is still only 5 minutes: [LINK]"},{"id":31850587,"author":"jmyeet","month":"2022-06","story":"Investigating the effects of bike lanes in Minneapolis","stance":"reference","why":"Dutch-style intersections as improvement for cyclist safety","quote":"Netherlands style intersections designed to improve cyclist safety","text":"People not familiar with the US may not realize just how hostile US cities are to cyclists (and pedestrians). Some examples: 1. Turning right at red lights. Legal in most of the US. This is a huge safety issue for pedestrians and cyclists; 2. Many streets don't even have widewalks; 3. Many major roads don't even have any form of pedestrian crossings. For example, I had Google Maps tell me to just run across an 8 lane highway (at least there was somewhere safe to stand in the middle) where the speed limit was 45mph. Why? There was absolutely no alternative. In comparison, many freeways or highways in other countries will regularly have dedicated pedestrian crossing bridges. 4. In cities with a dedicated cycling lane in many US cities it gets used as extra parking, forcing cyclists out onto the road. Some I have sympathy for (eg delivery drivers) but many other people just don't give a shit they're blocking a lane of traffic and creating a safety issue. 5. Many Americans never walk anywhere so are an annoyance (if not an outright safety hazard) on any space shared with cyclists. I routinely see runners refuse to get out of the way in a dedicated cycle path . Many are oblivious and will just walk onto a cycle path without looking. I've had many near-misses this way. So there are a lot of things you can do to improve this situation, most of which doesn't take exttra room. For example: 1. Netherlands style intersections designed to improve cyclist safety [1]; 2. Suburbia is domina"},{"id":25412069,"author":"Muximize","month":"2020-12","story":"Sharrows, the bicycle infrastructure that doesn’t work and nobody wants","stance":"reference","why":"Dutch intersections as a solved problem Copenhagen could learn from","quote":"It's one of the things Copenhagen can learn from the Netherlands, where it's a solved problem at most intersections","text":"It's one of the things Copenhagen can learn from the Netherlands, where it's a solved problem at most intersections: [LINK] [VIDEO] Also see [LINK] from 6 minutes on for right turns in Copenhagen."},{"id":20098413,"author":"Xylakant","month":"2019-06","story":"Roads with protected bike lanes make both cycling and driving safer: study","stance":"reference","why":"Dutch intersections where bike and car lanes meet at right angles","quote":"Intersections of car lanes and bike lines should also be designed in such a fashion that the lanes meet mostly at a right angle.","text":"The Dutch have that figured out, too. There’s so many bicycles around that you practically have to expect them everywhere any time. More bicycles make all bicycles safer. Intersections of car lanes and bike lines should also be designed in such a fashion that the lanes meet mostly at a right angle. Sharp turns and buffer spaces. A sharp turn slows the car traffic and the right angle at the intersecting point makes it easy to check for crossing (cycle) traffic. See for example this video [VIDEO]"},{"id":14591373,"author":"bane","month":"2017-06","story":"Why is cycling so popular in the Netherlands?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Dutch mixed-mode junction design","quote":"Lots of thought also into mixed-mode junction design","text":"The impression I got from Amsterdam is that the city (and by extension the Dutch) has simply accepted all the behaviors that cyclists will do in a less cyclist friendly environment (run stop signs, ignore pedestrians, turn without signing and so on), and then just built infrastructure to support that natural behavior. As a pedestrian unfamiliar with the city, at first I found it hugely annoying that so little space was left to pedestrians, but once I figured out where not to stand, I started to have a much better appreciation for how it contributes the functioning of the city. In fact, I was kind of surprised at how little boat traffic there is these days. Having read and watched some videos on how the Netherlands does road planning for safety, the approach seems to be a much more \"descriptive\" approach to adapting road shapes and forms and cycle lanes to the natural behavior of people using those modes of transportation. For example, squeezing the width of lanes down naturally causes car drivers to slow rather than covering the area with signs telling drivers to slow down. This approach is slowly being adopted elsewhere ( [LINK] ). Lots of thought also into mixed-mode junction design [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14427806,"author":"raarts","month":"2017-05","story":"Do We Overvalue Bike Lanes?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Dutch road safety design","quote":"OP makes some good points but should go on vacation in the Netherlands. Way ahead in road safety.","text":"OP makes some good points but should go on vacation in the Netherlands. Way ahead in road safety. For example: [VIDEO] Or more extensively: [LINK]"},{"id":13207338,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2016-12","story":"How Sweden Has Redesigned Streets to Route Around Bad Behavior","stance":"reference","why":"Dutch solution for safe cyclist and driver intersections","quote":"Well guess what, we've figured this out decades ago in the Netherlands","text":"As a Dutchman living in Stockholm I can tell you one thing: they should fix their goddamn bike lanes. Sure, they have them, which makes them better than 90% of the rest of the world. They're still shit. There's no separation of car- and bike traffic. To turn left a cyclist first has to cross parallel car traffic, making this unsafer for cyclists (and annoying for the people in cars, for that matter). Key issues: - the cyclist and driver should meet at a (near) 90 degree angle, so neither requires eyes in the back of their head - separate the goddamn lanes so neither car nor cyclist feels uncomfortably close to the other - don't require extra space because every square meter counts in a big city Well guess what, we've figured this out decades ago in the Netherlands: [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":11261843,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2016-03","story":"The Bike Wars Are Over, and the Bikes Won","stance":"reference","why":"Example of roads designed to accommodate all users","quote":"You can choose to design them in a way that accomodates all[1].","text":"As a Dutchman, the first two paragraphs were quite infuriating to read; a list of arguments similar to the now-classic \"Schödinger's immigrant\"-joke (\"simultaneously too lazy to work and stealing your job\"). > Well, no wonder: Roads are built for buses, cars, and trucks, not for people on bikes. Well good to see that the 1920s auto-industry propaganda[0] still has it's firm grip on American society! Roads were not made for cars, the cars claimed the roads once they got on the scene. You can choose to design them in a way that accomodates all[1]. The only reason I'd hate leaving the Netherlands is because no other country comes close in bike-friendliness at the moment (simply put: good bike infrastructure is so common it's barely noticed by the locals, because it's the norm[2]). I do hope that changes soon. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":8299114,"author":"arjunnarayan","month":"2014-09","story":"Bike lanes have sped up car traffic in New York City","stance":"reference","why":"Dutch video showing the protected intersection design already in use","quote":"here is this dutch video showing that exact design in use - today -","text":"This site is brilliant, and thank you for that link. I followed some of the citations. The video on the site makes it seem like it's an experimental idea, but to be clear, here is this dutch video showing that exact design in use - today - [VIDEO] I can only dream of living in a place where I could bike with such safety. I regularly get honked at/shouted at in Philadelphia --- for biking in bike lanes, because, apparently, drivers in Philly believe that it is their sovereign right to drive in green painted bike lanes when they wish to turn right. Maybe I should revise my \"move to Canada\" life plan, and make it \"move to Amsterdam\"."},{"id":5540875,"author":"maurits","month":"2013-04","story":"Electric Velomobiles: fast, comfortable, and more efficient than cars","stance":"reference","why":"Dutch intersection design compared with the two-stage turn","quote":"Compare the 2-stage turn with the dutch design","text":"In short, build bike lanes like the dutch. [1] Big, physically separated, omnipresent bike lines. Ensure that bikes are first class citizens on intersections. Compare the 2-stage turn with the dutch design [2] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":4594452,"author":"maurits","month":"2012-09","story":"To Encourage Biking, Cities Lose the Helmets","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Dutch intersection design with separated lanes and no lane crossing","quote":"This how the dutch design an intersection. Note the absence of the ridiculous ASL boxes, and most important, no crossing of lanes.","text":"To Encourage Biking, Cities should do two things, and two things only: - Bike lanes. Physically separated bike lanes. This is not rocket science. Trains have rails, airplanes have a nice bit of tarmac and bikes should have a space tailored to their specific needs as well. Typically in the Netherlands, bikes are banned from roads that are 50km/h and up. And they don't need to battle it out with fast traffic, for there are bike lanes. And the limited space in this country means that something truly has to give when adding them. This how the dutch design an intersection. Note the absence of the ridiculous ASL boxes, and most important, no crossing of lanes. [VIDEO] - Traffic laws that protect the weaker participant in traffic. Again, case in point, the Netherlands where in a (civil) dispute after a bike-car accident the burden of proof lies with the car. Not to mention the dreaded article 5 that broadly states that endangering traffic is a felony. This helmet discussion imho is just a way to deviate from arguably more costly and difficult choices when it comes to embracing the bicycle. And while we are are at it. I am convinced that making helmets, 4 point harnesses and leathers mandatory in cars for all occupants would save lives as well. What perhaps surprises me the most in the car-bike entanglement is the seemingly bad engineering. Cities like Londen, Geneva, all (claim) to cater for bikes, have rental programs, paint the odd lane, but their intersections tell a different st"}],"r":[["GFosQtEqzSE",1],["XpQMgbDJPok",1],["pX8BXH3SJn0",1]]},"GyFkWb903aU":{"wy":"HN links it as a balanced, full-length interview with a leading alignment researcher on AI risk.","q":{"x":"Also this whole interview was pretty interesting.","i":35894316,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35894316,"author":"barking_biscuit","month":"2023-05","story":"Hugging Face Releases Agents","stance":"rec","why":"Balanced take on AI risk from Christiano","quote":"Also this whole interview was pretty interesting.","text":">Do you have a counterexample of someone who's gotten their predictions right? Because if not, that should only terrify you even more. If there's anyone out there who predicted how LLMs would work in a way Eliezer failed to, and if that person is predicting \"AGI will be cool and will naturally prioritize our well-being\", I would love to know. A recent interview with Paul Christiano is about the closest I've come to this. He does note some semi-accurate predictions at the linked timestamp, but the forecast for how things are likely to go is not exactly rosy, though he's quite a bit more optimistic than Eliezer. [VIDEO] Also this whole interview was pretty interesting. Near the end he details how few people world-wide actually work on X-risk from AGI. He also outlines how the academic ML community in general just continually keeps getting predictions really wrong, and many aren't taking X-risk seriously. Overall his is the most balanced take I've seen. A lot better than Eliezer."},{"id":38415841,"author":"mitthrowaway2","month":"2023-11","story":"Cicero","stance":"reference","why":"Christiano describing AI takeover as a coup","quote":"Christiano says that an AI takeover might follow the dynamics of a coup","text":"I somewhat misremembered; it looks like his point is less about mutual trust and more about supporting whoever has control of reward channels. It starts here where Christiano says that an AI takeover might follow the dynamics of a coup: [VIDEO] And goes into more detail here: [VIDEO] \"Suppose that I've been tasked with helping defend you from some other AIs.... My job is, someone is coming to hack your computer and I'm supposed to help defend you. Supposed to help improve your security situation, whatever. And I'm wondering, what is it I could do that will get me a high reward. And one thing I could do that will get me a high reward is actually helping defend your computer, doing the task you actually asked me to do. But another way I can get a high reward is by saying at the end of the day what actually matters is just how you measure my performance. And your measurements of my performance ultimately are just entering some numbers into a dataset somewhere, something a computer says about how well I did. And it would really be much better if I were to just work with this AI who is attempting to attack you and say hey, AI who is invading, you know what, if you just help me, and we both make it look like I did a really good job, like I win, you win because you got the person's stuff; I'm going to get a really high rating because all the numbers that are going to be entered in the dataset are going to be really high, this is a win-win, everyone is happy.\" \"In some sense what all"},{"id":35715134,"author":"CharlesW","month":"2023-04","story":"“I think [AI] is the single most likely reason that I will personally die.”","stance":"reference","why":"Full interview with Paul Christiano on AI risk","quote":"","text":"This quote is by Dr. Paul Christiano, PhD Inventor of OpenAI's Deep Reinforcement Learning from Human Preferences (RHLF). Start of thread: [LINK] Full video, \"How to Solve AI Alignment with Paul Christiano\": [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["4-SGpEInX_c",1]]},"Hdpf-MQM9vY":{"wy":"Shared as a striking example of what prefab modular construction can achieve in speed.","q":{"x":"Super impressive timelapse","i":5935239,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":5935239,"author":"platypii","month":"2013-06","story":"Razor Thin Margins Make Construction Ripe for Innovation","stance":"rec","why":"Timelapse of a 30-story building in 15 days","quote":"Super impressive timelapse","text":"Something like this, where they put up a 30-story building in 15 days in China. Super impressive timelapse: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9934187,"author":"reustle","month":"2015-07","story":"On Full-Stack Startups","stance":"reference","why":"Tangential construction timelapse in a thread about full-stack startups","quote":"","text":"This format is painful to read, so I'm copying the content here. This is a series of tweets by [LINK] -- If you are using software to go after a legacy physical vertical, we are most excited if you do it \"full stack\" [0]. With @counsyl, we found that one cannot live by bioinformatics alone. So built a full clinical lab + software + national salesforce. Here is a video of our robotic genome factory: Also: insurance coverage, clearances, clinical integration... [1] The reason to do full stack is that it's hard to replace just one layer of an outdated legacy stack. CAC & integration costs can kill. For example, consider \"restaurant powered by technology\" vs \"tech for restaurants\". With code, reinvent industry and make better margins. You cannot automate something till you've done it manually many times. You cannot abstract an API till you've seen many specific examples. Need to build a site of the scale of [LINK] - and encounter all the problems - before you could do [LINK] . Reinventing agriculture? Build new kind of farm, w/ solar panels & self-driving tractors. Control all factors, show that code cuts costs. More examples of full stack startups.. Education: [2], Autos: [3], Hospitality: [4] By doing it end-to-end, you see things. First, sometimes a good reason beyond tradition (eg regulatory, logistical) for legacy practices. Second, your control of adjacent layers may eliminate the initial problem you were trying to solve. Analogous to: [5] Third, solving whole problem build"},{"id":4808030,"author":"ChuckMcM","month":"2012-11","story":"World's tallest skyscraper to be built in just 90 days","stance":"reference","why":"Timelapse showing prefabricated building erection","quote":"That part consists entirely of lifting pre-fabricated sections in place","text":"Actually not BS, see the Broad Group's web site and their timelapse of the 'demo' project [1]. They don't count the time to put in the foundation and drive the pilings, just the erection part. That part consists entirely of lifting pre-fabricated sections in place, connecting pipes and wires, welding to the rest of the building and moving on to the next one. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"MkeS_0NQUZs":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as a concrete example of police seizing cash from innocent people on flimsy pretexts.","q":{"x":"They have so many heart-breaking stories on their youtube channel!","i":30390526,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30390526,"author":"iliketrains","month":"2022-02","story":"Amazon sent the FBI to take my family’s bank accounts","stance":"rec","why":"Cops seizing cash during a traffic stop despite withdrawal receipts","quote":"They have so many heart-breaking stories on their youtube channel!","text":"Recently I've found the Institute for Justice, a nonprofit that among other things fights to end civil asset forfeiture practices. They have so many heart-breaking stories on their youtube channel! For example, did you know that cops can seize all your cash during a traffic stop, regardless of you having receipts of withdrawal? See [VIDEO] Or, government took all of a family’s money (incl. from bank accounts of two teenage daughters) based on mere suspicion that one family member committed a crime: [LINK]"},{"id":32844922,"author":"yellowapple","month":"2022-09","story":"Increase: Banking API","stance":"reference","why":"Example of police seizing cash on flimsy pretext","quote":"Cops have used cocaine residue as \"cause\" to rob people of cash","text":"> It'll def just be ignored, as it's on a high percentage of notes[1] for any transaction anyways [2]. Cops have used cocaine residue as \"cause\" to rob people of cash: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29849187,"author":"notch656a","month":"2022-01","story":"Mozilla Foundation pausing cryptocurrency donations","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":">This is a garden-variety description of egotistical selfishness Yes, I selfishly didn't want to put a gun to the head of the individual who doesn't want to pay a tax to build a gigantic wall to keep out a former president's perceived boogey-man, the Mexicans. I'm selfish that way. You'd have fit in great on the slave patrols, calling those running the underground railroad egoistical law-breakers who violate \"our shared morality and conception of fairness\". Yes Yes we both have a clear picture of each other, don't we? I understand democracy very well. Two of three children vote to take the third child's marble, that's democracy. The majority whites vote to have minority blacks as their slave, that's democracy. Most Americans support legalizing pot? Nah fuck it we'll keep it illegal anyway; that's American democracy. >you are fundamentally an anarchist Wow, you sure found me out. What was it that finally betrayed me, my intense dislike of supporting the tasks that allegedly represent the majority like waterboarding foreign nationals, or my intense disdain for coercion? What if I were gay? Then I'd be double bad. I am an anarchist. Am I supposed to feel bad about that? I don't, it feels great to not have to justify in my head why I support a system that kills thousands of innocent people worldwide in the last couple decades, many of them innocent children whos last moments were concrete ripping apart and tumbling on top of their heads, smothering their family as the bombs colla"}]},"NMq0vncHJvU":{"wy":"Cited in sync-engine and local-first threads for Kleppmann's argument for a generic, standardised sync protocol or service.","q":{"x":"Martin Kleppmann in his talk last year spoke [1] the need for a generic sync protocol, which was very interesting.","i":44143329,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44143329,"author":"rapnie","month":"2025-05","story":"Reverse engineering of Linear's sync engine","stance":"rec","why":"Kleppmann argues for a generic sync protocol","quote":"Martin Kleppmann in his talk last year spoke [1] the need for a generic sync protocol, which was very interesting.","text":"There is a proliferation of sync tools and little standardization. Here some in local-first space [0]. Martin Kleppmann in his talk last year spoke [1] the need for a generic sync protocol, which was very interesting. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":44474450,"author":"samwillis","month":"2025-07","story":"Local-first software (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Kleppmann favors centralised standardised sync engines over decentralisation","quote":"He is a proponent of centralised standardised sync engines that enable the ideals of local-first.","text":"> This is trying to solve a business problem (I can't trust cloud-providers) with a technical trade-off (avoid centralized architecture). I don't think that's quite correct. I think the authors fully acknowledge that the business case for local-first is not complexly solved and is a closely related problem. These issues need both a business and technical solution, and the paper proposes a set of characteristics of what a solution could look like. It's also incorrect to suggest that local-first is an argument for decentralisation - Martin Kleppmann has explicitly stated that he doesn't think decentralised tech solves these issues in a way that could become mass market. He is a proponent of centralised standardised sync engines that enable the ideals of local-first. See his talk from Local-first conf last year: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43795392,"author":"sorrythanks","month":"2025-04","story":"Atuin Desktop: Runbooks That Run","stance":"reference","why":"Defines what local-first actually means","quote":"This is what local-first means","text":"I see, I hear you. That isn't what local-first means, though. This is what local-first means: • [LINK] • [VIDEO]"},{"id":43436611,"author":"rapnie","month":"2025-03","story":"Sync Engines Are the Future","stance":"reference","why":"Kleppmann on need for a generic sync service for local-first","quote":"Martin Kleppmann in one of his recent talks about the future of local-first, mentions the need for a generic sync service","text":"Martin Kleppmann in one of his recent talks about the future of local-first, mentions the need for a generic sync service for the 'local-first end-game' [0] as he calls it. Standardization is needed. Right now everyone and their mother is doing sync differently and building production platforms around their own protocols and mechanisms. [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["2JB1_e5wZmU",1],["uD4izuDMUQA",1],["rC4FCS-oMpg",1]]},"O9upVbGSBFo":{"wy":"Mentioned as a favorite podcast episode and as a source of firsthand computing history, such as timesharing.","q":{"x":"Loved the episodes with Jim Keller [0] and Brian Kernighan [1] as well.","i":25557320,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25557320,"author":"Pandabob","month":"2020-12","story":"Ask HN: Favorite Podcast Episode of 2020?","stance":"rec","why":"Kernighan interview on Lex Fridman's show","quote":"Loved the episodes with Jim Keller [0] and Brian Kernighan [1] as well.","text":"Loved the episodes with Jim Keller [0] and Brian Kernighan [1] as well. Lex really does a great show and has amazing guests. [0]: [LINK] [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32534733,"author":"naves","month":"2022-08","story":"Brian Kernighan adds Unicode support to Awk","stance":"reference","why":"Kernighan's conversation mentions his computers","quote":"as per his conversation with Lex Fridman","text":"Two years ago, he used macOS on a 13\" MacBook Air and an iMac, as per his conversation with Lex Fridman: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26446033,"author":"not_knuth","month":"2021-03","story":"RethinkDB: why we failed (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Kernighan on timesharing history and its link to cloud computing","quote":"Relevant piece of history on timesharing in relation to cloud computing by the legend that is Brian Kernighan","text":"Relevant piece of history on timesharing in relation to cloud computing by the legend that is Brian Kernighan: [VIDEO] (05:40 - 10:50)"}],"r":[["Nb2tebYAaOA",1]]},"QM3W36COnE4":{"wy":"Pointed to as the canonical presentation of compile-time regex in C++ and its template metaprogramming, including benchmark results.","q":{"x":"A 54:47 presentation at CppCon 2018 is worth more than a thousand words","i":45783080,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45783080,"author":"canucker2016","month":"2025-11","story":"The Impossible Optimization, and the Metaprogramming to Achieve It","stance":"rec","why":"Presentation on compile-time regex class in C++ templates","quote":"A 54:47 presentation at CppCon 2018 is worth more than a thousand words","text":"FYI: This is a C++ template version of compile time regex class. A 54:47 presentation at CppCon 2018 is worth more than a thousand words... see [VIDEO] followup CppCon 2019 video at [LINK] As the above github repo mentions, more info at [LINK]"},{"id":37516722,"author":"lcvw","month":"2023-09","story":"Compile time regular expression in C++","stance":"reference","why":"Contains benchmarks of the compile-time regex library","quote":"at 39 minutes she shares the benchmarks.","text":"One of the stated benefits is that the compiler optimizer can optimize the regex state machine that gets created, resulting in significantly faster match times at runtime. There are some benchmarks if you look up her cppcon talk about the library iirc. Edit: here is her talk, at 39 minutes she shares the benchmarks. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24396307,"author":"nikki93","month":"2020-09","story":"C++20 has been approved","stance":"reference","why":"Example of parsing regular expressions at compile time in C++","quote":"Here's an example of parsing regular expressions in C++ at compile time.","text":"> Passing strings into a templates ... wouldn't work in C++ ... [VIDEO] Here's an example of parsing regular expressions in C++ at compile time. Code here: [LINK]"}]},"UTqZNujQOlA":{"wy":"HN cites it as an early demonstration of compile-time code execution, predating its popularity in languages like Zig.","q":{"x":"Here’s an old video where he demonstrated this","i":28120188,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28120188,"author":"codetrotter","month":"2021-08","story":"Constexpr is a Platform (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Early demo of running code during compilation.","quote":"Here’s an old video where he demonstrated this","text":"Jonathan Blow’s programming language allows almost anything to execute during compilation as well. It’s neat. Here’s an old video where he demonstrated this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33104945,"author":"Tozen","month":"2022-10","story":"What Makes the Zig Programming Language Unique?","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence Blow popularized compile-time execution before Zig.","quote":"2014, Jonathan Blow, who made lots of videos and gave lots of talks","text":"> Zig's use of comptime is fairly revolutionary in a c-like language Not revolutionary. Before Zig came out, which was 2016, there was Jonathan Blow talking about and arguably repopularizing it. Lots of people (who knows how many developers) and languages were influenced by him too. 2014, Jonathan Blow, who made lots of videos and gave lots of talks, before going on to officially promote Jai (2016). And it appears he was playing around with prototypes much earlier and talking about it (from 2014). [VIDEO] (Demo: Base language, compile-time execution)"},{"id":17534884,"author":"atesti","month":"2018-07","story":"Compile Time C++ Snake Game (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Demo running a game at compile time.","quote":"Execute a invaders game with OpenGl while compiling and embedding the score in the code","text":"Sounds like what Jonathan Blow demoed in his new programming language JAI: [VIDEO] Execute a invaders game with OpenGl while compiling and embedding the score in the code (Just a demo, the idea is to replace makefiles with compile time execution, etc.)"},{"id":10063658,"author":"zenogais","month":"2015-08","story":"C2 Programming Language","stance":"reference","why":"Blow's language work on compile-time execution.","quote":"Jonathan Blow is working on at least part of this","text":"Jonathan Blow is working on at least part of this (sorry, the only links I could find were to his videos about it) [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["AJ-auWfJTts",1],["2wZ1pCpJUIM",1],["lnnsDi7Sxq0",1],["MtzCLd93SyU",1],["aMcJ1Jvtef0",1]]},"UXZGCwAJpbM":{"wy":"Cited as the evidence that Denuvo DRM measurably hurts game performance.","q":{"x":"The folks at Digital Foundry did a phenomenal job breaking down the specifics, as usual.","i":28317629,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28317629,"author":"smoldesu","month":"2021-08","story":"Silent changes to Western Digital’s budget SSD may lower speeds by up to 50%","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed breakdown of Denuvo's performance impact in Resident Evil Village","quote":"The folks at Digital Foundry did a phenomenal job breaking down the specifics, as usual.","text":"The folks at Digital Foundry did a phenomenal job breaking down the specifics, as usual. I strongly suggest checking out their video if you're interested in the stuff: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39905206,"author":"badsectoracula","month":"2024-04","story":"Bypassing Denuvo in Hogwarts Legacy","stance":"reference","why":"Denuvo version showed worse performance and frame pacing","quote":"they did find Denuvo to both have lesser performance and affect frame pacing[1]","text":"DF's claims isn't that it doesn't have any noticeable effect in performance (in fact in a recent video[0] they claim the opposite when Denuvo was introduced to Assassin's Creed Mirage after reviews were done - it can affect performance and thus because of that introducing it after the fact invalidated the CPU performance metrics reviewers had done) but - at least in the cases i remember - they cannot tell if the issues were due to Denuvo or not. Note though that in a case where they could test both versions, they did find Denuvo to both have lesser performance and affect frame pacing[1] (though that is for a 2yo game, things might be better more recently). [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29152227,"author":"shmde","month":"2021-11","story":"Denuvo-Protected Games Rendered Unplayable After Domain Expires","stance":"reference","why":"Confirms cracked version runs better than Denuvo-protected game","quote":"Resident evil 8 runs better when it is stripped off its denuvo protection. As confirmed by Digital foundry's video.","text":"> Well used, Denuvo is not that bad. Ubisoft admitting that Denuvo ( idk why they used the term third party lol ) causes PC stuttering. [LINK] Resident evil 8 runs better when it is stripped off its denuvo protection. As confirmed by Digital foundry's video. [VIDEO] [LINK] You will now see a huge influx of video games being stripped of denuvo because it literally shits itself in 12th gen Intel chips and games become unplayable with denuvo. > And generally, it only serves to protect the first few months of sales, it is not uncommon to strip out DRM after that. Nope, disagree. The only time they strip Denuvo out is when the game is cracked by warez scenes or if the cost of paying denuvo gets bigger than the people actually paying for it. Some video games which are out for well over an year but just got denuvo removed in the last 2-3 months out of the blue. It is because of denuvos incompatibility with win11. 1.Shadow of Tomb Raider (3 years) 2.Rise of tomb raider (6 years ) 3.Injustice 2 (4years) 4.Jedi fallen order (2years ) 5.Nier replicant. 6. Tekken 7 (6years). > AAA games rely on advertising for that and run close to market saturation so pirate copies are more of an opportunity cost. Trust me when I say people who pirate are usually the ones who never ever buy video games. Pirates can never be considered an opportunity cost. Not trying to glorify pirates, but people waited patiently for over an year to play RDR2 till it got cracked, so that they don't have to pay outta the"}],"r":[["b9IkpUYlOx8",1],["8fi7uSYlOdc",1]]},"V74AxCqOTvg":{"wy":"Recommended as a short leadership talk that commenters have shown to many people.","q":{"x":"I can't count how many people I've shown your leadership talk to","i":6878424,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":6878424,"author":"aidos","month":"2013-12","story":"OK, Milt Olin, I'll start writing again","stance":"rec","why":"Leadership talk the commenter has shown to many people","quote":"I can't count how many people I've shown your leadership talk to","text":"Sorry to hear of the loss of your friend. As a devote cyclist it always hits me hard to hear of another cyclist being injured - especially when it appears as though it was through no fault of their own. Maybe it's not as obvious to you but, I feel, your writing adds some much needed colourful philosophy to our community. As you say yourself, all too often we end up with our heads buried deep in virtual technical problems and forget to embrace the world that surrounds us. I can't count how many people I've shown your leadership talk to [0]. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21999956,"author":"peteforde","month":"2020-01","story":"Pursuing a Business I'll Love","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I found your discussion of the sheet metal concept really interesting because as you found out the hard way, there's an awful lot of behavioural science in play. The line about Word having too many features is that everyone uses 10% of the features... the problem is that everyone uses a slightly different 10% of the features. One thing you should try hard not to do is look at the competition and write it off as crap. It's highly unlikely that your antecedents set out to make a shit product. It's likely that when they were starting out, they wanted to create something efficient and streamlined, but then years of paying customer feedback took things to where they are today. The folks at 37signals always used to remind us that it's much easier to add a feature than it is to remove one. I wonder how much of the shopkeepers reactions were fighting change vs just not trusting someone who didn't come from where they came from. There's real class anxiety around the blue collars vs the 20-something software millionaire set (which is likely how they saw you, as the other, even if you're actually their neighbour). I wonder what would happened differently if it was the owner of the first shop leading the pitch. I often advise founders that they shouldn't get into any business where one of the founders doesn't have deep industry experience and connections. If nobody on your team has ever managed a hotel, it's highly likely that you will fail to disrupt hotel management. Anyhow, it's uncle"},{"id":1959564,"author":"timelinex","month":"2010-12","story":"Defecting by Accident - A Flaw Common to Analytical People","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"hmm... I think many rationals problem is that they don't interact enough and then use their positive social interactions to social proof their bad ones. Consider his first scenario where the person puts up his hand and says that they don't have the budget to do that. The speaker might indeed be mad and likewise some others in the room. Invariably there would be someone there that likes your outspoken or directness[just by the share number of people in the room, probability dictates it]. Now, if our rational guy stands there by himself and refuses to interact with others, the speaker and his allies will brand him as rude. Every move he makes, no matter how trivial would only be seen in this light. If he had started interacting until he found someone who likes his disposition, he is then social proof by that person. Then he begins to climb a ladder of social proof. He would meet someone else who because that one person liked him they would like him too, and you can see how this spreads exponentially. Now, it would be just the speaker left hating him; well no actually, the speaker will now think his evaluation of the situation was previously wrong and that the guy is alright.[Appropriate reading: [LINK] ] And if our rationalist is lucky he can turn his new found social proof into Authority by running for some prestigious position such as president. At which point, people will like him cause he is in that given position. Being nice has it's only problems also. For instance, peopl"}],"r":[["lY8Ol2n4o4A",1],["495nCzxM9PI",1],["ShEez0JkOFw",1],["fNk_zzaMoSs",1]]},"ay-gqx18UTM":{"wy":"Used as the reference example when people argue Windows UI responsiveness regressed after XP.","q":{"x":"Here is a random link about it","i":14740965,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":36904774,"author":"kasabali","month":"2023-07","story":"Kill DWM (on Windows11) for less input lag and better performance","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstrates Windows desktop compositor lag.","quote":"Especially follow the YouTube links demonstrating the problem","text":"Here's a more honest and direct introduction than Microsoft itself could wrote: [LINK] Especially follow the YouTube links demonstrating the problem: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":45450046,"author":"ndiddy","month":"2025-10","story":"Windows 10 refuses to go gentle into that good night","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Windows 7 UI being less responsive than XP.","quote":"I remember when 7 came out and people complained about how the UI was less responsive than XP! Example","text":"I remember when 7 came out and people complained about how the UI was less responsive than XP! Example: [VIDEO] I guess Windows UI responsiveness has just been steadily declining since the early 2000s."},{"id":23733522,"author":"StillBored","month":"2020-07","story":"MaXX Interactive Desktop: A Re-Implementation of the IRIX Interactive Desktop","stance":"reference","why":"Benchmark comparisons of old versus modern Windows GDI performance.","quote":"30 seconds poking around on youtube, should find you a bunch of comparisons like this","text":"While you might be slightly right, my experience tuning windows machines leads me to believe your missing the mark. I'm going to say the three largest contributions to general desktop lag are: Animations and intentional delays. It can't be said, how much faster a machine feels when something like MenuShowDelay is decreased to 0, or the piles of animations are sped up. Too many layers between the application draw commands and the actual display. All this compositing, vsync, and minimal 2d acceleration creates a low level persistent lag. Disabling aero on a win7 machine does wonders to its responsiveness. But even then pre-vista much of the win32 GDI/drawing API was basically implemented in hardware on the GPU. If you get an old 2d win32 API benchmark, you will notice that modern machines don't tend to fare well in raw API call performance. 30 seconds poking around on youtube, should find you a bunch of comparisons like this [VIDEO] . Keep in mind that even in 2020 pretty much every application on the machine is still relying on GDI (same as linux apps relying on xlib). Input+processing lag, USB is polling with a fairly slow poll interval rate (think a hundred or so ms). Combined with the fact that the keystrokes/events then end up queued/scheduled through multiple subsystems before eventually finding their way to the correct window, and then having to again reschedule and get the application to retrieve and process it via GetMessage()/etc. Basically, this is more a function of"},{"id":14740965,"author":"StillBored","month":"2017-07","story":"24-core CPU and I can’t move my mouse","stance":"reference","why":"Shows GDI acceleration loss after XP.","quote":"Here is a random link about it","text":"Actually, its not as accelerated as it was back in the winXP days. Here is a random link about it: [VIDEO] . Basically, the GPU card vendors could hook any part of the win32 GDI pre windows vista, and they did. Post vista, only a tiny portion of the GDI is accelerated. In theory you can avoid this by writing your application using a more modern API, but the vast majority of native windows applications continue to basically be GDI based due to age, or various GUI toolkits still being GDI based. Worse there are a number of toolkits (or browsers) which implement their own drawing routines rather than calling the system supplied ones. The final composited results with aero are of course accelerated, but that only really tends to add additional latency. Switching to one of the basic themes makes win7 noticeably more responsive, but also tends to tear a lot. I've got an incredibly high end desktop machine, carefully tweaked/optimized and I can frequently see it the ~1/10 of a second lags while windows update after being maximized/etc. Compared to the 10 year old pretty high end XP machine (with upgraded SSD/etc, and also carefully tuned) it doesn't seem to be faster on basic desktop type operations. Fire up a recent game, or doing builds its massively faster but running word/firefox/whatever the old machine \"feels\" faster. (tuned, as in I have a dozen or so, tweaks I've been collecting/researching for the past decade+, on ways to make the machine feel more responsive, it all starte"},{"id":6565249,"author":"createaccount0","month":"2013-10","story":"Windows 8.1 now available","stance":"reference","why":"Explains Windows 7+ GUI lag versus XP.","quote":"Probably it's because of this: (\"Windows 7 GUI slowness\")","text":"I tried it on a new computer (Haswell desktop with SSD). But Windows 8.1 is still slower than XP. Even though I disabled all animations I can feel some lag between clicks and response. Probably it's because of this: [VIDEO] (\"Windows 7 GUI slowness\") I'm going back to XP, but I needed to buy a dedicated graphics card because Intel HD doesn't have drivers for XP. And there's also 3GB RAM limit."},{"id":3155854,"author":"testz3453","month":"2011-10","story":"Ten years of Windows XP: how longevity became a curse","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Windows 7 GUI is 5 times slower than XP and has bugs","text":"I will use Windows XP for another 5 years - until new software and hardware will stop working on XP. Windows 7 GUI is 5 times slower than XP and has bugs: [VIDEO] (Windows 7 GUI slowness) [LINK] (Windows 7 GUI slowness: file explorer) Windows 7 uses 5 times as much resources: XP: 233MHz, 64MB RAM, 1.5 GB. 7: 1GHz, 1GB RAM, 16 GB."}]},"fAxHlLK3Oyk":{"wy":"HN cites it as the canonical work behind repeated re-recording experiments, and a pivotal piece of minimalist music worth listening to patiently.","q":{"x":"It's a pivotal piece of minimalist music.","i":38403194,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35413275,"author":"frereubu","month":"2023-04","story":"If Philip Glass Were a Film","stance":"rec","why":"Patient listening pays off in minimalist work","quote":"I'd recommend setting aside the time to listen to these beginning to end if you can.","text":"I love the earlier, even more minimal pieces like Music With Changing Parts: [LINK] And I love the parallels to things like I Am Sitting In A Room by Alving Lucier: [VIDEO] (If you're not familiar with this piece, it might be worth reading about it first - [LINK] ) I hesitate to say this because it depends on your tastes, but I'd recommend setting aside the time to listen to these beginning to end if you can. You'll probably feel a bit fidgety for the first 10 / 15 minutes, but in my view it's really worth persevering because I find the payoffs are worth it."},{"id":38403194,"author":"austinjp","month":"2023-11","story":"I Am Sitting in a Room (1969)","stance":"reference","why":"Recording of the piece, where room resonances replace words","quote":"It's a pivotal piece of minimalist music.","text":"A piece of sound art in which Alvin Lucier repeatedly played back and re-recorded his voice in a room, until his words became completely unintelligible, and only resonant frequencies of the room remained. It's a pivotal piece of minimalist music. Edit to add link to YouTube of the recording: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34050548,"author":"kamiheku","month":"2022-12","story":"Enhance Speech from Adobe – Free AI filter for cleaning up spoken audio","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"That could be a fun exercise, I'd imagine you might end up with something like \"I Am Sitting In A Room\" [0][1] [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24812646,"author":"necubi","month":"2020-10","story":"Teageneration (or: why I don’t trust transporters) (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Classic piece of repeated re-recording until speech dissolves","quote":"a classic work of experimental music","text":"This is like a physical version of \"I Am Sitting In A Room\" [0], a classic work of experimental music. In that piece, Alvin Lucier speaks the words \"I am sitting in a room different from the the one you are in now...\" into a recorder, which is then played back into another recorder, on and on until it becomes unintelligible. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11675248,"author":"thevibesman","month":"2016-05","story":"This Is What Happens When You Re-Upload a YouTube Video 1,000 Times (2010)","stance":"reference","why":"Original Lucier piece that the re-upload experiment homages","quote":"This is a cool homage to Alvin Lucier's I'm Sitting in a Room","text":"This is a cool homage to Alvin Lucier's I'm Sitting in a Room [1][2]. I was lucky to get a chance to hear Lucier give a colloquium at Brandeis when I was an undergrad---earlier in his career Lucier had been faculty at Brandeis. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["NIu_dJGyIQI",1],["uUmIdq0D6-A",1]]},"jXf04bhcjbg":{"wy":"Cited as a mainstream summary of antitrust arguments and tech-company behavior in related threads.","q":{"x":"John Oliver also covered tech monopolies recently.","i":31771692,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":35131508,"author":"NayamAmarshe","month":"2023-03","story":"Dalai: Automatically install, run, and play with LLaMA on your computer","stance":"rec","why":"Historical patterns showing free tech and broken monopolies advance society.","quote":"Here's a nice video that showcases the same patterns in history","text":"> Leave us behind whom or what? Whom: The corporations with enough money to burn. What: Technological progress. Here's a nice video that showcases the same patterns in history and how having free and open tech + breaking monopolies helped move society forward - [VIDEO]"},{"id":34412900,"author":"fxtentacle","month":"2023-01","story":"Why Is Amazon Selling These Fake 16TB Portable SSD Drives?","stance":"reference","why":"Claims antitrust bills blocked by Amazon-linked lobbying.","quote":"John Oliver claims that the 2022 antitrust bills are being blocked by Schumer","text":"John Oliver claims that the 2022 antitrust bills are being blocked by Schumer, whose daughters work as Lobbyists for Amazon. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33222383,"author":"ofer2","month":"2022-10","story":"AT&T to pay $23M fine for bribing lawmaker’s ally in exchange for vote","stance":"reference","why":"Segment covering politicians' family members lobbying for tech.","quote":"","text":"Lobbyists probably also tend to hire ex-politicians as a form of (perfectly legal) delayed bribery; and hire family members of politicians ( [VIDEO] )."},{"id":32070197,"author":"ChrisMarshallNY","month":"2022-07","story":"A/B testing gets misused to juice metrics in the short term","stance":"reference","why":"Rant on tech companies treating users as cattle.","quote":"John Oliver did a rant about this, recently","text":"> means you don't respect your users. I was just pontif- er, talking about this to someone, a couple of days ago. I love the users of my products. Most of my products are free, and are carefully-crafted, highly-polished, complete deliverables, and I fret over how they are used -even if by a tiny number of end users-, like a nervous hen. I do what I do, out of love for the craft, and out of a genuine desire to make people's lives easier, through the technology I have at my disposal. It is my belief that most tech companies despise their user base. Users are little more than cattle, to be fattened and slaughtered. \"Caring about the user\" means optimizing for \"engagement,\" or keeping them trapped within their own ecosystem. John Oliver did a rant about this, recently[0]. It has nothing to do with actually caring about the user, or solving their problem. It is about harvesting users. In fact, my discussion about this, came about, because someone wanted to keep users inside the app I'm writing, as opposed to linking them to a more familiar app, on their phone (for the record, it was for videoconferencing). Linking is a \"no-brainer,\" as I can link out to dozens of installed apps, using the simple URL scheme method, built into iOS[1], and \"keeping them in the app,\" would have required several months of extra work, polluting the app with megabytes of junk code, because I'd need to use SDKs, and also kill the ability to easily scale to add new clients (contrary to popular belief, Zoom"},{"id":31771692,"author":"throwaway_1928","month":"2022-06","story":"Calling for Antitrust Reform","stance":"reference","why":"John Oliver segment on tech monopolies.","quote":"John Oliver also covered tech monopolies recently.","text":"John Oliver also covered tech monopolies recently. Tech Monopolies: Last Week Tonight with John Oliver (HBO) [VIDEO]"}]},"o2_SZ4tfLns":{"wy":"Used repeatedly in right-to-repair threads as a concrete example of Apple overcharging for repairs that independent shops fix cheaply.","q":{"x":"He is extremely good at repair while despising the company’s despicable practices - and warns people about them.","i":27475162,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27475162,"author":"andrei_says_","month":"2021-06","story":"New York Senate passes Right to Repair bill","stance":"rec","why":"Televised report on Apple Genius Bar overcharging versus independent repair","quote":"He is extremely good at repair while despising the company’s despicable practices - and warns people about them.","text":"Watch some of his videos. He has a lot of insight about the subpar quality of Apple devices, about the outrageous lies of Apple Genius Bar quoting people thousands of dollars for repairs that take him 5-10 minutes, for apple’s efforts to make the devices non serviceable, withholding schematics, controlling access to replacement parts etc. Here’s a televised CBS report. [VIDEO] He is extremely good at repair while despising the company’s despicable practices - and warns people about them."},{"id":18184274,"author":"Fricken","month":"2018-10","story":"Apple Said to Have 'Dramatically Reduced' iPhone Repair Fraud in China","stance":"reference","why":"CBC report on Apple quoting $1k for a 2 minute repair","quote":"aired an episode exposing Apple attempting to charge a customer $1k for a 2 minute repair","text":"On Sunday CBC's The National aired an episode exposing Apple attempting to charge a customer $1k for a 2 minute repair: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18185552,"author":"mistersquid","month":"2018-10","story":"Apple Said to Have 'Dramatically Reduced' iPhone Repair Fraud in China","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The diagnosis of the first repair case is a Macbook Pro that has had liquid spilled in its internals. [0] From a business and service perspective, Apple repair must first fix the liquid damage that may be causing the dim screen. Replacing the screen without first fixing the liquid damage may simply reproduce the issue and/or damage a new screen. The customer balks at the quoted price asks the Apple Genius Bar rep if there are other options. The Genius Bar rep hesitates and informs the customer there are no other options under Apple repair. Of course there are other options outside of Apple authorized repair, but it is likely against Apple policy to recommend an alternative (i.e. assuming indirect liability for poorly executed 3rd-party repairs). That said, in this case it seems entirely reasonable for Apple repair to proceed by first fixing the most obvious damage that may be causing the issue. While this approach may be more expensive than is necessary, it's also more likely to produce the desired result if the issue were not caused by the screen itself. Paying money for premium service is anathema to many consumer advocates, but Apple is well known to meet their obligations for devices protected by AppleCare while only doing the bare minimum for devices outside of warranty. From a practical standpoint, buying AppleCare is highly advisable given the likelihood of device failure inside of 3 years, the cost of repairing such failures, and (most importantly in my opinion) the e"},{"id":37665829,"author":"circuit10","month":"2023-09","story":"iPhone 15 teardown reveals software lockdown","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Here's an example of them charging for a whole motherboard replacement when the issue was a bent pin","text":"OK, some people downvoted this, so let me explain: Overcharging: Apple tends to replace whole assemblies rather than individual parts, and don't do board-level repairs or anything. Apple staff are generally just following a procedure and aren't allowed to/aren't trained to solve problems in the best way. Here's an example of them charging for a whole motherboard replacement when the issue was a bent pin: [VIDEO] Some people might be OK with this, but not everyone; competition is important! About the self/independent repair program: The self repair program allows you to order one part at a time and you have to have the device to do it. Realistically, almost no one will do it themselves, and will use a repair shop. The program is impractical for repair shops, because they can't stock parts in advance. The other option is the independent repair program, which effectively turns you into a shipping centre for Apple; it bars you from doing anything but the most basic repairs without sending it to sending things off to Apple and they will do random inspections on your store and fine you if you're actually offering good service like board level repair or using cheaper aftermarket parts. So it's likely that both are mostly just PR stunts to get ahead of regulation while also not making a significant change to their business"},{"id":36234209,"author":"circuit10","month":"2023-06","story":"OpenGL 3.1 on Asahi Linux","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Apple tells a journalist they have to buy a new Mac, and a 3rd party repair shop fixes it for free","text":"Because as a customer you should have the choice to get a repair from whoever you want. Even if you only go to Apple, competition will force their prices down somewhat. \"pay the Apple\" It's not just Apple doing this \"then spend lots of extra time trying to save $50 on a repair.\" What if you're trying to save precious data? Apple don't make much effort to do data recovery because they don't care; you can fund 3rd party repairers who absolutely do. What if you got the device second hand? What if it's not $50, but $1000+? I've seen a video where Apple tells a journalist they have to buy a new Mac, and a 3rd party repair shop fixes it for free because it was such a small issue that Apple didn't even check for. [VIDEO] \"Macs are all identical, so repairs boil down\" This is the problem; Apple is pairing parts to devices, so if you use a 100% genuine part from a new device it still won't work"},{"id":27808032,"author":"TaylorAlexander","month":"2021-07","story":"Podswap: Keep airpods alive and out of the landfill","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Apple laptops and phones objectively last longer than the competition. I'm not sure if you've spent much time watching Louis Rossman's youtube channel, but it is full of examples of Apple telling customers a device needs to be replaced when simple repairs are possible. [1][2][3] Apple worked with US Customs and Border Patrol to seize replacement batteries for laptops that apple will no longer service. [4] Rossman has a lot of videos so I did not find original sources for the following, but he has also called Apple support about replacing a charging port on a phone. Apple support told him the charging port was soldered to the motherboard and the phone needed to be replaced. But that charging port on that device is attached via a cable and is not soldered to the board. It can be replaced for a few dollars in five minutes. Rossman also says that apple prevents third party chip manufacturers from selling to repair shops. So Rossman could repair certain macbooks with a $6 chip from (I believe) Intersil, but Apple (being an 800lb gorilla) has asked Intersil not to sell those chips to anyone else. So apple won't replace that chip on your motherboard but they also won't let anyone else do it. It's great that apple will replace batteries, but I seem to recall there was significant consumer (or government?) pressure to offer those replacements. And I would be curious if they do that worldwide or only where legally required. But watching Rossman's youtube channel, it is clear that rep"},{"id":27814514,"author":"TaylorAlexander","month":"2021-07","story":"Podswap: Keep airpods alive and out of the landfill","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"> most people dont care or think about it at all This is orthogonal to the question of whether or not the practice is environmentally friendly. Most people have been buying gasoline for 50 years but that’s also causing environmental problems. All of these manufactured devices take a lot of resources to create. Often “unusability” means the operating system has outgrown the hardware, but we can easily imagine Apple allowing third party operating systems on their unsupported phone and tablet devices. This would extend the life of the hardware, significantly reduced waste, and lower people’s cost of living. But even when Apple devices can be fixed for free the company will quote exorbitant repair prices and suggest the customer replace the device. (See links below which I also shared in another comment) From this we can see that Apple is not making the effort to keep old devices functional and they will mislead customers about it to sell them a new device. This leads to hardware waste and higher costs for consumers. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":26488045,"author":"pbasista","month":"2021-03","story":"Right to Repair Gaining Momentum","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Apple's suggested \"solution\" to a bent pin issue was to replace the logic board and the top case","text":"> they have great service Apple typically does not perform component-level repairs. They tend to replace larger parts like motherboards, display assemblies or cases piece for piece, which is cheap and quick for them and expensive for the customer. And it also typically means that the customer will lose their data. I am not sure on what basis can such a service be considered \"great\". On top of that, Louis Rossmann has published multiple videos showing the sheer incompetence of Apple's official Genius Bar service. For example: * [VIDEO] (Apple's suggested \"solution\" to a bent pin issue was to replace the logic board and the top case) * [LINK] (Apple is unable to resolve an issue with the SSD, bends a display cable pin in the process and tells the customer that there might be a motherboard issue) * [LINK] (Apple spends $ 10,000 while troubleshooting an \"issue\" of backlight being turned all the way down)"},{"id":24148609,"author":"mellow2020","month":"2020-08","story":"Epic Games has filed legal papers in response to Apple [pdf]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"> People pay huge overhead to apple exactly because apple makes choices for them - regarding what settings are reasonable to have, what software they want you to use etc. Well, they think it gives them quality. Instead they kinda get fleeced and pushed into a threadmill of planned obsolescence, e.g. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23662126,"author":"dkersten","month":"2020-06","story":"Dell XPS 13 Developer Edition with Ubuntu 20.04 LTS pre-installed","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Only because apple won’t do simple repairs.","text":"> need a new motherboard / some drastic problem Only because apple won’t do simple repairs. Eg, blwn capacitor worth 40 cent? New motherboard worth hundreds! [1] Of course, to the end user, the effect is the same... [1] [VIDEO] and [LINK] for just two examples"},{"id":20649354,"author":"ChuckNorris89","month":"2019-08","story":"Apple Is Locking iPhone Batteries to Discourage Repair","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"customer's MacBook's display is not working correctly so Geniuses tell him he needs a new motherboard and display replacement","text":"Apple doesn't want their customers to get f'd over you say? You might want to check out Luis Rosmann's videos, a famous Apple repair geek who fixes for cheap the products Apple themselves misdiagnose[1] in order to rip off their customers[2]. [1] [LINK] // teenager's MacBook's display is not working correctly so Geniuses tell him he needs a new motherboard replacement while Luis discovers in 2 seconds that the lid-close sensor cable is broken so he replaces it for $8 [2] [VIDEO] // customer's MacBook's display is not working correctly so Geniuses tell him he needs a new motherboard and display replacement while Luis discovers in two minutes that the display connector has a bent pin and fixes it for free"},{"id":20674591,"author":"tomxor","month":"2019-08","story":"Rotten Apple: Right to Repair Roundup","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"This is such a bad article, I half suspect it's astroturfing and food intended for Apple apologists. It's focused on a single small part of the problem and unnecessarily misrepresented it. As a reminder of the broader picture: Apple has a strong anti-repair stance, they have lobbied for this [1], have engaged in broad, deceptive strategies to remove 3rd party repair options, by confiscating legally refurbished hardware under the guise of \"counterfeits\" [2], attempting to confiscate grey market parts under the guise of \"trademark violation\" and threatening the 3rd party repair shops [3]. They profit from this continued attack by deceiving customers into expensive unnecessary part replacements, suggesting repair is not possible and generally coercing customers into buying new products instead [4]. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20249158,"author":"bborud","month":"2019-06","story":"Slack Is Going Public at a $16B Valuation","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"I don't know much about the X since I stopped at the iPhone 7 and I am probably not going to buy any of their exorbitantly priced phones. That being said, in their pursuit of thin phones they ran into design issues with boards flexing so that BGA components will eventually fail. They became aware of the problem and then proceeded to make the same mistake again in later models rarther than fixing the problem. (The flexing problem isn't that they will break after one \"bending event\", but rather than they fail over time as your phone is subject to mechanical stresses of what is within the range of normal use. Typically because the tiny balls under BGAs will let go) I'm mostly talking about their laptops and their iMacs. The USB C connectors are of course inconvenient, but I actually like them. Again, the problem is that in their pursuit of thinness they ended up designing keys that are very sensitive to dust (and not very nice to use). In subsequent models they kept at it, so the quality of keyboards doesn't seem to be a priority. Some laptops have a tendency to develop display problems due to bad design. For instance blowing hot air on parts that can't take hot air or laying out connectors so that they will fail more easily. Now these are design flaws, which brings us to the \"abusive and fraudulent\" part. If you want to get these things fixed under warranty you _may_ be okay. Except the process appears to be entirely decided by chance. For instance they have put moisture indica"},{"id":18502930,"author":"prolikewh0a","month":"2018-11","story":"Apple iPhone Supplier Foxconn Planning Deep Cost Cuts","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Not sure why you're being downvoted for something that is very possibly going to happen. People hate hearing crony capitalist reality on HN and trust too much in large corporations that are solely there for profit to shareholders and will do so at any cost. Look at the dongle nonsense Apple has pulled on customers for profit. Look at their genius bar overcharing and lying to people about their broken devices (see: Louis Rossman [VIDEO] )."},{"id":18184347,"author":"rasz","month":"2018-10","story":"Apple Said to Have 'Dramatically Reduced' iPhone Repair Fraud in China","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Thats a nice PR piece pushing criminal fraud narrative, forgetting to mention Apple is at war with people providing 3rd party repair service. The goal? remove alternatives, _any_ small defect = 'genius' telling you to buy new $1-2K device. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18306999,"author":"dis-sys","month":"2018-10","story":"Copyright Office Ruling Imposes Sweeping Right to Repair Reforms","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I watched the following video last week and I was shocked.","text":"I watched the following video last week and I was shocked. APPLE charges its customer up to almost $2k ($1200 for the motherboard and $780 for the screen) to repair something a small laptop repair shop did for free. What is more shocking is the fact that dozens of such cases happen everyday in that small laptop repair shop. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["H9B5mYfBPlY",1],["SWEts0rlezA",1],["8bZh5LMaSmE",1],["PY9DcIMGxMs",1],["5f9R9MtkpqM",1],["iG9CE55wbtY",1]]},"tBJ0GvsQeak":{"wy":"HN people share it as first-hand evidence that driverless Waymo rides were already happening, including navigating a busy mall parking lot.","q":{"x":"Check out this video, it works really well","i":25338615,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25338615,"author":"badestrand","month":"2020-12","story":"Uber sells self-driving unit in deal that will push Aurora’s valuation to $10B","stance":"rec","why":"Ride in a driverless Waymo with nobody up front","quote":"Check out this video, it works really well","text":"Check out this video, it works really well: [VIDEO] (\"Riding in a Self Driving Car - With Nobody Up Front\")"},{"id":30560113,"author":"kobalsky","month":"2022-03","story":"A 2030 Self-Driving Car Bet","stance":"reference","why":"Shows driverless car navigating a busy shopping mall parking lot","quote":"it's still impressive seeing the thing driving around a shopping mall parking lot with pedestrian traffic","text":"yeah I reckon they started on the easiest place to implement. but it's still impressive seeing the thing driving around a shopping mall parking lot with pedestrian traffic, they put a lot of trust in this thing: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29997165,"author":"kobalsky","month":"2022-01","story":"Felony charges are first in a fatal crash involving Autopilot","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence Waymo rides are already happening in Phoenix","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] can't you get a ride in phoenix like today with waymo? or is not open to the public? I mean I understand that their AI is not perfect but it doesn't seem like a pipe dream"},{"id":29080746,"author":"drawkbox","month":"2021-11","story":"Walmart delivery powered by Cruise – Self-driving deliveries are here","stance":"reference","why":"Channel showing rides with no driver in Waymo vehicles","quote":"this JJRicks Studios channel [3] did a series on it for a while to show rides with no driver","text":"Yeah Cruise announced this Walmart delivery with self-driving cars in Nov 2020 to launch in 2021 [1] and it is now available at the first location in Arizona. Cruise has been testing here but probably more on proving grounds like Apple has. Apple just bought a proving ground this year that they have been leasing since 2017 [2]. Testing picking up as well as competition. I am sure Cruise will expand it in Arizona. Most of the self-driving operations are in the East Valley (Chandler, Gilbert, Tempe) as the roads are pretty simple and square. The Scottsdale area this is in also pretty simple and by the reservation. Self-driving cars are everywhere with Waymo in Arizona as well, many entirely unattended. If you drive anywhere you see them. For Waymo samples, this JJRicks Studios channel [3] did a series on it for a while to show rides with no driver. Waymo does have a team that follows cars in trouble, on the infamous \"cone\" one you can see it [4]. For the most part the operation is solid, very few incidents like the \"cone\" incident where the cone was just in between LiDAR and CV detection and probably caused a covering of the line that confused the decision process. These types of issues are best to resolve in a baseline test with little change before the more challenging ones. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO] [4] [LINK]"}]},"ta7nlkI5K5g":{"wy":"Pointed to as proof that you can weigh micrograms cheaply at home, and as a clear breakdown of how such a scale works.","q":{"x":"YouTube just dug up a a real nice breakdown that Ben Krasnow did","i":18647526,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18647526,"author":"jcims","month":"2018-12","story":"Making Penicillin at Home","stance":"rec","why":"Real nice breakdown of a DIY microbalance by Ben Krasnow.","quote":"YouTube just dug up a a real nice breakdown that Ben Krasnow did","text":"Saw this DIY one a few years back on youtube, looks like the Erowid one, thought it was a neat build - [LINK] YouTube just dug up a a real nice breakdown that Ben Krasnow did - [VIDEO]"},{"id":46535601,"author":"pvillano","month":"2026-01","story":"A4 Paper Stories","stance":"reference","why":"Related to calibrating tiny scales using paper.","quote":"Paper's uniform mass per area makes it useful calibrating very tiny scales.","text":"Paper's uniform mass per area makes it useful calibrating very tiny scales. 1mm² of 80 gsm paper will weigh about 80 micrograms. \"Measure the mass of an eyelash with a DIY microbalance\" by Applied Science [VIDEO]"},{"id":43951440,"author":"s0rce","month":"2025-05","story":"Strain gauge made out of PCB","stance":"reference","why":"Comparable to a relative's homemade microgram scale.","quote":"maybe similar to this applied science video","text":"My dad built one with an electromagnetic coil many years ago, can't find the design, capacity was limited but had 100? microgram resolution, maybe similar to this applied science video [VIDEO] but I think simpler. maybe something like this: [LINK] [LINK] I found some old emails about the scale from 2005/6 but can't find the link and it would probably be gone anyways."},{"id":36809511,"author":"mikewarot","month":"2023-07","story":"An Invitation to a Secret Society","stance":"reference","why":"Example of cheaply weighing micrograms.","quote":"You can weigh micrograms without too much effort.[1]","text":"Measuring things precisely is way cheaper than it's ever been. You can weigh micrograms without too much effort.[1] You can buy time/frequency standards for $200 or so that time to the national standards.[2] Float glass is a cheap and relatively flat surface plate that you can buy almost anywhere. Lasers, detectors, computing, everything is dirt cheap compared to the past. If you have something you want to make, or measure, this is likely the easiest time ever to do so. Don't worry about being at the leading edge, just do something, have fun, and you might be the first to notice some weird little quirk that turns out to be important. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"}],"r":[["lY8Ol2n4o4A",1],["DXYje2B04xE",1],["7Hk9jct2ozY",1],["N3bJnKmeNJY",1],["E1AxVXt2Gv4",1],["aoczYXJeMY4",1],["bPhUhypV27w",1],["cdiD-9MMpb0",1],["krB0enBeSiE",1],["2hf9yN-oBV4",1],["7pSqk-XV2QM",1],["GhsZUZmJvaM",1],["pwaWilO_Pig",1],["LQmTKxI4Wn4",1],["UDtlvZGmHYk",1],["P-2P3MSZrBM",1],["TfYf_rPWUdY",1],["3dl9Ovwmnxw",1]]},"2yfXgu37iyI":{"wy":"HN mostly uses it as a joke about secrecy or as an illustration of mutually assured destruction.","m":[{"id":34567862,"author":"082349872349872","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: What happens if a nuclear missile is air-destroyed before detonation?","stance":"reference","why":"Doomsday machine scene illustrating mutually assured destruction.","quote":"The essence of MAD is that a strike is never \"the\" nuclear missile.","text":"The essence of MAD is that a strike is never \"the\" nuclear missile. It will consist of many missiles, more warheads, and yet more decoys. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33481288,"author":"ethbr0","month":"2022-11","story":"Multiple assertions are fine in a unit test","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Couldn't not read that in Peter Sellers' voice","text":"> ... where this is not only possible, but the common case. Couldn't not read that in Peter Sellers' voice [VIDEO] > ... to find a 630 lines long test \"case\" with 22 nondescript assertions along the way. This is where tech team managers are abrogating their responsibility and job. It's the job of the organization to set policy standards to outlaw things like this. It's the job of the developer to cut as many corners of those policies as possible to ship code ASAP. And it's the job of a tech team manager to set up a detailed but efficient process (code review sign offs!) that paper over the gap between the two in a sane way. ... none of which helps immediately with a legacy codebase that's @$&@'d, though."},{"id":24481298,"author":"082349872349872","month":"2020-09","story":"Comparative advantage and when to blow up your island","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Bonus clip","text":"If I understand correctly, one reason there seem to be so many results for zero-sum games is that they have defined solutions, whereas in other situations, a mathematically valid strategy is to be a jerk (\"blow up the island\") and attempt to take the lion's share[1] of the surplus for oneself. One of the early game theory papers considers two people with radios, parachuted onto a grid, who must rendezvous. It derives a theoretical strategy: broadcast one's location, then destroy one's radio, forcing the other player to do all the walking. Bonus clip: [VIDEO] @ 3:42 whew, still there: [LINK] all OK, Boomers! @ 3:55 unlike the House of Windsor during '14-'18, Kongo-Müller didn't bother to change his name: [LINK] (esp. 53:40-56:15) Those who prefer their skulls virtual are advised to avoid 30:10-33:30 and instead regard [LINK] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":23544760,"author":"OliverJones","month":"2020-06","story":"CIA hacking unit failed to protect its systems, allowing Vault 7 disclosure","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"ze whole point of ze secret hack is lost if you don't keep it a secret.","text":"How does somebody exfiltrate 34 TERABYTES from a secure facility without getting noticed? To misquote Dr. Strangelove, \"ze whole point of ze secret hack is lost if you don't keep it a secret.\" [VIDEO] Oh, maybe they have a firewall built on a RaspberryPi somebody ordered online. Seriously, WTF? This is as insecure as having contract sysadmins with root privilege spread all over the globe. And when will these state actors with unlimited funding figure out that NOBODY can keep secrets forever, not even them?"}]},"9GQoHIBDogU":{"wy":"HN mostly uses it to criticize or mock Bezos, not as a recommendation.","m":[{"id":33307885,"author":"Morizero","month":"2022-10","story":"William Shatner experienced profound grief in space. It was the overview effect","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Bezos was too busy playing with champagne.","text":"I'm glad NPR took the time to get his thoughts since Bezos was too busy playing with champagne. [VIDEO]"},{"id":47869521,"author":"georgemcbay","month":"2026-04","story":"What I Learned About Billionaires at Jeff Bezos's Private Retreat","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The clip humanizes Shatner and makes Bezos seem like a creepy narcissistic ghoul in comparison","text":"Interesting read. I'm fully willing to buy the author's assessment of Jeff Bezos' detachment from the reality that we normal folk live in by viewing his (Bezos') interaction with William Shatner post Blue Origin's NS-18 launch. [VIDEO] The clip humanizes Shatner and makes Bezos seem like a creepy narcissistic ghoul in comparison (which is quite a feat assuming stories told about Shatner by many of his former coworkers are accurate). Given all of the events since then its a bit interesting that despite the thinly veiled not-quite-name-dropping (eg. Gaiman, Chabon, Andrés) he didn't mention the presence of Ghislaine Maxwell who has been to Campfire several times including in 2018."},{"id":43736822,"author":"vinceguidry","month":"2025-04","story":"Cozy video games can quell stress and anxiety","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Reminds me of this clip of Jeff Bezos being a total dick to William Shatner.","text":"> Lack of such frantic craziness is seen as boring (I never heard of this word boring in my childhood). Slow life in general is being viewed as socially unacceptable. We are frogs in a boiling pot. Reminds me of this clip of Jeff Bezos being a total dick to William Shatner. [VIDEO] Shatner later realized that 11 minutes in space wasn't really anything to write home about and that billionaires are assholes."}]},"ARJ8cAGm6JE":{"wy":"HN invokes it as a joke whenever a machine or algorithm refuses to do what a person wants.","m":[{"id":42899638,"author":"walterbell","month":"2025-02","story":"How to turn off Apple Intelligence","stance":"joke","why":"HAL 9000 jab at Apple's OS.","quote":"but at least the OS isn't channeling HAL 9000,","text":"It's a disaster for Apple devices breached by an iOS zero day. A \"workaround\" is to track every outbound network connection, block C&C traffic at the router, then keep using older iOS-infected-with-dormant-malware. If attackers create ransomware (instead of spyware) for iOS, governments will have to force Apple to enable 3rd party backup and restore to a clean DFU install of their existing iOS version. Currently testing Google Pixel tablet and phone with GrapheneOS, where Debian Linux VMs will be possible in Android 16. Better security, weaker hardware, less usability, but at least the OS isn't channeling HAL 9000, [VIDEO]"},{"id":30150244,"author":"CharlesW","month":"2022-01","story":"When the algorithm is your boss","stance":"joke","why":"HAL quote used as punchline.","quote":"","text":"\"I'm sorry, Dave. I'm afraid I can't do that.\" [VIDEO] The current version of this is \"Computer says no\", with humans reduced to being meat-based I/O devices: [LINK]"},{"id":15880154,"author":"LearnerHerzog","month":"2017-12","story":"I gave Amazon a key to my house and regretted it","stance":"joke","why":"HAL scene compared to Amazon smart home lockout.","quote":"when HAL wouldn't open the pod bay doors","text":"Anyone else find the whole setup strikingly similar to HAL-9000's role in 2001: A Space Odyssey ? The article, too, presented the author with the same issue Dave experienced, when HAL wouldn't open the pod bay doors [VIDEO] HAL's decision was a conscious one, but I find it rather interesting a 1960s-Kubrick (& Arthur C. Clarke) could predict the casual use of multiple currently -cutting-edge devices, and incorporate them into the story: - One being HAL's resemblance to a new amazon smart-home, at least in general purpose functionally, plus the vibe of sentience and omnipresence - Another prediction being realtime face-to-face chat as shown in one scene, which would [LINK] Naturally this makes me wonder if artificial consciousness is possible or not (be it X years/decades/centuries into the future), what fundamental questions it may answer, and what consequences it may have. I am not necessarily referring to the consequences of recursively self-improving super-intelligence, but not excluding it either I suppose. I am referring to true consciousness, with human-level feelings and emotions, where the personality may have an effect on what it does rather than simply and emotionlessly taking the logical (or programmed) route every time. Creation of artificial consciousness may be many steps away from trying to undertake; however, given the ever-accelerating rate of both digital-tech and neuroscience, if true consciousness is at all possible to program, it will likely come far soon"}]},"EBxgrr0wL8M":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as an example of the film's satire of militarism and fascist propaganda delivered deadpan.","q":{"x":"The guys at Red Letter Media did a pretty good breakdown of it[0] all in all.","i":18497169,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":31884618,"author":"mLuby","month":"2022-06","story":"We need to make adulthood more desirable","stance":"reference","why":"Classroom scene on veterans and the vote.","quote":"A veteran accepts personal responsibility for safety of the body politic, defending it with his life.","text":"Teacher: Why are only [veterans] allowed to vote? Student: It's a reward for doing government service. Teacher: No. No! Something given has no value. Teacher: What's the moral difference between a civilian and [veteran]? Student: A veteran accepts personal responsibility for safety of the body politic, defending it with his life. A civilian does not. -Starship Troopers classroom scene [VIDEO] (Heinlein had some weird ideas and the movie's satire isn't obvious enough to those who most need it to be.)"},{"id":23754053,"author":"dragontamer","month":"2020-07","story":"How “Starship Troopers” Aligns with Our Moment of American Defeat","stance":"reference","why":"The film's fascist propaganda satire in the Failure of Democracy speech.","quote":"Or maybe the \"Failure of Democracy\" speech?","text":"> devolved into a bunch of people running around with guns shouting randomly at giant bugs. That was part of the satire actually. When a pro-militaristic society knows nothing but violence, they continuously look at the problem through a violence-only lens. > It basically stripped away all the interesting science fiction bits The propaganda bits and sarcastic take-down of fascism is the big part of the movie. [LINK] Its probably easier to see the sarcasm with all of the Federal News Network clips in a row? Or maybe the \"Failure of Democracy\" speech? [VIDEO]"},{"id":18497169,"author":"dmreedy","month":"2018-11","story":"The Predictions of Robert A. Heinlein, from the Cold War to the Waterbed","stance":"reference","why":"Classroom scene on citizenship showing the film's satire played straight.","quote":"there's a whole lot in that dialog, and it's all played deadly straight","text":"I think there's more depth to the film than you might be giving it credit for. There's a lot of goofy heavy-handedness on the surface, but there are some layers underneath, in the writing and the arcs of the characters The guys at Red Letter Media did a pretty good breakdown of it[0] all in all. But even just consider the scene in the beginning when Rasczak is teaching the class about citizenship[1]. It's goofy and funny on the meta-level, because, well, it's Michael Ironside, and he's saying things that at first pass sound insane . But there's a whole lot in that dialog, and it's all played deadly straight. And then compare Rico's genuine, \"I don't know\" there, to where his character is at the end of the film. I think it recommends itself to a lot of different levels of examination, without sounding like you're trying too hard. --- [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"Hrbq66XqtCo":{"wy":"HN cites it for Huang's own words on TPUs and on opposing export restrictions to China.","q":{"x":"the Jensen Huang episode where they discuss TPUs","i":48728817,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":49078280,"author":"girfan","month":"2026-07","story":"Our position on open-weights models","stance":"reference","why":"Huang opposes barriers on accelerator exports to China.","quote":"Jensen himself is a strong opponent of any such barriers","text":"If nothing else, Anthropic has explicitly acknowledged the threat to its moat and that the margin is thin between its models and open-weight models. I'd take that as a win for open-weight models. Good luck preventing distillation and limiting the supply of accelerators to China when Jensen himself is a strong opponent of any such barriers [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":48728817,"author":"gardnr","month":"2026-06","story":"LongCat-2.0, a large-scale MoE model with 1.6T total and 48B Active","stance":"reference","why":"Jensen Huang episode where TPUs are discussed.","quote":"the Jensen Huang episode where they discuss TPUs","text":"Dwarkesh Patel has AI/ML guests on his podcast. BoorishBears may have been referring to the Jensen Huang episode where they discuss TPUs: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48124647,"author":"jmyeet","month":"2026-05","story":"The US Is Winning the AI Race","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Back in the dot-com bubble, people started inventing new metrics to \"value\" dot-com companies that lost money hand over fist. My favorite was \"revenue multiples\". So instead of a a P/E ratio, it was just a multiple of revenue no matter how much money you lost. We've invented a new term here too: revenue backlog. OpenAI and Anthropic in particular need to recover probably at least $2 trillion to recoup their capex investments. Now Claude code has had an impact on software engineering but for a lot of AI uses you're just not going to recover $2T on $20/month subscriptions. It reminds me of Twitter trying to dig itself out of a $44B hole and losing half their ad revenue with $8/month blue ticks. The only commercial product AI sells is labor displacement and the resulting wage suppression. You lay off 10-20% of your staff and nobody is asking for raises. The people left are happyt o still have jobs (and thus a house). They'll work even harder doing unpaid labor of the displaced workers to keep those jobs. That's what OpenAI and Anthropic are selling. The problem is that if these companies get their way, 10-20% of the population is going to be out-of-work and society is going to fall apart. Data centers are going to be the targets of increased societal desperation and anger as this gets worse. There was a report this week that roughly 50% of singles in the US aren't dating because they can't afford to [1]. This goes well beyond the well-understood problems of not being able to aff"},{"id":48171415,"author":"jmyeet","month":"2026-05","story":"Mistral's CEO: Europe has 2 years to stop becoming America's AI 'vassal state'","stance":"reference","why":"Jensen Huang on China chip markets and export restrictions.","quote":"And you know who realizes all this? The Nvidia CEO","text":"The China example was such a massive blunder (from the perspective of US soft power). Like you say, it created a captive market for Chinese chips where Chinese companies might've otherwise bought foreign chips. That's inbuilt demand. But China also has something Europe doesn't and that's central planning. China has realized that chipmaking and AI are issues of national security. Europe just doesn't have that level of governance and forethought. Within 5 years China will be producing their own EUV chips despite the ASML export ban. And you know who realizes all this? The Nvidia CEO [1][2]. There is now a 70 year history of Europe being designed as and becoming comfortable with being a US vassal state, from the end of WW2 to Bretton-Woods, the Marshall Plan and NATO. I don't see fascist takeovers in the UK, France or Germany as changing any of this. If anything, it'll just fracture any semblance of European unity. It'll be the enemy within as those forces will be most aligned with the US. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"}]},"IrrADTN-dvg":{"wy":"Cited purely as a joke in threads about sex robots, deepfakes and chess cheating.","m":[{"id":38427078,"author":"tzs","month":"2023-11","story":"Titled Tuesday [Chess] Cheating","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"this educational film about the dangers of sex robots","text":"It is pretty well known that all civilization was just an effort to impress the opposite sex (and sometimes the same sex), as explained in this educational film about the dangers of sex robots [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24994337,"author":"magicalist","month":"2020-11","story":"The Economics of Sex Robots","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"that's already been done a lot better elsewhere (DON'T DATE ROBOTS! [1])","text":"This is a pretty flimsy article. Fine for someone's random blog post, but it's a good bit of amateur philosophizing, substituting citation word association with actual causal (or even correlative) connections. People have been having sex with inanimate (and sometimes animate but non-human) objects since forever. The focus on men and their ability to get off without another human present is a little weird, as vibrators and dildos (not to mention just plain old masturbation) are extremely popular with women. I'd say any changes in human relationships, child count, \"incel\" behavior, etc have less to do with the availability of a solo orgasm and much more to do with technological, social and economic changes. If you can have fulfilling social relationships online without leaving the house, a lot of people are going to end up struggling (in motivation and skill) to strike up relationships in the real world. What's left of this is just spinning a scifi story from a \"what if\" premise, and that's already been done a lot better elsewhere (DON'T DATE ROBOTS! [1]). [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20061405,"author":"LastZactionHero","month":"2019-05","story":"Deepfake porn and the ethics of being able to watch whatever you desire","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"why should he bother?","text":"Ordinarily, Billy would work hard to make money with his paper route, then he'd use the money to buy dinner for Mavis, thus earning a slim chance to perform the reproductive act... But in a world where teens can date robots, why should he bother? Why should anyone bother?! - Futurama, I Dated a Robot [VIDEO]"}]},"JXv8VlpoK_g":{"wy":"Referenced as an example of an innovative, ahead-of-its-time desktop interface.","m":[{"id":33510373,"author":"theCrowing","month":"2022-11","story":"Lazy – A Capture Tool for Knowledge","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Reminds me of the looking glass [0] demo from sun","text":"Reminds me of the looking glass [0] demo from sun for the app itself there is sadly not much to see yet. closed beta. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31712940,"author":"exitb","month":"2022-06","story":"Wayfire is a Wayland compositor based on wlroots","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"That’s a cool video, I’m also going to leave this here, from around the same time, but a totally different world: [VIDEO]"},{"id":1180445,"author":"bad_user","month":"2010-03","story":"Good Artists Copy, Great Artists Steal","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of Sun's innovative Looking Glass desktop","quote":"it was definitely not a clone ... pretty innovative actually","text":"Project Looking Glass, while certainly inspired by current UIs, it was definitely not a clone ... pretty innovative actually, although it wasn't usable yet. You can take a look at a demo here: [VIDEO]"}]},"LMmdl4VltD4":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as the go-to parody of startup pitch culture whenever buzzword-heavy startups come up.","q":{"x":"The did a great parody of many startup pitches that I've heard over the years.","i":22056135,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22056135,"author":"lgleason","month":"2020-01","story":"CollegeHumor shuts down","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The did a great parody of many startup pitches that I've heard over the years.","text":"The did a great parody of many startup pitches that I've heard over the years. Hopefully they can save it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22061137,"author":"_nedR","month":"2020-01","story":"CollegeHumor shuts down","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"How can you talk about Sam Reich in CollegeHumour videos in a hacker news thread and not choose \"Startup guys\" as an example","text":"How can you talk about Sam Reich in CollegeHumour videos in a hacker news thread and not choose \"Startup guys\" as an example : [VIDEO] Looking at it now, this video seems to have been particularly relevant, considering that the company went bankrupt because they trusted a bunch of silicon valley frauds i.e. Facebook.."},{"id":6957376,"author":"dylandrop","month":"2013-12","story":"RapGenius Growth Hack Exposed","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"they're dressed and acting _exactly_ like CollegeHumor's The Startup Guys.","text":"Oof, that video is cringe-worthy (\"baller-sourced\" please no..). Moreover, they're dressed and acting _exactly_ like CollegeHumor's The Startup Guys. [VIDEO]"},{"id":4080157,"author":"JonnieCache","month":"2012-06","story":"Vooza","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"collegehumor did this better a year ago","text":"Woefully inaccurate: it has vowels in the name. In fact, mostly vowels in the name. Also, collegehumor did this better a year ago: [VIDEO] For that matter, so did Color."},{"id":4104556,"author":"confluence","month":"2012-06","story":"Peter Thiel gives new class of students $100K to forgo college","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Feeling was implied I just gave it a cynical twist, and I quote: Ritik Malhotra (19, San Jose, CA) Ritik began programming at age 8; started a popular web forum at the age of 12 that grew to over 32,000 members; and ran a web hosting and software consultancy business at the age of 13, garnering over a 600x return on his initial investment. I wonder what he started with - $10? Taylor Wilson (18, Texarkana, AR) became the youngest person in history to create nuclear fusion. Since then, he has produced the lowest-cost and lowest-dose active interrogation system for the detection of enriched uranium ever developed. As a Thiel Fellow, Taylor will focus on both counter-terrorism and the production of medical isotopes for use in the diagnosis and treatment of cancer. I ... no ... I can't even address this. Omar Rizwan (18, East Hanover, NJ) wants to change the world through the control and analysis of information. Nothing big there. Just changing the world. Don't mind me. Jimmy Koppel (20, St. Louis, MO) has a passion for software engineering – and a plan to make it much more efficient. Modifying software today often involves hundreds of thousands of small, similar adjustments that require a great deal of time and money. James will fix that problem by developing new tools to automate the process. It's almost like software engineering is real work. But hey, this kid is going to \"automate the process\". Yoonseo Kang (18, Mississauga, ON, Canada) recognizes that society’s potential for "},{"id":3315732,"author":"cnorgate","month":"2011-12","story":"I Don't Understand What Anyone Is Saying Anymore","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Anyone who has spent time in Silicon Valley will find this clip awesome!","text":"Too true. Some guys over at college humor are on to this problem already... Anyone who has spent time in Silicon Valley will find this clip awesome! [VIDEO] Enjoy!"},{"id":3161615,"author":"hansy","month":"2011-10","story":"What are some inspiring videos I can watch to renew my passion for startups?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I love how it parodies the startup culture.","text":"This is just for fun (not informational at all), but I love how it parodies the startup culture. College Humor Start-up Guys: [VIDEO] At the very least the video will cheer you up :)"}]},"LTJvdGcb7Fs":{"wy":"HN commenters post it as a joke when a thread touches on the internet and porn.","m":[{"id":40370708,"author":"vasco","month":"2024-05","story":"Jan Leike Resigns from OpenAI","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I mean I didn't want to say it in my original reply but your comment makes it even more difficult to resist: \"The internet is for porn\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":32953174,"author":"cainxinth","month":"2022-09","story":"Are You Ready for the Internet? (1994) [video]","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"As early as 2003, popular culture was catching on to the fact that the ‘information superhighway’ had some potholes: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27954069,"author":"bsder","month":"2021-07","story":"What annoys me about YouTube videos","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Avenue Q had it right","text":"HAH! But I'm not sure how many people are going to get your joke ... That's pretty specialized jargon. But, Avenue Q had it right: [VIDEO] The only thing he can't make fun of on Twitter is \"YouTube Face\" ..."},{"id":12112300,"author":"johan_larson","month":"2016-07","story":"When Yahoo Ruled the Valley: Stories of the Original ‘Surfers’","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The internet is for porn.","text":"The internet is for porn. [VIDEO] I seem to recall that basically all lists of top search terms are filtered. Any raw list would be at least half dirty."}]},"LfmAG4dk-rU":{"wy":"HN cites it as an example of Autopilot failures in discussions about the limits of Tesla's driver assistance and self-driving.","m":[{"id":33998457,"author":"tim333","month":"2022-12","story":"Waymo's collision avoidance testing","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I don't know - I must have seen five or so headlines in the papers but don't really follow the stuff. Here's one [VIDEO]"},{"id":26352584,"author":"Danieru","month":"2021-03","story":"Honda's now selling the first production car with level 3 self-driving","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Autopilot attempting to murder yet another driver","text":"Here is your \"but this time it is fine\" Autopilot attempting to murder yet another driver just 9 months ago: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25896192,"author":"mrtksn","month":"2021-01","story":"Waymo CEO dismisses Tesla self-driving plan: “This is not how it works”","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"There are some spectacular failures that are very scary because the car does something that a person would never do","text":"People die all the time, there are even higher profile cases like the Apple Engineer who slammed in the barriers at full speed: [LINK] There are some spectacular failures that are very scary because the car does something that a person would never do, unless unconscious, like this one: [VIDEO] You don't hear much about it probably because Tesla fanboys are plenty and rabid, so people avoid talking about it online. The defence is usually stats about human drivers crashing more often and it makes sense until you dive deeper into the numbers because these stats are usually oranges v.s. Apples. It feels like they have some playbook with statistics to slap when someone says something negative. If that doesn't cut, they say that the victim should have followed the manual that says \"your attention should always be on the road\" then proceed posting a video about how thanks to the latest update they can sleep drive to work and attach a banana to the driving wheel to disable the attention safeguards. If someone asks how is this an autopilot, there are usually two ways to handle it: 1) Autopilot is just a brand name, the self driving software is in beta, so the victim should pay attention all the times. 2) Autopilot is like on the planes, so only fools think that it is autonomous, therefore it was working as intended but they should have been using it like an airline autopilot. Crash due to user error. I'm actually a fan of Musk and Tesla but I feel like the community engagement is very"},{"id":23384459,"author":"dragontamer","month":"2020-06","story":"Tesla Model 3 Drives into Overturned Truck in Apparent Autopilot Failure","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"my conclusion is that the M3 doesn't even brake","text":"[VIDEO] There are no brake lights on the Model 3. None at all. Based on this \"reverse video\", my conclusion is that the M3 doesn't even brake. ------- Somewhat worrying is the pedestrian (the truck driver?) who jumps back to get out of the way of the M3. Not only did the M3 miss the truck, but the M3 didn't seem to see the pedestrian either."}]},"MShv_74FNWU":{"wy":"It is posted as a joke whenever threads discuss unit confusion, bad numeracy or customer service failures.","m":[{"id":45512614,"author":"burnt-resistor","month":"2025-10","story":"Oilfield Units: a Measurement System so Cursed it made me Change Careers","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"made me immediately think of Verizon math","text":"Hearing about decimal feet tape being recorded directly as X'Y\" (feet inches format) made me immediately think of Verizon math: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35344141,"author":"ftxbro","month":"2023-03","story":"ChatGPT Outperforms Crowd-Workers for Text-Annotation Tasks","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It's this ancient meme, where a guy records a customer service call with Verizon","text":"I was nitpicking about 0.06 dollars versus 0.06 cents. It's this ancient meme, where a guy records a customer service call with Verizon where they have some back-and-forth about whether these are the same or not. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31083166,"author":"forferdet","month":"2022-04","story":"Everyone gets numbers wrong, even the New York Times","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This reminds me of this hysterically funny customer service call to Verizon","text":"This reminds me of this hysterically funny customer service call to Verizon: [VIDEO] More of the backstory: [LINK]"},{"id":31083671,"author":"tmp_cond_trav","month":"2022-04","story":"Everyone gets numbers wrong, even the New York Times","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"it was definitely worth it. Very funny!","text":"Ha, never heard of this before. I had to hunt the audio down (found it at [VIDEO] ) but it was definitely worth it. Very funny! Thank you!"},{"id":28193257,"author":"jsploit","month":"2021-08","story":"The time Pepsi got sued for a $33M fighter jet","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Reminded me of this classic recording [0] of Verizon Customer Service failing to understand the difference between cents and dollars.","text":"Reminded me of this classic recording [0] of Verizon Customer Service failing to understand the difference between cents and dollars. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":10210413,"author":"zintinio3","month":"2015-09","story":"The Math I Learned After I Thought Had Already Learned Math","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Verizon Math [3]","text":"Americans generally struggle with mathematics, and not just advanced concepts. The problem is cultural, because it is acceptable to be poor at math, it's viewed as wholly unnecessary. Some examples are A&W's Third Pounder vs McDonald's Quarter Pounder (3 < 4 so quarter pounder is bigger) [1], how long does it take to go 80 miles if you're driving 80 miles per hour [2], and Verizon Math [3]. As for Chinese struggling with math, I can give you firsthand experience at my American University, where the foreign (Chinese and Indian) students are known for rampant cheating, in both the Undergraduate and Graduate levels. We have a serious problem with logic, math, and science. People who are good at the three are ridiculed and alienated rather than celebrated, although it's not a hard rule, just something I've noticed. Reading for pleasure is the exception rather than something normal. We're very anti-intellectual once you get out of the big cities. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"}]},"Pdk2cJpSXLg":{"wy":"HN mentions it mostly as a joke or illustration of new iOS versions slowing older iPhones.","m":[{"id":2254612,"author":"recoiledsnake","month":"2011-02","story":"Microsoft Open XML embarrassment: spaces go missing between words","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":">Who said anything about Netscape? You did, in a way. By saying this: >>I think Microsoft's view of software quality has contaminated the industry. >They couldn't hire programmers to correctly implement their own spec years after it being published? Throwing bodies at something doesn't make it right in software engineering. Haven't you heard of bugs and issues in Google's or Apple's products? After all, Apple is a bigger company now. I was going to link to some Apple bugs but Apple Discussions was down (cue 'why can't a 200+ billion company keep their forums up? Can't they hire more people to fix it?' ) Unable to add beyond 100 pages to document(maybe they need to hire more people to hit 200?) [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":1518877,"author":"klinquist","month":"2010-07","story":"IOS 4.01, iPad 3.2.1 Released","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Reports show that it is no faster, unfortunately. If you haven't seen it, here's a parody of the original iPhone commercial showing a 3G on iOS4: [VIDEO]"},{"id":2452631,"author":"Pewpewarrows","month":"2011-04","story":"Android 3.0 tablets allegedly delayed due to low hopes","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"this is what happens when you try to put new versions of iOS on older iPhones","text":"For reference, this is what happens when you try to put new versions of iOS on older iPhones: [VIDEO] I think you have your argument reversed. You're beholden to Apple for all your product updates. With an Android phone you're free to update with whatever ROM you feel like (official from the manufacturer or not)."}]},"_hnOCUkbix0":{"wy":"Mostly cited as a joke or as the commenter's old laptop, in threads about Apple laptops.","m":[{"id":5805208,"author":"claudius","month":"2013-06","story":"Ask HN: Which computer/laptop do you use?","stance":"reference","why":"Commenter's previous laptop, the Lenovo X300","quote":"","text":"Hrm, that's what I feared. I was a proud owner of a [0] before this one, but even the Core i520M slowly gets…slow, especially one of the newer graphics chips would be wonderful sometimes. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20452902,"author":"Dunedan","month":"2019-07","story":"Jony Ive: 8 hits and 8 misses from 20 years at Apple","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Lenovo made a really good parody of the original MacBook Air ad","text":"Back then Lenovo made a really good parody of the original MacBook Air ad ( [LINK] ), showing the advantages of their ThinkPad X300: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4105529,"author":"LinXitoW","month":"2012-06","story":"New Apple Macbook Pro RAM is soldered to the motherboard | Ian Chilton","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This ad satire is about the Air","text":"Single connector, but not single adapter. Daisy chaining also seems error prone. If all the adapters you have to take with you weigh 400g, you haven't really gained anything. Also, having to reattach everything all the time seems like a hassle if you have a lot of external devices to connect(is there a Macbook dock?). This ad satire is about the Air, but i think it sums up me reservations perfectly: [VIDEO]"}]},"abapFJN6glo":{"wy":"Shared in dial-up nostalgia threads as a way to both hear and understand the handshake.","q":{"x":"And this version combining the graphic and the sound used to make the graphic","i":44843763,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":46412290,"author":"tcbawo","month":"2025-12","story":"Dialtone – AOL 3.0 Server","stance":"reference","why":"Recording of the modem handshake sound.","quote":"I came across this recording","text":"The dial tone was prior to dialing. Upon connection, there was an audible sound from the modem handshake. Through some google searches, I came across this recording: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44843763,"author":"gregsadetsky","month":"2025-08","story":"Dial-up Internet to be discontinued","stance":"reference","why":"Combines the explanatory graphic with the modem sound.","quote":"And this version combining the graphic and the sound used to make the graphic","text":"I like this explanatory graphic (after many many years of listening to the dialup sounds) - [LINK] And this version combining the graphic and the sound used to make the graphic: [VIDEO] And this alternative version (h/t @Kye): [LINK]"},{"id":15637913,"author":"muxator","month":"2017-11","story":"The sound of the dialup, pictured (2012)","stance":"reference","why":"Animation of the modem handshake picture.","quote":"Animation of the picture illustrated in this article","text":"3d spectrogram: [LINK] Animation of the picture illustrated in this article: [VIDEO] If you live with someone >= 30, turn up the volume and wait for him/her to appear at the door for a nostalgia moment..."}]},"lskpNmUl8yQ":{"wy":"HN cites it as an example of how even sharp people dismissed a transformative technology, often invoked in crypto debates.","q":{"x":"Similarly people said the web was useless in the 90s [0]","i":29499132,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29499132,"author":"fossuser","month":"2021-12","story":"The biggest crypto lending company is a ponzi scheme","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as an example of people dismissing the web in the 90s","quote":"Similarly people said the web was useless in the 90s [0]","text":"> “ By constraining it to transfer of an intermediate representation you're not making a good faith representation of the process.” Yeah, I’m betting on the future utility enabled by the tech, not what it can do right now. Similarly people said the web was useless in the 90s [0] when the future utility (imo) was obvious. > “Second, the only thing you can do better with crypto than a centralized exchange is crime, grift and regulatory arbitrage.” There are non-crime advantages to being able to use decentralized systems. These are most obvious in places with hostile governments and unstable currencies. The new stuff you can do with decentralized state and public auditable records is super new and the applications there I think will be really interesting. > “ What takes longer sometimes is AML/KYC and security.” Is this true? I thought there was a complex system of clearing houses, record updates, etc. a lot of which is done manually? Am I wrong? > “Like any other regulatory arbitrage you can certainly make things faster or cheaper, for instance by dumping chemical waste into lakes. Once again comparing a car to a little buggy with a 2-stroke engine and no catalytic converter on the basis of fuel efficiency is disingenuous too.” I think you’re failing to steelman this. It’s possible to build a lot of stuff to handle things like KYC in a way that’s better because of crypto tech. Imagine using an NFT as a pseudonymous ID that you KYC once and then can use anywhere unless it change"},{"id":16366902,"author":"mseebach","month":"2018-02","story":"2018 Annual Letter from Bill and Melinda Gates","stance":"reference","why":"1995 Gates interview showing he did not yet grasp the internet","quote":"I really like this interview (Bill Gates on Letterman in 1995).","text":"Microsoft did a lot of ruthless things and even some illegal things, yes, but it requires a very unkind reading of history to deny the absolutely huge role they played in bring about the world we take for granted today. It's a common, but not at all obviously correct, analysis that where we are today was manifest destiny and thus Bill Gates and Microsoft was just randomly the medium providence chose to carry out this destiny. Microsoft got so incredibly many things right, and they started flagging almost the second they got a major thing wrong (the internet). I really like this interview (Bill Gates on Letterman in 1995). He says the internet is a big deal, but he clearly doesn't get it , not does he provide any sort of thought leadership. Baseball and cigars? Snowcrash had been out for three years by then. [VIDEO]"},{"id":14554183,"author":"moduspol","month":"2017-06","story":"The blockchain paradox: Why DLTs may do little to transform the economy","stance":"reference","why":"Bill Gates explains the internet to Letterman, who does not get it","quote":"Watch this video [1] of Bill Gates explaining the value of the Internet to David Letterman, where he's just not getting it at all.","text":"Watch this video [1] of Bill Gates explaining the value of the Internet to David Letterman, where he's just not getting it at all. Bill's telling him the things it can do relative to David's interests, but he just sees those as problems that don't really exist because they've already been solved. It took decades before the Internet was useful to \"the masses.\" I don't think there's some conspiracy among VCs, but I do think dismissing cryptocurrencies for the reasons you have is short-sighted. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21504782,"author":"moduspol","month":"2019-11","story":"Tesla Owners Can Edit Maps to Improve Summon Routes","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Reminds me of Bill Gates trying to explain the value of the Internet to David Letterman. \"Does 'radio' ring a bell? 'Tape recorders' ring a bell?\" [VIDEO]"}]},"mJLr0KMsRAA":{"wy":"Cited in surveillance threads as a demonstration of wide-area aerial tracking that existed years ago.","m":[{"id":38326647,"author":"spyder","month":"2023-11","story":"Lockheed is now tracking phones and walkie-talkies from space","stance":"reference","why":"Aerial surveillance capturing a murder.","quote":"a video about it capturing a murder","text":"Yep, a video about it capturing a murder (blurry video from 7 years ago I'm sure it's even better today): [VIDEO]"},{"id":33364335,"author":"rogers18445","month":"2022-10","story":"Extracting Zooming Shots from 600 Hrs of Police Helicopter Surveillance Footage","stance":"reference","why":"Wide-area aerial surveillance capability from years ago.","quote":"AFAIK this was done with commercially mass produced silicon, and it was 10+ years ago.","text":"If the article writer is concerned about some helicopter footage, he would have an aneurysm over this: [VIDEO] AFAIK this was done with commercially mass produced silicon, and it was 10+ years ago. Nowadays they can probably do 10x+ better."},{"id":23081295,"author":"LargoLasskhyfv","month":"2020-05","story":"Surveillance plane starts flying over Baltimore","stance":"reference","why":"Aerial surveillance firm recording crimes in Baltimore.","quote":"","text":"Would a demo of what is possible at 4000 feet suffice? [LINK] (4min33sec) Or what was state of the art at DARPA in 2013? [LINK] (4min40sec) Edit: Or more commonly available, just search for \"Nikon Superzoom\" on youtube, and watch some short demos there. And then imagine those pointed in all directions downwards, the videostream(s) saved to some small storage array, or even transmitted live to some ground station(s), indexed and overlaid on something like google maps, just better, with a time slider. Mark some car at some point (in time) and see where it moves...and so on. Edit: like THIS [LINK] (3min13sec) Edit: Oh! And something from 2016 about \"The Surveillance Firm Recording Crimes From Baltimore's Skies\" (7min29sec) [LINK] Another demo of the same thing from the same person/company in another town, also 2016: \"The Eyes in the Sky That Catch Murders on Tape\" [VIDEO] (5min) So why is this news?"}]},"qHepKd38pr0":{"wy":"Used as the canonical reference for the 'enhance' trope whenever image upscaling or recognition comes up.","q":{"x":"the grand daddy of all enhance scenes","i":7311122,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":8842067,"author":"dmix","month":"2015-01","story":"Identifiable Images of Bystanders Extracted from Corneal Reflections (2013)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Chinatown (food/texts), the girls outfit (being a dancer), and an identifying tattoo","text":"Yes in the 3 minute scene there were multiple indications from the picture to indicate: Chinatown (food/texts), the girls outfit (being a dancer), and an identifying tattoo. Which would point to only a few dance clubs in the city. It was also a connection to the artificial snake scale being found in the bath tub that happened to be rare and only created by a few possible engineers. Engineers who work on robotics. One Chinatown engineer happened to be employed by the people he was looking for, who were robots in need of an engineer... Not a far fetched connection by Hollywood's standards. [VIDEO]"},{"id":5586262,"author":"anigbrowl","month":"2013-04","story":"Facial Recognition Failed in Boston Bombing","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"those jokes refer to a scene in Blade Runner","text":"\"zoom in, enhance\" feature much mocked about on TV crime shows Actually, those jokes refer to a scene in Blade Runner : [VIDEO]"},{"id":46285966,"author":"carabiner","month":"2025-12","story":"SHARP, an approach to photorealistic view synthesis from a single image","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Or if you prefer Blade Runner","text":"Black Mirror episode portraying what this could do: [LINK] . If Apple ran SHARP on this photo and compared it to the show, that would be incredible. Or if you prefer Blade Runner: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7311122,"author":"pesenti","month":"2014-02","story":"Depixelizing Pixel Art","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"the grand daddy of all enhance scenes","text":"I can't believe you are linking to CSI instead of Blade Runner, the grand daddy of all enhance scenes [VIDEO] makes me feel old..."}]},"rMz7JBRbmNo":{"wy":"Mostly used as a joke or parody reference, and occasionally as the canonical illustration of leveled, second-order thinking.","m":[{"id":32470103,"author":"morley","month":"2022-08","story":"Second-Order Thinking","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates leveled thinking in competitive games.","quote":"There's no better illustration of what you're talking about than the poison scene in Princess Bride","text":"Leveled thinking is common in competitive games, for obvious reasons. There's no better illustration of what you're talking about than the poison scene in Princess Bride: [VIDEO] The way I've heard most competitive gamers talk about the line between leveling and tail-chasing is to play at level 2: Level 1: The obvious strategy Level 2: Strategy that beats the obvious strategy <-- Default to this level Level 3: Strategy that beats level 1 The problem is, if you're playing in a competitive environment, everyone knows about leveling, so you may actually gain more edge by picking a strategy randomly. I've heard competitive Magic player Huey Jensen would make random plays when he was behind on the chance he could throw his opponent off and trap them in leveling second-guessing."},{"id":44829723,"author":"FrustratedMonky","month":"2025-08","story":"Open models by OpenAI","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"it feels like this","text":"I can't argue with any of that, since technically everyone could have been playing everyone else. Maybe Republicans are easily fooled, or maybe Trump is playing 5-d chess and playing both sides. I just think 'playing like a fiddle' typically means a lopsided power dynamic where one person has much more knowledge, or skill. So I'd assume it was implying Democrats were in a superior position. Not, that Democrats just got lucky once. This going back and forth pointing fingers about who was playing , seems like too many layers deep. it feels like this [VIDEO]"},{"id":38378066,"author":"cableshaft","month":"2023-11","story":"We have reached an agreement in principle for Sam to return to OpenAI","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It's not assassination. It's a Princess Bride Battle of Wits","text":"It's not assassination. It's a Princess Bride Battle of Wits, that they initiated and put the poison into one of the chalices themselves, and then thought so highly of their intellect they ended up choosing and drinking the chalice that had the poison in it. Corresponding Princess Bride scene: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36962182,"author":"samstave","month":"2023-08","story":"A Room-Temperature Superconductor? New Developments","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"YOU IDIOT! W need the gold to turn the slaves back into carbon,m so we can make more slaves to harvest the gold!!* - [VIDEO] -- I know we hate jokes - but apropos"},{"id":37200790,"author":"js2","month":"2023-08","story":"Modernity has made us allergic","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Boy, you teed this right up, didn't you?","text":"Boy, you teed this right up, didn't you? \"Alright, where is the poison? The battle of wits has begun. It ends when you decide and we both drink, and find out who is right, and who is dead.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":33966744,"author":"krapp","month":"2022-12","story":"Picol, a Tcl interpreter in 550 lines of C code","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It's a parody comment based on the poison goblet scene from the movie \"The Princess Bride.","text":"It's a parody comment based on the poison goblet scene from the movie \"The Princess Bride.\" [VIDEO]"}]},"uXlQuTRSmzc":{"wy":"Linked as an example of the honest, self-aware way Tom Scott treats VPN ads and their legitimate use cases.","m":[{"id":41945290,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2024-10","story":"Post World War II Food","stance":"reference","why":"Tom Scott's ad read with more honest VPN claims","quote":"","text":"> Given the amount of advertising for vpns you might think they are a scam. Tom Scott did a video in 2019 entitled \"This Video Is Sponsored By [redacted] VPN\" where he explains why a lot of the ad copy at that time was often misleading, and why he didn't take money from them: * [LINK] In 2022 he made video with an ad read from a VPN provider with more honest claims about their use cases: * [VIDEO]"},{"id":35152853,"author":"throw0101b","month":"2023-03","story":"NordVPN library and client code open-sourced","stance":"reference","why":"Tom Scott's follow-up on legitimate VPN use cases","quote":"","text":"This reminds me of the 'original' 2019 video by Tom Scott called \"This Video Is Sponsored By [redacted] VPN\" where he explains most of the reasons listed to use VPN by ads is useless: * [LINK] And then a few years later with \"My robot double sells out (so I don't have to)\" he did a follow-up listing useful reasons (geo-based content, better prices on vacation sites, etc) which was sponsored by NordVPN: * [VIDEO]"},{"id":35152900,"author":"throw0101b","month":"2023-03","story":"NordVPN library and client code open-sourced","stance":"reference","why":"Tom Scott's follow-up on legitimate VPN use cases","quote":"","text":"> [LINK] This is Tom Scott's 'original' 2019 video called \"This Video Is Sponsored By [redacted] VPN\" where he explains most of the reasons listed to use VPN by ads is useless. However a few years later with \"My robot double sells out (so I don't have to)\" he did a follow-up listing useful reasons (geo-based content, better prices on vacation sites, etc) in which he was sponsored by NordVPN: * [VIDEO]"},{"id":34438214,"author":"ben_w","month":"2023-01","story":"Boston Dynamics’ bipedal robot Atlas can now grab and toss, like humans","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Maybe, but based on what I saw in my mother and grandmother in their old age, I think androids in general (not just Boston Dynamic) have a long way to go just on the \"accidentally terrifying\" front, let alone capabilities, for example this is the wrong side of uncanny valley for me: [VIDEO]"}]},"xNzXze5Yza8":{"wy":"Cited critically as an example of US advertising that glorifies overwork and lack of holiday time.","m":[{"id":44490274,"author":"EGreg","month":"2025-07","story":"Nobody has a personality anymore: we are products with labels","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"If we want to reduce the incidence of distress in people and families, we need approach all these issues with compassion and an open mind. The open mind means considering various possibilities, while still being rational. Yes, one theory is that everything “just happens to be” innate from birth, and unchangeable, and we simply discover it later. This essentialist type of theory may in fact be the best one for extreme cases of any condition. But we have to be very careful applying essentialism across the board — is it true for even mild, increasinly widespread cases? Could we also not investigate the factors upstream of the issue? Not just before birth, but also in the environment, in the socialization, culture, economics, etc. And if we find factors that heavily seem to affect it, should we not consider trying to fix the problems upstream such that the “disorders” downstream become reduced in many cases? In the case of gender dysphoria, and to a lesser extent of depression being caused by serotonin, is too politically charged to start with. Many extremely creative people we know were born biologically male - the Wachowskis, Justine who made redbean, etc. transitioned their identity. They are adults who make a decision to live in ways they feel are more authentic to themselves amid the current culture and technology and that is their choice, as it should be. But we have to be careful not to therefore say there are no major issues in society upstream of millions of people who a"},{"id":41825031,"author":"EGreg","month":"2024-10","story":"Psilocybin bests SSRI for major depression in first long-term comparison","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Didnt a lot of studies finally come out by 2022 showing that SSRIs are not actually effective for most people, no more than a placebo? And the whole link between serotonin and depression was spurious… 2021: [LINK] 2022: [LINK] 2023: [LINK] In any case, I believe that ADHD, Depression and many other “umbrella” psychiatric diagnoses are being overdiagnosed, much like “hysteria” was for hundreds of years (eg 1 in 4 middle-aged women is on antidepressants now, and the opioid epidemic in men was largely exacerbated by the pharma industry, as has the overdiagnosis of ADHD prescribing amphetamines to kids). To be fair, the actual incidence of phychiatric conditions from ADHD to autism to depression has increased, as well as autoimmune disorders, diabetes etc. although not as much as the pharma industry wants us to believe. It could very well be the result of upstream changes in nutrition (eg wild increases in sugars like fructose across the board, antibiotic overuse on factory farms, buildup of microplastics everywhere etc.), and to a large degree society (including loneliness, dating, relationships, work, etc) and medicating them downstream (eg with amphetamines or opioids) is just a bandaid. Late stage capitalism has had a direct role in exacerbating this. For example, the tech industry (which many on HN are involved in) has redefined dating, relationships, job searches, political discourse, and much more. All of these affect how people interact. A seminal book even back in 2001 w"},{"id":37610672,"author":"EGreg","month":"2023-09","story":"The Age of the Grift Shift","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This is what happens in capitalism. There's profit to be made educating the masses, and setting yourself up for social capital and intellectual capital by being a thought leader . In fact, these centrally controlled Big Tech platforms share this profit with influencers who help get eyeballs and engagement . On the startup side, venture capitalists encourage startups to fail fast and remove friction aka propping up money-losing economics, so they can have an IPO to wall street stockholders. After which the company must extract rents from its ecosystem and report every quarter to its shareholder class how well it's doing that. At every level, the system is designed to extract money from the masses of people. Whether it's \"I will teach you how to flip a house\" in the real estate bubble, to \"I will teach you how to do AI stuff\". The entire system optimizes for that. People might say \"what, are you advocating communism\"? Well, the alternative doesn't have to be that. It can be a universal basic income providing a floor to people, financed by pigovian taxes on fossil fuels (already done in Alaska for decades), plastic pollution, tolls for congestion of roads, etc. While at the same time subsidizing open source software. If the government did these two things, it would massively improve society. As the UBI would grow, we would not have so much need for labor unions, minimum wage laws, etc. to protect workers. People would spend time with their own families, learn new things, practic"},{"id":37282376,"author":"EGreg","month":"2023-08","story":"Social media does not cause depression in children","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"No, because mass media was carefully curating its images to children (eg Sesame Street, Mr Rogers, Fox Kids, Animaniacs, Batman, PG-13 etc) and its profit motive wasn’t aligned with an algorithm that is designed to get people addicted to social media. Social media totally changed the expectations for teens… just like selling in a flea market vs selling on Amazon, you’re now competing against the whole world. Actually, there was a profit motive in children’s programming, it was for merchandise, and also had predictable results as capitalism always has: [LINK] [LINK] Many of the children’s shows (ahem, YuGiOh, Pokemon) were explicitly around selling shit to them, but at least it was external and not about their body image and social goals and expectations. Actually, the best analogy I can give, in terms of destructive power, to the TikTok/Instagram effect on teen girls, was the Gangsta Rap effect on teen boys (typically Black boys because that is who was selected by the music industry to be promoted). And with the same industry always claiming there is “no effect”, while an entire generation started cussin’ and acting much more violently than their parents. Sure, there are of course other socioeconomic factors, such as the rise of single-parent households typically without a father in the home, or both parents working long hours and neglecting the kids. That cannot be dismissed either. But that is also a result of corporations and their profit motive, selling a collective dream"},{"id":34783530,"author":"EGreg","month":"2023-02","story":"Female teen depression at all time highs","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I suppose the common thread is that they are all outspoken Black people, from different walks of life, who have all recognized that corrosive influences are coming from certain capitalist entertainment industries and contributing to cultural/moral degradation as a negative externality made in the name of profit. Cosby spent YEARS playing \"America's dad\" for the Black family, as did James Avery, Reginald VelJohnson and others. They provided a wholesome role model to look up to, of a married family man, who provides for his kids and teaches them manners and morals, as opposed to \"role models\" poppin bottles and bangin models that the rap industry provides. Misogynistic lyrics, racial slurs, materialistic lifestyles, sometimes even glorifying violence. What good does it do? If outsiders can't criticize it because it is \"authentic Black expression\", then it falls to Black voices to do so. And they do, increasingly. To try to combat moral / cultural degradation and restore family values takes both economic and cultural reform. This used to be the subject of Black sit coms: [LINK] And even CNN anchor Don Lemon used to OPENLY say stuff like this: [LINK] So Steven A Smith said the same: [LINK] Deloris Tucker: [LINK] I mean, I think you're tilting at windmills here when you're going after everyone's moral character who has said similar things. I could give you a lot more examples, but they haven't and don't make a dent in the capitalist machine. It has adapted. The reason it's on topi"},{"id":43413930,"author":"EGreg","month":"2025-03","story":"","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"while corporations have them climbing the corporate ladder","text":"What's toxic is the system, for both men and women. A lot of the modern feminists and redpillers are anti-marriage, and advocate for men and women to never get married, for various reasons. Usually both gender warriors claim it's an oppressive institution. Meanwhile people have less children, and neglect their children, while corporations have them climbing the corporate ladder: [VIDEO] I was there at Sheryl Sandberg's event when released her book \"Lean In\", about how women can do better climbing the corporate ladder. The culture now teaches kids to find life's meaning in something that will soon be automated by AI anyway. We'll have to figure out other ways of connecting again, spend time with our kids instead of sending them to government schools, take care of our parents instead of sticking them in nursing homes, etc. The problems are upstream (social networks, microplastics, factory farms, sugar, etc.) causing record teenage sadness, diabetes, autoimmune diseases, etc. The current solutions in USA are mostly medication downstream: amphetamines for kids with ADHD, SSRI inhibitors for depression, ozempic for diabetes, vaccines for a coronavirus that mostly affected people with diabetes / obesity (a real persistent epidemic that is hardly addressed at its root cause). This is what you should be worried about, in my opinion: [LINK] And the reason that boys end up looking to redpillers, blackpillers, MRAs, and guys the Tates etc. is because the system failed them. Dating sucks"},{"id":38421914,"author":"EGreg","month":"2023-11","story":"Hunter-gatherer childcare suggests new key to mother and child well-being","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Sometimes the mask comes off and they do it too openly","text":"Corporations, just like governments, engage in pervasive propaganda, from impressionable school years all the way to adulthood. Edward Burnays said propaganda got a bad name after WW1, so they renamed it to \"PR\". It's how corporations got women to smoke, too: [LINK] How they control the crowd, featuring Edward Burnays and his uncle Freud (whose theories became famous ironically because of his nephew): [LINK] How the corporate world market to kids: [LINK] They marketed it to Black youth: [LINK] They marketed it to teen girls: [LINK] Sometimes the mask comes off and they do it too openly: [VIDEO] The for-profit model of news corporations and social networks has led more and more adults to become perpetually angry and tribal politically: [LINK] There is even a term for what they resort to in order to please shareholders: surveillance capitalism: [LINK] I was at the event in NYC when Sheryl Sandberg unveiled her book, \"Lean In\": [LINK] Make no mistake. YCombinator and shareholder capitalism leads to this inevitably. Corporations have to please shareholders forever. And there is more and more push for inclusivity and part of that means adapting people from traditional roles (e.g. women homemakers) into types of work that benefit the market. As both sexes flooded the labor pool, wages got depressed, and marriage declined. People are distracted into being e.g. gender warriors (Red Pill vs Feminism) rather than realizing they are all being exploited by a for-profit capitalist system "},{"id":34973939,"author":"EGreg","month":"2023-02","story":"Future Fords Could Repossess Themselves and Drive Away If You Miss Payments","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"And sometimes they dial it to an 11","text":"This is like talking about \"de\"Platforming... why doesn't anyone talk about how these people GOT their platform and why people kept giving them a platform? Capitalism. \"re\"Possess? How about talking about Possessing in the first place? Why do we have so much \"garbage\" parked on our streets, namely cars that are owned but not used? Just like the American Dream of owning a home (and working 30 years to pay the money you rented to do that), or De Beers diamonds, or College, or whatever, there is a concerted effort to make you crave that thing and make that big-ticket purchase. Mainly to give into all the social pressure mechanisms. The car manufacturer industry relies on this kind of stuff -- to sell more cars. And sometimes they dial it to an 11: [VIDEO] And the fossil fuel industry had relied for decades on colluding with them to make sure cars are locked into one type of fuel (fossil fuels). All your other appliances have long ago been able to take electricity -- so they can be powered by hydro, geothermal, solar, wind, nuclear fission, and even fusion as soon as it's enabled. But not cars, no. And government of course worked hand-in-hand with industry to subsidize fossil fuels and delay electric cars and battery tech: [LINK] The only people that this article would alarm are those who believe they want to own a self-driving car. Most people would just time-share them, and self-driving cars would be operated by Uber (another hyper-capitalist company that currently takes 50% of"},{"id":34576924,"author":"EGreg","month":"2023-01","story":"Ejaculation Frequency and Prostate Cancer","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"there are even crass commercials that dial up the capitalism memes to an 11","text":"Well, there can be various explanations, but France is not an underdeveloped country, neither is Finland. Their medical systems do report ADHD, although they might not so zealously overdiagnose it. French parents are more involved with their children, have far lower divorce rates, and work shorter hours than USA. Having \"no time\" is a point of national pride in the USA and there are even crass commercials that dial up the capitalism memes to an 11: [VIDEO] Here are possible reasons why kids have ADHD: 1) The world is far more fast-paced. ADULTS haven't fared much better, and you'd hardly argue that everyone is genetically predisposed to having the attention span of a goldfish... it's clearly a societal thing: [LINK] 2) Technology affects society. Corporations are incentivized to put ever more ads in front of you, and now we all have cell phones in our pocket, connected to the internet at all times. Being distracted at dinner, notifications you have to check to make sure it's not important, etc. 3) Now that people can be reached anytime by their phone, any SMS or email causes anxiety that maybe it's from your boss, etc. 4) Even the tools that HN loves so much are often detrimental. Real-time is a gimmick, and studies show that Slack, etc. is actually worse for mental well-being and productivity overall. Here is a recent NYTimes article: [LINK] Again, ALL THESE THINGS are related to how capitalism and technology affects society and all of us. The very things that we are Hacker "},{"id":32224957,"author":"EGreg","month":"2022-07","story":"BMW’s Heated Seats as a Service Model Has Drivers Seeking Hacks","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Look at these modern ads specifically to US Americans, making them PROUD of how little free time they have","text":"That was talking about land, and Georgists were discussing creating one land tax. How many Americans own land? What is the distribution? Oh yeah: [LINK] Money is the new land. They are making more of it but they know everyone needs it, and the banking system has a monopoly on it. The “American Dream” was promoted was a way to get the plebs to take out large mortgages (to finance home construction, etc.) and be nice little workers and pay it off (this creates demand for the bank’s finance products and by extension the dollar). At least back then, only one parent worked long hours for corporations, because the culture mostly had women be homemakers and raise kids. Once both sexes started flooding the labor market, wages got repressed and corporations started promoting a new “Lean in” version of the “American Dream” to the men AND women… now everyone’s self-worth and satisfaction was supposed to be determined by climbing the corporate ladder: [LINK] Look at these modern ads specifically to US Americans, making them PROUD of how little free time they have… they don’t even try to hide it: [VIDEO] This doesn’t lead to more happiness for the population, as measured."},{"id":23476282,"author":"EGreg","month":"2020-06","story":"No Recent Automation Revolution","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Things become more fulfilling when you see everyone else doing them, such as raising kids. There are so many things people could be doing which are not compensated by the market and corporations. “Leaning in and earning $1 instead of 70c” is a meme that corporations use to get more humans to trade quality time with parents and kids for them. [1] Why don’t we ask about “Leaning out and spending more time with our families?” [2] Open source Science Philosophy Family Personal development Volunteering in community You’ve been CONDITIONED to think that large corporations are better ways to allocate your time and organize us all. That’s why many in NYC lamented when Amazon was not given tax breaks to come to our city. It’s almost as if we need Bezos’ company to organize ourselves an our time so we can get paid the money. Society spends tons on fossil fuels and commuting and for what? To sit in a chair 30 miles away? 1. [VIDEO] 2. [LINK]"},{"id":20510940,"author":"CodeMage","month":"2019-07","story":"Want to hire the best programmers? Offer growth","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"> So yeah, I detest this sense of entitlement that programmers have, where they want to a) get paid a lot and b) have lots of free time on the side. That mentality reminds me of the 2014 commercial for Cadillac ELR Coupe: [VIDEO] It's not entitlement to want work-life balance and be paid well for your skills."},{"id":13938470,"author":"gambiting","month":"2017-03","story":"The Gig Economy Celebrates Working To Excess","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"They openly ridicule the idea that Europeans can take a month of holiday per year.","text":"There's a Cadillac ad for US, where the narrator openly says that those Europeans enjoy their holidays, but an American works hard and gets to enjoy nice car cue cadillac car enters the scene . They openly ridicule the idea that Europeans can take a month of holiday per year. I do feel a bit sorry for that attitude. [VIDEO]"},{"id":9988160,"author":"hans0l074","month":"2015-08","story":"Living in Switzerland ruined me for America and its lousy work culture","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Since 2001 I've lived and worked in Finland, Denmark & Sweden. Achieving a healthy & meaningful work-life balance is emphasized in the Nordic countries (I do not know about Luxembourg) The authors experience in Switzerland strikes a familiar chord with me. I have close friends in the Valley who envy me because I get 6 weeks a year off. I don't think this article is necessarily about high salaries and the luxuries you can afford with that salary (and I'm not saying that that was what you were saying) but I have come to believe that there is a better work-life balance in certain northern european countries than anywhere in the U.S.A (I'm willing to be corrected of course) especially when it comes to maternity and paternity leave. In discussions like these, I am reminded of a video my American friend sent me a while ago : [VIDEO]"}]},"zqyZ9bFl_qg":{"wy":"Shared as a visualization of Ralph Merkle's theoretical work on molecular manufacturing.","q":{"x":"Ralph Merkle (of public key cryptograpy fame) has done some recent theoretical work","i":9541625,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":37965154,"author":"adastra22","month":"2023-10","story":"International team develops novel DNA nano engine","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"A machine scaled down by 1/10th in each linear dimension is able to operate at 10x speed.","text":"Yes, gigahertz operation speeds. This is a straightforward result from mechanical scaling laws. A machine scaled down by 1/10th in each linear dimension is able to operate at 10x speed. So a machine operating at 10Hz in ~1m regime (perfectly reasonable in an industrial setting) will be able to operate at 1GHz speeds when scaled down to be ~10nm in size. Biology operates by means of Brownian motion. Enzymes randomly jiggle around as they are bombarded in solution, until by totally random chance one of the reactants they catalyze hits in the right spot and sticks, then waits until the other reactant hits the other spot. This can take a very, very long time to happen. Animations you might watch showing their operation are lies: they show reactants sliding into place, reactions happening, and products being ejected. This is not what happens. If you actually watched a molecular dynamics simulation of a typical enzymatic reaction, and slowed it down just enough that you could see molecules moving around, it would take YEARS to watch typical reactions happen. For the fastest reactions, the ones you mention, maybe weeks or months. Biology works by molecules just randomly jostling around, sometimes moving forward and sometimes moving backward, but a small set of one-way reactions driving things forward over extended time. Kinesin, for example, has no process that drives its motion forward. It relies on random jostling to move its \"feet\" into the next position, at which point it can re"},{"id":14958636,"author":"EugeneAZ","month":"2017-08","story":"Percentage of Europeans Who Are Willing to Fight a War for Their Country","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Man. They are all corrupted or just too ignorant. J.R.Nyquist can tell you even more : ) Have you ever heard of thista? For any engineering problem one can make a \"brute-force\" algorithm, which theoretically finds its solution, describe that algorithm as a logical scheme (a directed graph with logic gates as nodes) and then one can use an AI agent to make that logical scheme easier to compute, by simplifying (through transformation rules like the De Morgan rule) unnecessary computation steps, input variables, etc. The solution to the problem can be found by calculation of that simplified version of the logical scheme. The AI agent may be built from Neural Nets, or from the algorithm, which I call Evolution of Neural Graphs [0]. Some of interesting engineering problems are: - to find a machine code for a controller of a bipedal robot [1], which makes it able to work in warehouses and factories; - to find a machine code for a multiprocessor system [2], which behaves as an AI agent purposed for a given work to be done; - to find a CAD file [3], which describes the design of a spheromak working with MHD generators; - to find a file [4], which describes the manufacturing steps to produce the first molecular nanofactory in the world; - etc… Officials of what country would take it seriously, can you guess? [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [VIDEO] I have a reddit account, you may discuss it there too."},{"id":9541625,"author":"chriswarbo","month":"2015-05","story":"There's Plenty of Room at the Bottom (1959)","stance":"reference","why":"Animation of Merkle's nanofactory concept","quote":"Ralph Merkle (of public key cryptograpy fame) has done some recent theoretical work","text":"Drexler's name is synonymous with the idea of a nano-robots and self-replication. I think he also coined the term \"clanking replicator\" to describe a traditional robotic approach to replication (eg. RepRaps, robot arms, etc.) Ralph Merkle (of public key cryptograpy fame) has done some recent theoretical work, eg. his on \"nanofactory\", which consists of exponentially-smaller gantry-like systems (a bit like the print head on a cartesian 3D printer). He's also looked at \"mechanochemistry\" (forming molecules by moving specific atoms together in the right ways) and concrete studies of particular molecules, the energy levels and forces required, etc. His most recent work I can find on Google Scholar are patents from a few years ago. Some links: [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] Those are all \"grey goo\" ideas, ie. 'real' machines. There's also the \"green goo\" approach, repurposing biological systems like DNA, proteins and ribosomes, eg. [LINK]"}]}});
